HT_DATA.load("detail", {"qCGofLIzX6g":{"wy":"It is HN's standard answer to why Python cannot easily get much faster, and is recommended by people who work on JITs and alternative runtimes.","q":{"x":"This excellent talk describes how the semantics of the \"add\" operation are much more complex than generally described.","i":28706370,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":30048445,"author":"fault1","month":"2022-01","story":"Faster CPython (2021) [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Enlightening presentation on Python's leaky internals.","quote":"I thought this presentation by Armin Ronacher (the creator of Flask and Jinja) was very enlightening","text":"I thought this presentation by Armin Ronacher (the creator of Flask and Jinja) was very enlightening: How Python was Shaped by leaky Internals(2016): [VIDEO] See also this HN post (Attempts to make Python fast): [LINK]"},{"id":29165476,"author":"haberman","month":"2021-11","story":"Pyjion – A Python JIT Compiler","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deep dive into Python's special-case semantics like a + b.","quote":"I really recommend the talk I linked","text":"I think \"a + b\" is a good example. I really recommend the talk I linked ( [VIDEO] ), it gets deep into this stuff. I don't think anyone benefits from \"a + b\" having so many special cases."},{"id":28706370,"author":"haberman","month":"2021-09","story":"Writing Python Extensions in Assembly","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows how complex Python's add operation semantics really are.","quote":"This excellent talk describes how the semantics of the \"add\" operation are much more complex than generally described.","text":"Python is difficult to optimize due to complex language semantics. This excellent talk describes how the semantics of the \"add\" operation are much more complex than generally described. a+b is not just a.__add__(b), you have to read 200+ lines of CPython interpreter code to fully resolve its semantics: [VIDEO] Not saying it's impossible, but it's more of an uphill battle than optimizing other languages that have more regular semantics. Python is also constrained by its C API, which is a very low-level and exposes a lot of implementation details to C extensions. Changing the API could improve performance but it would also make Python incompatible with a large ecosystem of C extensions. I sometimes wish for Python to define a new C API that exposes far less, and to begin a long-term migration towards the new API so that eventually the interpreter would be less constrained. It would be a large migration, but unlike Python 2 -> 3 it wouldn't affect everyday users, only extension authors. I don't know if it will ever happen, but a person can dream."},{"id":24850511,"author":"arc776","month":"2020-10","story":"Attempts to make Python fast","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Details limits of Python optimization.","quote":"I highly recommend the two videos posted here","text":"As the OP states: > mutable interpreter frames, global interpreter locks, shared global state, type slots On top of this, Python is extremely dynamic and nothing can be assured without running through the code. So this leads to needing JITs to improve performance which then give a slow start up time and increased complexity. Even with JIT, Python is just not fast thanks to the above issues and it's overall dynamism. It can be optimised and for sure there's some impressive attempts at doing so. However I don't think pure Python will ever be considered \"fast\" as these things necessarily get in the way. I highly recommend the two videos posted here that go into more detail as to why there are limits to how far optimisation can go: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":24497980,"author":"nerdponx","month":"2020-09","story":"Python's Innards: Introduction (2010)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Accompaniment to article on Python's leaky internals.","quote":"I highly recommend this talk by Armin Ronacher","text":"I highly recommend this talk by Armin Ronacher as an accompaniment (or prologue, or epilogue) to the article: \"How Python was shaped by leaky internals\", [VIDEO]"},{"id":22290241,"author":"eigenspace","month":"2020-02","story":"JuliaLang: The Ingredients for a Composable Programming Language","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great talk on fundamental problems in Python semantics.","quote":"Here's a great talk from the author of the famous Flask library in Python","text":"As others have mentioned, Python does have JIT compilers. The problem is that havign a JIT doesn't solve the problem. PyPy is often a factor of 10 behind julia performance and projects like Numba, PyTorch (the PyTorch people had to build their own Python JIT compiler yikes!), etc. will always have a more restricted scope than a project like Julia because Python's very semantics make many optimizations impossible. Here's a great talk from the author of the famous Flask library in Python: [VIDEO] where he discusses the fundamental problems with Python's semantics. If you fix these problems, you will end up changing Python so fundamentaly that you'll really have a new language. Generic CPython 3._ code will certainly not be compatible with it."},{"id":13320285,"author":"ericfrederich","month":"2017-01","story":"Grumpy: Go running Python","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows CPython internals lack a spec and hinder new runtimes.","quote":"This talk shows how bad of shape it's really in.","text":"Python needs a new runtime. This talk shows how bad of shape it's really in. [VIDEO] Basically, the language doesn't have a \"spec\" per-se. The language is whatever the defacto CPython implementation happens to do within it's giant eval loop. Another great talk about CPython internals: [LINK]"},{"id":43111277,"author":"maxwelljoslyn","month":"2025-02","story":"A tail calling interpreter for Python (already landed in CPython)","stance":"rec","why":"Fascinating look at the __add__ path and interpreter difficulties.","quote":"I recommend this talk by Armin Ronacher (Flask creator)","text":"VWWHFSfQ, you may already know this, but: I recommend this talk by Armin Ronacher (Flask creator) on how Python's implementation internals contribute to the difficulties of making Python faster. [VIDEO] One case study Ronacher gets into is the torturous path taken through the Python interpreter (runtime?) when you evaluate `__add__`. Fascinating stuff."},{"id":37424530,"author":"gobdovan","month":"2023-09","story":"Mojo: It’s Finally Here","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why Python can't run much faster without losing features.","quote":"This video [0] from the creator of Flask explains it quite well.","text":"You can't have Python run that much faster without loosing lots of features. I guess if they open sourced it, people wouldn't be that impressed with what they implemented. This video [0] from the creator of Flask explains it quite well. Here [1] is Guido talking about Mojo being in early days and sharing some technical details. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":32384850,"author":"afiori","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: Best way to learn advanced Python?","stance":"rec","why":"Helps make sense of confusing Python language quirks.","quote":"They won't teach you Python, but they might help in making sense of the confusing bits you might encounter","text":"I know very little about Python, but I would suggest these talks about a few ways in which Python is unique. They won't teach you Python, but they might help in making sense of the confusing bits you might encounter: How Python was Shaped by leaky Internals, Armin Ronacher: [VIDEO] What Does It Take To Be An Expert At Python?: [LINK] and maybe also When Python Practices Go Wrong: [LINK]"},{"id":27572949,"author":"eigenspace","month":"2021-06","story":"Is Julia Really Fast?","stance":"rec","why":"Semantic choices in Python make many optimizations impossible.","quote":"There's a really nice talk here from the creator of the Flask framework","text":"Python's classes can change dynamically though during runtime. So each iteration of a loop would need to check if the shape has changed. I'm no Python expert though, so please correct me if I'm mistaken. But it's more than that. There's a really nice talk here from the creator of the Flask framework about a bunch of different semantic choices in the language design, leak out into the ecosystem and make many optimizations impossible: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26081001,"author":"nemothekid","month":"2021-02","story":"Node.js 14 is over 20x faster than Python3.8 for fib(n)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why Python internals make JIT difficult.","quote":"Armin Ronacher, author Flask, actually has a good talk about this.","text":"Armin Ronacher, author Flask, actually has a good talk about this. The gist of it the way Python's internals leak into the language makes it very difficult to build a performant JIT that wouldn't break a large amount of userspace code. Python lets you do _far_ more shenanigans that Javascript does; and a lot of large libraries depend on some of that behavior. Breaking it would probably cause a new 2 -> 3 situation. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24849302,"author":"ilyagr","month":"2020-10","story":"Attempts to make Python fast","stance":"rec","why":"Informative on the C API and why Python is hard to speed up.","quote":"I found the following talks by Armin Romacher very informative on these topics","text":"I found the following talks by Armin Romacher very informative on these topics (C API, why python is more difficult to speed up than JS). [VIDEO] [LINK] I wish these were the things Python 3 addressed, rather than Unicode. I guess it's much more obvious in hindsight than back when Python 3 was designed."},{"id":20672199,"author":"ssivark","month":"2019-08","story":"Python Is Eating the World","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting talk by Python veteran on language internals.","quote":"I found interesting a couple of talks by Python veteran Armin Ronacher","text":"Python's success is richly deserved. The fact that it has been a long time coming (Python is almost 30 years old!) is an indication of the amount of sheer effort involved, and should give heart for all those working on building up the next generation of languages. Since this forum is likely to have some folks looking towards the future, I found interesting a couple of talks by Python veteran Armin Ronacher [1, 2]. Watching that presentation makes me wonder whether I should think of this in the context of \"Worse is better\". That said, Python's runtime introspection is such a killer feature that it's difficult to explain how empowering it feels for someone who is relatively new to programming. [1]: How Python was Shaped by leaky Internals -- [VIDEO] [2]: A Python for Future Generations -- [LINK]"},{"id":17684602,"author":"ssivark","month":"2018-08","story":"How Numba and Cython speed up Python code","stance":"rec","why":"Why Python's design resists performance optimization.","quote":"on why the design of python makes it fundamentally unfriendly to performance optimizations","text":"There's a reason most high-performance Python libraries are not written that way, and core routines are just written in C instead. Proof of the pudding is in the eating! See this talk by Armin Ronacher (creator of Flask) on why the design of python makes it fundamentally unfriendly to performance optimizations: [VIDEO] Julia has be designed ground up to avoid several such problems. See this discussion: [LINK] If your domain falls under the umbrella of numerical and scientific computing, writing Julia is as painless as writing python, with code that automatically runs roughly as fast as C. If you're used to writing numpy, you can hit the ground running in Julia, with maybe a few hours to become comfortable with the slightly different syntax and the names of useful libraries."},{"id":17210160,"author":"StefanKarpinski","month":"2018-06","story":"My favorite things that are coming with Julia 1.0","stance":"rec","why":"Explains what makes Python hard to optimize.","quote":"Perhaps the best way to understand what makes Julia fast is to watch these two videos","text":"Perhaps the best way to understand what makes Julia fast is to watch these two videos about Python and R and what makes them so hard to optimize: [VIDEO] [LINK] Take everything mentioned in these videos that make Python and R really hard to optimize and don't do those things :D"},{"id":14580036,"author":"dom0","month":"2017-06","story":"RTL MJIT – Register transfer language VM and JIT for Ruby","stance":"rec","why":"Covers a long list of Python implementation problems.","quote":"I'll just refer you to this talk","text":"Instead of typing a long list, I'll just refer you to this talk: [VIDEO] Some of that has been fixed / got make up applied to play pretend."},{"id":13320956,"author":"the_duke","month":"2017-01","story":"Grumpy: Go running Python","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why CPython can't improve on many things.","quote":"It explains why CPython can't improve on many things.","text":"Someone else already posted this link: [VIDEO] It explains why CPython can't improve on many things."},{"id":24851986,"author":"dralley","month":"2020-10","story":"Attempts to make Python fast","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the author's in-depth talks on Python speed.","quote":"he's given entire talks on this subject.","text":"You're arguing with mitsuhiko, he's given entire talks on this subject. [VIDEO] PyPy is faster for pure Python code, but that comes at the expense of having a far slower interface with C code. There's an entire ecosystem built around the fact that while Python itself is slow, it can very easily interface with native code (Numpy, Scipy, OpenCV) with very little overhead. So sure, you can make Python much faster, if you're willing to piss off the very Python users who care the most about performance in the first place (the data science / ML people and anyone else using native extensions)."},{"id":14941475,"author":"dom0","month":"2017-08","story":"Python Oddities","stance":"reference","why":"Somewhat related video on Python oddities.","quote":"Somewhat related video","text":"Some of these are pretty simple and not odd, e.g. immutability of tuples, iterator behaviour or modifying collections during iteration. ————————————————————————————————————— Somewhat related video: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["IeSu_odkI5I",3],["kc9HwsxE1OY",2],["iph500cPK28",1],["wo84LFzx5nI",1],["YSfCdBBqNXY",1],["tiwVMrTLUWg",1],["36myc8wQhLo",1],["gCWtkvDQ2ZI",1],["o_AIw9bGogo",1],["YR5WdGrpoug",1],["mSgXWpvQEHE",1],["dmWQtI3DFFo",1],["U7VwtOrwceo",1],["wrwgIjBUYVc",1],["rCfPQLVzus4",1],["n8m7lFQ3njk",1],["uGlzRt-FYto",1],["39iKLwlUqBo",1],["Qi1Yry33TQE",1],["HStF1RJOyxI",1]]},"o_AIw9bGogo":{"wy":"The most-recommended balanced take on systemd, from a BSD perspective, handed to anyone caught up in the systemd debate.","q":{"x":"is an interesting talk on this topic from the view of someone (Benno Rice) in BSD land. Highly recommended.","i":41175576,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":45314736,"author":"nickysielicki","month":"2025-09","story":"Systemd can be a cause of restrictions on daemons","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues systemd manages services; unified service management beats the alternative.","quote":"you should really watch this talk, particularly 12:45 through 16:20","text":"I linked it elsewhere in this thread, but you should really watch this talk, particularly 12:45 through 16:20: [VIDEO] tl;dr: systemd isn't meant to be an init system, it's meant to manage services, and the alternative world where you don't have a unified system for managing services and events actually sucks."},{"id":42886297,"author":"fermigier","month":"2025-01","story":"Sixos: A nix OS without systemd [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Insightful, entertaining, and not only negative about systemd.","quote":"There is a wonderful talk by Benno Rice a few years ago. Insightful and entertaining.","text":"There is a wonderful talk by Benno Rice a few years ago. Insightful and entertaining. Not focussed only on the negatives, BTW. [LINK] [VIDEO] HN discusion: [LINK] LWN comments: [LINK]"},{"id":41175576,"author":"SahAssar","month":"2024-08","story":"Systemd, 10 years later: a historical and technical retrospective (2020)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Systemd discussed from BSD perspective.","quote":"is an interesting talk on this topic from the view of someone (Benno Rice) in BSD land. Highly recommended.","text":"[VIDEO] (The Tragedy of systemd) is an interesting talk on this topic from the view of someone (Benno Rice) in BSD land. Highly recommended. EDIT: this is mentioned in the article, thanks koalacola"},{"id":40024465,"author":"TimeBearingDown","month":"2024-04","story":"Ubuntu 24.04 (and Debian) removed libsystemd from SSH server dependencies","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent and amusing discussion of systemd's technical merits.","quote":"has an excellent and amusing discussion of systemd’s technical merits.","text":"It’s interesting you say that? It runs completely counter to my experience coming from the “BSD-style” rc.d init Arch used to use, and migrating to systemd. Any specific issues? I didn’t see any. No offense. One factor may be that Arch prioritizes not patching upstream - helped save them from targeting here, and it doesn’t go overboard with default configs, which I’ve long appreciated. Not to distro-war, I’m very grateful for Debian. My background is finding Linux in the mid-00s and breaking many SuSE, Ubuntu, and one or two Debian systems before finding something I could understand, repair, and maintain in 2008 Arch. systemd accentuated its ability to stay relevant with enterprise Linux, made it even easier to package for, and has been a useful tool in diagnosing service issues and managing bad software for me. I’m not sure how often it’s posted here but Benno Rice formerly of FreeBSD Core Team has an excellent and amusing discussion of systemd’s technical merits. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40169433,"author":"eropple","month":"2024-04","story":"A BSD person tries Alpine Linux","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fair BSD-grounded view of systemd strengths and weaknesses.","quote":"Benno Rice has an excellent presentation on systemd that's worth watching through to the end","text":"> Turning Linux into Windows by replicating svchost.exe shouldn't be applauded by the Linux community. ... We've got Linux's PID 1 (for most distros) controlled by a MS employee, who replicated Windows' svchost.exe. And people are all excited? systemd was pretty consciously patterned after launchd, not svchost. The goal was, and for good reasons, to make Linux behave like a more integrated Unix-like that already existed: MacOS. Benno Rice has an excellent presentation on systemd that's worth watching through to the end; unlike most of the table-pounding (and \"it's just svchost.exe!!\" is exactly that), he provides what I think is a pretty fair--and, interestingly to me, a BSD-grounded--view as to where systemd is strong and is weak. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41574215,"author":"kemotep","month":"2024-09","story":"A word about systemd (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Fairly covers positives, negatives, and post-systemd world challenges.","quote":"I think it covers in a fair shake the positives, negatives","text":"If you haven’t seen this talk[0], I think it covers in a fair shake the positives, negatives, and what even a post-systemd world would have to deal with. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38891561,"author":"nerdponx","month":"2024-01","story":"Systemd through the eyes of a musl distribution maintainer","stance":"rec","why":"Reasonable and balanced take on systemd.","quote":"I always appreciated this presentation for being a reasonable and balanced take","text":"I always appreciated this presentation for being a reasonable and balanced take, if you're interested in other examples: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38642418,"author":"jimmaswell","month":"2023-12","story":"Xorg being removed. What does this mean?","stance":"rec","why":"Made systemd seem reasonable to a neutral viewer.","quote":"systemd seems reasonable enough after considering it.","text":"I never had any horse in systemd/init.d and wasn't too familiar with the issue until I watched this presentation. systemd seems reasonable enough after considering it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37209098,"author":"klysm","month":"2023-08","story":"Version 5 of systemd-free Debian remix Devuan is here","stance":"rec","why":"History of anti-systemd feelings.","quote":"I found this presentation useful","text":"For those looking to understand some of the history of the anti-systemd feelings, I found this presentation useful: The Tragedy of systemd ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":29929791,"author":"IntelMiner","month":"2022-01","story":"Slackware Linux 15.0 RC3","stance":"rec","why":"FreeBSD developer on the hatred systemd attracts.","quote":"There was a great talk by a FreeBSD developer about systemd","text":"There was a great talk by a FreeBSD developer about systemd, and specifically the (in his eyes, somewhat unwarranted) hatred it has attracted and why [VIDEO]"},{"id":27650562,"author":"Ballas","month":"2021-06","story":"Avoiding Complexity with Systemd","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting explanation of why systemd exists.","quote":"I found this an interesting explanation to the why question","text":"You have probably already seen this, but I found this an interesting explanation to the why question: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26052355,"author":"mapgrep","month":"2021-02","story":"FreeBSD 13.0 Beta1 Now Available","stance":"rec","why":"Why integrated init systems are common and sweeping in scope.","quote":"This is a good talk I saw recently (by a FreeBSD contributor)","text":"Wouldn’t have to be systemd, macOS has launchd for init, FreeBSD could come up with its own thing. This is a good talk I saw recently (by a FreeBSD contributor) on why these sorts of systems have become more common, and why they seem so sweeping in scope. [VIDEO] (In fact systemd and launchd are so tailored to their respective operating systems that FreeBSD likely would have to do their own init replacement if they cared to.)"},{"id":25266952,"author":"bromonkey","month":"2020-12","story":"macOS to FreeBSD migration a.k.a. why I left macOS","stance":"rec","why":"Informed view of systemd from a BSD developer.","quote":"I suggest anyone that thinks systemd is terrible and doesn't spend lots of time in Linux to watch this presentation by Benno Rice.","text":"\"Linux has systemd\" and that's why the author didn't select it supposedly. I hear this line of thought from people that use FreeBSD often, although I suspect if you ask many of those people why systemd is so horrible you won't actually hear a legitimate (and still relevant) reason from them. I suggest anyone that thinks systemd is terrible and doesn't spend lots of time in Linux to watch this presentation by Benno Rice. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19960362,"author":"masklinn","month":"2019-05","story":"Linux distros without systemd","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting, enlightening BSD perspective on systemd controversy.","quote":"I found Benno Rice's The Tragedy of Systemd[0] interesting and enlightening.","text":"> SystemD definitely spurred some controversies years ago but is it that bad in 2019? The reasons for the controversies have not really changed (lennart, the idea that it's not \"unixy\", a takeover of many subsystems by various systemd components, …) so people who didn't like systemd years ago probably still don't. As somebody who's not really involved, I found Benno Rice's The Tragedy of Systemd[0] interesting and enlightening. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19960611,"author":"holstvoogd","month":"2019-05","story":"Linux distros without systemd","stance":"rec","why":"Gives perspective on systemd hate.","quote":"I recommend watching this to give you some perspective on the SystemD hate","text":"I recommend watching this to give you some perspective on the SystemD hate: [VIDEO] I too was furious about the existence of systemd, but now I realize that was mostly because I don't want new things and consider many new things to be bad because they are new and there have been a few 'bad apples' in my personal experience"},{"id":19208676,"author":"isostatic","month":"2019-02","story":"The Tragedy of Systemd [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Section on change explains resistance to systemd.","quote":"I think that the section on change at is great.","text":"I think that the section on change at is great. The first 1500 seconds are about systemd any why it is and what it's trying to do, but that doesn't really get to the core of why I complain about it. [VIDEO] Systemd is change. Massive change that's being forced on us by a twat. Unlike Linux, which was a kernel delivered by a twat but was take-it or leave-it, this is being forced on us. It's untrustworthy and threatening and unknown. In some cases it's an attack on our identity -- \"the way you did it in the past is rubbish, do it this way\". Clearly we are going to be defensive about it. This isn't unique to people who've used linux for the last 3 decades. Douglas Adams knew this 20 years ago: 1) everything that’s already in the world when you’re born is just normal; 2) anything that gets invented between then and before you turn thirty is incredibly exciting and creative and with any luck you can make a career out of it; 3) anything that gets invented after you’re thirty is against the natural order of things and the beginning of the end of civilization as we know it until it’s been around for about ten years when it gradually turns out to be alright really.” His original article (in a newspaper - remember them?) is at [LINK] He mentions John Humphrys and his sneering view of this new fangled internet. Humphrys was (and still is) the bbc radio 4 breakfast presenter. At the same time Terry Wogan was the radio 2 breakfast presenter, a similar age (about 60 at the time). His attit"},{"id":38427143,"author":"Levitating","month":"2023-11","story":"PipeWire 1.0.0","stance":"reference","why":"Benno Rice's idea of a system layer between kernel and userspace.","quote":"I like what Benno Rice said[1] concerning a third \"system\" layer other than kernel and userspace.","text":"I like what Benno Rice said[1] concerning a third \"system\" layer other than kernel and userspace. Systemd tries to fill that space entirely by providing a suite of tools that integrate well with another. But you are not forced to use these all of these, you're not forced to use resolved over resolvconf. > It should only do one thing well. I know this is a popular principle that some believe to be part of the unix philosophy. But an OS should also be coherent. Some tools can do their own thing and be independent from the rest of the system. But I think this works best for simple userland tools, not tightly integrated system services. In any case I really like resolved. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25015443,"author":"znpy","month":"2020-11","story":"Ask HN: What stops you from using BSD as main workstation OS?","stance":"reference","why":"Why BSDs lack system management layer like systemd.","quote":"BSDs lack that completely -- see","text":"- Hardware support out of the box I can be given a work laptop, slap some Debian or derivative along with XFCE, and I know that most hardware will work out of the box without much fuss (including but not limited to: webcams, video cards, wireless/bluetooth, thunderbolt docking station). - Containers They're just very good. FreeBSD looks like it has most of the pieces to get containers going but it's not their priority to get that going. The people at Joyend did that, reimplementing the docker server APIs to manage underlying zones. I wonder why don't FreeBSD do something similar. - Reference documentation I like to use RHEL and derivatives (CentOS) when I can, and the reference documentation its so, so, so good , ample and complete. The FreeBSD reference guide is good, but not as thorough and/or up to date as the RHEL documentation. - systemd & NetworkManager They're rock solid, battle tested and well-thought. systemd is much more than init and does a lot of system management, stuff that previously was simply not being done by anything else. BSDs lack that completely -- see [VIDEO]"},{"id":21315192,"author":"dijit","month":"2019-10","story":"Why Did ArchLinux Embrace Systemd? (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"which is the same talk","text":"oooooo, the grandparents video is very different in tone than the version I watched, which is the same talk: [VIDEO] I'm sorry, I had assumed I had seen the exact video, but I had just seen the talk from another venue."},{"id":20376645,"author":"AsyncAwait","month":"2019-07","story":"The death watch for the X Window System has probably started","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why sysvinit is not a real systemd alternative.","quote":"(watch if you don't know why)","text":"Additionally, no alternative project is even providing close to the set of features that i.e. systemd or pulseaudio does, not even via modules. sysvinit for example is only an alternative to systemd the same way a SUV is an alternative to F1. (watch [VIDEO] if you don't know why)"}],"r":[["UiS27feX8o0",1],["SxdOUGdseq4",1],["h47zZrqjgLc",1],["mbEfzuCLoAQ",1],["WVDQEoe6ZWY",1],["06yy88tLWlg",1],["stxVBJem3Rs",1],["5pYeoZaoWrA",1],["G8cbIWMv0rI",1],["CTV-wwszGw8",1],["UmKf8smvGsA",1],["qCGofLIzX6g",1],["gCWtkvDQ2ZI",1],["YR5WdGrpoug",1],["SEwTjZvy8vw",1],["wM8Xli2KTzI",1],["SCAGc-rkIfo",1]]},"D1R-jKKp3NA":{"wy":"HN returns to it for years as an inspirational talk to send to graduates, founders and anyone at a career crossroads.","q":{"x":"This is no doubt one of the best speech i ever heard.","i":595537,"y":"2009"},"m":[{"id":6968266,"author":"jesseendahl","month":"2013-12","story":"Ask HN: How do I develop fanatical resolve?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"","quote":"If you haven't seen it, I highly recommend it.","text":"The single best motivator for me is remembering that I'm going to die one day. I think often about the commencement speech Steve Jobs made in 2005. If you haven't seen it, I highly recommend it. Here's a link: [VIDEO] I still allow myself time to relax/slack off, but if I had intended to only watch a few episodes before getting down to working on something I'm passionate about, and then have the urge to just do the easy thing and keep watching instead of doing whatever I'd planned to do, I remember that my time is slowly running out and if I don't take steps to achieve my dreams now, then I will likely never achieve them before there's no time left. This applies to everything in life, not just work. Travel, falling in love, learning new things--you name it--everything is affected by how limited your time is. Time and your body (there's a direct relationship between them) are the most limited resources you have, and even if you strike it rich you can never rewind the clock. This is what motivates me more than anything else to get shit done. On a related note, once you realize just how limited your time is, you realize how pointless it is to live your life for other people or be constrained by dogma. Anyway, at this point I'm just rehashing the speech, and Jobs says all this much better than I do. If you haven't seen it yet, go watch it. If you have seen it, go watch it again and listen very carefully to what he says."},{"id":4001112,"author":"sushilchoudhari","month":"2012-05","story":"Ask HN: What are the most inspirational blog posts you've ever read?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"","quote":"It is absolutely one of my favorites.","text":"How come no one mentioned Steve Jobs and his commencement speech at Stanford? It is absolutely one of my favorites. I watch it everytime i feel that i could use a dose of motivation. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3103729,"author":"seejay","month":"2011-10","story":"I quit my job last March and it was a bad idea.","stance":"strong_rec","why":"","quote":"worth every second!","text":"Please listen to the famous Stanford Commencement Speech by Steve Jobs. It has helped me greatly in situations I feel depressed about life. I still listen to it again and again! worth every second! Here's the youtube link [VIDEO]"},{"id":1179221,"author":"mrduncan","month":"2010-03","story":"Good Artists Copy, Great Artists Steal","stance":"strong_rec","why":"","quote":"I highly recommend it to anyone who's never heard (or seen) it.","text":"That's always been one of my favorite speeches. I highly recommend it to anyone who's never heard (or seen) it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":595537,"author":"hwijaya","month":"2009-05","story":"'You've got to find what you love'","stance":"strong_rec","why":"","quote":"This is no doubt one of the best speech i ever heard.","text":"This is no doubt one of the best speech i ever heard. It gives me a lot of new perspectives when i heard it for the first time back in 2006. Here's the video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3174,"author":"pashle","month":"2007-03","story":"'You've got to find what you love,' Jobs says (text only; video link in comments)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"","quote":"the few who've slipped through the cracks must not be denied this utterly inspiring speech","text":"Jobs' speech is about 1.5 years old. It's probably a classic for this audience, but the few who've slipped through the cracks must not be denied this utterly inspiring speech. We should be so lucky to one day be able to give a speech that chronicles a life of amazing achievements. Don't focus so much on whether what he's saying is true, of if you think he's this or that. All that matters is that if you feel something deep within you move, listen to THAT. Also, here's the YouTube video of the speech - [VIDEO] enjoy!"},{"id":3102968,"author":"reconor","month":"2011-10","story":"I quit my job last March and it was a bad idea.","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"It is a keen observation and is certainly worth listening to","text":"Your life is essentially a series of experiences unfolding in ways you are likely to never predict with any reasonable degree of accuracy. Steve Job's graduation commencement speech touches on this idea of being unable to \"connect the dots in your life\" forward, one can only do it retrospectively. It is a keen observation and is certainly worth listening to: [VIDEO] Try and minimize the amount of time you let your mind spend regretting a decision you made in March and focus more on looking forward and taking the next positive step in your life. You are only 20 and are bound to make many more decisions that you may consider to be a mistake in the short-term, but you have no idea how your life will ultimately unfold. What you once considered to be a mistake could turn out to be a great decision, even if it only serves the purpose of teaching you some lessons to grow yourself further. Kudos on your bravery to take a risk, I'm sorry to hear it isn't working out at the moment but I assure you it is totally within your domain of influence to change things around for yourself and realize new opportunities. Just take it one step at a time and try and frame the experience as simply an opportunity to grow through a challenge. I read edw519's comment and he is certainly right, we do care and things are never as bad as they may seem."},{"id":2923984,"author":"ffffruit","month":"2011-08","story":"Steve Jobs Resigns as Apple CEO (Official Letter)","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"His Standford graduation talk, always inspiring","text":"A true pioneer of the computing industry - farewell. His Standford graduation talk, always inspiring: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2726369,"author":"mahrain","month":"2011-07","story":"What should I do with my life?","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"Oh, and watch Steve Jobs at Stanford on this","text":"By, when you get to the point where you have to choose with either your heart or your head (wallet, benchmarks etc.) picking the former. Oh, and watch Steve Jobs at Stanford on this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2225394,"author":"usamaqasem","month":"2011-02","story":"Ask HN: Taking the next 6 months off, advice?","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"Check this video","text":"Cool, I will just simply tell you my opinion, in the end it is you who should make the decision, for me school is for people who don't have imagination and courage to learn by themselves. But in early age and with lack of experience it is easy to get carried away and take the wrong direction. If you already found your passion and you are sure that whatever is it, it is what you want to do for next few years of your life. Take the 6 months off and go explore in that direction and see what you get. Otherwise if you are not sure yet, stay in school and try to find what you like to do, you still have long way and if the next 6 months are not them, be patient sooner or later you will find what you like to do. Then everything is going to be clear. Explore and try to meet people and talk to them whether in school or off school. Check this video: [VIDEO] Good Luck."},{"id":593987,"author":"pistoriusp","month":"2009-05","story":"Renowned reverse engineer Fravia says goodbye","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"I like to watch Steve Jobs' Stanford commencement speech, every now and again","text":"I find it strange how the imminent demise of another makes me reflect upon myself: I found myself wondering what I would write in the same situation, and then realizing that this is a fate that I cannot escape. I like to watch Steve Jobs' Stanford commencement speech, every now and again, to remind me of that fact: [VIDEO] Transcribed: [LINK]"},{"id":421169,"author":"SapphireSun","month":"2009-01","story":"Steve Jobs speaks out about his health","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"was quite interesting and spoke deeply to me.","text":"Jobs gave an address to Stanford university's graduating class in 2005. Here's a link to youtube if you want to watch [VIDEO] was quite interesting and spoke deeply to me."},{"id":208294,"author":"wave","month":"2008-06","story":"8 Things I Wish I Knew When I Was 22","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"If you are graduating this year, please watch it and learn from his experience.","text":"I find Steve Jobs' Stanford commencement speech to be a very good real life advice. If you are graduating this year, please watch it and learn from his experience. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10752417,"author":"wsc981","month":"2015-12","story":"Sheryl Sandberg Says an MBA Doesn't Matter in Tech","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"That's also what Steve Jobs said in his commencement speech. He talked about the DTP industry not existing before the Mac, but by adding greatly designed fonts to the system a whole new market was created. And Steve got the fonts idea back from his college years, when he followed a calligraphy class at Reed college [0]. --- [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4090460,"author":"hogeberg","month":"2012-06","story":"Work on unimportant problems","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Is the main point of this article, to work on unimportant problems as you can never know how important they may actually become, essentially the same as that of Steve Jobs in the Stanford commencement speech? E.g. at the time of of taking calligraphy classes, he could not know that this would majorly impact OS font handling? [VIDEO]"},{"id":2294259,"author":"holdenc","month":"2011-03","story":"51 hours left to live","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"For some reason I am reminded for Steve Jobs' Stanford graduation speech when he says \"you will die one day.\" In the video, it seems to roll off the audience as a cliche. But, it seems to have tempered much of his career with fearlessness, and the willingness to take risks. [VIDEO]"},{"id":1667649,"author":"rblion","month":"2010-09","story":"Steve Jobs \"never had any designs. He has not designed a single project\"","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's not a myth or legend. I look up to great people that moved our species forward. Here is where I learned some of this 'crap': [VIDEO] And, the rest is from Steve Wozinack's autobiography... Steve is a polarizing figure with a vision that unites spirituality with technology. That's why Apple keeps attracting more and more attention..."},{"id":1472299,"author":"kadavy","month":"2010-06","story":"Ask HN: Can startup founders afford to spend time on \"hobbies\"?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"You shouldn't deny your curiosities, because you never know what they'll lead to.","text":"You shouldn't deny your curiosities, because you never know what they'll lead to. Steve Jobs once dropped in on a Calligraphy class, and fell in love with beautiful typography. It had no practical purpose to him at the time, but later in life, when he was building the Mac, he built that beautiful typography into it, changing computers into a design tool.* Taking time to play the piano will help you relax and think more creatively. Additionally, as your mind makes connections between notes and identifies patterns, it will find parallels in the challenges you face in your startup. *Story from Jobs's Stanford Commencement Address: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12145,"author":"amichail","month":"2007-04","story":"inspirational videos to push you forward when you're down","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Here's a commercial I like: [LINK] And a news story that you might recall: [LINK] A commencement speech: [VIDEO] Which ones are your favorites?"}],"r":[["QPKKQnijnsM",1],["HA7GwKXfJB0",1],["BLGFriOKz6U",1],["PE4gwstWhmc",1],["hQVTIJBZook",1],["ZW5_eEKEC28",1]]},"XetplHcM7aQ":{"wy":"Heavily recommended as a masterpiece of popular science and the classic illustration of how fragile interdependent systems are, trotted out in every blackout or supply chain thread.","q":{"x":"It's a masterpiece of popular science production made better with the grainy film patina of the 70s and 80s.","i":32802873,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":48100836,"author":"jh00ker","month":"2026-05","story":"The Greatest Shot in Television: James Burke Had One Chance to Nail This Scene","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Pilot episode on dependence on technology we don't understand.","quote":"Connections is one of the greatest shows in television.","text":"Connections is one of the greatest shows in television. The pilot episode is amazing, highlighting the problem with dependence on technology that we don't understand. \"Never have so many people understood so little about so much.\" Even more true today. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32802873,"author":"DoubleDerper","month":"2022-09","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best documentaries you've seen?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"A masterpiece of popular science production.","quote":"It's a masterpiece of popular science production made better with the grainy film patina of the 70s and 80s.","text":"^^^ Thank you! ^^^ Came here to reccommend Connections by James Burke and glad to see someone else has mentioned it. It's a masterpiece of popular science production made better with the grainy film patina of the 70s and 80s. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25007653,"author":"jihadjihad","month":"2020-11","story":"James Burke Interview [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows how losing electricity sends us back to the plow.","quote":"It's a little dated (1978), but it's an excellent watch even today.","text":"For the uninitiated, Connections is a documentary series from the seventies exploring the interconnectedness of technology. In the first episode, titled \"The Trigger Effect\" [0], Burke shows that if electricity were taken away from us, we'd be sent back to the plow. It's a little dated (1978), but it's an excellent watch even today. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21130908,"author":"Angostura","month":"2019-10","story":"New Studies Warn of Cataclysmic Solar Superstorms","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Original Connections series on technology and society.","quote":"I highly recommend James Burke's Connections","text":"I highly recommend James Burke's Connections (the original series if this kind of thing interests you) [VIDEO]"},{"id":20748118,"author":"jihadjihad","month":"2019-08","story":"A World Without Power (2013)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Episode on society's reliance on power and fragility without it.","quote":"Years back I was introduced to a terrific BBC show called Connections with James Burke (1978).","text":"Years back I was introduced to a terrific BBC show called Connections with James Burke (1978). The first episode, available on YouTube [0], is all about the world's reliance on power and that within a short period of time we'd be catapulted back to the plow. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":49271703,"author":"detourdog","month":"2026-08","story":"Compression Is Prediction","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation of technical development meeting the commenter's standard.","quote":"it is a presentation of technical development that meets my standard.","text":"They are not blog posts but it is a presentation of technical development that meets my standard. james burke connections [VIDEO] Tim Hunkin Secret Life of Machines [LINK] I have read plenty of blog posts that meet my standard. If I can think of one I will post it."},{"id":43830320,"author":"balazstorok","month":"2025-04","story":"Widespread power outage in Spain and Portugal","stance":"rec","why":"Classic on fragility of interdependent systems, from the 1965 blackout.","quote":"I think James Burke's classic talking about the fragility of our complex interdependent systems starting the episode from the 1965 Northeast blackout is still relevant","text":"I think James Burke's classic talking about the fragility of our complex interdependent systems starting the episode from the 1965 Northeast blackout is still relevant and an interesting watch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40627910,"author":"aspenmayer","month":"2024-06","story":"British Nuclear Warning System","stance":"rec","why":"Disturbing first episode on the 1965 NYC blackout.","quote":"the first episode of James Burke’s Connections describes some of the same concepts and themes in the context of the NYC blackout of 1965","text":"There’s definitely not a lot to like, but there is a lot to appreciate. If you want a somewhat toned down version, the first episode of James Burke’s Connections describes some of the same concepts and themes in the context of the NYC blackout of 1965, and is only somewhat less disturbing, but is easily the most upsetting episode of the otherwise charming educational program. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":33309116,"author":"gmoes","month":"2022-10","story":"Pouring the 200 inch disk at Corning Glass Works","stance":"rec","why":"Compared to the excellent How We Got to Now series.","quote":"similar to James Burke’s excellent series “Connections”.","text":"I recall visiting the museum many years ago and seeing a demo where they heated a glass rod and pulled a fiber. This was probably when fiber optics was in its infancy. It was definitely a highlight for a young science nerd. I would love to go back some day. Along those lines I highly recommend the episode of the show “How we Got to Now” about glass [0]. It tells the story of how clear glass was developed, something I always took for granted, but early glass was not clear and took a major effort to create. The show then goes into how Corning took it to a new level for fiber optics. The whole series is excellent and similar to James Burke’s excellent series “Connections”. [1] [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31771810,"author":"hn_version_0023","month":"2022-06","story":"Younger Dryas cosmic impact of 12,800 years ago (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Opening episode asks the question raised.","quote":"Your comment reminds me of the excellent series \"Connections\" by James Burke.","text":"Your comment reminds me of the excellent series \"Connections\" by James Burke. The opening episode in the series asks that exact question. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24999809,"author":"ThrowawayR2","month":"2020-11","story":"How Do You Know When Society Is About to Fall Apart?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what happens when interlinked systems break down.","quote":"Watch episode one of James Burke's excellent documentary \"Connections\" to get an idea of what occurs when that breaks down","text":"> \" we're not going to stop being able to produce enough food to feed everyone in the US \" The Great Depression? Hyperinflation in the Weimar Republic? I'm sure there are modern examples as well. There may be a plentitude of food and other products but if the societal and economic structures that process it and get it to where it needs to be collapse, that food might as well be on the far side of the moon. And even producing that food is questionable. Modern production is reliant on mechanical and electronic systems nowadays, even farming. Without replacement parts and consumable supplies, everything rapidly grinds to a halt. We saw an example of this in the recent COVID-19 shortages; lack of packaging and other consumables from overseas blocked domestic production of products even when the actual content of the product was still available. Everything is interlinked today. Everything. Watch episode one of James Burke's excellent documentary \"Connections\" to get an idea of what occurs when that breaks down: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25180400,"author":"sxp","month":"2020-11","story":"We have no idea what happens next","stance":"rec","why":"Introduction to Connections-style history.","quote":"For those who haven't experienced Connections-style history, four episodes are on YouTube","text":"I completely agree. For those who haven't experienced Connections-style history, four episodes are on YouTube: [VIDEO] My favorite is this one about proto-computers that came about due to the Black Death: [LINK]"},{"id":21060741,"author":"notthemessiah","month":"2019-09","story":"Advice for a climate change apocalypse","stance":"rec","why":"Burke on how fragile complex infrastructure is.","quote":"he talks about how fragile our complex infrastructure can be","text":"John Baez's blog has a series on how civilizations collapse, first looking at the Anasazi, and then how one might model it using agent-based modeling and also looking at general collapse through differential equations: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] James Burke is a science historian and BBC documentarian, and in his first episode of \"Connections\" a series on the nature of innovation, he talks about how fragile our complex infrastructure can be, and, much like this article, how hopeless individualist survival skills would be once we \"leave our technological womb\". [VIDEO] In his later series, After the Warming, a 1988 documentary which imagines what it would look like in 2050 if we failed to act on climate change (in which it predicted refugee crises, wars, hurricanes, mass flooding), and looks back on thousands of years of climate shaping civilizational rise and collapse, from the Ice Age to the Nabataeans to today and beyond. It gets most things scarily right, with the notable exception of failing to see Japanese economic bubble popping in 1991. [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":27729382,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2021-07","story":"Technology Traps (1978) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Full episode context; Burke teaches viewers to make connections.","quote":"The goal of the series after all is in its title: to teach viewers how to make their own connections.","text":"Arguably, that's the rest of the programme. The next scene opens in Egypt, at the dawn of agriculture, with the invention of the plough itself. See the full episode: [VIDEO] The scene linked in this post ends at 29:33. Burke's style isn't to explicitly state all lessons and relationships. The goal of the series after all is in its title: to teach viewers how to make their own connections."},{"id":26277921,"author":"Hammershaft","month":"2021-02","story":"How prepared are we for the next giant solar flare?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The cost of a company not preparing for a catastrophe is hugely externalized, resulting in a lopsided risk equation that represents a market failure. In texas, the cost of that market failure can be measured in preventable deaths, none of which private energy companies needed to account for when managing risk. In the event of a solar flare knocking out national power for months, the cost could be the collapse of modern civilization. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24913910,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2020-10","story":"Type in the exact number of machines to proceed","stance":"reference","why":"Burke covers the 1965 cascading power cut.","quote":"(James Burke's Connections, ref. cascading power cut 1965)","text":">\" overloading the remaining servers and crashing the subsystem. The failure snowballed until \" the entire Eastern Seaboard was without power? [VIDEO] (James Burke's Connections, ref. cascading power cut 1965)"},{"id":22780625,"author":"sbuttgereit","month":"2020-04","story":"Too Much Food in the Wrong Places","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I was going to edit but I think a new comment here is more fair: The other comments about commercial vs. residential TP are good, too. Turns out our economy is complex. Think of how even this discussion about TP reveals a large scale bifurcation of how a singular, simple product is produced and supplied. Once you bring in the rest of the upstream and downstream supply chains... it's incomprehensibly complex. This gets to something I'm concerned about which is the readiness of policymakers to designate this business \"non-essential\" and so should shut down, and that business \"essential\" and should remain in operation. How much does the essential economy depend on the non-essential economy? I mentioned logistics, I bet trucks carrying TP (of either class) also depend on other, less essential products... how many of these trucks were marginal in the good times and will end up closing up shop reducing overall readily available container space? Some of my clients are companies that make use of industrial CNC machines; and I've seen some of the same machines in use in very different businesses. For example, one client made higher end bicycle parts and the other made lab equipment for bio-tech applications. One is likely considered essential, the other not: the company that makes the CNC machines will still get business from the \"essential\" business while they won't from the non-essential business. How will that CNC machine company deal with the reduced economies of scale or their ow"},{"id":22641327,"author":"sbuttgereit","month":"2020-03","story":"Truckers Facing Coronavirus Hurdles in Keeping Supply Chains Moving","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates deep connections in modern life.","quote":"Modern life depends on surprisingly deep connections.","text":"I wouldn't be surprised if it is spreading this way. If not already, then certainly in future. Having said that, a steady stream of supplies is what will allow everyone else to stay holed-up. If you can't get the supplies you need to get through the ordeal then you'll start to see individuals go out on far-and-wide \"hunting trips\" to find those supplies (at best) or you'll start to see societal breakdowns which will give Covid-19 a run for its money on lives and damages. That's more than just trucks delivering food. It may be trucks delivering electrical equipment to keep refrigeration running, to keep other trucks, moving, etc. Modern life depends on surprisingly deep connections. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["z6yL0_sDnX0",2],["8--5LwHRhjk",1],["Ilg3gGewQ5U",1],["vgT4Y30DkaA",1],["V-vFtiDYiIw",1],["gdK4y6O-llE",1],["nO5vjmDFZaI",1],["x1bX3F7uTrg",1],["CJlrbMHLBd4",1],["pUF5esTscZI",1],["hUhisi2FBuw",1],["doz1QJ7LwjA",1]]},"RIctzAQOe44":{"wy":"HN treats it as the definitive, funny and educational answer to why X could not be fixed, and links it in almost every Wayland vs X argument.","q":{"x":"incredibly funny and educational talk[1] from 2013","i":26001017,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26001017,"author":"DyslexicAtheist","month":"2021-02","story":"Wayland is not ready as a 1:1 compatible Xorg replacement just yet","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Funny, educational talk on why X is a bad tradeoff.","quote":"incredibly funny and educational talk[1] from 2013","text":"> when in fact the lead developers on Wayland are the actual Xorg maintainers. Daniel Stone[0] incredibly funny and educational talk[1] from 2013 could help many critics who missed the 3 decade long ongoing discussion of why X is bad . We've talked about this already in the mid 90ies (when there were no alternatives) and now 8 years after this popular linux.conf.au talk, people still having troubles understanding why X has always been a terrible tradeoff for both security and performance (well for literally everything). [0] [LINK] [1] The Real Story Behind Wayland and X [VIDEO] edit: BUT X IS THE UNIX WAY! << so what is the 1 thing X does, and what is it doing well?"},{"id":26002011,"author":"SXX","month":"2021-02","story":"I'm tired of this anti-Wayland horseshit","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mandatory viewing for X fans.","quote":"Mandatory video to watch for all X fans","text":"Mandatory video to watch for all X fans: > Daniel Stone ~ The real story behind Wayland and X' [VIDEO]"},{"id":24257938,"author":"jabirali","month":"2020-08","story":"Some Ugly Code Can Get Nvidia's Linux Driver Working with Accelerated XWayland","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk from someone who worked on both X and Wayland.","quote":"I remember finding this video enlightening","text":"I remember finding this video enlightening, a talk on the subject by someone who has worked on both X and Wayland: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5841247,"author":"jmhain","month":"2013-06","story":"Facts about Wayland vs X","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk covering Wayland vs X by a charismatic speaker.","quote":"He's actually a really charismatic speaker.","text":"Daniel Stone actually did a talk involving much of the same subject matter called \"The Real Story behind Wayland and X\". I'd recommend that over this article (which was partially written by the same guy). He's actually a really charismatic speaker. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47457119,"author":"nickelpro","month":"2026-03","story":"Wayland set the Linux Desktop back by 10 years","stance":"rec","why":"Full talk on the motivation for Wayland.","quote":"Daniel Stone has a whole talk on the motivation for Wayland","text":"Don't take it from me, Daniel Stone has a whole talk on the motivation for Wayland: [VIDEO] Broadly, the X Server has a bunch of capabilities which are irrelevant. The modern model is really Window <-> Compositor based, and the X Server protocol is just a pointless middle man in that exchange."},{"id":46059577,"author":"big-and-small","month":"2025-11","story":"KDE Plasma 6.8 Will Go Wayland-Exclusive in Dropping X11 Session Support","stance":"rec","why":"Explains what is wrong with X11.","quote":"This 12 years old video explains it better than anything I ever seen","text":"> I live in a cave and am not a system programmer -- what's so wrong with X11 so people need to replace it with something else instead of improving it? This 12 years old video explains it better than anything I ever seen: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31972042,"author":"indolering","month":"2022-07","story":"Wayland is not ready as a 1:1 compatible Xorg replacement just yet","stance":"rec","why":"Developer explains why X11 could not be fixed.","quote":"just go watch this XFree86/Xorg/Wayland developer's talk","text":"> Screenshots/recording should have been on the board from day one and they could have gotten to standardizing on how to handle them in oh 2013-2015? I agree that Wayland's development has taken far too long and the transition has been rocky. But what development team couldn't use more funding? Ubuntu throwing a tantrum after the ecosystem rejected Mir and not contributing to Wayland certainly didn't help. > What other OS makes you think about display servers? I am sympathetic to arguments that Wayland/Weston could provide more functionality out-of-the-box. The bazaar's fragmented mess of \"desktop environments\" is certainly not a strength compared to what the Cathedrals have produced. But Linux's uniquely modular GUI stack necessitates coordination across lots of projects to develop and adopt standards. But not doing anything and sticking with X11 would have condemned us to being stuck with an insecure, buggy, and visually glitchy mess forever. > Red Hat doesn't believe in the Linux ecosystem I have my qualms with Red Hat too, but my point was that Wayland was created by Xorg developers because they tried and failed to make X11 competitive. Seriously, just go watch this XFree86/Xorg/Wayland developer's talk [1]. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24887164,"author":"emersion","month":"2020-10","story":"The X.Org Server Is Abandonware?","stance":"rec","why":"Longtime X developer debunks Wayland misconceptions.","quote":"may debunk some misconceptions in this thread","text":"This talk \"The Real Story Behind Wayland and X\" by Daniel Stone (a longtime X developer) may debunk some misconceptions in this thread: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13196440,"author":"lomnakkus","month":"2016-12","story":"Fedora 25: With Wayland, Linux has never been easier (or more handsome)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows obsolete X11 cruft that Wayland cleared out.","quote":"See the talk by Daniel Stone[2] if you're interested in the kind of crud that's been cleared out.","text":"No. It's already basically feature-complete as compared to which parts of X11 are actually used these days[1]. X11 is burdened by absurdly obsolete extensions like drawing stippled lines, a font server, etc. See the talk by Daniel Stone[2] if you're interested in the kind of crud that's been cleared out. It's basically the kind of stuff that we will never go back to wanting in any type of server process. The trade-off between bandwidth and latency has changed fundamentally in basically all areas of computing, but especially anything to do with graphics. [1] Alright, there might be a few bits and bobs that are missing, but nothing major as witnessed by the very article under discussion. [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13278479,"author":"lvillani","month":"2016-12","story":"Ubuntu 16.04 Unity 8 current state [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of why X developers started Wayland.","quote":"provides a good overview as to why people that have been working on X11 for many many years decided to start Wayland","text":"\"X isn't network transparent anymore\" according to Daniel Stone (longtime X.org and Wayland developer): [VIDEO] If you use X11 forwarding with any application that uses a modern toolkit (Gtk+ 3, Qt 5, maybe also Gtk+ 2 and Qt 4) you are just pushing uncompressed pixmaps over the wire, as a bad substitute for VNC/RDP/etc. Xpra adds image compression on top, AFAIK, so it should be slightly better. The entire video ( [VIDEO] ) provides a good overview as to why people that have been working on X11 for many many years decided to start Wayland."},{"id":7542297,"author":"forgottenpass","month":"2014-04","story":"Xwayland","stance":"rec","why":"Better explanation of ditching X and what Wayland is.","quote":"For a better explanation on ditching X, and what Wayland is: see this talk","text":"The larger idea this fits into is getting rid of X11 all together. The inevitable replacement is Wayland.* One of the challenges of such a switch will that once you start using wayland, you'll have some older applications that cant talk to wayland, you'd have to start an X server to run that old application. If you have a wayland session on your computer, you don't want to hit ctrl-alt-8 to switch over to an X session running that single application, and then switch back and forth to wayland to use every other program. It would be nice if there was a X server that worked as a wayland client and could put the X-only application on your wayland desktop. That's Xwayland. * For a better explanation on ditching X, and what Wayland is: see this talk from one of the X dudes working on Wayland [VIDEO]"},{"id":5320197,"author":"jerf","month":"2013-03","story":"Ubuntu will switch from X window server to Mir","stance":"rec","why":"Wayland developers explain X's problems with deep experience.","quote":"The Wayland people really sound like they know what they are talking about, based on decades of experience.","text":"The Wayland people really sound like they know what they are talking about, based on decades of experience. See, for instance: [VIDEO] Are these people designing a better answer to Wayland because it's really better, or because it always looks easy when you don't intimately understand the problem space? I honestly don't know, but it's a question I'd like answered before I get excited."},{"id":5321032,"author":"runeks","month":"2013-03","story":"Ubuntu will switch from X window server to Mir","stance":"rec","why":"Explains problems with X's design.","quote":"Here's a good video explaining some of X's problems","text":"Here's a good video explaining some of X's problems: [VIDEO] Long story short: the X11 protocol (which Xorg/X is an implementation of) was created in 1980, and was made for drawing stuff like this: [LINK] The important distinction between X and any modern display server is that nowadays we separate compositor (that which composites application content into what you see on the display) and toolkit (that which draws text boxes, buttons, progress bars etc.) into two. X was designed to do both these things. This isn't practical for several reasons. So far people have worked around this issue, but many feel it's time to stop working around it (since the X codebase is a complete mess due to this \"working around\"), and develop something from scratch that does what we want for a modern desktop in 2013."},{"id":48952290,"author":"hacker_homie","month":"2026-07","story":"Frame – the first Linux Assembly X server","stance":"reference","why":"Cites Stone's talk on Wayland and X.","quote":"","text":"Daniel Stone from linux.conf.au 2013, The Real Story Behind Wayland and X [VIDEO] You can always use XWayland if you feel that way."},{"id":37573442,"author":"kevincox","month":"2023-09","story":"Fedora 40 Eyes Dropping Gnome X11 Session Support","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Core X11 being reduced to inefficient IPC.","quote":"The X11 architecture was network-transparent drawing primitives.","text":"The problem is that the X11 architecture has been dead for a decade. The X11 architecture was network-transparent drawing primitives. Almost all of modern X is done via extensions. Your drawing is done using OpenGL or some similar interface, buffers are passed using shared memory, lots of display metadata and configuration is done via extensions as well. The actual rendering is done by the window manager (assuming you are doing compositing). As Daniel Stone said, all that is left of \"Core X11\" is really bad IPC. (As the client talks to X which talks to the window manager which talks to X which talks to the client, requiring 2 round-trips for every request) [VIDEO] So saving Core X11 is a dead end. All you have is a basic IPC system with unnecessary round-trips. So all that is left to save is the extensions. You can save GLX and the Vulkan equivalent but tearing them from the IPC is leaving not much behind. So really, no. The things that could be saved are so basic (like feeding input events) that it is better to just re-implement them using a better IPC protocol. For clients that need backwards compatibility there is XWayland (although it is limited for security reasons)."},{"id":18032605,"author":"bitwize","month":"2018-09","story":"Pause/unpause any X11 application","stance":"reference","why":"Explains X's chatty protocol and weak network transparency.","quote":"See this Daniel Stone talk","text":"Not many people use X forwarding, and X isn't really network transparent anyway. The X wire protocol is so chatty that modern clients do anything they can to work around it. See this Daniel Stone talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15052661,"author":"kronos29296","month":"2017-08","story":"X: The First Fully Modular Software Disaster","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on X's origins and extension to GUIs.","quote":"Youtube talk about it","text":"Microsoft spends millions for backward compatibility and they charge a fortune for that. This is an open source project with not as much resources and the core protocol of X was never made for GUI. It was made for displaying a cute clock on the screen when there was no concept of GUI. They extended it to do something it was never meant to do. Youtube talk about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11226895,"author":"lake99","month":"2016-03","story":"Show HN: Sway, a tiling window manager and compositor for Wayland","stance":"reference","why":"Source for claims X is bloated and wasteful.","quote":"X is severely bloated in terms of CPU usage, interface complexity, and useless code.","text":"According to people in the know[1], X is severely bloated in terms of CPU usage, interface complexity, and useless code. X spends a lot of time performing useless actions, such as drawing a gray rectangle even when it knows that part of the screen is going to be rewritten by something useful. Going full-Wayland is bound to be lighter on the system. But traditional X clients run within XWayland. I don't know if XWayland offers anything more than compatibility, but going forward, as more and more toolkits and window managers wean themselves off X, \"weaker\" computers will have more resources to spend on doing useful stuff. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6331397,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2013-09","story":"Gnome Developers Re-Implement Middle-Click Paste","stance":"reference","why":"Talk the commenter has watched on Wayland's motivation.","quote":"seen the talk[1]","text":"> [href] Seen it, and seen the talk[1]. > we don't really \"use\" X these days GTK+ and QT do not use a lot of X, as they render all their widgets with (e.g.) libcairo.so and libpango-1.0.so that only treat X as a framebuffer. Unfortunately, projecting your own usage habits onto others is an easy trap to fall into. For example, one minor data point, from my desktop system - I use the built-in font server: $ rdb -q | grep 'URxvt.*font' URxvt*font: -xos4-terminus-medium-r-normal--14-140-72-72-c-80-iso10646-1 Why? Because it's (MUCH) faster to draw, doesn't slow down even remotely, and after is much clearer to read on the screen than the blurry mess[2] that pango/etc gives you. /// that said /// As I mention briefly, I was warming up to the idea of wayland. GTK+ and QT are certainly popular, and some things DO need to be fixed. The linked phoronix article (or talk) does bring up some good points. The massive amount of (serialized) round-trips to the x-server (even locally) that are mandated by the protocol is paticularly outrageous. If backwards compatibility and remoting can be maintained through some sort of extension (or whatever), then it could even be a nearly-seamless drop in. The clipboard and selection, on the other hand, have nothing to do with any of that. They aren't a performance problem, and there is no reason to remove it. In fact, given this is a big change in general, and an opportunity to fix some of the core protocols, why not /expand/ the idea to include a full "},{"id":6114969,"author":"slacka","month":"2013-07","story":"Ubuntu: One OS, one interface, all devices","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as backing for criticism of X.","quote":"","text":"First of all, the UI is not the same when in phone mode and desktop mode. In desktop mode it runs traditional Unity and in mobile mode it runs Ubuntu Touch, a gesture based UI that only looks similar to Unity. I spent some time on a BlackBerry 10, and I loved it. Gesture based UIs are the future for smartphones. I'm willing to cut Ubuntu some slack on Unity and Mir. Despite all the haters, the latest Unity is actually turning out to be a decent UI. For those of us on 16:9 monitors, a vertical taskbar was the right choice. Vertical taskbars were broken for over 10 years GNOME[1], so I can understand Shuttleworths frustration. I also love the search bar lense UI. The mistake they made was shipping Unity half-baked. Yes, it can get unresponsive on low-end hardware. But much of this comes from poor 3D driver support under Linux, and bloated, slow, and outdated X[2] and compiz. The sooner those two die, the sooner we can have a responsive modern 3D accelerated compositing window managers under Linux. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vs41JrnGaxc",1],["S2xHZPH5Sng",1],["t-O-Qdh7VvQ",1],["oI_X2cMHNe0",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["bvim4rsNHkQ",1],["yY9GAyJtuJ0",1],["bHIhgxav9LY",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1],["pTn6Ewhb27k",1],["r6Rp-uo6HmI",1],["gB9n2gHsHN4",1],["bL6hp8BKB24",1],["CaasbfdJdJg",1],["yL_-1d9OSdk",1],["kPRA0W1kECg",1],["cefnmjtAolY",1],["ar9WRwCiSr0",1],["GFMyMxMYDNk",1],["2Vrhk5OjBP8",1]]},"7Hk9jct2ozY":{"wy":"Recommended whenever people want to see the mechanical, jostling reality of molecular biology, which many find awe-inspiring.","q":{"x":"Seeing the mechanical nature of gene transciption and chemical signaling blew my mind.","i":25151104,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25151104,"author":"anm89","month":"2020-11","story":"I should have loved biology","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Animations showing mechanical nature of transcription and signaling.","quote":"Seeing the mechanical nature of gene transciption and chemical signaling blew my mind.","text":"The thing that drove this home for me were the biovisions animations which got me to go back and basically learn high school biology. Seeing the mechanical nature of gene transciption and chemical signaling blew my mind. [VIDEO] [LINK] On the other hand, I don't buy his thing about wishing his high school teacher had presented it differently. No on else can make you interested in this stuff. The best they can do is provide resources like the ones above."},{"id":48805766,"author":"jwgarber","month":"2026-07","story":"Introduction to Genomics for Engineers","stance":"rec","why":"Incredible molecular animations from WEHI.","quote":"Also gotta shout out to these incredible molecular animations by WEHI","text":"I'm working on a project in malaria genetics this summer, and I was shocked to find out that the entire analysis toolkit is entirely based on math and statistics (and some non-trivial stuff too, e.g. hidden Markov models to predict CNV). Genotype likelihoods throw an extra wrench into the process, since even basic stuff like predicting allele frequencies requires a maximum likelihood estimator instead of simple counting. This whole area was quite eye-opening, and I'm still amazed that reading billions of base-pairs in DNA sequencing reliably works. Also gotta shout out to these incredible molecular animations by WEHI: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47957888,"author":"bhagyeshsp","month":"2026-04","story":"Biology is a Burrito: A text- and visual-based journey through a living cell","stance":"rec","why":"High-fidelity 3D animation of DNA transcription and cell signalling.","quote":"What a beautiful depiction.","text":"What a beautiful depiction. Reminds me of high fidelity 3D animation videos I used to watch about DNA replication, cell signalling etc. One of the most fascinating parts to me was DNA transcription. The engineering is quite precise. Found the video I was referring to: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39863990,"author":"bmitc","month":"2024-03","story":"Is the Sun Conscious? (2021) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Shows life made of molecular machines.","quote":"Take a look at these videos","text":">> I think our brains are machines for predicting what happens next. > Technologist thinks that humans are machines (breaking news) While I also dismiss or at least be careful of the inherent biases of technocratic viewpoints, humans and life in general are very much made of many types of machines. Take a look at these videos: [LINK] [VIDEO] I.e., life is insane and bewildering."},{"id":35318481,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2023-03","story":"Close-Up of the First Mechanical Gear Ever Found in Nature","stance":"rec","why":"Close-up of gene transcription as a complex production line.","quote":"a close-up of super complex production line (gene transcription) driven by DNA strands","text":"Reminds me of this pretty common but all the more spectacular: a close-up of super complex production line (gene transcription) driven by DNA strands: [VIDEO] / [VIDEO]"},{"id":26653250,"author":"dnautics","month":"2021-03","story":"Moderna mRNA sequence released to GitHub [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Animations make molecular motion look random, from stuff blundering about.","quote":"I like the wehi videos because they take effort to make the molecular motions appear to be random","text":"I like the wehi videos because they take effort to make the molecular motions appear to be random, a result of stuff blundering about: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24975773,"author":"jcims","month":"2020-11","story":"How the Coronavirus Hacks the Immune System","stance":"rec","why":"Collection of DNA animations from a channel with great videos.","quote":"There's a collection of their DNA ones here","text":"ATP synthase is amazing. It uses the driving force from the potential gradient (aka voltage) built by the proton pump in the first video to spin at ~21,000 rpm and crank out ATP. I read that your body creates (and then consumes) about its own weight in ATP every day. This channel has a ton of great videos: [LINK] Here's one on kinetichore and mitosis (which also shows kinesin and dynein which are comically odd) - [LINK] There's a collection of their DNA ones here: [VIDEO] Here's another view of the ATP synthesis process that shows the chaos a bit more: [LINK] When I think of these systematically I just wonder what the intermediate steps were. The proton pump uses energy to create the potential gradient that ATP synthase runs on. You can't have a proton gradient without the proton pump, and without the proton gradient you don't have ATP. So these two exquisitely complex machines depend on each other and had to evolve together. It boggles my mind. Edit: Ah! Was looking for this one, sodium potassium pump - [LINK] This is how our neurons propagate signals. They are driven by ATP."},{"id":22766364,"author":"Balgair","month":"2020-04","story":"What's a virus, anyway? The bare-bones basics","stance":"rec","why":"Good molecular animations of the cell.","quote":"Some other good ones","text":"Some other good ones: [VIDEO] [LINK] The GP is right in that our intuitions aren't cutting it down at the bottom. All these animations tend to edit out the water molecules so that the audience can understand better. The proteins aren't colored blobs at all. At these scales you'd clearly see individual atoms instead of blobs. But watching a bunch of really detailed atomic ball-stick models would be confusing. And not only are the H2Os battering everything like TIE fighters in the Hoth asteroid field [0], there are ions in there too (Na, K, Cl, etc) that really effect the cell. The membranes are also edited, as many cells are porcupined with receptors like an over crowded wave pool [1]. In addition to the water/saline being edited out, most of the other proteins, vesicles, and interior membranes are also gone for the sake of clarity. There is nearly no free 'space' in the cell, think of a really crammed subway car [2], with the air being like the water/saline. But only much much tighter. The cell is more like a frantically efficient liquidy crystaly thingy than a wonky wobbly blobby poofed-up juice box. And each one is utterly deeply profoundly fascinating. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":22624139,"author":"pp19dd","month":"2020-03","story":"Covid-19 Open Research Dataset","stance":"rec","why":"Realtime DNA replication animation, humbling and awe-inspiring.","quote":"Take a few minutes to watch these videos of supercomputer simulations","text":"It takes downloading one of these files, gunzipping them, extracting the tar and opening up a JSON file at random to really understand just how distant the title of the project is from its contents. I fully realize this is about natural language processing, but... this is beyond reach. You'd have to teach a computer to become a doctor first. Feels like where AI (and computing in general) should go in case of Covid-19 and other illnesses is analyzing and simulating how the heck we work to begin with. Take a few minutes to watch these videos of supercomputer simulations that show -- fragments -- of the fundamentals of how we exist. Multi Scale Modeling of Chromatin and Nucleosomes - [LINK] DNA animation showing realtime DNA replication - [VIDEO] Somewhere in these mind-boggling processes is where the disruption called Covid-19 puts a stick in our wheels. Compared to the complexity of the simulations, abstract ideas contained in these files are so macroscopic by comparison. It's both humbling and awe-inspiring."},{"id":32036081,"author":"khaledh","month":"2022-07","story":"I should have loved biology","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"When I watch animations of how the cell works at a molecular level [0]","text":"When I watch animations of how the cell works at a molecular level [0], I can't help but wonder how can this level of sheer complexity in dna transcription, protein production, and many other supporting functions in a single cell works in perfect harmony. It's mind boggling. I admit that I'm biased, but I don't think this could have evolved through random processes. I'm a believer in Intelligent Design. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":23299187,"author":"acqq","month":"2020-05","story":"Learn Genetics","stance":"reference","why":"Gene transcription shown at actual speed.","quote":"The reconstruction of gene transcription, also played in the speed it actually happens","text":"Even if that narration is something easily relatable by software developers, the simplification is too big to allow the reasonable comprehension of the subject. So I still suggest anybody interested to really try to learn more about the actual science topics, instead of comforting themselves falsely believing they \"understood\" anything. For the start, I would suggest the nicely produced courses: [LINK] [LINK] Just to illustrate how non-intuitive our \"common sense\" is, the current estimate of the number of human cells in human body is 30,000,000,000,000 (30e12). The current estimate of the number of bacteria in human body (in the mouth and guts) is 3 times more. The size of human genome (present in each human cell) is around 3 billion (3e9) base pairs (encoding information units of DNA [LINK] ). But not all the information is in the DNA alone. The biochemical reactions happen in parallel even when a single cell is observed. The reconstruction of gene transcription, also played in the speed it actually happens: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["LQmTKxI4Wn4",2],["JvBT4XBdoUE",2],["lY8Ol2n4o4A",1],["DXYje2B04xE",1],["N3bJnKmeNJY",1],["E1AxVXt2Gv4",1],["aoczYXJeMY4",1],["bPhUhypV27w",1],["cdiD-9MMpb0",1],["krB0enBeSiE",1],["2hf9yN-oBV4",1],["7pSqk-XV2QM",1],["GhsZUZmJvaM",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["ta7nlkI5K5g",1],["UDtlvZGmHYk",1],["P-2P3MSZrBM",1],["TfYf_rPWUdY",1],["3dl9Ovwmnxw",1],["AyOAjFNPAbA",1]]},"YnWhqhNdYyk":{"wy":"HN people cite it constantly in C versus C++ debates as the case for teaching modern C++ idioms rather than C first.","q":{"x":"Not only is she a great presenter, but she makes amazing points about what is fundamentally wrong with how C++ is taught in classrooms around the globe.","i":10992494,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":31158996,"author":"tialaramex","month":"2022-04","story":"C++20 Ranges Algorithms – Non-Modifying Operations","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Smart talk by a C++ teacher against starting with C.","quote":"you should definitely listen to her","text":"If what you need is C++ (which IMNSHO it is not) then starting with C is a bad idea. Here's Kate Gregory's talk, you should definitely listen to her even if you think I'm an idiot because she actually teaches people C++ and I (thankfully) do not. [VIDEO] Now here's my rant, which is unrelated to Kate's very smart talk: C has an excuse, because it was written in the 1970s for the PDP-11. In the 1970s struct qualifies as a fancy new feature. In the 1970s a wide pointer is very expensive. In the 1970s a linked list is often a good choice of data structure. C++ comes along quite a lot later. These things are already untrue or fast becoming untrue, but rather than risk being unpopular Bjarne's language just cargo cults C. It is almost all of C, plus more... stuff. C++ does not fix any of the worst problems with C, because doing so would risk losing compatibility and hurt Bjarne's adoption story. As a result, with only a handful of exceptions all of that broken garbage is still there . C++ still has the almost useless built-in array type from C, it keeps weird syntax from C that was too hard to ban in the 1970s compiler but you should never use, its built-in types have the same stupid, confusing names like \"long\" and \"double\", it has this awful \"macro pre-processor\" which makes even trivial tooling problems very difficult and set back software quality by at least a decade. Kate's approach avoids teaching beginners about most of this. It's still there, which is bad, but at least you"},{"id":11721769,"author":"e12e","month":"2016-05","story":"Why I don't spend time with Modern C++ anymore","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent talk on introducing C++ without the C legacy.","quote":"Of special relevance is the rather excellent talk by Kate Gregory","text":"I've been spending a few days going through talks from last years cppcon[1]. They're all good, but they've all left me with a strong desire for a strongly opinionated, there-is-no-past-only-the-future introduction to C++14 (and incidentally, C11). I've added a few of those talks I've watched and liked to a small playlist (with a couple of other talks on programming, unrelated - the relevant talks are prefixed with \"cppcon 2015\"): [LINK] The full cppcon playlist is here: [LINK] Of special relevance is the rather excellent talk by Kate Gregory on \"Stop Teaching C\" (when you're supposed to do an intro course to C++): \"CppCon 2015: Kate Gregory “Stop Teaching C\"\": [VIDEO] (But again, I want an open wiki/live tutorial, along the lines of \"How I start\", but longer, which goes through all this stuff. We really should make one, both for C++14 and for C11). CppCon 2015: Herb Sutter \"Writing Good C++14... By Default\": [LINK] Is also rather uplifting, and: CppCon 2015: Sean Parent \"Better Code: Data Structures\": [LINK] makes a pretty good case for simple, modern C++ being clear, easy and efficient. Finally, CppCon 2015: Phil Nash “Test Driven C++ with Catch”: [LINK] apart for being a pretty straight forward intro to a simple testing framework, appears to reveal some secrets combining template magic and still get sane compile times (hint: don't recompile more (template) code on every iteration than you need to). There's a couple of good ones that touch on meta-programming too (Look for t"},{"id":10992494,"author":"autoreleasepool","month":"2016-01","story":"The Landscape of Parallelism in C++ [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues C++ should not be taught as C with classes.","quote":"Not only is she a great presenter, but she makes amazing points about what is fundamentally wrong with how C++ is taught in classrooms around the globe.","text":"> It's as if you had to be there from the start to have a full image of how the language grew and truly understand the interplay between all the \"features\", otherwise it's too late to become an expert, and in some cases even too late to become comfortable writing good maintainable code in C++. This is funny to me (not in a condescending way) because I had the exact opposite thought today. I actually came back to C++ on my own fairly recently after learning about the modern version of the language. I was lamenting about how lucky I was to have started learning with C++14 because I didn't have to go through the horrible C++98 and C++03 eras. In fact I could simply ignore most of it. It's all about diving straight into the modern stuff. The biggest problem with learning C++ is that most textbooks, classes, university courses don't actually teach you the real C++ parts until the last 4 weeks of the courses. Instead they teach you \"C with classes.. and std::cout\". This has been acknowledged as an problem by some of the more prominent members of the committee. I urge you to watch Kate Gregory's talk from cppcon 2015 \"Stop Teaching C\". [VIDEO] Not only is she a great presenter, but she makes amazing points about what is fundamentally wrong with how C++ is taught in classrooms around the globe. I really hope her talk makes some impact. I know I used to hate the language. That was when I was writing it for my university that for some reason didn't want to upgrade their 2007 gcc compil"},{"id":26738099,"author":"tediousdemise","month":"2021-04","story":"A simple C implementation to stream H.264 to browser using WebRTC","stance":"rec","why":"Good talk arguing that C should no longer be taught.","quote":"Kate Gregory has a good cppcon talk on the topic","text":"I just can’t recommend using it for new code in light of the sexy new alternatives that can compile down to the same binary with source code that’s way more intuitive and readable. I do have nostalgia for C since it was my first language, and I think a lot of people are in the same boat, which is a problem. That’s why I think we need to stop teaching C. Kate Gregory has a good cppcon talk on the topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18335641,"author":"simias","month":"2018-10","story":"Teaching C (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Good talk on why C should not be taught before C++.","quote":"good talk about how we should","text":"My point was that I don't think Smalltalk was ever more popular in the industry than C regardless of the time frame. By the mid 90's lisp machines were pretty long gone, betting on Lisp instead of C if employability is the metric seems rather bold. MIPS I concede, it was remained fairly popular for a long time. I guess my overall point is that the subjective choices made by this university back then don't really mean much by now. The fact that they taught Pascal instead of C alone proves that they weren't particularly prescient. >Regarding language comparisons, actually C++ is Italian and C Latin. I thought about that while writing this bad analogy but I couldn't come up with a better one. I wasn't really talking about the historical relationship between C and C++ but rather about their relative complexities. Learning C++ is vastly more complicated than learning C. On top of that the coding style of a modern C++ application (which generally involves a lot of metaprogramming, OOP and even some functional constructs) is fairly different from a standard C codebase. You don't write C like you write C++ and vice-versa. There's this good talk about how we should \"stop teaching C\" (if we want to teach C++): [VIDEO] I think the opposite holds true as well for very much the same reasons."},{"id":16536336,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2018-03","story":"Ask HN: Best way to learn modern C++?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among recommended resources for learning modern C++.","quote":"Some of the Kate Gregory videos,","text":"- Tour of C++ ( [LINK] ) - Principles and Practice Using C++ ( [LINK] ) - From Mathematics to Generic Programming ( [LINK] ) - The Scott Meyers books Some of the Bjarne Stroustrup videos, \"Learning and Teaching Modern C++\" - [LINK] Some of the Herb Sutter videos, \"Writing Good C++14... By Default\" - [LINK] \"Back to the Basics! Essentials of Modern C++ Style\" - [LINK] Some of the Kate Gregory videos, \"Stop Teaching C\" - [VIDEO] \"10 Core Guidelines You Need to Start Using Now\" - [LINK] \"It's Complicated\" - [LINK]"},{"id":14422922,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2017-05","story":"Coroutines are now in Clang trunk, libc++","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why to learn C++ idioms rather than C.","quote":"talk at CppCon 2015 explains better than me.","text":"First of all, and most important one, learn C++ idioms, not C. This means making use of library types for memory allocation, vectors and strings. There is a place for old C style low level features, but those should come later, after the safer concepts are already ingrained. Kate Gregory's “Stop Teaching C\" talk at CppCon 2015 explains better than me. [VIDEO] Get a nice IDE, QtCreator, Clion, VC++ Community, KDevelop, XCode. Besides the \"Tour of C++\", check Stephan T. Lavavej talks at Channel 9. \"Core C++ Lectures\" [LINK] \"Advanced STL\", these ones only when you already have a good understanding of STL as user [LINK] Also check video presentations from NDC, ACCU, CppCon, CppNow and C9 Going Native."},{"id":10820858,"author":"daemin","month":"2016-01","story":"} // good to go","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on what topics to teach newcomers to modern C++.","quote":"For Modern C++ I found this video interesting in regards to the topics she chooses to teach people new to C++.","text":"For Modern C++ I found this video [VIDEO] interesting in regards to the topics she chooses to teach people new to C++."},{"id":43311369,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2025-03","story":"Zig's dot star syntax (value.*)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited against learning C as a path to C++.","quote":"no one should be learning C as means to learn C++","text":"Since at least C++14, no one should be learning C as means to learn C++, unless they want to keep writing security exploits on regular basis. CppCon 2015: “Stop Teaching C\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":30644638,"author":"gattr","month":"2022-03","story":"Learning Standard C++ as a New Language (1999)","stance":"reference","why":"Related talk about teaching C++ constructs before C-era ones.","quote":"teaching (the newcomers) C++ constructs first before going through C-era ones","text":"Somewhat related: [1]. A bit provocative title, but it's actually about \"teaching (the newcomers) C++ constructs first before going through C-era ones\". [1] CppCon 2015: Kate Gregory “Stop Teaching C\" ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":30181483,"author":"phendrenad2","month":"2022-02","story":"The Problem with C (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of an anti-C talk at C++ conferences.","quote":"In fact at C++ conferences you will see many anti-C talks","text":"I wonder if the reason for there being no C language conference is: It's generally accepted that C is \"dead\" or should become dead. In fact at C++ conferences you will see many anti-C talks: * [VIDEO] And also talks promoting proselytizing to C programmers and bringing them to C++: * [LINK] And of course, now, Rust is supposed to supplant C."},{"id":27799330,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2021-07","story":"Using IceWM and a Raspberry Pi as my main PC, sharing my theme, config and some","stance":"reference","why":"Example of C versus C++ debate.","quote":"C vs C++ flame wars have been a thing since C++ was born at AT&T.","text":"Everything that implies taking sides is politics, no matter what. You haven't seen any drama, really? [LINK] C vs C++ flame wars have been a thing since C++ was born at AT&T. Since you aren't following up the news, \"CppCon 2015: Kate Gregory “Stop Teaching C\" [VIDEO] \"We stopped teaching C\" [LINK] You can find similar stuff from C side, specially if you back in time to GNOME mailing lists, Usenet archives from comp.lang,languagex"},{"id":27086606,"author":"nly","month":"2021-05","story":"The Byte Order Fiasco","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in agreement on how C++ should be taught.","quote":"I tend to agree with this talk","text":"For sure, it's a good way to teach that C is insufficient to deal with even the simplest of tasks. Unfortunately teaching has a bad habit of becoming practice, no matter how good the intention. With regard to teaching C++ specifically I tend to agree with this talk: CppCon 2015 - Kate Gregory “Stop Teaching C\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":23064828,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2020-05","story":"OCaml is Pretty Great (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited about C-style code in C++ from outdated teaching.","quote":"there is a community that keeps writing C style code in C++","text":"Why you see them? Because developers should know better, but there is a community that keeps writing C style code in C++, unfortunately, as their teachers are stuck in the 90's. \"CppCon 2015: Kate Gregory “Stop Teaching C\" [VIDEO] But what to expect when schools keep using Turbo C++ as teaching tool, [LINK]"},{"id":18039888,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2018-09","story":"C++ Lifetime profile v1.0 posted","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to show the community promotes avoiding C style in C++.","quote":"the community does try to promote not doing C in C++.","text":"True, but you have missed the part that makes C++ safer than C. - User defined types with invariants - templates, const, constexpr and if constexpr instead of macros - proper string types - proper vector types - type safe enumerations - many implicit conversions in C are compiler errors in C++ - RAII - contracts - generic data structures The iterator invalidation is easy to find out in compilers like Visual C++, their STL implementation can be customized to validate iterator invariants. The danging pointers, use-after-free, use-after-move mentioned by you are the purpose of this proposal. I was an early C++ adopter in 1992, LLVM rejuvenated language research in 2003, DirectX and Metal shaders are a C++ dialect, AUTOSAR has migrated from C to C++14 only two years ago, NVidia GPUs since Volta (2017) are now being designed to run C++ code efficiently. A time frame of about 30 years for C++ to become widespread as it is. I am all for Rust or similar language to replace C++ and do away with C copy-paste culture, but I am not throwing away all my code just because. So yeah, it isn't perfect, but it surely is way better than plain C coding and the community does try to promote not doing C in C++. CppCon 2015: Kate Gregory “Stop Teaching C\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":16280168,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2018-02","story":"Why hasn't D started to replace C++","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for not learning C on the way to C++.","quote":"C should never be learned on the path of being a good, programming safe conscious C++ programmer.","text":"C should never be learned on the path of being a good, programming safe conscious C++ programmer. CppCon 2015: Kate Gregory “Stop Teaching C\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":16413250,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2018-02","story":"Rust things I miss in C","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as part of the push to stop C-style programming in C++.","quote":"the need being discussing since the last three CppCon, to stop using C style programming in modern C++.","text":"The major issue with C++, is what also helped the language gain market adoption, copy-paste compatibility with most of C89 code. So security conscious C++ developers tend to use enum class , string, vector and array classes, RAII, iostreams, wrapping data access in classes with invariants,... Developers with more C oriented mindset, tend to just code away like \"C with C++ compiler\" programming style. Hence the need being discussing since the last three CppCon, to stop using C style programming in modern C++. CppCon 2015: Kate Gregory “Stop Teaching C\" - [VIDEO] CppCon 2015: Bjarne Stroustrup “Writing Good C++14” - [LINK] CppCon 2017: Bjarne Stroustrup “Learning and Teaching Modern C++” - [LINK] Of course, preventing copy-paste compatibility with C, already solves many of those issues from the get go."},{"id":12896050,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2016-11","story":"C for Python programmers (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited against learning C first for C++.","quote":"That is the path to write unsafe and unidiomatic C++.","text":"That is the path to write unsafe and unidiomatic C++. CppCon 2015: Kate Gregory “Stop Teaching C\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hEx5DNLWGgA",2],["sWgDk-o-6ZE",1],["p6K4iIMlouI",1],["eX9m3g5J-XA",1],["LiWlvBro9eI",1],["EKWGGDXe5MA",1],["_ahvzDzKdB0",1],["dK3zRXhrFZY",1],["oKg1hTOQXoY",1],["Av0PQDVTP4A",1],["6wZmYMWKLkY",1],["4bM3Gut1hIk",1],["Eg_ToU7m1MI",1],["hOE6nVVr14c",1],["p2LZLYcu_JY",1],["30jNsCVLpAE",1],["JZE3_0qvrMg",1],["rAl-9HwD858",1],["YEBfamv-_do",1],["NH1Tta7purM",1]]},"7ImvlS8PLIo":{"wy":"HN regards it as a famous, mesmerizing talk, especially for the point that distant galaxies will become unobservable.","q":{"x":"There is also one of my favorite talks tangentially related by Lawrence Krauss.","i":25502536,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25502536,"author":"atomi","month":"2020-12","story":"The universe is expanding faster than expected","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Future civilizations will see only their own galaxy and misjudge the universe.","quote":"There is also one of my favorite talks tangentially related by Lawrence Krauss.","text":"There is also one of my favorite talks tangentially related by Lawrence Krauss. He explains that eventually a civilization exploring the universe around them will only see it's own galaxy and likely believe that's all there is. We are living in an extraordinary time where we are still able to see other galaxies. [VIDEO]"},{"id":937731,"author":"ZenzerNet","month":"2009-11","story":"Weird Physics Facts","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Krauss talk on the universe from nothing.","quote":"It had me mesmerized and mystified for the whole run.","text":"I feel this video is apt for this topic - Laurence Krauss - A universe from nothing. [VIDEO] It had me mesmerized and mystified for the whole run."},{"id":29524609,"author":"Isinlor","month":"2021-12","story":"Ask HN: Long-form info-dense videos on YouTube you would recommend to anyone?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among long-form info-dense video recommendations.","quote":"","text":"Everything from 3 blue 1 brown: [LINK] Visualization of Quantum Physics (Quantum Mechanics): [LINK] history of the entire world, i guess: [LINK] TIMELAPSE OF THE FUTURE: A Journey to the End of Time (4K): [LINK] Humanity was born way ahead of its time. The reason is grabby aliens. and Grabby aliens: when we'll meet them, how big they are, and other predictions [LINK] [LINK] Everyday Astronaut videos (he has 90 min documentary on Soviet rocket engine): [LINK] Cool Worlds - a lot of 25-35 min very nice astronomy explanations: [LINK] 'A Universe From Nothing' by Lawrence Krauss: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14817519,"author":"justifier","month":"2017-07","story":"Why isn't everything normally distributed?","stance":"rec","why":"Anecdote about Hubble's failed 1929 expansion-rate attempt, plus a log-log joke.","quote":"i'd recommend the entire lecture","text":"reminds me of this joke from a lecture Lawrence Krauss gave in 2009 of his work in 'A Universe From Nothing': > it was made after the discovery that on a log log plot everything is a straight line(o) i'd recommend the entire lecture, but definitely at least check out the anecdote this joke bookends it is about hubble's original 1929 embarrassing failed attempt to calculate the rate of the expansion of the universe(i) (o) [VIDEO] (i) [VIDEO]"},{"id":7898148,"author":"2Pacalypse-","month":"2014-06","story":"The Disappearing Universe","stance":"rec","why":"Krauss's entertaining and famous talk on this topic.","quote":"Lawrence Krauss talked about this in his entertaining and famous talk","text":"Lawrence Krauss talked about this in his entertaining and famous talk, A Universe From Nothing[1]. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":3796821,"author":"goblin89","month":"2012-04","story":"Notch's next game is an MMO","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how distant galaxies will become unobservable as the universe expands.","quote":"how mercilessly correct he is about physics of our space-time","text":"Slightly off-topic, but—how mercilessly correct he is about physics of our space-time. We all know the universe is expanding, but many consequences of this phenomenon are not obvious—such as that after some time we'll be unable to see (and therefore ever reach) even nearby galaxies, since they're constantly getting farther and farther away. We'll be completely alone forever, and future scientists would have completely wrong picture about universe in such an absence of information. Thinking about that, we already miss a lot of information because of red shift… There're facts potentially explaining the eternal question ‘about universe and everything’, but they are slipping away from us, crossing the event horizon never ever to be learned about. Not a fun thing to know. Personally for me that was a discovery thanks to the explanation by Lawrence Krauss in his ‘A Universe From Nothing’ popular talk ( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":1658420,"author":"joelangeway","month":"2010-09","story":"Stephen Hawking's answer to God question tiresome","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture explaining why the universe does not need a first cause.","quote":"here is a lecture on one explanation for why the universe does not need any first cause,","text":"\"I confess that something in me tightens whenever I hear a scientist pontificating on issues that belong to the arena of philosophy or metaphysics. I will gladly listen to Stephen Hawking when he holds forth on matters of theoretical physics, but he’s as qualified to talk about philosophical and religious issues as any college freshman.\" The existence of the universe is very much a matter of theoretical physics and Steven Hawking, understanding this, is surely more qualified to talk about such issues than Rev. Robert Barron regardless of his opinion that they are philosophical or religious. I don't know what prompted Steven Hawking's appearance in the media with this opinion, but here is a lecture on one explanation for why the universe does not need any first cause, [VIDEO]"},{"id":41745572,"author":"S04dKHzrKT","month":"2024-10","story":"Why Is Light So Fast?","stance":"reference","why":"We live at a time when we can still see everything around us.","quote":"He says that we live in a good time because we can still see \"everything\" around us.","text":"Lawrence Krauss has given a talk that mentions something similar. He says that we live in a good time because we can still see \"everything\" around us. At some point in the far future, any observers won't be able to determine many things about the universe because the \"stuff\" in it will be too far away to observe. [VIDEO] @ 50:57"},{"id":32621806,"author":"zugi","month":"2022-08","story":"We don't know how the universe began, and we will never know","stance":"reference","why":"Krauss argues we may never know how the universe began.","quote":"In his famous talk \"A Universe from Nothing\", Lawrence Krauss makes the same point","text":"In his famous talk \"A Universe from Nothing\", Lawrence Krauss makes the same point, if slightly less definitively. (\"We may never know\", and that's okay - [VIDEO] ) By analogy he describes how, due to the expansion of the universe, Milky Way residents 100 billion years from now will be unable to observe the cosmic background radiation or any galaxy but our own. Civilizations wil evolve, invent science, use the best tools possible, and incorrectly conclude that the universe consists of one single galaxy alone in a vast sea of empty space. When we triply rewrite hard drives, we understand that information can be destroyed. Sometimes the universe does the samething. Sometimes information is irretrievably lost."}],"r":[["bq2_wSsDwkQ",1],["FACK2knC08E",1],["uD4izuDMUQA",1],["oAVjF_7ensg",1],["p7bzE1E5PMY",1],["xuCn8ux2gbs",1],["l3whaviTqqg",1]]},"NdSD07U5uBs":{"wy":"HN repeatedly cites it as a thought-provoking talk on reducing software complexity and on the state of computing research.","q":{"x":"Easily over of the most thought provoking presentations I have seen recently.","i":11184618,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":39614551,"author":"vhiremath4","month":"2024-03","story":"Alan Kay's talk at UCLA – Feb 2024 [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lightbulb moment for a young programmer","quote":"Highly recommend his talk on the power of simplicity.","text":"Highly recommend his talk on the power of simplicity. This was one of those lightbulb moments for me when I was a younger programmer: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11184618,"author":"josteink","month":"2016-02","story":"Rich Hickey Fanclub","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Thought-provoking presentation on simplicity","quote":"Easily over of the most thought provoking presentations I have seen recently.","text":"Completely unrelated: Alan Kay - the power of simplicity [VIDEO] Easily over of the most thought provoking presentations I have seen recently."},{"id":46315093,"author":"mablopoule","month":"2025-12","story":"Don MacKinnon: Why Simplicity Beats Cleverness in Software Design [audio]","stance":"rec","why":"Kepler ellipses illustrate simplifying via better building blocks","quote":"had a great illustration of this specific phenomenon using astronomy","text":"In his \"Power of Simplicity\"[1] talk, Alan Kay had a great illustration of this specific phenomenon using astronomy: Before Johannes Kepler had the insight of describing the orbits of the planets with ellipsis, peoples were using the (conceptually simpler) circles which didn't completely match the observed movement of celestial body such as Mars, thus resulted in complicated circle-within-circles orbits to try to model reality. By introducing a more complex basic shape (ellipsis instead of circle) which happened to match the underlying reality more, the overall description of orbits got greatly simplified. It's a phenomenon I've seen a few time in my career so far: that while often there's complex code because there are actually complex hedge case to handle (essential complexity), sometime it's really because the data structure used to model the thing you're handling is slightly missing the mark, making things fit almost-but-not-quite, and many operation done around to handle data can be greatly simplified (if not avoided altogether) by changing the underlying data-structure. (Also, Alan Kay apparently did another talk called \"Is it really complex, or did we just make it complicated\"[2] that seems pertinent to the thread, though I haven't watch it yet) [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":21762471,"author":"fovc","month":"2019-12","story":"Simple Software Manifesto","stance":"rec","why":"Principled approach to reducing accidental complexity","quote":"The TLDW is that Kay thinks we haven't discovered all the fundamental rules of computing","text":"Not sure which generation you're in and which generation you'd (relatively) consider Alan Key in, but here is a much more principled approach to what the OP is trying to do: [VIDEO] The TLDW is that Kay thinks we haven't discovered all the fundamental rules of computing, a la Maxwells Equations for physics. If you are able to figure these out, you can substantially cut down your accidental complexity. FWIW, my unscientific view is that achieving simplicity is really hard and doesn't scale people-wise. Instead of C99 without a bunch of stuff, I'd probably use Scheme to create very dense (lots of bespoke abstractions and idioms) but simple software. That approach works reasonably well, but try onboarding a new developer!"},{"id":16840041,"author":"a_d","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among favourite tech talks","quote":"","text":"Bret Victor - Inventing on principle [LINK] Alan Kay - Power of Simplicity [VIDEO] Steve Jobs - Marketing (unveiling Think Different) [LINK] Robert Cialdini - On Influence [LINK] Peter Thiel - On Zero to One (Notes on Starts Ups or How to Build the Future) [LINK] Naval Ravikant on Reading, Happiness, Systems for Decision Making, Habits, Honesty and More (Farnam Street podcast) [LINK] Josh Wolfe - On \"This is who you are up against\" podcast [LINK] Richard Feynman - Fun to Imagine [LINK] Paul Graham - Startup school (2008) [LINK] James Burke - Connections (BBC documentary) <No link> Jim Al-Khalili tells the story of the Atom (BBC) [LINK] Elon Musk's 2003 Stanford University Entrepreneurial Thought Leaders Lecture [LINK] Ken Robinson - Do schools kill creativity? [LINK] Paul Stamets on mycology, bioremediation and fungi- Joe Rogan Experience [LINK]"},{"id":14320028,"author":"_pius","month":"2017-05","story":"Alan Kay at Startup School [video]","stance":"rec","why":"More expansive version of Kay's talk","quote":"watching a different, more expansive version of the talk like this one, given at Qualcomm","text":"I've seen him give this great talk before, but IMO the delivery this time feels rushed and a bit disjointed. That said, the last few minutes summarize his powerful \"Gretzky Game\" idea so succinctly that they're worth the price of admission alone. I would recommend watching the last 18 seconds of this version (starting at [LINK] ), internalizing the Gretzky Game slide on the screen, backing it up and watching the fuller explanation in the preceding ~3 minutes (starting at 54:18, [LINK] ), and then finally watching a different, more expansive version of the talk like this one, given at Qualcomm: [VIDEO] . Oddly specific advice, I know, but hopefully helpful to somebody."},{"id":11940634,"author":"josteink","month":"2016-06","story":"Alan Kay has agreed to do an AMA today","stance":"rec","why":"How big horizons drove invention at PARC","quote":"See it anyway. It's a good talk.","text":"I'm not Alan, but I saw one of his talks[1] about how they did invent all those things we're using now, which you are very much referring to. What he said was important was to have a big horizon, at least 10 years. Anything less would not do. Then envision what should be possible in that time. \"It would be ridiculous if $X wasn't possible in $Y years\" and then backtrack on what would technically be required to make that happen. Rinse repeat. He said that with good company support and a good team, you often found yourself delivering at half that time none the less (but again, you should never constrain yourself to that little, or else you will stress towards performing, instead of thinking properly about the problems needing to be solved). Alan: Hope I didn't butcher your message too much :) [1] I think it might be this one, but I honestly haven't checked. See it anyway. It's a good talk. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40435485,"author":"ekzy","month":"2024-05","story":"Clever code is probably the worst code you could write (2023)","stance":"reference","why":"Simplicity via more sophisticated building blocks","quote":"you get simplicity by finding a slightly more sophisticated building block to build your theories out of.","text":"Yes. A relevant quote from Alan Kay in his talk “the power of simplicity”: > one of the things that's worked the best the last three, or four hundred years, is you get simplicity by finding a slightly more sophisticated building block to build your theories out of. its when you go for a simple building block that anybody understand through common sense, that is when you start screwing yourself right and left, because it just might not be able to ramify through the degrees of freedom and scaling you have to go through, and it's this inability to fix the building blocks that is one of the largest problems that computing has today in large organizations. people just won't do it [VIDEO]"},{"id":39632916,"author":"morphle","month":"2024-03","story":"Alan Kay's talk at UCLA – Feb 2024 [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Xerox laser printer return on investment","quote":"Xerox made a ROI of 20000% on the laser printer alone","text":"Xerox made a ROI of 20000% on the laser printer alone [1]. A better version lecture Alan did for Ycombinator startups [2] but doesn't focus on the return of investment. Xerox did shrink a bit since then. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":23465220,"author":"Jhsto","month":"2020-06","story":"Formal Systems in Biology","stance":"reference","why":"Scaling software like biological systems","quote":"he had an argument about how software systems cannot scale unless the basis is the most complex","text":"Do you think that software should be architected as autonomous agents for it to scale infinitely? I watched an Alan Kay video [1] some time back. In the video, he had an argument about how software systems cannot scale unless the basis is the most complex \"computation\" system that we know -- that system being our own biological system. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23255489,"author":"fossuser","month":"2020-05","story":"Ask HN: Why isn’t visual programming a bigger thing?","stance":"reference","why":"Kay on lack of real research into tools","quote":"I just watched this earlier today","text":"You’d probably like Bret Victor’s inventing on principle talk: [LINK] He demonstrates some good examples. I’m not quite sure it’s what you mean by visual, but it seems obviously valuable and tools that enabled some of what he shows would be really useful. I’m not sure why we don’t have these things - I think Alan Kay would say it’s because we stopped doing real research to improve what our tools can be. We’re just relying on old paradigms (I just watched this earlier today: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":22350470,"author":"AlchemistCamp","month":"2020-02","story":"Running servers and services well is not trivial (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Systems simpler with more sophisticated building blocks","quote":"Alan Kay has made powerful arguments that systems can be made much simpler","text":"On the other hand Alan Kay has made powerful arguments that systems can be made much simpler if only their building blocks were slightly less simple! It's a tough question. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19256907,"author":"bch","month":"2019-02","story":"Computer Software (1984)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for sophistication versus simplicity","quote":"","text":"> “What [the computer field] needs first is a William of Occam, who said “Entities should not be multiplied unnecessarily.” The idea that it is worth while to put considerable effort into eliminating complexity and establishing the simple had a lot to do with the rise of modern science and mathematics, particularly from the standpoint of creating new aesthetics... The tricky thing is he argues for “sophistication” here[0], which on the surface seems antithetical. Picking (and committing-to) a (correct) abstraction can be tricky. Also, these “axioms” are often made true by useless qualifiers like “unnecessary” complexity, or even “complexity”. If it’s required , it’s not “complexity”, but a necessary implementation detail. Pick in good faith, keep a loose grip, have fun. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15955965,"author":"romaniv","month":"2017-12","story":"A plan to rescue the Web from the Internet","stance":"reference","why":"Kay on internet resilience","quote":"Here is Kay talking about Internet's resilience in a bit more detail","text":"Having watched nearly every talk/interview of Kay I could find on YouTube (which includes the OOPSLA keynote) and having read some of his writings I think I have a reasonably good understanding of what he is advocating for. Here is Kay talking about Internet's resilience in a bit more detail: [VIDEO] >His position is strongly aligned with the \"mechanism, not policy\" philosophy. I don't see how this opposes or contradicts any of what I wrote above."},{"id":11952371,"author":"MichaelMoser123","month":"2016-06","story":"Alan Kay has agreed to do an AMA today","stance":"reference","why":"Talk advocating tool building","quote":"well, in this talk it sounds like you do advocate tool building","text":"well, in this talk it sounds like you do advocate tool building - isn't tool building a way to try elliptic orbits instead of the circular ones ? [VIDEO] 'Power of simplicity'"},{"id":11469816,"author":"alayne","month":"2016-04","story":"Ask HN: Is there evidence regarding PG's theory about powerful languages?","stance":"reference","why":"Building up abstractions","quote":"Alan Kay has a more generalized idea about building up abstractions that I like better.","text":"Alan Kay has a more generalized idea about building up abstractions that I like better. I think he mentioned it in this video [VIDEO] I, like many in this thread, think that good people are more important than a particular programming language."},{"id":10323263,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2015-10","story":"Alan Kay on re-creating Xerox PARC's design magic at CDG","stance":"reference","why":"Known Alan Kay talk on research","quote":"This a known Alan Kay talk, he did it already a few times.","text":"Check this video running in another thread. This a known Alan Kay talk, he did it already a few times. [VIDEO] As an example of the type of research being done, you must be willing to build something that will be considered commodity in 15 years time at today's price. You won't get there with basic income."},{"id":18333734,"author":"leoc","month":"2018-10","story":"Soup – Alan Kay on Objects","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"if you follow the idea of computers as virtualisers where it leads you do not","text":"Reposting my comment from discussion [LINK] with a fixed YouTube link: > Now this right here [VIDEO] is the problem: if you follow the idea of computers as virtualisers where it leads you do not , in the general case, end up with the flat backplane talked about from 23:15 and shown in the accompanying slide. (Actually, this is in turn probably just a symptom of another, more general problem.)"},{"id":14389808,"author":"leoc","month":"2017-05","story":"Alan Kay at Startup School [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"if you follow the idea of computers as virtualisers where it leads you do not","text":"Now this right here [VIDEO] is the problem: if you follow the idea of computers as virtualisers where it leads you do not , in the general case, end up with the flat backplane talked about from 23:15 and shown in the accompanying slide. (Actually, this is in turn probably just a symptom of another, more general problem.)"}],"r":[["q7K0vRUKXKc",1],["mPqWstVnRjQ",1],["ras_VYgA77Q",1],["Fzmm87oVQ6c",1]]},"JqNg819PiZY":{"wy":"HN repeatedly recommends it as an approachable, math-light route to understanding mass and the Higgs field.","q":{"x":"I highly highly recommend this talk by Leonard Susskind","i":33267209,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":41438282,"author":"tsimionescu","month":"2024-09","story":"How the Higgs field gives mass to elementary particles","stance":"strong_rec","why":"How fields give particles mass with little math","quote":"there is an excellent lecture on YouTube by Leonard Susskind.","text":"If anyone wants to dig deeper, there is an excellent lecture on YouTube by Leonard Susskind. This goes into some details on how fields in general give mass to (composite) particles, and how the Higgs field has certain properties that allow it to give mass to elementary particles. It goes only into a tiny bit of math, absolutely intelligible at the high-school or at least undergraduate level. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33267209,"author":"tsimionescu","month":"2022-10","story":"Inside the Proton","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Layman path from what mass means to the Higgs mechanism","quote":"I highly highly recommend this talk by Leonard Susskind","text":"If you're interested in the Higgs mechanism as a layman, I highly highly recommend this talk by Leonard Susskind: [VIDEO] It's about 1 hour long (plus some questions) and it goes through some basic ideas of what it means for something to have mass and all the way to the Higgs mechanism."},{"id":31989141,"author":"simiones","month":"2022-07","story":"Large Hadron Collider discovers three new exotic particles","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Approachable math-light hour on the Higgs mechanism","quote":"I highly recommend it if you are interested","text":"The mass of the massive gauge bosons, the W and Z bosons. Edit: there is an excellent in-depth (but math-light) explanation about the Higgs mechanism by Leonard Susskind. I highly recommend it if you are interested, it lasts about 1h (plus some Q&A) and is extremely approachable, while being presented by an established authority in the field. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12382685,"author":"bitkrieg","month":"2016-08","story":"There are no particles, there are only fields (2012)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent explanation of Higgs field and basic quantum mechanics","quote":"I found this lecture to be an excellent explanation","text":"If anyone is interested in the mechanics of the higgs field / basic quantum mechanics in general, I found this lecture to be an excellent explanation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33267315,"author":"tsimionescu","month":"2022-10","story":"Inside the Proton","stance":"rec","why":"Why energy and mass are interchangeable","quote":"There is a very good video of a lecture by Leonard Susskind","text":"The quarks that are bound in a way we call a proton are held together by the strong force at a certain distance from each other - that distance is what we consider the volume of the proton, with the quarks being \"inside that volume\". This system of 3 quarks has some amount of potential energy, and/or some amount of kinetic energy from the relative movement of the 3 quarks. The mass of this system is then given essentially by E=mc^2, with E being this kinetic energy. There is a very good video of a lecture by Leonard Susskind that explains why energy and mass are interchangeable in this way if you want a more in-depth explanation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31190048,"author":"simiones","month":"2022-04","story":"It took me 10 years to understand entropy","stance":"rec","why":"Easy to follow lecture on where mass comes from","quote":"This lecture by Leonard Susskind explains most of these things about mass in a way I found easy to follow","text":"> no one really knows what mass is. We only know that mass bends space-time curve, hence gravity. Mass is much better understood by its role in inertia. Basically mass is the amount of energy you need to exchange with a thing to change its current speed. This observation works from Newtonian mechanics to QM and GR as well. Now, why do things have mass? The famous E=mc² explains this for most things: they have mass because something inside them has potential or kinetic energy. This works all the way down to the atomic level - the mass of a proton for example is almost entirely explained by the potential energy of the quarks being held together in a small volume; the total mass of the quarks themselves is only a small fraction of that. Now, the mass of the elementary particles is somewhat more complicated, but the Standard Model does have explanations for those - symmetry breaking for fermions, and the Higgs mechanism for the massive bosons. The next mystery is: why is inertial mass equal to gravitational mass? GR has essentially explained this, by showing that acceleration is equivalent to gravitational attraction depending on your frame of reference. So overall, I'm not sure what Brian Greene means by that - mass is at least as well understood as other basic properties of particles (charge, spin, color charge). This lecture by Leonard Susskind explains most of these things about mass in a way I found easy to follow: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["8X69_42Mj-g",1],["nsaUX48t0w8",1],["zcqZHYo7ONs",1]]},"fhOHn9TClXY":{"wy":"HN uses it in debates about what OOP originally meant, since Kay's message-passing view lines up with Erlang's processes.","q":{"x":"To get some perspective on both of their perspectives, here's \"Joe Armstrong interviews Alan Kay\"","i":29370370,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":49069690,"author":"mempko","month":"2026-07","story":"A Love Letter to Object Orientation","stance":"rec","why":"Great discussion between Armstrong and Kay on messaging","quote":"There is this great discussion between Joe Armstrong and Alan Kay if you haven't see it.","text":"Thanks, I'll check it out! When I implemented messaging for Abject, I used the Erlang model. I also added P2P support so objects can talk across the network. There is this great discussion between Joe Armstrong and Alan Kay if you haven't see it. [VIDEO] EDIT: Just checked your smalltalk implementation. Very cool! Do you remember the Open Croquet project in the early 2000s? Way ahead of it's time. I wonder if a modern version of that could work today now that we have good VR headsets."},{"id":43949946,"author":"AceJohnny2","month":"2025-05","story":"Alan Kay did not invent objects (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Kay and Armstrong discuss messaging between objects and agents","quote":"I loved this interview/discussion between Kay & Joe Armstrong, inventor of Erlang","text":"Indeed, here's a email from Alan Kay about how his perspective is about \"messaging\" between objects: [LINK] (edit: derp, this is directly cited in TFA) Also, I loved this interview/discussion between Kay & Joe Armstrong, inventor of Erlang, where the topic comes up again (because Erlang is also a lot about messaging between agents) [VIDEO]"},{"id":29370370,"author":"Rendello","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: If OOP is about message passing, why not sender.send(receiver, message)?","stance":"rec","why":"Armstrong and Kay discuss their differing perspectives on messaging and objects","quote":"To get some perspective on both of their perspectives, here's \"Joe Armstrong interviews Alan Kay\"","text":"One sort-of exception is Erlang (and anything else that runs on the BEAM vm, like Elixir). It doesn't match Alan Kay's vision exactly, because the message passing is done a bit differently (for example it's asynchronous), but creator Joe Armstrong called it something like \"the best of functional programming and OOP\". If you come from C++, Java, or other \"OOP\" languages, and look at Erlang, it's completely different than what you expect. In Erlang, the \"objects\" are less like mutable structs and more like functional mini-programs that can create new programs, pass messages, and fail as you would expect. To get some perspective on both of their perspectives, here's \"Joe Armstrong interviews Alan Kay\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":23148911,"author":"gens","month":"2020-05","story":"Single Writer Principle (2011)","stance":"rec","why":"Alan Kay's take on what OOP means","quote":"Checking out what Alan Kay has to say about it might help","text":"Checking out what Alan Kay has to say about it might help: [VIDEO] The way i see it OOP is about encapsulating/bundling data and the code that processes that data into one \"object\", that is isolated from other objects. The objects could only \"communicate\" with other objects by sending \"messages\" (data/events/something). Personally i don't like OOP, and think that focusing on data and how it's processed is a better way forward (CSP and such). It's.. a complicated matter."},{"id":22903283,"author":"gens","month":"2020-04","story":"Gleam: A statically typed language for the Erlang VM","stance":"rec","why":"Armstrong's interview of Kay as part of learning concurrency","quote":"I also recommend the interview he did of Alan Kay","text":"Concurrency.. the way i understand it, it is about making parts of the program run independent of each other and thus can run at the same time. But you might want to check out someone who knows better, like Rob Pike: [LINK] Yes, Erlang makes concurrent programs easier to write. Again it is better to listen to someone who knows more, like the creator of Erlang Joe Armstrong: [LINK] Any talk by him is great. Erlang, in how i see it, makes it easier to write concurrent programs because: 1. Parts of a program communicate with other parts by passing messages 2. Every variable is static, as in if you declare that A is 12 you can not change it to be 13 Joe Armstrong had a way of explaining these things so that even i could understand, so i do recommend watching every talk you can find of him. I also recommend the interview he did of Alan Kay [VIDEO] . But if you want to really get into concurrency i recommend a paper called \"Communicating Sequential Processes\" by C. A. R. Hoare. See [LINK] It's a \"formal language\" for dealing with concurrency. Go, and some other languages, implement a \"CSP style\" way of doing concurrency. Erlang doesn't do SIMD, nor GPU. However, it does world scale low latency systems. Of course, it's still up to the programmer to do everything correctly and Erlang is just a tool. I don't quite like *JS, so i won't say anything about that."},{"id":19706607,"author":"dpeck","month":"2019-04","story":"Joe Armstrong has died","stance":"rec","why":"Joe Armstrong's interviews and presentations on better software","quote":"Highly recommend his thesis (2003) and a few of his great interviews/presentations","text":"That is so sad, he was in his late 60s and it seemed like he had a lot of life left in him, talking about how software can get better and having a great (and sometimes snarky) outlook on the profession. Highly recommend his thesis (2003) and a few of his great interviews/presentations for anyone who isn’t familiar with Joe, it captures a lot of what he thought about and pushed for in his professional life. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] I hope his family and friends can find some comfort in how much he was appreciated and admired in the development community."},{"id":13407750,"author":"lliamander","month":"2017-01","story":"What Happens to OO When Processors Are Free? (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Erlang closer to Kay's intent for OOP than Smalltalk","quote":"You should check out the video[0] of Joe Armstrong interviewing Alan Kay","text":"> Erlang != OOP as imagined by Kay... as I understand Kay's intent. You should check out the video[0] of Joe Armstrong interviewing Alan Kay - it seemed to me that Erlang was in some respects closer to what Alan Kay intended for OOP than even smalltalk was. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39760114,"author":"Phiwise_","month":"2024-03","story":"What does Alan Kay think about LLMs?","stance":"reference","why":"Kay says Erlang should be the modern programmer's assembly","quote":"this lecture where he says explicitly that Erlang should be the modern programmer's assembly","text":">But Alan Kay doesn’t say “use Erlang”, nor does he go into any depths about what the desirable feature set is and what would make it work or would fail to make it work. Oh yeah? Then how do you explain this lecture where he says explicitly that Erlang should be the modern programmer's assembly? [VIDEO] In my experience, people who criticize Kay for being too vague or kvetching too pointlessly or lacking practical experience in computer science today almost always have not bothered to familiarize themselves with the vast majority of his work before deciding their opinion on it. It's hard to disagree with him that this makes us more like a pop culture than a profession after seeing it."},{"id":26587840,"author":"inopinatus","month":"2021-03","story":"Why OO Sucks by Joe Armstrong (2000)","stance":"reference","why":"Kay's behavioural view of OOP, which fits Erlang and functional programming","quote":"Erlang ranks as one of the most OO languages, if one accepts the Alan Kay concept of OOP","text":"The irony of course being that Erlang ranks as one of the most OO languages, if one accepts the Alan Kay concept of OOP, which is rather more behavioural than structural, and thereby entirely compatible with the functional paradigm and algebraic forms generally. See also [VIDEO]"},{"id":20157536,"author":"angleofrepose","month":"2019-06","story":"Desktop Neo – rethinking the desktop interface for productivity","stance":"reference","why":"Kay laments lack of serious CAD systems for programming","quote":"","text":"Well, hn seems to have collectively shit on this project in this comment thread. I agree with some of the negatives, but I don't understand why those would be the facets that stick out in this conversation. Yes, his site text explaining motivations are pretty bad. App control is nothing new. And many gestures are similar to other systems. But criticisms based on security, or the originality of his ideas or his general use of swipe gestures are just bullshit. It is backwards and naive to think that desktops are just fine, and computer programmers are historically the last to understand and embrace change (now here is where we could put some Alan Kay quotes, I'll start with his exasperation at our lack of a real CAD system for programming) > \"we would have something like what other engineering disciplines have in serious cad system, a serious simulation of the cad designs and a serious fab facility, to deal with the real problems of doing programming. Ivan [Sutherland] just jumped there [with sketchpad].\" [1] So lets talk about the interesting features he presented, and do ourselves the service of learning from this work. Panels: Top to bottom for content is DIFFERENT THAN ALL MAINSTREAM OSes. He is lamenting permanent status bars, the windows ribbon, the chrome tab bar and more with this feature. And he goes on to explain alternate features to replace that functionality he moved for this goal of more vertical space. He also displayed a number of situations regarding navigation"},{"id":19707168,"author":"noblethrasher","month":"2019-04","story":"Joe Armstrong has died","stance":"reference","why":"Armstrong starstruck while interviewing Kay","quote":"He seemed to be pretty starstruck when he was interviewing Alan Kay on stage","text":"Not at all hard to believe that he was sincere. He seemed to be pretty starstruck when he was interviewing Alan Kay on stage just three years ago: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":19416828,"author":"macintux","month":"2019-03","story":"Alan Kay on the Meaning of “Object-Oriented Programming” (2003)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I don't think I've watched it previously","text":"I don't think I've watched it previously, and I don't have the time to now, but I have to imagine Joe Armstrong interviewing Alan Kay would at least touch on that (although from one comment it sounds like they didn't do more than that). [VIDEO]"},{"id":19418406,"author":"anthony_doan","month":"2019-03","story":"Alan Kay on the Meaning of “Object-Oriented Programming” (2003)","stance":"reference","why":"Kay says computer science is pop culture that repeats past mistakes","quote":"Alan Kay acknowledge that Computer Science is pop culture where the past aren't learn from","text":"I like Joe Armstrong's definition of OOP and what he did to make Erlang an OOP language: [LINK] Alan Kay went on the record to state that current OOP miss his point of OOP where message passing is the main point. Joe Armstrong's definition of OOP in the mailing list give a little bit more context on the other modern features of OOP that aren't truly needed. In Alan Kay and Joe Armstrong talk, Alan Kay acknowledge that Computer Science is pop culture where the past aren't learn from and why past mistakes are repeated. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19212286,"author":"spinningslate","month":"2019-02","story":"Let’s Talk Concurrency: Panel with Sir Tony Hoare, Joe Armstrong, Carl Hewitt","stance":"reference","why":"Kay asks what comes next that builds on Erlang","quote":"Kay posed the wonderful question (paraphrasing): \"what comes next that builds on Erlang?","text":"One of the most balanced, insightful and respectful critiques I've read on the topic. Brightened up my day reading it. Thanks. I've stated previously I'm an Erlang fan, for several of the reasons you've highlighted. I similarly don't believe it's a \"global maxima\". Perhaps the most saddening observation is the number of languages that have come after Erlang - intended for server-type loads - that haven't learned from and built on its strengths in concurrency and fault tolerance. I remember a separate discussion between Joe Armstrong and Alan Kay[0] where Kay posed the wonderful question (paraphrasing): \"what comes next that builds on Erlang?\" That's a tempting prospect. My personal wish list would include 1st class dataflow semantics and a contemporary static type system and compiler that's as practically useful as Elm's today. The key point is to build on what's been proven to work well, not throw it away and have to re-learn all the mistakes from scratch again. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14181176,"author":"nicklaf","month":"2017-04","story":"Tree Oriented Programming [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Kay traces ideas of Erlang and Smalltalk to early papers","quote":"Alan Kay traced some of the key ideas of Erlang and Smalltalk to early papers by John McCarthy","text":"I am quite interested to hear if anybody has any thoughts on the work of Valentin Turchin, the author of Refal. In an interview [1] conducted by Joe Armstrong, Alan Kay traced some of the key ideas of Erlang and Smalltalk to early papers by John McCarthy (Advice Taker), Carl Hewitt (Planner), Simula, and other work (including META 2, which apparently inspired OMeta). The theme here seems to be a favorite of Kay's: \"late binding of meaning\" used to build systems that scale just as well as biology. In his interview, Kay mentions Minsky's Computation: Finite and Infinite Machines , which discusses various models of computation. Now, Alan Kay has long looked to biology for hints on how to scale. (For example, in the interview I mentioned, he says that a computer is a \"universal atom of meaning\".) I mention all this because what I am reading of Valentin Turchin [2] is making me very tempted to add (English) expositions of his work alongside my reading of Minksy's Computation . Specifically, Turchin's work on Supercompilation [3,4], which seems to draw upon a tradition of cybernetics [5], seems to me to be in somewhat of the same spirit as that of Kay's, at least when you consider that the influence of cybernetics largely means that we take cues from biology and attach meaning to existing patterns in nature and technology alike in our endeavors to replicate its success (and of course couple our constructions with them in feedback loops, as in the elecrical engineering that largely "},{"id":13410346,"author":"gens","month":"2017-01","story":"What Happens to OO When Processors Are Free? (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"OO designed around message passing between processes","quote":"OO was designed for multi processing by sending msgs between multiple processes[0].","text":">OO has been designed to model the world in the hope that this would make expressing business logic easier. OO was designed for multi processing by sending msgs between multiple processes[0]. Like nature does. However I do not find it suitable for most parallel computing work because race conditions. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13488141,"author":"zengid","month":"2017-01","story":"What's all this fuss about Erlang? (2007)","stance":"reference","why":"Kay's remarks on languages as assembly code for the next expression layer","quote":"What should Erlang be an assembly code for?","text":"Alan Kay once said (at an Erlang conference): \"By the time the software engineering of a language gets in good shape, the language has become obsolete in terms of 'needed expressiveness'. [Therefore] in a 'Real' computer science, the best languages should serve as the 'assembly code' for the next generation of expression!\" [1] He then proposed the hypothetical question: \"What should Erlang be an assembly code for?\" [2] I'm not sure if he knew about Elixir.. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13035643,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2016-11","story":"Tutorial: Metacompilers Part 1","stance":"reference","why":"Kay and Armstrong mention the META II paper","quote":"Was just mentioned again in the \"Joe Armstrong interviewing Alan Kay\" talk [2][3].","text":"Ahh..META II [1]. This is awesome. Was just mentioned again in the \"Joe Armstrong interviewing Alan Kay\" talk [2][3]. One of Alan's favourite papers. Joe: \"Dan said if you look at this, you will lose a month of your life. And I did\" Or words to that effect. Also the basis for OMeta[4]. I have to admit I've been avoiding it so far in order not to get diverted from architectural insights[5] but this may just be accessible enough to dive on in. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK]"},{"id":13035794,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2016-11","story":"Joe Armstrong Interviews Alan Kay [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Kay on advancing system state over time","quote":"Functions are nice, but you need to advance the state of the system over time","text":"I obviously can't tell you what he's thinking, but I can make an educated guess: In the video (and almost anywhere else you look) you'll note he talks about recursion on the concept of \"computer\" as a way to scale, because that way the parts are as powerful as the whole. If you split that concept into the sub-notions of \"procedures\" and \"data\", you no longer have that recursive principle, and \"functions\" in this context are sufficiently equivalent to procedures. Going the other way, functions don't really scale up, many if not most things in computing are not functions to be computed. One could argue that computers and \"computer science\" are misnamed, as computation is more the exception than the rule. \"Data-around-shufflers\" would be more appropriate. I didn't see the exact monad references, but they look like ways of working around the fact that the \"function\" primitive is inappropriate. It is really nice , mind you, and has wonderful properties. Kind of like circles as the basis for astronomical orbits: they are \"perfect\", but the world isn't perfect in the same way, so trying to model the imperfect world with these perfect primitives leads to having to introduce epicycles/monads. My 2 €¢ Edit: “Functions are nice, but you need to advance the state of the system over time” — [VIDEO]"},{"id":13040476,"author":"espeed","month":"2016-11","story":"META II: A Syntax-Oriented Compiler Writing Language (1964) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Talk where Kay references the META II paper","quote":"This is one of the papers referenced by Alan Kay in the talk posted to HN one day ago","text":"This is one of the papers referenced by Alan Kay in the talk posted to HN one day ago: Joe Armstrong Interviews Alan Kay [video] ( [VIDEO] ) Discussion: [LINK]"}],"r":[["MdmQUlD7P40",1],["lKXe3HUG2l4",1],["hyry8mgXiTk",1],["rmueBVrLKcY",1],["c12cYAUTXXs",1],["UCgoxQCf5Jg",1],["IroPQ150F6c",1],["RSTNhvDGbYI",1],["wlvKAT7SZIQ",1],["vHWiDx_l4V0",1],["ARKbfWk4Xyw",1],["DpXy041BIlA",1],["wo84LFzx5nI",1],["VVUeqxXwCA0",1],["rQhzEnWCgHA",1],["KSWqx8goqSY",1],["tt2AYafET68",1],["Uhx-26GfCBU",1],["fLA_d9q6ySs",1],["KrksBdWcZgQ",1]]},"o_4EX4dPppA":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an insightful look at the people side of open source maintenance, especially in Elm and maintainer-burnout threads.","q":{"x":"Regardless of whether you agree with his approach to developing a programming language, it is a talk worth listening to. Very insightful.","i":26158243,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26158243,"author":"spicyusername","month":"2021-02","story":"Elm Roadmap","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Experiences and social dynamics of open source development","quote":"Regardless of whether you agree with his approach to developing a programming language, it is a talk worth listening to. Very insightful.","text":"For those interested in hearing about Elm creator Evan Czaplicki's experiences with developing open source software and the complexities of internet social dynamics, there is an exceptional talk 'The Hard Parts of Open Source Software'. [VIDEO] Regardless of whether you agree with his approach to developing a programming language, it is a talk worth listening to. Very insightful."},{"id":18271925,"author":"GolDDranks","month":"2018-10","story":"Must-See Tech Talks for Every Programmer (2014)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on the people side of open source","quote":"It's not a talk about technology, but it's a talk on people _in_ technology.","text":"I enjoyed \"The Hard Parts of Open Source\" by Evan Czaplicki (the creator of the Elm programming language) a lot. [VIDEO] It's not a talk about technology, but it's a talk on people _in_ technology. As an open source enthusiast, I found the discussion very important, especially in context where we easily tend to be distracted by technical details."},{"id":25157040,"author":"allenu","month":"2020-11","story":"The Few, the Tired, the Open Source Coders","stance":"rec","why":"Challenges of maintaining open source projects","quote":"There's a great Strange Loop talk by Evan Czaplicki (of Elm fame)","text":"There's a great Strange Loop talk by Evan Czaplicki (of Elm fame) where he alludes to the challenges of maintaining an open source project.[1] In it he describes how feedback often starts as an innocuous \"Why don't you just...?\" where the person making the request doesn't have the full understanding of the system as a whole and may see their request as a simple one-off that can be done quickly. To answer them fully, the open source maintainer has to respond with a long explanation of why their suggestion isn't an option. Or, maybe they don't respond and now the original poster doesn't feel the community is supporting them. This asymmetry can lead to poor communication in the community. I've been working on an app and was reading about the pros and cons of going freemium vs. pay up-front. I was surprised to read that people suggested the pay model is better for indie devs because with free, you'll have more users, but you'll also get worse feedback from the free users. Maybe there's a correlation between seeing something as free and feeling that the work behind it is trivial? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23533214,"author":"TheDong","month":"2020-06","story":"Adobe to remove Flash Player from web site after December 2020","stance":"rec","why":"Why 'why don't you just' is a harmful phrase","quote":"He goes into some of the reasons it's a bad phrase and some of the problems behind it. I'd recommend the talk.","text":"\"why don't you just\" is called out in Evan's \"The hard parts of open source\" [0] talk. He goes into some of the reasons it's a bad phrase and some of the problems behind it. I'd recommend the talk. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22824318,"author":"jlokier","month":"2020-04","story":"Why I’m Leaving Elm","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining talk on open source maintainer demands","quote":"I found Evan's talking style really entertaining and enjoyable","text":"For me, the best thing I got from this critique was the link to this 2018 talk by Evan: The Hard Parts of Open Source, [VIDEO] I found Evan's talking style really entertaining and enjoyable, and I totally relate to the first part about \"why don't you just...\" and \"have you thought about delegation...\"! The HN comments here are near uniformly negative towards Elm and supportive of Luke's criticisms. Fair enough, it's a reasoned critique and those are definitely issues that would affect users. I found the critique informative and useful. I don't use Elm though I've watched with passing interest for a while. Now I feel there are gotchas or expectations I should be aware of if I'm considering using it, about the way the project is heading. But I felt it came off way more entitled than I'm comfortable, mainly when Luke starts moralising , placing ethical obligations upon Evan. That came over to me as \"you are obliged to do a lot more work that I want you to do, on your own time and personal cost, and to stop developing the project according to your own vision or you are a bad person\"."},{"id":22825700,"author":"klntsky","month":"2020-04","story":"Why I’m Leaving Elm","stance":"rec","why":"Idea of intent field in posting forms","quote":"Great talk indeed.","text":"Great talk indeed. I feel like it might be helpful to online communities to add a real \"intent\" field to their posting forms, like here in this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22074406,"author":"mannycalavera","month":"2020-01","story":"Actix project postmortem","stance":"rec","why":"Elm designer on open source maintainer challenges","quote":"has a great talk about this issue","text":"Evan Czaplicki (Elm designer) has a great talk about this issue \"The Hard Parts of Open Source\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":18276267,"author":"shady-lady","month":"2018-10","story":"What does Stack Overflow want to be when it grows up?","stance":"rec","why":"Community lessons Stack Overflow could adopt","quote":"hits on some things which SO should adopt.","text":"I think the 2018 Strange Loop talk \"The Hard Parts of Open Source\" by Evan Czaplicki hits on some things which SO should adopt. relevant screengrab: [LINK] full talk at [VIDEO]"},{"id":22826464,"author":"olvn","month":"2020-04","story":"Why I’m Leaving Elm","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I've used Elm professionally for about a year, having done full rewrites of both a bloated React/Redux SPA and a map data visualizer with complex JS interop using ports to Leaflet, AWS Cognito, etc. Luke Plant's blog post doesn't at all reflect my experiences with Elm or my feelings about the core team. For those not privy to the Elm tea, a brief primer: this blog post is primarily concerning a months-old issue regarding the removal of the ability to use native modules, which are effectively patches to the Elm runtime[1] which circumvent the core features of the language that provide its greatest strengths: a genuinely helpful compiler and ironclad runtime guarantees (still have yet to encounter a production runtime error that wasn't on the JavaScript side of things!) This \"feature\" (used loosely) was largely undocumented, always verboten from distribution in user packages, and never intended as anything more than a stopgap measure in extremely rare cases.[2] It was /officially/ not a core language feature intended for widespread usage, and consistently advised against by the Elm team. The removal of native modules was spoken about publicly months before the breaking upgrade. It came as no surprise to me. As an engineer who tries really hard to be responsible, I do not build software which relies on features which are advertised as 'do not use unless absolutely necessary' and are soon-to-be deprecated unless I'm willing to accept the inherent risk of doing so. The core Elm te"}],"r":[["biYciU1uiUw",1],["9FdHq3WfJgs",1],["esPRsT-lmw8",1],["loCBvaj4eSg",1],["fHsa9DqmId8",1],["ZuvK-od647c",1]]},"rOzrJjdZDRQ":{"wy":"HN commenters call it one of the most entertaining and informative con talks, and a good window into elevators from a hacker's view.","q":{"x":"This version is the best, with all details, no secrets withheld.","i":18218258,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18218258,"author":"unixhero","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best, most detailed version of the elevator talk.","quote":"This version is the best, with all details, no secrets withheld.","text":"Elevator Hacking: From The Pit To The Penthouse by Deviant Ollam and Howard Payne [VIDEO] They've done several iterations of this, including a censored/shorter version for DEFCON 22. This version is the best, with all details, no secrets withheld. I find the content rather stunning, and I've even watched it several times."},{"id":12232941,"author":"unixhero","month":"2016-08","story":"Demystifying the Secure Enclave Processor [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite sec-conference video of all time.","quote":"This is my favorite sec-conference video of all time","text":"Does anyone know where the Blackhat 2016 videos will be posted? I've always found them incredibly educational. Okay I'll leave something behind as well. This is my favorite sec-conference video of all time: [HOPE X] Elevator Hacking: From the Pit to the Penthouse [VIDEO] Closely followed by DEF CON 18 - Joseph McCray - You Spent All That Money and You Still Got Owned... [LINK]"},{"id":10138986,"author":"nyolfen","month":"2015-08","story":"Remembering When Driverless Elevators Drew Skepticism","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Entertaining panel on an esoteric topic.","quote":"one of the most entertaining con panels i've seen, and interesting information on an esoteric topic.","text":"this reminds me of a truly excellent HOPE X presentation about elevator hacking[0]. one of the most entertaining con panels i've seen, and interesting information on an esoteric topic. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8589609,"author":"alexggordon","month":"2014-11","story":"The Lives of Elevators (2008)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Changed commenter's view of elevators entirely.","quote":"It changed my entire view of elevators and if you haven't seen it, I would highly recommend you watch.","text":"This reminds me of an excellent talk on elevator hacking [0] given by Deviant Ollam and Howard Payne at [HOPE X] a little while ago. It changed my entire view of elevators and if you haven't seen it, I would highly recommend you watch. it [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34303959,"author":"danpalmer","month":"2023-01","story":"The secret undo codes for elevator floor buttons","stance":"rec","why":"Gives insight into elevators from a hacker perspective.","quote":"gives a lot of insight. All from a hacker perspective, but fascinating and fun.","text":"“From the Pit to the Penthouse” gives a lot of insight. All from a hacker perspective, but fascinating and fun. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18525215,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2018-11","story":"An elevator in a Chicago skyscraper fell 84 floors, requiring a dramatic rescue","stance":"reference","why":"Link to the longer HOPE X talk.","quote":"Here's the link to the HOPE X one you mention","text":"Ohh thank you! I suspected the one I saw was longer than 1 hour. Here's the link to the HOPE X one you mention: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8223189,"author":"Albuca","month":"2014-08","story":"Earthquake early-warning system gave 10-second alert before Napa quake felt","stance":"reference","why":"Elevator safety sensors detect building sway.","quote":"","text":"I completely agree! A few seconds can certainly save one’s life. The main issue is how to broadcast this warning without suffering from any delay or latency... For example, elevators in areas prone to earthquakes have sensors installed that can detect the movement of the building [1]. If it sways too far it will automatically move the elevator (up or down) to get the car as far as possible from the heavy heavy counterweight - as there is the possibility that it could break free and smush the car. [1]Elevator Hacking: From the Pit to the Penthouse - [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["nphxoUxSvgY",1],["q5SRrsr-Iug",1],["C4VHfmiwuv4",1],["rxOKEsBx4NU",1],["iD9xk3SDSYc",1],["vYLEuQrvND0",1],["mII9NZ8MMVM",1],["ZUvGfuLlZus",1]]},"YX3iRjKj7C0":{"wy":"HN cites it in discussions of language evangelism, community dynamics and zealotry, as an accessible account of how a good language can die for non-technical reasons.","q":{"x":"I find this talk [0] very informative and useful while interacting with other languages and communities.","i":32992695,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":39572248,"author":"bayindirh","month":"2024-03","story":"Rewrite the VP9 codec library in Rust","stance":"rec","why":"Great talk about language zealotry and flamewars","quote":"A great talk I like about this issue is Robert Martin's","text":"> your arguments against Rust come off as biased. Yes, I'm pretty aware of this, and I keep this bias voluntarily, because Rust is generally touted (hyped?) as a Silver Bullet, and I'm wary of Silver Bullets. So, I want to highlight that fact. > Sorry if I came across as rude. No hard feelings. As I said, I'm not under delusion of a perfect life. I just said it. > I just find it slightly puzzling how your stance on it is kind of going up and down. :) I have a habit of mirroring the tone of the person I'm talking with. Combine it with the fact that English is not my native language, it's possible that \"tone adjustment\" is far from perfect. > it's been quite a while since I've seen Rust zealotry on HN... Yes, there's no zealotry here, and it's great, but I'm exposed to a wider community than HN people. Unfortunately, HN represents very small percent of the programmers around. A great talk I like about this issue is Robert Martin's \"What Killed Smalltalk Could Kill Ruby, Too\" [0]. Let's say, I'm marred and bitter from tons of zealotry and flamewars over the years (yes, I'm not young folk). > While that likely does wonders for your mental health... [Snipped for brevity] First of all, thanks. I pray that you work in a place you love and never work again. I'm very aware that how being solo makes me different and biased, and I think I highlighted at a couple of places. On the other hand, I understand what teams and team-related activities are important for code quality. Let's say th"},{"id":32992695,"author":"bayindirh","month":"2022-09","story":"Go runtime: 4 years later","stance":"rec","why":"Informative, perspective-broadening talk on language communities","quote":"I find this talk [0] very informative and useful while interacting with other languages and communities.","text":"We always make this discussion with friends. I don't think every language should tick the boxes the same way. I also personally like Go a lot. It's filling the gap between C++ and Python for me. If I need something compiled with proper threading support, but C++ would be an overkill, I reach for Go. Go is designed with a human centric view, IMHO: \"Make writing great programs easier rather than design a language with novel/cutting edge features, but with a high cognitive load\", and I find it as a noble aim as Rust's guarantees and aspirations. I understand why people love Rust, but I don't think it's the only one, or the proverbial silver bullet that we finally achieved. Yes, it's nice. Yes, it brings many things to the table, but it's not the final solution for once and for all. You like Rust? Great, Go for it (unintended pun alert), but I think we need all languages from all paradigms and perspectives. I find this talk [0] very informative and useful while interacting with other languages and communities. I'm not sharing this with any insinuations towards you, but just find it generally perspective broadening. [0]: [VIDEO] - What Killed Smalltalk could Kill Ruby."},{"id":12850751,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2016-11","story":"Keep Ruby Weird Again [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Older talk worth checking as related","quote":"maybe one should also check this older talk","text":"Quite interesting, maybe one should also check this older talk: Robert Martin, \"What Killed Smalltalk Could Kill Ruby\", 2009 [VIDEO]"},{"id":10008272,"author":"duncanawoods","month":"2015-08","story":"Redline Smalltalk for the JVM","stance":"rec","why":"Reasons Smalltalk died, summarized as tldw","quote":"The tldw reasons he gives for why Smalltalk died","text":"After watching a few Alan Kay talks where he pines for Smalltalk, I happened to watch this Robert Martin talk \"What killed Smalltalk could kill Ruby\". [VIDEO] The tldw reasons he gives for why Smalltalk died: - too easy to make a mess where adding complexity would be punished late. - arrogance - community isolated itself from the world and did not address common industry needs - commercial only - alternative free languages dominated"},{"id":9851851,"author":"brudgers","month":"2015-07","story":"Some Functional Programmers Are Arrogant","stance":"rec","why":"How arrogance affected Smalltalk and language communities","quote":"probably worth watching if you enjoy Uncle Bob's soulful stylings","text":"For some context on Martin's comments his talk on how arrogance effected Smalltalk is probably relevant: [VIDEO] and probably worth watching if you enjoy Uncle Bob's soulful stylings and are interested in his thoughts about programming language communities."},{"id":3899835,"author":"AlexFromBelgium","month":"2012-04","story":"In which we plot an escape from the quagmire of equality (Clojure and Emacs)","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on why Smalltalk died and lessons for Ruby","quote":"pretty interesting","text":"Thx for the responses. I just watched \"What Killed Smalltalk Could Kill Ruby, Too\" [VIDEO] pretty interesting"},{"id":48125751,"author":"bayindirh","month":"2026-05","story":"CERT is releasing six CVEs for serious security vulnerabilities in dnsmasq","stance":"reference","why":"Robert Martin's talk on what killed Smalltalk and could kill Ruby.","quote":"What Killed Smalltalk Could Kill Ruby, Too","text":"As another data point, I have gone through enough flame wars, incl. the usual ones, and Rust. There's certainly a fanatic group of Rust developers who really want to eradicate C and C++ from the people's knowledge and all codebases in this universe , so far so openly hating the developers and designers of the said languages. Same was (or still is) true for some LLVM/clang people w.r.t. GCC. This is why I use neither. I'm always happy to discuss PLT and merits of programming languages with neutral parties, even in lively fashion, but when open-mindedness gets thrown out of the window, I do leave the room. These kinds of healthy discussions will benefit both parties. Hubris, ego, closed-mindedness and fanaticism won't. Related: What Killed Smalltalk Could Kill Ruby, Too: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42947283,"author":"bayindirh","month":"2025-02","story":"Software development topics I've changed my mind on","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on what killed Smalltalk, cited in a discussion of programming evangelism.","quote":"","text":"Let me try to explain it a bit more. From my experience, functional programmers come in two flavors. The ones who constantly nag others by telling them \"everything you do is wrong\", and \"hey, come here, I want to show you something!\". Unfortunately, the first camp is way more dominant than others. Again, from the same article, there's observation \"People who care about the craft are rare. Cherish the who cares, meet the rest at where they are\". This is very true. The problem is, the people in the first camp can't cater to either group. First group has strong opinions and want a solid (not hostile, solid) discussion about how they can improve and how that tool works, and the second group doesn't care. Also, every programmer has an understanding of the machine, and they program on top of that abstraction. For me, \"C virtual machine\", or the modern hardware is very easy to grasp. For every line of code, I can have an educated guess about how my code will run (e.g. how will the branch predictor will behave or got inadvertently poisoned), hence imperative languages and the primitive memory management is easy for me. For others who think more meta, functional paradigm is a natural think to construct in their brains. Not meeting them at where they are is again off-putting. All in all, nobody wants to be constantly nagged to feel \"stupid\" about not understanding functional programming or how unelegant imperative is, and most of the functional programmers are doing this, even without "},{"id":34008393,"author":"ksherlock","month":"2022-12","story":"What killed Haskell, could kill Rust, too","stance":"reference","why":"Original talk that the essay adapts","quote":"","text":"\"_As one might have guessed, this is not an essay. It's a transcript of the following talk by R. Martin with some substitutions made (SmallTalk -> Haskell, Ruby -> Rust, and others). You are free to make any conclusions from this._ [What killed Smalltalk, could kill Ruby, too]( [VIDEO] )\""},{"id":11510437,"author":"brudgers","month":"2016-04","story":"C is Manly, Python is for “n00bs”","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on programming language hubris","quote":"Uncle Bob's talk on programming language hubris","text":"First I was reminded of Uncle Bob's talk on programming language hubris: [VIDEO] but as I went deeper thought about the effects of programming language tribalism, I was reminded of [LINK] it's reference to [LINK] and how this played out as a sound bite in US popular culture at the same time as the decline in women computer science enrollment began to decline. Being in the high school graduating cohort where the decline began, the decline of women in computing a subject I'm interested in. It's one of those subtle cultural/generational differences between me and my boy."},{"id":10075049,"author":"Ace17","month":"2015-08","story":"Why is Smalltalk Dead?","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Ward Cunningham quote on Smalltalk's messiness","quote":"","text":"Too much dynamicity? Ward Cunningham: \"What killed Smalltalk was it was just too easy to make a mess\" (source: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":9691768,"author":"brudgers","month":"2015-06","story":"Design Principles Behind Smalltalk (1981)","stance":"reference","why":"Why Smalltalk died, related to community model","quote":"But the lesson of Smalltalk is that \"better\" isn't just in the technical details.","text":"I read the closing remarks: Natural Selection: Languages and systems that are of sound design will persist, to be supplanted only by better ones. and thought of Bob Martin's What Killed Smalltalk Could Kill Ruby [1]. The technical merits of Smalltalk are such that few claims \"to do objects better\" are taken seriously. But the lesson of Smalltalk is that \"better\" isn't just in the technical details. It lives in the model of community that it's speakers adopt. People speak C and JavaScript because they rode along free with Unix and the browser. Even in it's darkest days, Lisp tagged along in AutoCad and Emacs. These languages had practical purposes that let them compete outside of the \"what language should I build my next project in\" marketplace and outside the segment of professional software developers. Smalltalk was killed by its business models. Without runtime licensing, providing a REPL meant that every application would come with a free copy. Not providing a REPL meant knee-capping the application. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5107533,"author":"mylittlepony","month":"2013-01","story":"What It's Like To Be Ridiculed For Open Sourcing A Project","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Having tried Ruby for a year, I have experimented this myself. With a strong OOP background, I can assert that it's not even an elite that enjoys ridiculing noobs. It reminded me of this: [VIDEO] Also, just look at the top comment in that video. The irony."},{"id":38695455,"author":"yakshaving_jgt","month":"2023-12","story":"I've Misunderstood the Single Responsibility Principle","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Sure. Balls: [VIDEO] Types: [LINK] I'll concede that I misremembered cajones as balls , but the sentiment is the same."}],"r":[["vVgc8ksstyg",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["0Rnq1NpHdmw",1],["KqFNbCcyFkk",1],["vtIzMaLkCaM",1],["CZIINXhGDcs",1],["jtMWEiCdsfc",1],["dljNabciEGg",1],["Tl9kD693ie4",1],["NH1Tta7purM",1],["xnqTKD8uD64",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1],["DRq2NgeFcO0",1],["z_dt7NG38V4",1],["JfmTagWcqoE",1],["D7Sd8A6_fYU",1],["cj02_UeUnGQ",1],["XkDEzfpdcSg",1],["hEx5DNLWGgA",1],["NqKyHEJe9_w",1]]},"Bv_5Zv5c-Ts":{"wy":"HN commenters call it one of the best JavaScript courses: rigorous and deep but not boring, and freely available.","q":{"x":"they are the holy grails of online education for me. Rigorous -- but not boring or too long.","i":12593810,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":13150138,"author":"sjnair96","month":"2016-12","story":"Learning Advanced JavaScript (2008)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deep, digestible JS course from favorite author","quote":"shamelessly plug my favorite course author Anthony Alicea","text":"Once again I am going to shamelessly plug my favorite course author Anthony Alicea and his JS/NodeJS/Angular1 course[1],[2],[3]. I really really liked Tony's course as it gave quite a deep look into topics, which are usually only attainable from books, while at the same time keeping it very interesting and also contain lots of best practice material and being extremely dense but easy to digest. Do let me know if you guys know any other courses of the same style. For quite a while I have been trying to find similar courses/tutorials of similar caliber when it comes to knowledge gained and being easy to digest. If you haven't watched any of Tony's courses, do give them a shot. Rigorous -- but not boring or too long. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] . Disclaimer: Because it may sound like it, I'd like to clarify I have absolutely no affiliation with Tony or his courses. I just really really liked them."},{"id":12593810,"author":"sjnair96","month":"2016-09","story":"Free Beginner and Intermediate JavaScript Courses","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deep, engaging, dense JS course","quote":"they are the holy grails of online education for me. Rigorous -- but not boring or too long.","text":"If anyone has ever tried Anthony Alicea's JS/NodeJS/Angular1 course[1],[2],[3], please let me know if rithmschool's or any other course holds a candle to it. I really really liked Tony's course as it gave quite a deep look into topics, which are usually only attainable from books, while at the same time keeping it very interesting and also contain lots of best practice material and being extremely dense but easy to digest. Do let me know if you guys know any other courses of the same style. If you haven't watched any of Tony's courses, do give them a shot -- they are the holy grails of online education for me. Rigorous -- but not boring or too long. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] Disclaimer: Because it may sound like it, I'd like to clarify I have absolutely no affiliation with Tony or his courses. I just really really liked them."},{"id":12593904,"author":"sjnair96","month":"2016-09","story":"Free Beginner and Intermediate JavaScript Courses","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best JS course, rigorous yet short","quote":"Here's the one I found to be the best by far","text":"We're two sides of the same coin - I know some JS (can't say I have any sort of mastery though) but don't have good HTML/CSS foundations. Let's trade resources? Here's the one I found to be the best by far - Anthony Alicea's Javascript Understanding The Weird Parts course. It's rigorous, and develops and intuition while not being long and boring. You can quite comfortably finish it in a week. Also, one thing that would really help if you're doing said course is is Joan Mira's notes[2] which I will myself be compiling into a Gitbook for future reference. [1] [VIDEO] [2] joanmira.com/javascript-understanding-the-weird-parts/ If you find anything similar in style to Tony's courses, I would love to start them right away. The thing is I'm right now in a patch of analysis paralysis of not being able to decide which course I should invest time in rather than just starting something."},{"id":18816642,"author":"SonnyWortzik","month":"2019-01","story":"Show HN: Learn and practice modern JavaScript","stance":"rec","why":"Well-explained deep dive into JS internals, still relevant","quote":"He goes very deep in the inner workings of javascript. I think you will like it.","text":"There is a yt video that I found very well done and explained very well. He goes very deep in the inner workings of javascript. I think you will like it. It is \"modern\" but not a new video. It is probably about 3 years old. Still relevant. He has a udemy course for $10 bucks. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11298626,"author":"adnanc","month":"2016-03","story":"Ask HN: What's the most useful online course you have watched?","stance":"rec","why":"Free first 3.5 hours of a JavaScript deep-dive course","quote":"Javascript: Understanding the Weird Parts - the first 3.5 hours free on Youtube","text":"Just came across this link from the course authors twitter Javascript: Understanding the Weird Parts - the first 3.5 hours free on Youtube: [VIDEO] Also has the entire course at 87% off [LINK]"}]},"C25qzDhGLx8":{"wy":"HN longevity fans point to it as a concise, fun statement of the case for fighting aging, and some say it changed their minds.","q":{"x":"CGP gray's videos on YouTube are maybe the most concise and fun to watch materials I've seen on the subject.","i":22002471,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":34552332,"author":"wilg","month":"2023-01","story":"$6.96B was raised by private longevity companies in 2022","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Changed the commenter's opinion on ending aging.","quote":"My opinion on the topic changed significantly after watching these great videos","text":"My opinion on the topic changed significantly after watching these great videos: - Why Age? Should We End Aging Forever? - Kurzgesagt - [LINK] - How to Cure Aging – Kurzgesagt - [LINK] - Why Die? - CGP Grey - [VIDEO]"},{"id":22002471,"author":"seer","month":"2020-01","story":"Scientists identify pathways that extend nematode worm lifespan fivefold","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Concise, fun explanation of anti-aging case.","quote":"CGP gray's videos on YouTube are maybe the most concise and fun to watch materials I've seen on the subject.","text":"CGP gray's videos on YouTube are maybe the most concise and fun to watch materials I've seen on the subject. [VIDEO] [LINK] And you'll be surprised how many people _are_ indeed thinking about all of the outcomes. Have in mind though that as with any technology it is not good or bad, and if it is possible someone will do it sooner or later. It makes no sense to not do it as your adversaries will, and then they'll dictate how it is used, probably not to your liking. If you develop it first you can talk and deliberate at your leisure and find solutions that look much better to you and yours."},{"id":45765681,"author":"willis936","month":"2025-10","story":"How ancient people saw themselves","stance":"rec","why":"Commenter's views mirror Grey's case for fighting aging.","quote":"CGPGrey's thoughts on this mirror a lot of my own.","text":"I don't think that death is a certainty. It is today, but we are working on it. We are not supernatural. CGPGrey's thoughts on this mirror a lot of my own. I grant that not all care given to aged is a kindness, but not fighting aging is not virtuous. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":17938012,"author":"chr1","month":"2018-09","story":"Startups Flock to Turn Young Blood into an Elixir of Youth","stance":"rec","why":"Argument that curing aging is most important.","quote":"","text":"> It tries to address something that was always considered a hard limit and the perfectly natural thing Death being \"perfectly natural\" is not a good argument, having slaves, eating people, not allowing women to vote, all have been considered perfectly natural at various points in time, and some time soon accepting aging will be considered equally backwards, as these other things. > There are plenty of movies on this topic so no need to go into details I am asking because i have not seen a single pro-aging argument that had made any sense to me, in the best case they are about some people killing other people to get their stuff, which is not directly related to aging, in the worst they are \"fox and the grapes\" kind of rationalizations saying that the thing people want but can't get is probably not worth it. On the other hand i have seen many compelling arguments that curing aging is the _most important_ thing that we can do now. For instance: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":15548818,"author":"yoz-y","month":"2017-10","story":"Anti-aging stem cell treatment proves successful in early human trials","stance":"reference","why":"Anti-death argument.","quote":"Well, on the anti-death side","text":"Well, on the anti-death side: [VIDEO] On the pro death side I think the book Risen Empire has quite interesting take on that as well. Edit: Put in the wrong link to a 24hour long video, fixed now"}],"r":[["cZYNADOHhVY",3],["GoJsr4IwCm4",2],["ObhK8lUfIlc",1],["Kas0tIxDvrg",1],["JurplDfPi3U",1],["t-O-Qdh7VvQ",1],["msVuCEs8Ydo",1],["D28uSzCs7-k",1],["PqPGOhXoprU",1],["rhgwIhB58PA",1],["APZCfmgzoS0",1],["QyJZzq0v7Z4",1],["CTV-wwszGw8",1],["MjdpR-TY6QU",1]]},"dNs_kNilSjk":{"wy":"HN points to it as an impressive, hands-on DIY demonstration of passive radiative cooling that works in practice.","q":{"x":"Great video about making home-made high reflectivity paint","i":38238476,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":39586846,"author":"slimsag","month":"2024-03","story":"Using a solar oven as a radiant refrigerator at night","stance":"rec","why":"DIY radiative cooling paint experiments","quote":"they've been working on making paint using this technology with an at-home DIY process","text":"You might like NightHawkInLight's videos on the topic[0] and Tech Ingredients[1] - they've been working on making paint using this technology with an at-home DIY process and experimenting with it. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39587074,"author":"20after4","month":"2024-03","story":"Using a solar oven as a radiant refrigerator at night","stance":"rec","why":"Cool radiant cooling projects","quote":"the YouTube channel \"Tech Ingredients\" do some cool things with radiant cooling","text":"Sort of related: the YouTube channel \"Tech Ingredients\" do some cool things with radiant cooling: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38238476,"author":"scotty79","month":"2023-11","story":"Passive radiative cooling ceramic with high solar reflectivity","stance":"rec","why":"Home-made high reflectivity paint that stays cooler than ambient air","quote":"Great video about making home-made high reflectivity paint","text":"Great video about making home-made high reflectivity paint: [VIDEO] Aluminium plate painted with this paint is cooler when exposed to the sun not only than unpainted plate but also ambient air which feels really magical for me."},{"id":36595467,"author":"Azrael3000","month":"2023-07","story":"Making Radiative Sky Cooling Paint (With Novel CaCO₃ Microsphere Synthesis) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Answers whether cooling paint beats regular white paint","quote":"Yes, it is better than regular white paint","text":"You can check out Tech Ingredients video [0] on the same topic, which will give an answer to your question. It is: Yes, it is better than regular white paint [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35866404,"author":"bertil","month":"2023-05","story":"Vietnam records highest ever temperature of 44.1C","stance":"rec","why":"Impressive build video on passive cooling","quote":"Their latest video is on passive cooling and very impressive.","text":"Technology Connection is a fantastic YouTube channel that shows how to build all sorts of useful things. Their latest video is on passive cooling and very impressive. [VIDEO] Warning: the “senior-engineer; knows how to make stuff” energy from that channel is off the charts. Enough to warn people on HN."},{"id":35536608,"author":"slimsag","month":"2023-04","story":"Energy-saving coating without pigment keeps the surface beneath cool","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how to make infrared cooling paint at home","quote":"There's definitely something here, hopefully someone sells it pre-made, I'd love to try some.","text":"Two YouTube researchers[0][1] (they make science content that is very much not in the traditional form of YouTube) have been working on Infrared Cooling Paint for almost a year now and have been detailing how individuals can make it at home (although a fairly involved/lengthy process today.) There's definitely something here, hopefully someone sells it pre-made, I'd love to try some. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":48692820,"author":"netsharc","month":"2026-06","story":"The French are painting their windows with chalk to beat the heat","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This science-lecturer-esque guy is using Barium Sulfate","text":"Using CaCO3 which is calcium carbonate, like the article. This science-lecturer-esque guy is using Barium Sulfate: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46187062,"author":"jcims","month":"2025-12","story":"Mechanical power generation using Earth's ambient radiation","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Tech Ingredients did one or two vids as well","text":"DIY radiative cooling paint from YouTuber NightHawkInLight - [LINK] It has pretty impressive performance. Tech Ingredients did one or two vids as well - [VIDEO] Was thinking of whipping up a batch for my rv."},{"id":45556748,"author":"Llamamoe","month":"2025-10","story":"The World's 2.75B Buildings","stance":"reference","why":"Radiative cooling paint reflecting nearly all light","quote":"","text":"Are you perhaps thinking of radiative cooling, e.g. paint that reflects close to 100% of all light, excluding the spectrum at which room-temperature objects radiate heat, resulting in a net cooling effect? E.g. [Revolutionary Paint: How to Make Surfaces Stay Cool in the Sun]( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":44358781,"author":"energ8","month":"2025-06","story":"NASA's Voyager Found a 30k-50k Kelvin \"Wall\" at the Edge of Solar System","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"there are a couple good videos about it from NightHawkInLight","text":"It's a thing in from thousands of years ago [LINK] and today [LINK] for PDRC there are a couple good videos about it from NightHawkInLight [LINK] , [LINK] and Tech Ingredients [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["qS34oV-jv_A",1],["42QuXLucH3Q",1],["b0EF0VTs9Dc",1],["KDRnEm-B3AI",1],["N3bJnKmeNJY",1],["2Vrhk5OjBP8",1],["099cHWSbAL8",1]]},"qID2B4MK7Y0":{"wy":"HN points to it as a funny, convincing take on number bases whenever dozenal or base 6 comes up.","q":{"x":"This video convinced me that base 6 would be even better for simple pen-and-paper math","i":23191959,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24405844,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2020-09","story":"Show HN: Base32H, a human-friendly duotrigesimal number system","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Funny comparison of decimal, dozenal and seximal","quote":"One of the best videos of number-base related comedy.","text":"Time to link the ConLang Critic's video \"A better way to count\" where he compares decimal and dozenal (base12) with seximal (base6) and base 6 wins. [VIDEO] One of the best videos of number-base related comedy."},{"id":23191959,"author":"ar-nelson","month":"2020-05","story":"Roman Finger Counting","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Convincing case for base 6 arithmetic","quote":"This video convinced me that base 6 would be even better for simple pen-and-paper math","text":"This video convinced me that base 6 would be even better for simple pen-and-paper math, as well as just about everything else: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26867705,"author":"cjhveal","month":"2021-04","story":"My Love Affair with Dozens (1972) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting take on dozenal and base 6","quote":"has an interesting video on Dozenal and \"Seximal\" (base 6) number systems.","text":"One of my favorite language Youtubers, jan Misali, has an interesting video on Dozenal and \"Seximal\" (base 6) number systems. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18473572,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2018-11","story":"International System of Units overhauled in historic vote","stance":"rec","why":"Argues base 6 beats other bases","quote":"all the rest of the benefits in that (totally serious) video.","text":"Why are you so obsessed with thirds? If you care about short, clean fractions on a human scale, base 6 is overall better, see this table: [VIDEO] Plus you get all the convenience of having five digits on a hand and 5 as the highest counting digit, so you can count to 55 on two hands, and all the rest of the benefits in that (totally serious) video."},{"id":44823986,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2025-08","story":"We'd be better off with 9-bit bytes","stance":"reference","why":"Satirical bit about dozenal digit symbols","quote":"Jan Misali in his comedy video on why base 6 is a better way to count than base 12 or base 10","text":"> \" (like you just need to remember 2 more digits) \" \"The standard among mathematicians for writing larger bases is to extend the Arabic numerals using the Latin alphabet, so ten is written with the letter A and eleven is written with the letter B. But actually doing it that way makes ten and eleven look like they're too separate from the rest of the digits so you can use an inverted two for ten and an inverted three for eleven. But those don't display in most fonts so you can approximate them with the letters T and E which also happen to be the first letters of the English words ten and eleven. But actually as long as we're okay for using the Latin alphabet characters for these digits then we might as well use X for ten like in Roman numerals. But actually now we're back to having them look too different from the other ten digits so how about instead we use the Greek letters Chi and Epsilon but actually if we're using Greek letters then there's no association between the X looking letter and the number ten, so maybe you can write ten with the Greek letter delta instead. And all you really need to learn is those 'two new digits' and you're ready to use dozenal.\" - Jan Misali in his comedy video on why base 6 is a better way to count than base 12 or base 10 [VIDEO] (which is a pisstake and ends up making the point that Base 10 isn't so bad). (\"in dozenal, a seventh is written as 0.186X35 recurring because it's equal to one gross eight dozen ten great gross ten gross three doze"}],"r":[["knbVWXzL4-4",1],["Uv1UfLPK7_Q",1],["TqKQ94DtS54",1],["VO6XEQIsCoM",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["aPvXnmBIO7o",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["4q1dgn_C0AU",1],["21j_OCNLuYg",1],["9OfBpQgLXUc",1],["kmFOBoy2MZ8",1],["IgF3OX8nT0w",1],["9LMr5XTgeyI",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1],["MnyeRlMsTgI",1],["CGRtyxEpoGg",1],["vxWjtpzCIfA",1],["uxykI30fS54",1]]},"Hy4QjmKzF1c":{"wy":"Recommended in urban planning and housing threads as a concise summary of what makes cities livable and beautiful.","q":{"x":"This video is really informative in this regard and has changed my opinion on a few things","i":16871162,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16871162,"author":"konschubert","month":"2018-04","story":"The Dow of Cities","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Informative on why some cities are beautiful","quote":"This video is really informative in this regard and has changed my opinion on a few things","text":"Because there are ugly cities and there are beautiful cities. And they are so for a reason. This video is really informative in this regard and has changed my opinion on a few things: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17371500,"author":"cleansy","month":"2018-06","story":"Future generations will laugh in horror and derision at the folly of facadism","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why building guidelines make cities beautiful","quote":"made a video that explains that pretty well.","text":"> Finally, it should be mentioned that buildings are owned by private companies and individuals, who generally have a right to do whatever they want to them! Err, no. A city can and should decide what the \"overall look and feel\" is. London fails at that spectacularly. Pretty much all cities that are considered beautiful have building guidelines in place that support that. Like: you can build only to the height of neighboring buildings, windows need to line up, specific styles and mandatory balconies to fit into the environment. Think of Amsterdam or Barcelona. The overall look of a city is part of the quality of life of its citizens. If you leave it to property developers, well, you get something like London. EDIT: \"The school of Life\" made a video that explains that pretty well. [1] 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10889428,"author":"matkam","month":"2016-01","story":"San Francisco's Fog Over Growth","stance":"rec","why":"Broader ideas and solutions for attractive cities","quote":"the same channel offers some more ideas and broader view solutions.","text":"Relevant to this topic, Youtube channel \"The School of Life\" talks about this issue where people are reluctant to build, causing the housing price to go up. One Reason Homes Cost So Much: [LINK] As a follow up, the same channel offers some more ideas and broader view solutions. How to Make an Attractive City: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10443247,"author":"ZenoArrow","month":"2015-10","story":"Land-value tax: Why Henry George had a point","stance":"rec","why":"Captures important principles of city planning","quote":"This video captures quite a bit of what I consider important in city planning","text":"This video captures quite a bit of what I consider important in city planning, would be interested to hear your thoughts on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10443277,"author":"ZenoArrow","month":"2015-10","story":"Land-value tax: Why Henry George had a point","stance":"rec","why":"Ideas for making a livable city","quote":"I believe it captures most of what is important in making a livable city that people enjoy living in.","text":"What do you think of the ideas in this video: [VIDEO] I believe it captures most of what is important in making a livable city that people enjoy living in."},{"id":10837902,"author":"ZenoArrow","month":"2016-01","story":"Home is where the cartel is: On the politics and economics of land-use controls","stance":"reference","why":"Tailoring new developments to their environment","quote":"tailor new developments to the environment they are being placed in","text":"The problem can be tackled by making efforts to tailor new developments to the environment they are being placed in. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["8S3B8a8N83k",1],["jVoSQP2yUYA",1],["Qaq9vlfoGnA",1],["KgzQuE1pR1w",1],["fg0_Vhldz-8",1],["R5Gppi-O3a8",1],["GLG9g7BcjKs",1],["ERbvKrH-GC4",1],["iRsSjh5TTqI",1],["iFDe5kUUyT0",1]]},"P73REgj-3UE":{"wy":"HN shares it as a mesmerizing example of building shelter and tools entirely from scratch.","q":{"x":"His videos are a great watch","i":10634112,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10634112,"author":"TomNomNom","month":"2015-11","story":"Making a pencil from scratch (2013)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Channel of making tools, kilns, tiles and huts from scratch.","quote":"His videos are a great watch","text":"This guy's YouTube channel [0] features videos of him making stone tools, cutting down a tree, digging up clay, making a kiln, roof tiles and huts; all from scratch. His videos are a great watch, and even with heavy editing it's pretty clear how difficult it all must be. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16883035,"author":"Jakob","month":"2018-04","story":"How to build a house alone [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Man builds house including its tools entirely himself.","quote":"This man is building a house including the tools you need to build that house completely by himself","text":"Impressive! Of course technically he’s building it not by himself but with the help of a lot of technology. ;) This man is building a house including the tools you need to build that house completely by himself: Primitive Technology: Tiled Roof Hut [VIDEO]"},{"id":13201171,"author":"11thEarlOfMar","month":"2016-12","story":"Chinese smiths forge a large flange on the street [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Mesmerizing hand-built tile-roofed hut.","quote":"Adding to my 'collection' of mesmerizing manual labor: Building a tile-roofed hut by hand","text":"Adding to my 'collection' of mesmerizing manual labor: Building a tile-roofed hut by hand: [VIDEO] Building a brick vaulted ceiling: [LINK] The cinder block wall: [LINK]"},{"id":11783847,"author":"11thEarlOfMar","month":"2016-05","story":"A Rock-Sorting Robot","stance":"rec","why":"One of two favorite videos of people working.","quote":"here are two of my favorites","text":"Don't know the source, but as far as 'watching other people working', here are two of my favorites: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13420994,"author":"Retric","month":"2017-01","story":"At Davos, IBM Chief Predicts Artificial Intelligence Won’t Be a Job Killer","stance":"reference","why":"Used as example that hand-building a house is slower but not hugely.","quote":"is slower than a modern house, but not that slow","text":"I don't think several orders of magnitude is really true. [VIDEO] is slower than a modern house, but not that slow. Further, with just hand tools the Amish where known for raising a barn in one day."},{"id":12497018,"author":"sapphireblue","month":"2016-09","story":"Why are Adults so busy?","stance":"reference","why":"Example that building a shack is not hard.","quote":"Building a shack isn't that hard, see","text":"Building a shack isn't that hard, see [VIDEO] Try building it in the forest though - it is most likely illegal due to some rentier (or government) already owning it. I think the grandparent has some truth to their words. Existing outside the dwelling is illegal. Dwellings are owned by the elite rentier class, so you need some money just to exist (unless you are a rentier)."}],"r":[["HxaD_trXwRE",1]]},"uB0gr7Fh6lY":{"wy":"HN recommends it, and Jordan's wider Flock series, as a well-documented look at the poor security of these surveillance cameras.","q":{"x":"This is an excellent video documenting some Flock camera vulnerabilities by Benn Jordan","i":46904237,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48708365,"author":"abalashov","month":"2026-06","story":"Flock cameras track more than your license plate, and they're spreading fast","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Security vulnerabilities in Flock cameras, intriguing to tinkerers.","quote":"always deserves an explicit shout-out. It's likely to intrigue the tinkerers here","text":"These horrific things are multiplying exponentially in my (rural GA) environs. There are a dozen of them along every conceivable cycling route I could take, and far more if I drive somewhere. If you think this is a city thing meant to deter urban crime, the explosive proliferation of Flock cameras in quite rural and suburban areas may shock you. I find them in the darndest of places, near but not on county lines, adjacent to minor bridges, etc. And next time I go through there, there are more. They seem to be procreating. As others have pointed out, they're not just ALPRs or traffic cameras, and their use-cases, official and unofficial, are extremely dynamic and expanding fast. They are not the only thing of their kind, but they justly earned the lightning rod status for their conspicuous cooperation with the administration's immigration thuggery and the douchy--but highly consequential--pronouncements of their CEO. Moreover, there's a ticker tape of daily news about police misuse of Flock's database, mainly for stalking exes and things like that. This _is_ a stop on the way to a Chinese-style surveillance state, and there's nothing inevitable about it. But it will happen if we allow it to happen. Ben Johnson's video on the security vulnerabilities, linked in the article, always deserves an explicit shout-out. It's likely to intrigue the tinkerers here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47690241,"author":"gorgonical","month":"2026-04","story":"US cities are axing Flock Safety surveillance technology","stance":"rec","why":"Series on Flock cameras' poor security and government interfacing.","quote":"I recommend them.","text":"Musician-turning-tech anarchist (?) Benn Jordan is making a very interesting series of videos about Flock cameras, their poor safety, and their gray-area interfacing with local governments: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] I recommend them."},{"id":46904237,"author":"text0404","month":"2026-02","story":"Flock CEO calls Deflock a \"terrorist organization\" [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Documents Flock camera vulnerabilities.","quote":"This is an excellent video documenting some Flock camera vulnerabilities by Benn Jordan","text":"This is an excellent video documenting some Flock camera vulnerabilities by Benn Jordan, a security hobbyist/researcher: [VIDEO] . It's a bit long, but worth it."},{"id":46171891,"author":"stanac","month":"2025-12","story":"Have I been Flocked? – Check if your license plate is being watched","stance":"rec","why":"Benn Jordan's videos on Flock cameras.","quote":"I would also recommend Benn Jordan's YT channel","text":"I would also recommend Benn Jordan's YT channel, he has a couple of videos covering flock cameras. His latest one is: [VIDEO] edit: grammar"},{"id":46249532,"author":"ultrarunner","month":"2025-12","story":"Home Depot GitHub token exposed for a year, granted access to internal systems","stance":"reference","why":"Cited about location data going to police via Flock.","quote":"","text":"Maybe you're not familiar with Flock Safety, but my comment is not about locking up high value items. It's more about my location information being shipped to weird police circles by big box stores. [0] deflock.me [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46361030,"author":"phyzome","month":"2025-12","story":"Flock Exposed Its AI-Powered Cameras to the Internet. We Tracked Ourselves","stance":"reference","why":"Security camera issues shown with Flock as example around 33:00.","quote":"around 33:00 here","text":"Sure, around 33:00 here: [VIDEO] The other video is [LINK] but I don't recall which one goes more into it. It's also possible I'm just remembering Flock-specific stuff from other sources, and the things he shows in these videos are more general issues with security camera companies (using Flock as the example). It would be great if this stuff was (also?) published as blog posts so that it could be easily skimmed..."}],"r":[["vU1-uiUlHTo",1],["Pp9MwZkHiMQ",1]]},"41VJudBdYXY":{"wy":"HN points to it in housing-cost threads as the standard example of government-built housing keeping rents down.","q":{"x":"if you supply at least some public housing, it can have an outsized ability to keep rents down","i":36067189,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38721326,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2023-12","story":"US homelessness up 12% from 2022, hits highest level since 2007","stance":"rec","why":"Vienna as example of government building housing.","quote":"Government can build if private industry will not.","text":"Government can build if private industry will not. If you argue \"we can only fix this with the private sector and private investment\", welp, we tried that and we're plain ol' fucked at the moment. Vienna is a great example. [VIDEO] (Youtube: Vienna's Radical Idea? Affordable Housing For All) [LINK] (Youtube: Does Vienna Have the World's Best Council Housing?)"},{"id":38117223,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2023-11","story":"The US Housing Market Has Become an Impossible Mess","stance":"rec","why":"Vienna as a great example of public housing.","quote":"Vienna is a great example.","text":"Vienna is a great example. [VIDEO] (Youtube: Vienna's Radical Idea? Affordable Housing For All) [LINK] (Youtube: Does Vienna Have the World's Best Council Housing?)"},{"id":36067189,"author":"readonthegoapp","month":"2023-05","story":"Imagine a Renters' Utopia. It Might Look Like Vienna.","stance":"rec","why":"Public housing supply can keep rents down.","quote":"if you supply at least some public housing, it can have an outsized ability to keep rents down","text":"i watched a couple youtubes over the past few months on this -- if you supply at least some public housing, it can have an outsized ability to keep rents down: Bloomberg Originals: Vienna's Radical Idea? Affordable Housing For All [VIDEO] About Here: The Non-capitalist Solution to the Housing Crisis (indy urban planning-types out of Vancouver, BC) [LINK]"},{"id":32096138,"author":"m_ke","month":"2022-07","story":"Manhattan rents cross $5k threshold for first time","stance":"rec","why":"Recommended video on Vienna housing.","quote":"Also recommend checking out this video about Vienna","text":"Which of these \"affordable\" options are you too good for: [LINK] Also recommend checking out this video about Vienna [VIDEO]"},{"id":49198296,"author":"jmyeet","month":"2026-08","story":"Why Do Fewer Renters Expect to Move?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"What we need instead is social housing like Vienna [1].","text":"You're so close to realizing the point but the difference is between a dystopian future and a future worth living. Corporate housing, and worker housing in general, is what we're bouldering towards. The only way to extract further wealth from most of the population is for them not to own anything. It's the Western equivalent of South Asian brick kilns. What we need instead is social housing like Vienna [1]. Americans in particular always associate such thing with ghettoes and Section 8. And here's another problem: US corporations already keep employees and pay them less with the fear of losing health insurance. That's why the system exists. How much worse would it be if it's also your housing you'll lose? [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47379577,"author":"jmyeet","month":"2026-03","story":"UBI Is Your Productivity Dividend – The Only Way to All Share What We All Built","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"A prime example is Vienna [1].","text":"So there are two basic versions of UBI: 1. The right-wing UBI is a tool to dismantle the social safety net. The idea of the likes of Milton Friedman is to replace all social safety programs with UBI. This doesn't make sense because, for example, being disabled in today's society makes everything more expensive; and 2. Left-wing UBI would seek to have everyone share in the wealth they create by supplementing social safety programs with UBI. UBI becomes a form of super-progressive taxation because it can be viewed as negative taxation. As for your inflation comment, you have a point, to which I'll say: UBI alone isn't sufficient. You need economic reform and planning on top of that. A good example is the US military. If you live off-base you get a housing allowance (ie BAH). Now around military bases, in the US and overseas, all the landlords know this so you'll find that weirdly all the houses to rent cost pretty much what BAH is. So a more equitable economic system, including UBI, needs social housing . That is, the government needs to be a significant supplier of quality, affordable housing so landlords (private and insitutional) can't artificially drive up prices, as is the case now. A prime example is Vienna [1]. Housing simply can't be a speculative asset in a healthy economy. If you wnat to see what an equitable planned economy looks like, look at China. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47435466,"author":"jmyeet","month":"2026-03","story":"Austin’s surge of new housing construction drove down rents","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The poster child for this is Vienna [1][2].","text":"There's no reason for houses to be as expensive as they are. They are expensive because we've created incentives to make them more expensive. This is because we treat houses as investments, allow people to hoard housing, build the wrong tyhpe of housing (because it's more profitable), allow non-residents to use housing to park wealth, etc. So voters vote in politicians who put up barriers to build more housing and to defeat any kind of public transit infrastructure even though it would absolutely benefit people who still want to drive (by removing people from the roads who don't). Why? Because taxes. Housing should be for residents of that city to provide a utility: shelter. Not as an investment vehicle. It simply doesn't have to be this way. The poster child for this is Vienna [1][2]. Increasing house prices are an illusion of wealth creation. Let's say you buy a house for $200k but over the years it goes up to $800k. But every house costs $800k so you still have only 1 housing unit's worth of wealth. You've simply increased the barrier for younger people to buy houses. Put another way: increasing house prices are simply stealing from the next generation and that money is really going to the already-wealthy and, to a lesser extent, the old. Just look at the median age of homebuyers in the US, currently 59 [3]. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":46654031,"author":"jmyeet","month":"2026-01","story":"Left in the cold: Study finds most renters shut out of energy-saving upgrades","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence state-provided housing keeps private sector honest.","quote":"The only solution to this that demonstrably works is for the state to provide a significant amount of housing to keep the private secotr honest [2].","text":"There is always going to be an adversarial relationship between landlords and renters. Make no mistake: that we allow this to happen is state violence. Why? Because we are allowing individuals and corporations to deny housing to people by raising prices to make it unaffordable. We even allow them to collude [1]. The only solution to this that demonstrably works is for the state to provide a significant amount of housing to keep the private secotr honest [2]. Everything else is just propping a system that steals from the poor to give to the already rich. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37326546,"author":"jmyeet","month":"2023-08","story":"How South Korea’s Birth Rate Dropped Below Japan’s","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yes there are restrictions on supply but you need to look at why for the things you mention. Zoning doesn't happen in a vacuum. It's implemented by politicians. Why? What forces are pushing for that? Is it the NIMBYs? Maybe. But money talks in politics. So what political interests are pushing for restrictive zoning? Why? The real problem here is that housing became financialized (like every aspect of our lives). People see their $300k house go up in value to $600k and think they've made money. But have they? You still need a place to live. If every house that cost $300k now costs $600k, you still only have one house unit of wealth. So what have you really gained? One extension of this is you decide you need to own multiple properties. You need to become a landlord. For many, this is their retirement. But what is being a landlord? It's withholding a basic human need (ie shelter) for profit. it's choosing to let people die on the streets if it makes you money. So this system only really benefits the truly wealthy to accumulate wealth by extracting wealth through rent at the threat of violence. Eviction is violence. Witholding shelter is violence. Now this might be fine if the government provided a baseline for shelter through what is most commonly referred to as \"social housing\". Americans, in particular, who hear this think \"projects\" or \"ghettoes\" (ie Section 8 Housing) but look at a city like Vienna where ~60% (IIRC) of the housing is owned by the government [1]. But we don'"},{"id":34209243,"author":"pastacacioepepe","month":"2023-01","story":"Why a global recession is inevitable in 2023","stance":"reference","why":"Austria's public intervention made housing affordable.","quote":"Austrians made prices affordable and improved housing quality with public intervention, and it worked well[0].","text":"> To lower house prices would require heavy deregulation of the housing market There's no actual requirement for deregulation. Austrians made prices affordable and improved housing quality with public intervention, and it worked well[0]. I yet have to see an housing deregulation experiment that worked. .0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28660477,"author":"xx511134bz","month":"2021-09","story":"How to make housing more affordable","stance":"reference","why":"Vienna's affordable housing model.","quote":"Vienna is doing a nice job, building new \"old town\" areas with 75% affordable units.","text":"Vienna is doing a nice job, building new \"old town\" areas with 75% affordable units. [Bloomberg] Vienna's Radical Idea? Affordable Housing For All [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["sKudSeqHSJk",1],["-fny99f8amM",1],["d6DBKoWbtjE",1]]},"4RSht_aV7AU":{"wy":"Cited in threads about Python's rise as a first-hand, entertaining account of why it won in scientific computing.","q":{"x":"I recommend one of the recent videos by Dave Beazly","i":30814167,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":40755684,"author":"Qem","month":"2024-06","story":"Connection Machine Lisp (1986)","stance":"rec","why":"Early days of Python on Connection Machine 5","quote":"This reminds me of an interesting talk by David Beazley","text":"> it is my understanding that what this paper describes was never actually shipped This reminds me of an interesting talk by David Beazley where he discuss the early days of Python, when IIRC he was a student at a national lab, and they managed to run the Python REPL in an connection machine 5 that people had a hard time programming in lower level languages. It seems the work they did back then, writing modules that allowed the Python interpreter to interoperate with scientific code was one of the foundations for it to snowball many years later. So I wonder if what this paper describes had shipped in time, if that would lead us by butterfly effect into the alternative timeline where Lisp dominates scientific computing, instead of Python. See [VIDEO]"},{"id":37309436,"author":"Qem","month":"2023-08","story":"Ask HN: Why Did Python Win?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Python winning as a wrapper language for scientific computing","quote":"Watch this talk","text":"According to David Beazley, it was lucky to already being around by the time scientific computing folks were in need for dynamic languages as wrappers to help with increasingly complex hardware and projects written in lower level languages, and the only other viable alternatives back then were Perl and tcl. Watch this talk: [VIDEO] Then it just snowballed since that time."},{"id":30814167,"author":"jackjackk0","month":"2022-03","story":"The counterintuitive rise of Python in scientific computing (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"First-hand insights on Python's rise in 90s scientific computing, entertaining","quote":"I recommend one of the recent videos by Dave Beazly","text":"I recommend one of the recent videos by Dave Beazly [1]. He lived through and contributed to the raise of Python in scientific computing first hand in the 90s, and offers some interesting insights. Plus he's always quite an entertainer. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29217023,"author":"tda","month":"2021-11","story":"The Design of the Connection Machine (1994)","stance":"rec","why":"Hour-long enjoyable talk on how Beazley got Python running on a Connection Machine","quote":"Quite enjoyable but light on in depth technical stuff.","text":"This is the talk: [VIDEO] , it is a full hour. Quite enjoyable but light on in depth technical stuff. Don't recall hem mentioning how git python running technically, it is more about how he came in the position to do so in the first place."},{"id":29363065,"author":"dalke","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: How much was Python used from 1991 to 2006? What for?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I started using Python full time in 1998 for cheminformatics. One of the first projects I did was to make a high-level/\"Pythonic\" interface to an existing C library. I described it in Dr. Dobb's Journal, at [LINK] . Going back in time to 1994, we had a molecular dynamics visualization tool called VMD. It had a home-grown scripting language. I had ideas for a new programming language designed for molecular modeling (because \"tiny languages\" was the Unix philosophy I had picked up). After looking around, I realized 1) I didn't know how to really make a new domain-specific programming language, and 2) there were existing programming languages that could be embedded in an application, and extended with domain-specific features. I choose Tcl, because it was a very similar, but better, than our home-grown scripting language. And it was easy for people to learn. (Yes, easier than Python.) I considered Python, but was thrown off by the whitespace. And Perl wasn't a good fit. (Perl 4 C extensions were hard to do). Instead, another visualization package, UCSF's Chimera, was the first to have Python integration. In ... the mid-1990s? In 1997 I was doing more Perl work for bioinformatics. I had to read the advanced Perl book to figure out Perl5 objects. I wrote the equivalent program in Python, and it was just so much easier, even for a neophyte Python programmer. But bioinformatics at the time was Perl-oriented (see \"How Perl Saved the Human Genome Project\" - the author, Lincoln Stein, "}],"r":[["meiU6TxysCg",1],["HOuZyOKa91o",1]]},"JRuNA2eK7w0":{"wy":"HN points to it as a reasoned rebuttal to the claim that AI risk is just Pascal's wager.","q":{"x":"the main point of the video is that kind of situation happens when the probability involved is vanishingly small","i":47068113,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47068113,"author":"circuit10","month":"2026-02","story":"There is unequivocal evidence that Earth is warming","stance":"rec","why":"Pascal's mugging needs vanishingly small probabilities, unlike real safety concerns.","quote":"the main point of the video is that kind of situation happens when the probability involved is vanishingly small","text":"Here’s a video called “Is AI safety a Pascal’s Mugging?”: [VIDEO] From what I remember the main point of the video is that kind of situation happens when the probability involved is vanishingly small, and all the events you listed don’t have a vanishingly small probability, so they are not Pascal’s wager situations, just a normal rational safety concerns with particularly high consequences"},{"id":37098683,"author":"circuit10","month":"2023-08","story":"AI Causes Real Harm. Let’s Focus on That over the End-of-Humanity Hype","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why AI risk differs from Pascal's wager.","quote":"You can’t just call anything that has very bad negative consequences Pascal’s wager","text":"The difference to Pascal’s wager is that with that the probability is vanishingly small, but given current progress the probability of AI causing an existential threat is actually quite high. You can’t just call anything that has very bad negative consequences Pascal’s wager Video about this: “Is AI Safety a Pascal’s Mugging?” [VIDEO]"},{"id":36607277,"author":"mptest","month":"2023-07","story":"OpenAI: Introducing SuperAlignment","stance":"rec","why":"Safety researchers have long debated whether AI risk is a Pascal's mugging.","quote":"Maybe we can look to them for insight in to these questions?","text":"All of this discussion really makes me think of Robert Miles \"Is ai safety a Pascal's mugging?\" from 4 years(!) ago[0]. All of this discussion has been had by Ai safety researchers for years in my layman understanding... Maybe we can look to them for insight in to these questions? [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19952821,"author":"chii","month":"2019-05","story":"Sam Altman’s leap of faith","stance":"rec","why":"Argues AI safety is not a Pascal's mugging.","quote":"See this video on a reasonable argument about this","text":"Pascal's wager is actually logically flawed. If there are two separate AGI invented, and you could only have chosen to invest in one, then where's the infinite capture of value? See this video on a reasonable argument about this: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ZuvK-od647c",1],["rFqL9CkNxXw",1],["QPKKQnijnsM",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1],["Cj4y0EUlU-Y",1],["nvK8fua6O64",1],["LJ4W1g-6JiY",1],["YGLNyHd2w10",1],["aircAruvnKk",1],["9i1WlcCudpU",1],["hEUO6pjwFOo",1],["3TYT1QfdfsM",1],["qv6UVOQ0F44",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1]]},"OqjcrfKjobY":{"wy":"Commenters who struggle with tab overload use it to show what tree-based tab management looks like and recommend the extension.","q":{"x":"Tabs Outliner puts every tab as a leaf in a tree, and you can nest leaves","i":7725126,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":9685646,"author":"ohaal","month":"2015-06","story":"“Why does Chrome not have a good tab management system built in?”","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Overview of Tabs Outliner features","quote":"it is fantastic, and has really reduced my memory usage back to normal levels.","text":"Tabs Outliner[1] is great for this. It's lacking sync (an extra top layer in the tree for each device would make sense), but other than that, it is fantastic, and has really reduced my memory usage back to normal levels. It allows you to suspend entire windows, and quickly browse all tabs. Allows for unlimited* levels of named trees/notes/windows/tabs. It does however NOT save the state of the page, only the URL. When the Chrome Sidebar API[2] is implemented, I'm sure it will only get better, as the need for an extra window should no longer be necessary. Here's a video overview of its features: [VIDEO] * I haven't reached the maximum yet... [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":9364215,"author":"toddkaufmann","month":"2015-04","story":"Tabvention – Manage Your Browser Tab Addiction","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Demo of Tabs Outliner features","quote":"THE GREATEST THING is Tabs Outliner (chrome only).","text":"Haven't tried, but I think I would find this intrusive and annoying--I'd be mentally keeping track of how many tabs I had open and then checking my bookmarks if I went over. Not for me, but maybe useful for some. I've tried session managers too, and those are okay for restoring state when things crash. THE GREATEST THING is Tabs Outliner (chrome only). This displays all your windows and tabs as a tree and lets you rearrange, easily close and restore windows, selectively restore, add notes to tabs, etc. A slight learning curve, but worth it for the power surfer. Demo of some of the features in this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7725126,"author":"Gambit89","month":"2014-05","story":"The Inventor of the Browser Tab","stance":"rec","why":"Demo of Tabs Outliner tree-based tab management","quote":"Tabs Outliner puts every tab as a leaf in a tree, and you can nest leaves","text":"You might be looking for the Tabs Outliner Extension for Chrome. I've been using it heavily for the \"middle-state\" of saved and not open, but not bookmarked; now I'm looking for a \"meta Tabs Outliner\"-esque program, but I can't quite articulate my needs... > What I want is to maintain a list of current references, preferably with some spatial context (tree-mode browsing is great for this) to what the relationship is between pages in my browser session. Tabs Outliner puts every tab as a leaf in a tree, and you can nest leaves and affect the visual ordering on the tab bar at the same time - you can also do this reordering while the tab isn't even open! This is good for a while, but for me when I have thousands, it's lacking in some retrieval sense: I guess I want to query for just these tabs that have connections, and cross-reference research annotations on these sets of connections. [LINK] Demo: [VIDEO]"}]},"_DTpQ4Kk2wA":{"wy":"Recommended as a charming, from-zero introduction to APL and a window into early interactive computing.","q":{"x":"I think this is a good youtube video for someone starting from zero","i":13906894,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":29248103,"author":"jazzyjackson","month":"2021-11","story":"IBM unveils 127-qubit quantum processor","stance":"rec","why":"REPL on a Selectric teletype upended view of computer history.","quote":"it's held a total mystique to me since seeing this video circa 1975","text":"Thanks for sharing. I worked on the UIUC campus for some time right by the greenhouses where they experimented with cultivars of corn and other grasses, always funny to see giant 8 foot tall plants lit with sodium lamps through the winter. As for APL, I haven't really got past an orientation in the language but it's held a total mystique to me since seeing this video circa 1975 [0] walking through the language with a Selectric teletype acting as a REPL, totally flipped my understanding of computer history, I assumed it was all punchcard programming back in the old black and white days xD (I am born 1990 for reference, trying to catch up with what happened before me) [0] (30min) [VIDEO]"},{"id":20729144,"author":"curiousgal","month":"2019-08","story":"J can look like APL or English","stance":"rec","why":"Charming original 1975 APL demonstration.","quote":"I love it!","text":"There is something so charming about the attached video [0]. It's very reminiscent of the original APL demonstration [1] from 1975. I love it! 0. [LINK] 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20818306,"author":"hhas01","month":"2019-08","story":"A+, the Programming Language of Morgan Stanley","stance":"rec","why":"Half-hour intro showing APL's motivation for non-programmer users.","quote":"","text":"I think the key to understanding APL is realizing that it’s not built for Programmers; it’s built for Mathematicians. Here’s a fine half-hour introduction to APL in action: [VIDEO] 1:00-2:00 is the motivation for APL—or any DSL—in a nutshell. (“conversational computing”, “users who are [not] professional programmers”) Or, if you’re happy to sit back and see math magic happen: [LINK] For all its mechanical advances over the last 40 years, it’s shocking how far our technology has regressed philosophically."},{"id":13906894,"author":"cgrubb","month":"2017-03","story":"GNU APL 1.7 Released","stance":"rec","why":"Good starting video for APL beginners.","quote":"I think this is a good youtube video for someone starting from zero","text":"I think this is a good youtube video for someone starting from zero: [VIDEO] An issue is how to enter the symbols. The way I do it is I created some input methods for Mac and Windows which use at-signs followed by the APL community name for the symbol: @times × @grade ⍋ @reverse ⌽ @domino ⌹ [LINK]"},{"id":8606328,"author":"progman","month":"2014-11","story":"J for C Programmers","stance":"rec","why":"Nice introduction to APL.","quote":"This is a nice introduction into APL","text":"APL without native fonts isn't fun at all. This is real fibonacci in APL: [LINK] [LINK] This is a nice introduction into APL: [VIDEO] And this is how APL is applied in the right way: [LINK]"},{"id":21478833,"author":"m463","month":"2019-11","story":"Python should take a lesson from APL: Walrus operator not needed","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the APL symbol at the 20-minute mark.","quote":"If you skip exactly 20 minutes into this video it IS EXACTLY a rectangle with a division symbol inside","text":"You have posted an amazing and comprehensive comment. > If you look at the symbol, it looks like a rectangle with a division symbol inside. If you skip exactly 20 minutes into this video it IS EXACTLY a rectangle with a division symbol inside: [VIDEO] I don't know whether to think \"thank goodness for unicode\" or \"unicode is an enabler of problematic practices\" :)"},{"id":17789367,"author":"3rdAccount","month":"2018-08","story":"My dad’s resume and skills from 1980","stance":"reference","why":"Shows early APL on a modified typewriter terminal.","quote":"I enjoyed learning how they added a rotating ball to that typewriter","text":"I enjoyed learning how they added a rotating ball to that typewriter and hooked it up to some kind of mainframe for the first APL implementation I think. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11938970,"author":"sanarothe","month":"2016-06","story":"Show HN: A Fortran web framework","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Young engineers gather around him just to marvel at this masterpiece","text":"I love it I'm imagining a sixty year old dude, revered by all around him, who spend the last forty honing his fixed-form Fortran skills. Young engineers gather around him just to marvel at this masterpiece: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["EH6vE97qIP4",1],["B1J2RMorJXM",1],["OhnjMdzGusc",1],["sj8Sg8qnjOg",1],["Og6cTlwBTrk",1],["g2tMcMQqSbA",1],["jEbRVNxL44c",1],["a9xAKttWgP4",1]]},"la-sGpTpkxE":{"wy":"Recommended as an entry point to a favorite electronics channel, valued for deep knowledge and no showy bravado.","q":{"x":"Recommended - this guy knows his stuff, and unlike certain other youtube channels in this genre there is no annoying bravado.","i":15166965,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":33747630,"author":"afandian","month":"2022-11","story":"iPod Nano V6 LCD Hacking","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Heavily modified BBC Micro from a favourite channel","quote":"Mikes Electric Stuff is one of my absolute favourite channels on YouTube. Highly recommend.","text":"Mikes Electric Stuff is one of my absolute favourite channels on YouTube. Highly recommend. A couple of classics: Heavily modified BBC Micro: [VIDEO] Design of LED sculpture with some interesting commentary: [LINK]"},{"id":15166965,"author":"johansch","month":"2017-09","story":"Miscellaneous Arduino bits and pieces","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Starting point for a rewarding channel with deep electronics knowledge","quote":"Recommended - this guy knows his stuff, and unlike certain other youtube channels in this genre there is no annoying bravado.","text":"If you can stand the video format, this one is unusually rewarding for being a youtube channel: [LINK] I started with this one: [VIDEO] \"My old BBC Micro system\" ... which kind of established his credibilities in my mind, and then I just kept going, devouring his entire channel. Recommended - this guy knows his stuff, and unlike certain other youtube channels in this genre there is no annoying bravado."},{"id":16385297,"author":"the-dude","month":"2018-02","story":"Chip Aging Accelerates","stance":"rec","why":"Mike's BBC Micro build, preferred over EEVBlog","quote":"Here is Mike's BBC micro : Great stuff.","text":"I dig the guy much better than David Jones ( EEVBlog ). Here is Mike's BBC micro : [VIDEO] Great stuff."}],"r":[["2V1FtfBDsLU",1],["K_nnUbX7uuQ",1],["tiJVJ5QRRUE",1],["0sHCQWjTrJ8",1]]},"17KCHwOwgms":{"wy":"Recommended in DRY versus duplication debates for its visuals and its case that copy/paste can beat a wrong abstraction.","q":{"x":"Seeing what a premature, wrong abstraction looks like visually was eye opening.","i":40527309,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40527309,"author":"halfcat","month":"2024-05","story":"Don't DRY Your Code Prematurely","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Visualizations show what a premature wrong abstraction looks like","quote":"Seeing what a premature, wrong abstraction looks like visually was eye opening.","text":"The visualizations in Dan Abramov’s talk “The wet codebase” [1] really burned this concept in for me. Seeing what a premature, wrong abstraction looks like visually was eye opening. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41287191,"author":"halfcat","month":"2024-08","story":"Algorithms we develop software by","stance":"rec","why":"Abramov argues copy/paste beats a wrong abstraction, with good visuals","quote":"He has some good visuals that illustrate how incorrectly dependent and impossible to unwind wrong abstractions can become.","text":"Dan Abramov talks about WET (write everything twice) [1] as generally a good approach, primarily because you often don’t know the right abstraction up front, and a wrong abstraction is way worse than a copy/paste. He has some good visuals that illustrate how incorrectly dependent and impossible to unwind wrong abstractions can become. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37309731,"author":"shadowfoxx","month":"2023-08","story":"I don’t buy “duplication is cheaper than the wrong abstraction” (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"Dan Abramov explains with visuals why premature DRY abstractions hurt","quote":"He says it better than I can sum up and has visual aids","text":"In my career as a software dev I've found one thing to be true - Every Paradigm I ingest that opens new windows of opportunity are great at first pass and as I learn more the more narrow the scope they can be applied. (This is kinda true in life, too. Like when people say, \"Its econ 101 or bio 101.\" etc. What seems like a statement about 'common' knowledge is actually an indication of how shallow your knowledge is!) Specifically related to this topic is a talk by Dan Abramov called, \"The Wet Codebase\" - He says it better than I can sum up and has visual aids : [VIDEO] Other have pointed out code that is similar in function vs similar by coincidence and I think that thought alone is worth chewing on."},{"id":37314492,"author":"gosukiwi","month":"2023-08","story":"I don’t buy “duplication is cheaper than the wrong abstraction” (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"Abramov's talk on the cost of removing all duplication","quote":"I've been guilty of doing just what he says in his talk at first, removing all duplications and making the codebase DRY.","text":"I like Dan Abramov's \"The Wet Codebase\" ( [VIDEO] ) -- I've been guilty of doing just what he says in his talk at first, removing all duplications and making the codebase DRY. But then I came to like \"prefer duplication over the wrong abstraction\", as Sandi Metz puts it. Sometimes it's good to wait to have more data to make an easier and more informed decision."},{"id":41908896,"author":"throwitaway1123","month":"2024-10","story":"OOP is not that bad","stance":"reference","why":"Abramov describes technical heuristics as lossy information compression","quote":"I watched a talk recently by Dan Abramov where he calls these heuristics (I'm paraphrasing) a form of information compression","text":"I agree with the general sentiment of your comment and I think there are several factors at play here. * The human brain is not capable of evaluating and re-evaluating every possible option amongst the plethora of technical choices developers are faced with. This forces us to develop certain coarse grained mental heuristics (prejudices and biases) to navigate technology, and even if these broad generalizations are roughly true initially, we tend not to re-evaluate them over time. This leads to stale biases (e.g. some library/language was missing an API 10 years ago, and someone formed an immutable opinion on it). * These broad generalizations lack nuance. I watched a talk recently by Dan Abramov where he calls these heuristics (I'm paraphrasing) a form of information compression [1]. That compression is lossy — it doesn't preserve the original context in which the heuristic was formed. * There's also some insecurity at play here too. Developers want to believe that they've chosen The One True Solution, and harshly invalidating the alternatives is one way to reenforce that fantasy. * And of course, social media has exacerbated this problem by rewarding inflammatory hot takes. You won't get nearly as many views/upvotes/likes for a sober take that says \"technology X is well suited for this narrow use case\" as you will for a hot take that says \"why technology X failed\", or \"why everyone hates technology X\". You might enjoy this link: [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["_b4qKv1Ctv8",1]]},"2FMBSblpcrc":{"wy":"HN treats it as a tiny practical life hack some adopt for good, while others mock it as peak TED.","q":{"x":"I actually improved my hand drying technique after the TEDx on it","i":26867968,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":42937839,"author":"snowwrestler","month":"2025-02","story":"Chris Anderson Is Giving Ted Away to Whoever Has the Best Idea for Its Future","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Practical talk the commenter still applies in public restrooms.","quote":"I think about this every time I wash my hands in a public restroom with paper towels.","text":"Best TED talk I’ve seen: [VIDEO] I think about this every time I wash my hands in a public restroom with paper towels."},{"id":26867968,"author":"Someone1234","month":"2021-04","story":"The “Granny Knot”","stance":"rec","why":"Improved the commenter's hand-drying technique.","quote":"I actually improved my hand drying technique after the TEDx on it","text":"People are very negative about these kinds of TED talks, but I actually improved my hand drying technique after the TEDx on it: [VIDEO] That and using the back of my hand/arm to push off the water from my body after a shower keep our towels much dryer and nicer."},{"id":21582554,"author":"leto_ii","month":"2019-11","story":"Ask HN: What's the most valuable thing you can learn in an hour?","stance":"rec","why":"Teaches drying hands with a single paper towel to cut waste.","quote":"How to dry your hands with a single paper towel (so that you prevent unnecessary waste)","text":"Two quick things from back when TED talks were actually interesting/useful: 1. How to tie your shoes the right way (so the knot doesn't come undone): [LINK] 2. How to dry your hands with a single paper towel (so that you prevent unnecessary waste): [VIDEO]"},{"id":42668999,"author":"nytesky","month":"2025-01","story":"We moved to the old Sun campus, I noticed the towel dispensers had a delay timer","stance":"reference","why":"Shaking hands while waiting means one towel suffices.","quote":"He should have been shaking his hands while waiting for it to dispense. Then he could use just one towel.","text":"He should have been shaking his hands while waiting for it to dispense. Then he could use just one towel. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11141733,"author":"minikites","month":"2016-02","story":"We Are Now at Peak TED","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Peak TED is actually this video on how to use one paper towel when washing your hands","text":"This banned TED talk is all you need to know about TED and where their motivations are: [LINK] Peak TED is actually this video on how to use one paper towel when washing your hands: [VIDEO] . You mean if you shake your hands and fling water all over the bathroom then you don't need as much paper to absorb the remaining water? How revolutionary."}],"r":[["7wC42HgLA4k",1],["yNzQ_sLGIuA",1],["0FacYAI6DY0",1],["QNznD9hMEh0",1],["zAFcV7zuUDA",1]]},"MYucYon2-lk":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a concise, convincing case for better testing practice short of full formal methods.","q":{"x":"I found this talk (by the same person being interviewed) to be concise and convincing on how to do better than typical practice","i":19672414,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19672414,"author":"jononor","month":"2019-04","story":"The Case for Formal Methods","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Concise, convincing talk on better testing without full formal methods.","quote":"I found this talk (by the same person being interviewed) to be concise and convincing on how to do better than typical practice","text":"I found this talk (by the same person being interviewed) to be concise and convincing on how to do better than typical practice, without going to full formal methods. Hillel Wayne - Beyond Unit Tests: Taking Your Testing to the Next Level - PyCon 2018 [VIDEO]"},{"id":34352739,"author":"spion","month":"2023-01","story":"What if writing tests was a joyful experience?","stance":"rec","why":"Property-based testing of whole systems via contracts, by Hillel Wayne.","quote":"Hilel Wayne's excellent video on this topic","text":"Most of the non-joyful experience of writing tests lies in the context preparation, not so much in the expected outputs. I think we need a modern version of design by contract instead. Its one of the most powerful techniques of testing software is unfortunately often ignored and overlooked these days... Some less known things about design by contract: - you can easily test way more than types in preconditions and postconditions - extreme web controller postcondition example: every time there is a 200 response to a POST /new request, there should be at least one document more in storage than there was before the request. (caveat: unless there are deletes - then sum the deletes up too) - things important to the business look a ton like contracts - e.g. a diff of two legal documents always contains the full content (list of words) of both documents (never accidentally swallow content!) - your postconditions can surprisingly often fully describe the desired function behavior - with sufficient amount of pre and postconditions, your property tests look like \"exercise the module or entire system with random actions\" -> unit and integration tests (see Hilel Wayne's excellent video on this topic [VIDEO] ) Some things that could really help modernize design by contract - Test at a certain sampling rate in production for expensive contracts (do not turn them off) - Report contract violations via observability mechanisms (tracing, sentry etc) - Better language support for contract DSLs"},{"id":18742515,"author":"sn9","month":"2018-12","story":"Ask HN: Best talks of 2018?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among favorite PyCon talks.","quote":"Some of my favorite talks from Strange Loop","text":"Some of my favorite talks from Strange Loop: * Contracts For Getting More Programs Less Wrong: [LINK] * \"It's Just Matrix Multiplication\": Notation for Weaving: [LINK] * Hackett: a metaprogrammable Haskell: [LINK] * Git from the Ground Up: [LINK] From PyCon: * Beyond Unit Tests: Taking Your Testing to the Next Level: [VIDEO] * Dataclasses: The code generator to end all code generator: [LINK] * Automating Code Quality: [LINK] There are probably more I might add later as I remember them."}],"r":[["T-TwcmT6Rcw",1],["Ik1AK56FtVc",1],["akVL7QY0S8A",1],["gMqZR3pqMjg",1],["lqJUIqZNzP8",1]]},"WDIkqP4JbkE":{"wy":"HN points to it as the go-to explanation of why memory layout beats pointer-chasing structures on real hardware.","q":{"x":"Scott Meyers had a very good talk which contained this information","i":35738297,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":18217862,"author":"drainyard","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on cache-aware, data-oriented programming.","quote":"They're mostly about C++ and cache aware/data oriented programming.","text":"Two of my favorites: code::dive conference 2014 - Scott Meyers: Cpu Caches and Why You Care: [VIDEO] CppCon 2014: Mike Acton \"Data-Oriented Design and C++\": [LINK] They're mostly about C++ and cache aware/data oriented programming."},{"id":35738297,"author":"coppsilgold","month":"2023-04","story":"Beautiful branchless binary search","stance":"rec","why":"Covers cache associativity and conflict misses.","quote":"Scott Meyers had a very good talk which contained this information","text":"Cache associativity, conflict miss. You have too many memory addresses mapping to the same cache lines all being used together at the same time. Scott Meyers had a very good talk which contained this information: < [VIDEO] >"},{"id":16751177,"author":"jimmy1","month":"2018-04","story":"Intel brings a six-core i9 CPU to laptops","stance":"rec","why":"Visualizes access speeds across cache levels and main memory.","quote":"Here is a good visualization on access speeds for each level of cache","text":"Here is a good visualization on access speeds for each level of cache, then main memory. Now take the main memory access time and essentially double it (for your state of the art SSD's today) and that's the access speed. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29751396,"author":"game_the0ry","month":"2021-12","story":"A single line of code made a 24-core server slower than a laptop","stance":"reference","why":"Relevant talk on cache effects in multithreading.","quote":"Relevant video","text":"Relevant video - Scott Meyers: Cpu Caches and Why You Care [VIDEO] Remember, boys and girls, small mistakes can have big consequences. Keep those data in immutable data structures when mutli-threading. Actor model also helps."},{"id":25273745,"author":"reificator","month":"2020-12","story":"An iOS zero-click radio proximity exploit odyssey","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why arrays beat linked lists on real hardware.","quote":"goes into more detail about why this happens","text":"> If your primary operation is inserting at a random location in the list, linked lists are faster than arrays at large sizes. You avoid having to move all the memory after the index you are modifying (to make space for the inserted element). This is false. Big O notation says it should be true, you'll get marked wrong if you say arrays are faster in your algorithms & data structures final, but when you're running on actual hardware the array is faster at all sizes of n and as n becomes larger so does the gap in performance. Here is a talk[0] by Bjarne Stroustrop (Creator of C++) that even includes imaginary graphs demonstrating this phenomenon. If you want a visual for what the missing graph was supposed to look like, here's a similar one.[1] Here's another video[2] by Scott Meyers (Author of Effective C++) that goes into more detail about why this happens. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33475036,"author":"insanitybit","month":"2022-11","story":"In defense of linked lists","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Cache prefetching works best for linear accesses like with a vector, not for random pointer lookups.","text":"I don't buy this. [VIDEO] Prefetching in CPUs predates this talk. [LINK] That talk is 2018. Cache prefetching works best for linear accesses like with a vector, not for random pointer lookups. Prefetchers are also going to have an extra harder time with double indirection since they're unable to see which value is going to be fed to the lea. Prefetching is also expensive in and of itself, the CPU doesn't do it unless you're already incurring cache hits. This makes cache misses even worse . There are also multiple prefetchers in the CPU and the L1 prefetcher is very basic and is only going to help with contiguous accesses. Your allocator might be doing some nice things for you that let the one of the L2 prefetchers help, I would expect that you'll see better L2 performance due to prefetching. If you have an example of a linked list being as fast as a vector for iteration, please do show it."}]},"bY7aRJE-oOY":{"wy":"HN points to it as the best talk on how people misunderstand probabilities and how designers handle that.","q":{"x":"Sid Meier (Civilization) gave a great GDC keynote in 2010","i":13387254,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":13387254,"author":"corysama","month":"2017-01","story":"How Designers Engineer Luck into Video Games","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great keynote on conveying that low-probability events still happen.","quote":"Sid Meier (Civilization) gave a great GDC keynote in 2010","text":"Sid Meier (Civilization) gave a great GDC keynote in 2010 where he describes his multi-decade struggle to convey to users ideas like \"Stuff that only has a 10% chance of happening still happens sometimes.\" [VIDEO] Multiply that by a million daily active users and it really works out to \"Stuff that has a 1-in-a-million chance of happening will happen to somebody every day\". Leading to player reviews like \"The game rolled me a 1-out-of-10 SIX TIMES IN A ROW!!! The game is explicitly cheating me!!!\" every day. Thus games tend to use random shuffles rather than independent random numbers so as to better align with common misunderstandings about statistics..."},{"id":21905651,"author":"jcrites","month":"2019-12","story":"Paean to Sid Meier's Alpha Centauri (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Entire talk interesting; section on player perception of probability.","quote":"The entire talk is interesting, but section on player perception of probability is about 20 minutes in.","text":"I believe you're thinking of Sid Meier's GDC 2010 keynote \"Everything You Know Is Wrong\" [1]. The entire talk is interesting, but section on player perception of probability is about 20 minutes in. One takeaway is to be careful about how strength numbers translate into odds. If your strength is 100 and mine is 1, does that mean I have a 1% chance to out-and-out beat you? Your armored tank shouldn't have an even 1% chance of being completely annihilated by my club-wielding warrior (that's somehow still around by then). The later Civ games have taken odds out of the equation, and I think it's for the better. Instead, the amount of damage each unit takes per combat depends on the difference in their strength deterministically. From my own perspective, this is overall more fun than 'randomly' having really strong units lose against weak units occasionally. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17459299,"author":"jcrites","month":"2018-07","story":"If You Say Something Is “Likely,” How Likely Do People Think It Is?","stance":"rec","why":"Sid Meier on how players perceive odds in games.","quote":"Here is a link to the actual GDC talk by Sid with the content about probability","text":"This article, and some of the comments here, reminded me of another article (and GDC talk) by Civilization game designer Sid Meier, about his experience with players' perception of probability in games. Sid's talk grapples with the issue \"If the game says you have 3-to-1 odds to win a battle, how often do players actually expect to win?\" > When designing the combat system in Civilization: Revolution, Sid Meier found himself up against some interesting design problems. His players didn't understand math. In Civ Rev, the strength of units were displayed up front to players before battle to show the odds of victory. For example, an attacking unit might be rated at 1.5 with the defending unit at 0.5. This is a 3-to-1 situation. > Unfortunately, the testers expected to win this battle every time despite there being a 25% chance of losing each time. Sid tweaked the math to make the player win more in this situation. Next, the reverse case was tested. The player had 1-to-3 odds. If they won, the math was functioning properly. They had a slim chance to win and they did. > Sid identified a few cases of interest. When the player was presented with 3-to-1 or 4-to-1 odds, they expect to win. With 2-to-1 odds, the player will accept losing some of the time, but expect to win at 20-to-10, which is just a larger expression of 2-to-1. When the numbers get larger, the perceived advantage grows. [LINK] Here is a link to the actual GDC talk by Sid with the content about probability: [VIDEO] (It"},{"id":12943401,"author":"espadrine","month":"2016-11","story":"How an accused drug dealer revealed Jacksonville's facial recognition network","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on how people misunderstand probabilities.","quote":"I am reminded of this talk by Sid Meier","text":"The worst part is that even if the algorithm perfectly computed probabilities and was completely open, humans would take away incorrect information. I am reminded of this talk by Sid Meier: [VIDEO] People don't understand probabilities. They don't accept that if someone has a 75% chance of being guilty, they may very well not be. It happened again with FiveThirtyEight when everybody accused their \"70% chance of Mrs Clinton winning\" of being wrong, when it doesn't preclude Mr Trump winning."},{"id":7189969,"author":"Fizzer","month":"2014-02","story":"Why is quality of pseudorandom number generators important?","stance":"reference","why":"Sid Meier discusses rigged odds in Civilization Revolution.","quote":"Sid Meier talked about it at length in this video","text":"\"Civilization Revolution\" does this. Say the player loses a 75% roll, then attempts another identical 75% roll. The game will actually give you better odds for the second roll. I think it's silly -- the game basically lies to you about your odds on the second attempt. Sid Meier talked about it at length in this video: [VIDEO] . It starts around 18:25."}],"r":[["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["Hvx4xXhZMrU",1],["wqu_IpkOYBg",1],["Tfh0ytz8S0k",1],["db-7J5OaSag",1],["sbQhgEJuExY",1],["aMcJ1Jvtef0",1]]},"eKl6WjfDqYA":{"wy":"Commenters point to it whenever AI-generated content, propaganda or internet garbage comes up, since Kojima seemed to predict it in 2001.","q":{"x":"You should play Metal Gear Solid 2, or at least watch the last codec call[1].","i":46343372,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":46343372,"author":"dokyun","month":"2025-12","story":"You have reached the end of the internet (2006)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Final codec call applies strikingly to the present day.","quote":"You should play Metal Gear Solid 2, or at least watch the last codec call[1].","text":"You should play Metal Gear Solid 2, or at least watch the last codec call[1]. See how much you can apply what it talks about to the current year. This game came out a month after 9/11. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43130282,"author":"dmonitor","month":"2025-02","story":"Apple pulls data protection tool after UK government security row","stance":"rec","why":"Colonel's ending dialogue on AI-driven manipulation and propaganda.","quote":"It's really brilliant to use a video game to deliver the message of the effectiveness of propaganda.","text":"The real prescient threat in that movie was the predictive AI algorithm that tracked individual behaviors and identified potential threats to the regime. In the movie they had a big airship with guns that would kill them on sight, but a more realistic threat is the AI deciding to feed them individualized propaganda to curtail their behavior. This is the villain's plot in Metal Gear Solid 2, which is another great story. This got me thinking about MGS2 again and rewatching the colonel's dialogue at the end of the game: [VIDEO] > Your persona, experiences, triumphs, and defeats are nothing but byproducts. The real objective was ensuring that we could generate and manipulate them. It's really brilliant to use a video game to deliver the message of the effectiveness of propaganda. 'Game design' as a concept is just about manipulation and hijacking dopamine responses. I don't think another medium can as effectively demonstrate how systems can manipulate people's behavior."},{"id":40436284,"author":"Lammy","month":"2024-05","story":"The internet is overflowing with bad content. Users are fighting back","stance":"rec","why":"MGS2 foresaw information overload.","quote":"Have you ever played Metal Gear Solid 2 (2001)?","text":"> The information age gave us information overload, something I don't think anyone predicted. Have you ever played Metal Gear Solid 2 (2001)? [VIDEO]"},{"id":44033152,"author":"ddq","month":"2025-05","story":"Remarks on AI from NZ","stance":"reference","why":"Prescient 2001 conversation about memetic evolution and consciousness.","quote":"The whole conversation was markedly prescient for 2001 but not appreciated at the time.","text":"Metal Gear Solid 2 makes this point about how \"over the past 200 years, a kind of consciousness formed layer by layer in the crucible of the White House\" through memetic evolution. The whole conversation was markedly prescient for 2001 but not appreciated at the time. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35605449,"author":"JPws_Prntr_Fngr","month":"2023-04","story":"KitKat ribs AI in hilarious new ad campaign","stance":"reference","why":"Kojima explored this AI idea in 2001.","quote":"Kojima was playing with the idea back in 2001","text":"Kojima was playing with the idea back in 2001: [VIDEO] It's probably a trope from even older sci-fi, though I don't know which."},{"id":33869340,"author":"vvvvvsvvs","month":"2022-12","story":"Drowning in AI Generated Garbage: the silent war we are fighting","stance":"reference","why":"MGS2 ending anticipated AI-generated information overload.","quote":"the ending of the video game Metal Gear Solid 2 predicted this way back in 2001","text":"Friendly reminder that the ending of the video game Metal Gear Solid 2 predicted this way back in 2001 [VIDEO]"},{"id":24577128,"author":"Lammy","month":"2020-09","story":"The Cement of Society Is...","stance":"reference","why":"MGS2 codec conversation on shared reality.","quote":"","text":"I could not disagree more! I've seen this demand for One Shared Reality Of Facts from all sorts of groups who will never be able to agree on one. I've watched our attempts set many well-intentioned humans at many other well-intentioned (and otherwise) humans' (and otherwise's) throats, yelling at each other because THEY'RE WRONG when they describe personally experiencing a reality that sounds at times completely alien to mine. It seems to be a path toward fragmentation and alienation and not a path toward unity. I do not wish to speak for anyone else here, of course, but the concepts of \"true\" and \"false\" don't really map on to my physical human dimension of reality very well at all, if I think about it! Observing something as \"true\" is just observing it. Maybe other peoples' thought patterns run differently? Serious question here for you all, my wonderful fellow HN readers: I don't really walk around in what I perceive as reality thinking \"that's false, and that, and that over there too\". Exactly the opposite! Everything I observe is true, because I observe it. I don't even think about perceiving reality at all. Everything that Is, just Is. This isn't meant to be coy or cutely condescending — I've legitimately just never thought about thinking very much before now. My concept of the \"necessary-shared-binding-force-between-things\", if you will, has thus moved away from constantly bikeshedding \"truth\" and toward the simple observation that all things that Exist exist and all t"}],"r":[["IOs6hnTI4lw",1],["goSEyGNfiPM",1]]},"jOyfZex7B3E":{"wy":"Recommended as an inspiring story of hardware reverse engineering and persistence.","q":{"x":"Related and _very_ inspiring; how the Sega Saturn CD was cracked after 20 years.","i":18654541,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18654541,"author":"dsco","month":"2018-12","story":"How the Dreamcast Copy Protection Was Defeated","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Inspiring tale of dedication cracking the Saturn CD protection.","quote":"Related and _very_ inspiring; how the Sega Saturn CD was cracked after 20 years.","text":"Related and _very_ inspiring; how the Sega Saturn CD was cracked after 20 years. Even my very non-technical girlfriend sat through this thing, being intrigued by the dedication. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29316341,"author":"iamtedd","month":"2021-11","story":"Full key extraction of Nvidia TSEC","stance":"rec","why":"Someone finally cracking the Sega Saturn long after its prime.","quote":"Here's a video of someone finally cracking into the Sega Saturn","text":"I love hearing these stories too! Here's a video of someone finally cracking into the Sega Saturn well after the console was current commodity: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12155798,"author":"drakenot","month":"2016-07","story":"Repairing the only known prototype of Nintendo PlayStation [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Story of cracking the Saturn CD protection after 20 years.","quote":"you should also checkout Sega Saturn CD - Cracked After 20 Years[0].","text":"For anyone who likes this video, you should also checkout Sega Saturn CD - Cracked After 20 Years[0]. [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":12076816,"author":"JoshTriplett","month":"2016-07","story":"Sega Saturn CD Cracked after 20 Years","stance":"reference","why":"Video shows the wobble edge on Saturn hardware.","quote":"The video shows it","text":"> I've never had a Saturn, so I don't know what this wobble edge looks like in person. Am I missing something? The video shows it: [VIDEO]"}]},"-EA2FQXs4dw":{"wy":"HN uses it as a reasonably detailed overview of Looking Glass tech, including a segment showing the pixel mapping behind the 3D effect.","q":{"x":"Linus Tech Tips covered them before that gives a pretty good overview","i":27840486,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27840486,"author":"cfjgvjh","month":"2021-07","story":"High-resolution holographic interface [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Pretty good overview of Looking Glass displays.","quote":"Linus Tech Tips covered them before that gives a pretty good overview","text":"Linus Tech Tips covered them before that gives a pretty good overview [0]. It's a really cool tech and I hope it becomes more affordable in the future. I'd imagine actually feeling like a person is physically present will make Zoom meetings and such a lot less boring. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26861364,"author":"icegreentea2","month":"2021-04","story":"This is what Doom looks like on a holographic display","stance":"rec","why":"Good footage of the display with beauty shots and the flattened image.","quote":"They have some good footage (though obviously it's setup as a hype reel too).","text":"Like other people have said, this looks like a Looking Glass dev unit or something. And here's a Linus Tech Tips video of it: [VIDEO] They have some good footage (though obviously it's setup as a hype reel too). Edit: Skip to follow for \"beauty shots\": 4:45 and 8:50. 6:12 for funky shot of the 'flattened' image to give you a sense of what 'trick' they're playing"},{"id":26240787,"author":"ortusdux","month":"2021-02","story":"Ask HN: What is a creative product you've discovered recently?","stance":"rec","why":"Decent review of the Looking Glass tech.","quote":"Linus did a decent review of the tech back in 2019","text":"It looks like their kickstarter has all the info: [LINK] Linus did a decent review of the tech back in 2019: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39523644,"author":"justinclift","month":"2024-02","story":"Lenovo's concept laptop is real, transparent, and ready to impress","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Pretty sure there's an existing crowd trying something like this a few years ago","text":"Looks like a useful step towards 3D displays. By stacking a bunch of these transparent panels on top of each other, you could display 3D objects without needing 3D glasses: ----o----- Layer1 ---o-o---- Layer2 ---o-o---- Layer3 ----o----- Layer4 (etc) The depth resolution would be kind of crap, but there'd probably still be uses for it. Maybe layers wrapped as cylinders? Pretty sure there's an existing crowd trying something like this a few years ago: [VIDEO] They're taking a different approach, though using a stack of panels too. --- That crowd is still around: [LINK]"},{"id":23315823,"author":"VikingCoder","month":"2020-05","story":"Looking Glass starts shipping its 8K holographic display","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the display uses 45 angles per pixel, unlike lenticular sheets.","quote":"45 angles for each pixel","text":"Yeah, that's not remotely accurate. 45 angles for each pixel: [VIDEO] That's far more complicated than a lenticular sheet glued to LCD."},{"id":23316624,"author":"robocat","month":"2020-05","story":"Looking Glass starts shipping its 8K holographic display","stance":"reference","why":"Has good tech detail and raw pixel mapping at 6:30.","quote":"has some good tech detail, with the raw pixel mapping shown at 6:30","text":"(Edited) The frog was on the low-res developer device. The much larger “8k” version introduced half way through [VIDEO] has some good tech detail, with the raw pixel mapping shown at 6:30. Calcs seem about right since raw pixel count was stated as 43 million, although it was implied elsewhere they split RGB sub pixels too (which sounds wrong I admit), so maybe 43 million divided by 15? Our eyes are more sensitive to luminance than colour, so maybe they did something there (however, not that I could see from the raw pixel mapping at 6:30)."},{"id":23316678,"author":"robocat","month":"2020-05","story":"Looking Glass starts shipping its 8K holographic display","stance":"reference","why":"Shows sub-pixel mapping at 6:30 refuting the simple lenticular claim.","quote":"At 6 min 30 in they show the pixel sub mapping","text":"That’s wrong: technically it is nothing like the vertical grated sheet you see on cheap 3D posters etc. At 6 min 30 in [VIDEO] they show the pixel sub mapping - and it isn’t just vertical bars. Edit: If they used a vertical grated sheet, there should be vertical lines at high contrast edges at a 45 pixel pitch. look at the green book at 6:22 and close-up at 6:27, and there seems to be vertical smear (not just what you might expect from horizontal smear). Example of a 3D lenticular print without the lenses to compare against: [LINK] from [LINK]"},{"id":23316817,"author":"dugditches","month":"2020-05","story":"Looking Glass starts shipping its 8K holographic display","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Interesting to see the 'modified' image before it's put on the display","text":"Interesting to see the 'modified' image before it's put on the display( [VIDEO] ) Seems similar to how pixel art worked, and tricks artists used to get a better image with CRTs. [LINK]"},{"id":23316256,"author":"fxtentacle","month":"2020-05","story":"Looking Glass starts shipping its 8K holographic display","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the effective resolution is <900px","text":"I believe they are shy to show it in high-quality video because the effective resolution is <900px. See my comment below for the math. Even in that TechCrunch gif, I believe I can already see pixel borders on the specular shading of the top part of the h. For an even clearer example, see the frog in full-screen at 3:31 here: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["3CWCqJl0BEs",1],["XFJnWp1tAdU",1],["GhsZUZmJvaM",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1]]},"8YUWDrLazCg":{"wy":"HN points to it whenever discussion turns to billionaire wealth or the human difficulty of grasping very large numbers.","q":{"x":"Tom Scott has a great video on it","i":34324660,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34324660,"author":"dijit","month":"2023-01","story":"UK: Excess deaths in 2022 among worst in 50 years","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how to visualise very large numbers","quote":"Tom Scott has a great video on it","text":"It's hard to actually visualise large numbers; a common problem actually Tom Scott has a great video on it: [VIDEO] EDIT: Decide to swap the examples to British cities, inkeeping with what you said about apples/oranges; which people may be less familiar with; but hopefully it gives the sense of scale."},{"id":29597963,"author":"murm","month":"2021-12","story":"Ask HN: How do you accept your place in the world?","stance":"rec","why":"Visualizes the mind-boggling scale of a billion","quote":"I think this[0] video might help to visualize the mind-boggling scale of the world","text":"I don't know the answer to your question, but I think this[0] video might help to visualize the mind-boggling scale of the world (in the video you can substitute dollars with human beings). And it's only one billion visualized so in order to go through the entire human population you would have to watch it 7.9 times which would take something over 10 hours. As the dollar meter keeps running relentlessly you can imagine yourself being one split of a second somewhere there, and then you can imagine the sea of people working hard at compilers, optimizers, math etc. blasting you all at once with their brilliant observations. It's only natural to get overwhelmed if you expose yourself to the aggregate of the most brilliant and productive humans, because as rare as they might be, the human population is so staggeringly big that there are LOTS of them. You might not be doing so bad compared to the rest of the humanity though ;) [0] [VIDEO] (A Million Dollars vs A Billion Dollars, Visualized: A Road Trip)"},{"id":28113712,"author":"yreg","month":"2021-08","story":"Wealth Shown to Scale","stance":"rec","why":"Road trip visualizing million versus billion dollars","quote":"Better visualisations imo","text":"I find these scrolling websites unergonomic and not at all that good at getting the point across (other than \"yeah, it's a lot\"). Also, with the text-length budget of that site I think it's a shame it doesn't explain what wealth, profit, liquidity and cash-on-hand is. Better visualisations imo: - Tom Scott: A Million Dollars vs A Billion Dollars, Visualized: A Road Trip [VIDEO] - How rich are Jeff Bezos and Elon Musk in Minecraft terms [LINK]"},{"id":47732349,"author":"LocalH","month":"2026-04","story":"Meta is set to pay its top AI executives almost a billion each in bonuses","stance":"reference","why":"Illustration of scale of billions","quote":"Relevant for perception, possibly the greatest such illustration","text":"I feel like we need a society where the amount of capital you get to keep is inversely proportional to how much you give back to society, on a percentage level. Have $100 billion dollars? Great. The more you give back in charity and community, the more you get to keep. Care more about \"number go brrr\" and less about your fellow man? We tax all kinds of personally harmful behavior like drinking and smoking. We should also tax anti-social behavior similarly. Someone like Musk with multiple hundreds of billions of dollars in capital should lose 75% of it unless you give back at least 25 or 30% of it, minimum. I'm not qualified to decide the exact percentage points. But we have to do something to keep the rich from vacuuming up almost every penny that exists. Relevant for perception, possibly the greatest such illustration: [VIDEO] Daily reminder that to this day, John Rockefeller is considered one of the wealthiest humans in history, despite the fact that today's billionaires dwarf his raw numbers."},{"id":44792703,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2025-08","story":"What Does One Billion Dollars Look Like?","stance":"reference","why":"Compares a million dollars to a billion physically","quote":"The length of one million USD is about the distance of a US football field","text":"Tom Scott did a video on this, \"A Million Dollars vs A Billion Dollars, Visualized: A Road Trip\": * [VIDEO] The length of one million USD is about the distance of a US football field or UK football pitch (which he demonstrates walking in a parking lot). The length of one billion USD is driving over an hour at a speed of 100 kph (55 mph), the rest of the video."},{"id":30480437,"author":"manquer","month":"2022-02","story":"Berkshire Hathaway 2021 annual report [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates how hard it is to grasp billionaire wealth","quote":"We don't fully grasp how rich billionaires are.","text":"In late 1970s he was worth $67M by 1982 it was $370M , $670M in 1983 and billonare in 1986 at age 56.[1] He bought control of Berkshire Hathaway in 1965 about 17 years prior to hiring Jain. His model and name were well known in the right circles by then. In the investment world and cricles where he would be searching for talent in early 80s he had demigod status, making that kind of returns as pure investor without being a founder of tech company like Apple/MS was unheard of . He was not as popular with general public , it wasn't as big a story for middle aged man making money by investing in boring stocks as compared to 20-30yr old tech rock stars with more colourful life like Jobs/Gates and type of products Apple /MS were building at that time. General public popularity came much later when he became the richest in the world --- [1] It is very very hard to 20x $50M by just investing in lower risk diversified assets. We don't fully grasp how rich billionaires are. Tom Scott video [VIDEO] comes to mind"},{"id":25892707,"author":"monopoledance","month":"2021-01","story":"Oxford pledged to donate rights to their Covid Vaccine, then sold them to pharma","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Also just assuming that someone who donates billions is evil without any research whatsoever is just lazy clickbait journalism. Assuming someone who donates billions is not evil is also lazy thinking... To me the article doesn't say Gates is \"evil\". They say Gates uses his immense influence to make the world how he sees fit. Do you have any evidence against Gate's being in strong favor of IP over open access? And let's roll with it... Do you think Gates should have the power to influence the lives of billions like that? I don't mean \"access to vaccines\" and other superficial no-brainers, I mean setting up the world way beyond the good bits. It's not like the \"patents: good, corporate power: good\" weltanschauung is general consensus, especially in research where he plays his cards[+]. [+] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["PHe0bXAIuk0",1],["rStL7niR7gs",1],["LdOe18KhtT4",1],["fD2briZ6fB0",1],["lIFE7h3m40U",1],["qLjgXPDzeZo",1],["m7PVZixO35c",1],["Jzfpyo-q-RM",1],["h4Uu5eyN6VU",1],["Pld8wTa16Jk",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["kCc8FmEb1nY",1],["iHzzSao6ypE",1],["WZskjLq040I",1],["T71ibcZAX3I",1]]},"HNkLcD3PKyk":{"wy":"HN links it whenever office-to-residential conversion comes up, as a good deep dive into why the idea is not simple.","q":{"x":"a good deep dive into why this is not a simple proposition.","i":46722730,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":46722730,"author":"mooreds","month":"2026-01","story":"Downtown Denver's office vacancy rate grows to 38.2%","stance":"rec","why":"NYC-focused deep dive on why conversions are not simple.","quote":"a good deep dive into why this is not a simple proposition.","text":"This podcast is about the NYC market, but a good deep dive into why this is not a simple proposition. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40288861,"author":"cduzz","month":"2024-05","story":"Can turning office towers into apartments save downtowns?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why office layouts clash with apartment requirements.","quote":"There's a good \"odd lots\" podcast about this[1].","text":"There's a good \"odd lots\" podcast about this[1]. There's another of their podcasts about how apartment zoning rules make it hard to make \"family\" apartments[2]. Basically you need two egress points, windows that can open, windows in kitches and bedrooms. All of these are directly in contradiction to modern office buildings with open floor plans, fixed windows, shared mechanical systems. So it may be possible to convert offices into apartments, but it's very expensive and you end up losing a bunch of floor space. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":38787666,"author":"mooreds","month":"2023-12","story":"Ask HN: Favorite Podcast Episodes of 2023?","stance":"rec","why":"Episode on what it takes to convert offices to residential.","quote":"Here are two of my favorite odd lots episodes.","text":"Here are two of my favorite odd lots episodes. How banking worked before computers: [LINK] What it takes to convert commercial buildings to residential buildings: [VIDEO] Sharing YouTube links because they don't have paywall free podcast pages, though the podcast is free to subscribe to (with ads)."},{"id":38820420,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2023-12","story":"The high price of empty office space","stance":"rec","why":"Interview with a developer about difficulties of office-to-residential conversion.","quote":"Bloomberg's Odd Lots podcast had an episode with someone who does conversions","text":"Bloomberg's Odd Lots podcast had an episode with someone who does conversions, \"What It Really Takes to Convert an Office Building Into Apartments\": > Big cities like New York have two real estate problems. Housing is scarce and office buildings are empty (or at least under-utilized.) So there would seem to be an obvious solution: turn the offices into homes. And indeed there has been a lot of talk lately about \"office-to-resi\" conversions. But it's very hard, for a wide variety of reasons. Zoning, financing, and then, of course, the operational aspects of the construction all need to be in place. So what does it take? On this episode, we speak with Joey Chilelli, managing director at the Vanbarton Group, a firm that's been involved with these projects for a decade and long before the pandemic upended both real estate markets. We discuss the challenges involved in actually pulling off these complex projects. * [LINK] * [VIDEO]"},{"id":40287707,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2024-05","story":"Can turning office towers into apartments save downtowns?","stance":"reference","why":"Episode on zoning, financing, and construction challenges of conversions.","quote":"A few months ago Bloomberg's Odd Lots podcast had an episode on this","text":"A few months ago Bloomberg's Odd Lots podcast had an episode on this: > Big cities like New York have two real estate problems. Housing is scarce and office buildings are empty (or at least under-utilized.) So there would seem to be an obvious solution: turn the offices into homes. And indeed there has been a lot of talk lately about \"office-to-resi\" conversions. But it's very hard, for a wide variety of reasons. Zoning, financing, and then, of course, the operational aspects of the construction all need to be in place. So what does it take? On this episode, we speak with Joey Chilelli, managing director at the Vanbarton Group, a firm that's been involved with these projects for a decade and long before the pandemic upended both real estate markets. We discuss the challenges involved in actually pulling off these complex projects. * [LINK] * [VIDEO]"},{"id":39552138,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2024-02","story":"Why a White House Plan to Fund Office-to-Housing Conversions Isn't Working","stance":"reference","why":"Episode on zoning, financing, and construction challenges of conversions.","quote":"","text":"July 6, 2023, \"What It Really Takes to Convert an Office Building Into Apartments\" > Big cities like New York have two real estate problems. Housing is scarce and office buildings are empty (or at least under-utilized.) So there would seem to be an obvious solution: turn the offices into homes. And indeed there has been a lot of talk lately about \"office-to-resi\" conversions. But it's very hard, for a wide variety of reasons. Zoning, financing, and then, of course, the operational aspects of the construction all need to be in place. So what does it take? On this episode, we speak with Joey Chilelli, managing director at the Vanbarton Group, a firm that's been involved with these projects for a decade and long before the pandemic upended both real estate markets. We discuss the challenges involved in actually pulling off these complex projects. * [LINK] * [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["fXW-QjBsruE",1],["QWWgr2rN45o",1],["LMxate9gegg",1],["qE7dYhpI_bI",1],["YcVSgYz5SJ8",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1],["owieQnPYfT8",1]]},"N9RUqGYuGfw":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a hands-on way to understand Haskell and parser combinators by watching a parser get built live.","q":{"x":"this guy writes a json parser live, giving his intuition along the way. It's helped me immensely.","i":23961926,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":30623618,"author":"ObiWanFrijoles","month":"2022-03","story":"What Is IO Monad? (2018) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"JSON parser using only Haskell standard library.","quote":"He has another great video that covers a bunch of topics while making a JSON parser with only the Haskell standard library","text":"Nice to see Tsoding on the front page. He has another great video that covers a bunch of topics while making a JSON parser with only the Haskell standard library: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24377187,"author":"ducaale","month":"2020-09","story":"List of YouTube channels for improving web development and programming skills","stance":"rec","why":"Parser combinator in Haskell from Tsoding's streams.","quote":"Some of the interesting random things I found there include","text":"I have recently discovered [tsoding twitch channel]( [LINK] ) which describes itself as Recreational programming. The topics range from webassembly and Game development to functional programming in haskell and ocaml. Some of the interesting random things I found there include: * [a parser combinator in haskell]( [VIDEO] ) * [stack based language compiler in ocaml]( [LINK] ) You can find all the things that were done during the streams in [LINK] . You can also find his schedule at [LINK]"},{"id":23961926,"author":"lubesGordi","month":"2020-07","story":"Basics of Haskell – Code and exercises","stance":"rec","why":"Live-coded JSON parser in Haskell with intuition explained.","quote":"this guy writes a json parser live, giving his intuition along the way. It's helped me immensely.","text":"Agreed! I've been trying to learn haskell for a long time, and this guy writes a json parser live, giving his intuition along the way. It's helped me immensely. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24319688,"author":"ducaale","month":"2020-08","story":"Inventing Monads","stance":"reference","why":"Parser combinators as natural use of monads.","quote":"","text":"To understand monads, I think it helps to know a language where expressing such a concept is more natural. Although I do not claim to know 100% what monads are yet, coming across these features made it easier for me to understand the concept a little bit. - Algebraic data types - OCaml's let expressions - F#'s computation expressions I also think that it is important to write something that naturally requires the use of monads. One thing that spring to mind is Parser Combinators. - [LINK] - [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["GuigptwlVHo",1]]},"SKhcan8pk2w":{"wy":"Cited as a clear, accessible explanation of why life uses only one version of chiral molecules.","q":{"x":"Here is a video talking about molecular chirality and how nature really only produces one version of molecules","i":33712767,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33712767,"author":"thinkyfish","month":"2022-11","story":"Pristine meteorite found within hours of hitting Earth","stance":"rec","why":"Molecular chirality and why life makes one version.","quote":"Here is a video talking about molecular chirality and how nature really only produces one version of molecules","text":"Here is a video talking about molecular chirality and how nature really only produces one version of molecules [VIDEO] . If it picked up the molecules in the atmosphere those atmospheric molecules should have been made by life on earth and those have a handedness bias depending on the molecule, some are produced right handed only or left handed only because they are made one atom at a time and need to be one handed for life to use them. Abiotic synthesis produces a 50/50 bias of left and right handed molecules because they are made all at once."},{"id":31784547,"author":"staindk","month":"2022-06","story":"Physicists link two time crystals in seemingly impossible experiment","stance":"rec","why":"Loosely related but potentially interesting on homochirality.","quote":"even though it's not directly related I think it may be interesting to you.","text":"Your question reminded me of this video about \"Homochirality: Why Nature Never Makes Mirror Molecules\"[1] - even though it's not directly related I think it may be interesting to you. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29032033,"author":"cyounkins","month":"2021-10","story":"The main thing about Phenylacetone meth is that there's so much of it","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why nature makes only one handedness of molecules.","quote":"Steve Mould has a great video [1] on homochirality in nature","text":"Can anyone comment on how producers could be isolating the the 'd' enantiomer? Are they using enzymes? Steve Mould has a great video [1] on homochirality in nature and says, \"Why are all the sugar molecules that you buy from the shops right-handed? ... If you were to make some sugar for yourself in a chemistry lab by mixing some chemicals together, you would get a 50/50 mix of left-handed sugar and right-handed sugar.\" He goes on to describe how enzymes in nature exclusively make homochiral molecules, and since all our sugar is made by enzymes, all our sugar is homochiral. Later in the video he describes how you can filter enantiomers by finding an enzyme in nature that 'eats' the undesired enantiomer, finding the DNA for it, and coercing bacteria into producing that enzyme. This seems quite complicated and potentially out of reach for a clandestine drug-making operation. Is there another way? [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["zdkyGDqU7xA",1]]},"i1vbvikDiI8":{"wy":"Jai had no public release, so commenters point to this beta user's videos as a way to see what the language is actually like.","q":{"x":"One of the beta testers has published some more up to date videos with his language introduction and initial experience","i":22204143,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25852524,"author":"viktorcode","month":"2021-01","story":"Jai Language Primer (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"More current impressions of Jai from a beta user","quote":"For more current info check out beta user's impressions","text":"Jai changed quite a bit since then. For more current info check out beta user's impressions (and the language primer) here: [VIDEO] (first video in the series)"},{"id":23803228,"author":"robaay","month":"2020-07","story":"A Programming Language for Games (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"CD Projekt Red programmer's overview of where Jai is now","quote":"You can watch[1] one of the lead programmers from CD Projekt Red joyfully give an overview of where Jai is at now.","text":"You can watch[1] one of the lead programmers from CD Projekt Red joyfully give an overview of where Jai is at now. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22204143,"author":"rehemiau","month":"2020-01","story":"The Odin Programming Language","stance":"rec","why":"Beta tester's language introduction and initial experience with Jai","quote":"One of the beta testers has published some more up to date videos with his language introduction and initial experience","text":"Jonathan Blow's language is the most interesting from these three for me because it will have nice metaprogramming features. The document you linked to seems slightly outdated. One of the beta testers has published some more up to date videos with his language introduction and initial experience: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["pO4_Wlq8JeI",1],["qp0HIF3SfI4",1],["ALc8CBYOfkw",1]]},"tMP5Pbx8I4s":{"wy":"HN commenters find the hypothesis elegant and the channel consistently thought-provoking, and point to it in threads on light, particles and wave functions.","q":{"x":"the explanation is beautiful in its simplicity","i":43393047,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43393047,"author":"TeMPOraL","month":"2025-03","story":"Did the Particle Go Through the Two Slits, or Did the Wave Function?","stance":"rec","why":"Light self-contained by non-linear vacuum could behave like particles.","quote":"the explanation is beautiful in its simplicity","text":"I don't know how fringe this is, but Huygens Optics gives a possible answer here: [VIDEO] . TL;DW: if you assume certain non-linear properties of vacuum as a medium, it seems possible for light (EM waves) to self-contain spatially instead of spreading out, and such trapped energy seems, for all intents and purposes, to behave like particles. Again, IANAPhysicist, so I don't know what to think of that video, but the channel seems legit, and the explanation is beautiful in its simplicity ."},{"id":43154751,"author":"TeMPOraL","month":"2025-02","story":"But good sir, what is electricity?","stance":"rec","why":"Elegant hypothesis of particles as self-confined waves in a non-linear vacuum.","quote":"this really seems to make a lot of sense and is quite elegant","text":"I don't know how established this is, but I'm partial to the \"vacuum is elastic, non-linear medium, and particles are waves trapped in the non-linearity - self-confining energy\" hypothesis. IANAPhysicist, but this really seems to make a lot of sense and is quite elegant: [VIDEO] And with this, there's no empty space in atoms, as the vacuum perturbations from each particle spread out to infinity, so inside the atom there's more energy in those perturbations than outside - the space is less empty inside than outside."},{"id":42709889,"author":"somat","month":"2025-01","story":"Why the weak nuclear force is short range","stance":"rec","why":"Video essay simulating field effects, turning waves into particles.","quote":"that channel constantly delivers high quality thought provoking content on the nature of light.","text":"There is an interesting video essay by the Huygens Optics channel where some simulations of these field effects are considered. Turning Waves Into Particles [VIDEO] And if unfamiliar, that channel constantly delivers high quality thought provoking content on the nature of light."},{"id":42741297,"author":"haxiomic","month":"2025-01","story":"Show HN: Real-time nonlinear optics simulation (JS/GLSL)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Huygens Optics' recent videos about modelling particles as confined light using a particular non-linear medium [0]","text":"This is extremely cool! Curious on the story behind this /u/westoncb! It reminds me of Huygens Optics' recent videos about modelling particles as confined light using a particular non-linear medium [0], is it possible to create similar 'light' particles with tweaks to your setup? [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["meHYBhcpdvQ",1],["NY66qpMFOYo",1],["eOti3kKWX-c",1],["i9i47sgi-V4",1],["SDtAh9IwG-I",1],["ydqReeTV_vk",1],["f8FAJXPBdOg",1],["ObvxPSQNMGc",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["jAhjPd4uNFY",1],["2o8MDCIlOEk",1],["FV8n_E_6Tpc",1],["3kEfedtQVOY",1]]},"wtIhVwPruwY":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an accessible layperson's introduction to the Reynolds number and fluid dynamics scales.","q":{"x":"I think this is a good lay-person's introduction to basis of Kolmogorov's length scale, specifically the Reynold's number","i":21775410,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":28166580,"author":"dagw","month":"2021-08","story":"Tesla valve","stance":"rec","why":"Good video introduction to Navier-Stokes, part 2","quote":"There is pretty a good video introduction to Navier-Stokes here","text":"When it comes to fluids (and gases) the keyword you are looking for the the Navier-Stokes equation. In most cases gases and fluids can be handled essentially the same way. There is pretty a good video introduction to Navier-Stokes here: [LINK] (part 1) and [VIDEO] (part 2) The big 'problem' with fluid dynamics is that from the equations it looks like a really simple problem. The Navier-Stokes equation is a couple of lines and you can explain it to a reasonably smart high school student who has taken calculus. And in the 'easy' case where you assume laminar flow and no turbulence it is pretty easy. The problem is when you introduce turbulence into the system then it becomes basically unsolvable and you are forced to rely on 'tricks' and approximations to make the problem computable. Most of the 'art' of CFD is picking the right tricks and approximations to solve the particular problem you have (the second 'art' is creating and a good mesh to run the solver on)."},{"id":24963252,"author":"bgilroy26","month":"2020-11","story":"Single-Celled Organism Appears to Make Decisions (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Helps layman understand Purcell 1977 and low Reynolds number","quote":"There are some nice YouTube videos out there to ramp you up to understanding Purcell 1977 better as a layman with undergrad maths","text":"Thank you for writing out this comment! Even in the Age of the Internet, being able to put your hands on the right review paper is not given at all My girlfriend and I both have mental health issues and that is most of my saw in wanting to understand Neuro 101 or comparative animal neuro or Evolutionary Neuro or anything that will incrementally add to an understanding of human brains that will improve the ability to advocate for ourselves in the US health care system. Serendipitously, I was independent studying on fluid mechanics and dynamics last year[0] and had been through Purcell 1977, so I'm excited to check out your work on that account as well! [0] There are some nice YouTube videos out there to ramp you up to understanding Purcell 1977 better as a layman with undergrad maths Numberphile -- Reynolds Number [VIDEO]"},{"id":21775410,"author":"eindiran","month":"2019-12","story":"First mathematical proof for a key law of turbulence in fluid mechanics","stance":"rec","why":"Layperson introduction to Reynolds number and Kolmogorov scales","quote":"I think this is a good lay-person's introduction to basis of Kolmogorov's length scale, specifically the Reynold's number","text":"I think this is a good lay-person's introduction to basis of Kolmogorov's length scale, specifically the Reynold's number: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["izxXGuVL21o",1],["OyfBQmvr2Hc",1],["86x-u-tz0MA",1]]},"x94PGgYKHQ0":{"wy":"Cited in threads on motorcycle and cyclist safety to explain why drivers look and still miss them.","q":{"x":"a longer but more relevant \"motorcycle invisibility training\" video","i":21157588,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":27908586,"author":"jascii","month":"2021-07","story":"Twike","stance":"rec","why":"Amusing video on why bikes are invisible to drivers.","quote":"FortNine has an amusing video on it","text":"For all practical purposes bicycles and motorcycles are invisible to drivers. FortNine has an amusing video on it: [VIDEO] My personal experience, after years as a courier on both upright, recumbent, and motorcycles is that I am more \"visible\" on a recumbent then the other options because I'm \"weird\" enough to make it into a drivers consciousness... None of this is ofcourse scientifically relevant data."},{"id":21157584,"author":"alosarv","month":"2019-10","story":"Lethal memory fail: Why drivers see, and then forget motorcyclists","stance":"rec","why":"Tongue-in-cheek but relevant on motorcyclist invisibility.","quote":"Also an excellent video here - tongue-in-cheek but highly relevant","text":"Also an excellent video here [VIDEO] - tongue-in-cheek but highly relevant \"invisibility training for motorcyclists\" by FortNine"},{"id":21157588,"author":"chrisweekly","month":"2019-10","story":"Lethal memory fail: Why drivers see, and then forget motorcyclists","stance":"rec","why":"Longer, more relevant motorcycle invisibility video.","quote":"a longer but more relevant \"motorcycle invisibility training\" video","text":"As a motorcyclist -- and lifelong learner w/ a degree in cognitive psychology -- I really appreciate demonstrations like this one^1 from Daniel Simons. It's a short video (< 90s), but for many first-time viewers, it's an eye-opening experience. ^1. [LINK] Edit: just saw @elosarv's link to a longer but more relevant \"motorcycle invisibility training\" video ( [VIDEO]"},{"id":19211158,"author":"pdovy","month":"2019-02","story":"US Cities Are Becoming More Dangerous for Cyclists and Pedestrians","stance":"rec","why":"Why motorcyclists are invisible to drivers.","quote":"There's a great, if a bit cheesy video from FortNine [1]","text":"There's a great, if a bit cheesy video from FortNine [1] on why motorcyclists can be basically invisible to even cautious drivers due to the way our brain processes information, and I think the same thing would apply for bicyclists. When I'm on my bike (of either type) I assume nobody sees me and act accordingly. For cyclists and pedestrians to really be safe we need to design our road infrastructure to counteract those issues from the start. I visited Copenhagen a few years ago and biked everywhere, and it was a real eye opener how safe and easy it was compared with my home in Chicago. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47697136,"author":"Swizec","month":"2026-04","story":"Škoda DuoBell: A bicycle bell that penetrates noise-cancelling headphones","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that motorcyclists are invisible to drivers.","quote":"Motorcyclists are invisible.","text":"> or even shocked (as if I appeared from thin air) Motorcyclists are invisible. Never rely on others seeing you, ride as if they're an obstacle you have to navigate. You can hide a whole truck behind the A-pillar of modern cars, let alone a motorcycle. At certain angles, human eyes have complete blind spots that we're not aware of because our brain filters them out. Motorcycles fit perfectly into those. Never hover in people's blind spots. Pass quickly or stay back. Do not drive parallel with another vehicle. Goes for cars too. When approaching another car perpendicularly (like an intersection), remember that humans lose depth perception because their nose covers one of the eyes. A driver literally cannot tell how far you are. Our usual proxy is the distance between headlights. Motorcyles have 1 headlight so this heuristic doesn't work, but we don't realize that it doesn't. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29468208,"author":"gregors","month":"2021-12","story":"In 2019 40% of San Francisco traffic fatalities are from left turns","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the role of saccades in not being seen.","quote":"He's video that talks about how saccades play a role","text":"As a motorcyclist I can confirm. Left turning vehicles are enemy number 1. But all SMIDSY's (sorry mate, didn't see you) are extremely dangerous. He's video that talks about how saccades play a role - [VIDEO]"},{"id":27324063,"author":"turbinerneiter","month":"2021-05","story":"Yamaha MOTOROiD","stance":"reference","why":"Perception psychology that makes motorcyclists hard to see.","quote":"Like knowing the perception psychology that makes it hard for cars to see you and try to counter that.","text":"No, cars are isolated. Safe driving practices are safe. Like knowing the perception psychology that makes it hard for cars to see you and try to counter that. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vJG698U2Mvo",1]]},"8XUutFBbUrg":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the best short introduction to what makes Prolog unique.","q":{"x":"check out this video introduction by Markus Triska about what makes Prolog unique","i":35624654,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34196321,"author":"ggzor","month":"2022-12","story":"Prolog at Work","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Tour of Prolog's syntax, theory and modern features","quote":"If you want a tour of Prolog, you can watch the video with the same name","text":"The Power of Prolog [0] is a fantastic blog/video series covering everything from basic syntax, theoretical basis, modern features and idiomatic constructs. I highly recommend it if you want to get the gist of Prolog and its modern features. If you want a tour of Prolog, you can watch the video with the same name [1]. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35624654,"author":"grose","month":"2023-04","story":"Why did Prolog lose steam? (2010)","stance":"rec","why":"Intro to what makes Prolog unique","quote":"check out this video introduction by Markus Triska about what makes Prolog unique","text":"I think that Prolog will rise again. If you have an hour to kill, check out this video introduction by Markus Triska about what makes Prolog unique: [VIDEO] Recently I have been working on getting Trealla Prolog to run in WebAssembly. Support is quite good now, with an easy-to-use JS library[1], Go library[2], and is now one of the best-supported languages for the Spin runtime[3]. I think it's particularly nice for validations, you can write some simple Prolog rules and run the same code on the client and the server now. I would like to explore this more with a dedicated library at some point. Built-in features like DCGs make parsing/generating all kinds of data trivial. I love it. I think the biggest barrier to Prolog adoption is that it has a really steep learning curve, and most people get burnt by half-assed introductions in college that tick the 'talked about logic programming' box in the syllabus. You really have to think in a completely different way to write a good Prolog program. But, once you get the right mindset, it makes solving complex problems incredibly easy. I don't really buy the argument that Datalog is enough. Most Datalog implementations I've seen hack on a bunch of extensions to do things like list membership. It would be awesome to see some serious effort put into an efficient Prolog-based database. The closest thing I can think of is TerminusDB, which I've never used but I wish I could convince my company to try it out ;). My dream is to have some kind"},{"id":41880532,"author":"rdtsc","month":"2024-10","story":"Use Prolog to improve LLM's reasoning","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I was watching some of the videos and in the intro Markus mentioned some of the most popular Prolog systems [VIDEO] SICStus, GNU Prolog, XSB, Ciao, Scryer, Traella, Tau but does not mention at all of SWI-Prolog. I remember using SWI-Prolog back in the day, did it somehow fall out favor, or is there some animosity between implementation and Markus is just not in the SWI-Prolog camp?"}],"r":[["HxaD_trXwRE",1],["3GObi93tjZI",1]]},"N9c7_8Gp7gI":{"wy":"HN readers cite Kay's talks for his views on programming languages as user interfaces, thinking beyond logic, and why people struggle with complex systems.","q":{"x":"I would really really encourage everyone here to watch Alan Kay's recent talks on how poorly we understand systems","i":17515727,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17515727,"author":"skadamat","month":"2018-07","story":"People Aren’t Dumb, the World Is Hard","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talks on how we understand and manage complex systems","quote":"I would really really encourage everyone here to watch Alan Kay's recent talks on how poorly we understand systems","text":"I would really really encourage everyone here to watch Alan Kay's recent talks on how poorly we understand systems and why it's critical we get better at managing them. Some good ones: - [LINK] - [VIDEO] Some people have strong opinions about Alan Kay's CS / programming work and I'd really encourage them to put them aside and deeply understand his points + context. The programming parts aren't ever the point, he's deeply interested in how as humans we improve our ability to collaboratively manage systems."},{"id":20506329,"author":"morphle","month":"2019-07","story":"Ask HN: What people skills do you wish you learned earlier in your career?","stance":"rec","why":"Kay analyses why most people behave as described","quote":"Alan Kay describes and analyses why the majority of humanity act like you describe in many of his lectures.","text":"I tend to want to teach these people, point them to books and knowledge so they can learn and be less shallow. That is a sure way to piss almost all of them off (unless they become your students), which rarely happens. Alan Kay describes and analyses why the majority of humanity act like you describe in many of his lectures. He answers and explains most of your points. Its hard to recommend these talks, the good bits are spread around 50 lectures. I'll be happy to discuss with you which bits you'll like. A first suggestion is [1][2]. >I really want is to talk to deeply self-aware people struggling with the boundaries of human knowledge. I love to talk to such people also, so I search for them in the (scientific) crowd and befriend them so we can have those interesting conversations. Please contact me (morphle @ ziggo dot nl) and lets find out if we can have such a meaningful and satisfying exchange. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11940295,"author":"0xdeadbeefbabe","month":"2016-06","story":"Alan Kay has agreed to do an AMA today","stance":"reference","why":"Kay says thinking is not about logic","quote":"In this video[0] you said thinking is not about logic and that was the mistake the greeks made.","text":"Well what is thinking about then? What was the mistake the greeks made? In this video[0] you said thinking is not about logic and that was the mistake the greeks made. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10930129,"author":"xjay","month":"2016-01","story":"Ask HN: Which people and groups are researching new approaches to programming?","stance":"reference","why":"Source of Alan Kay quote on programming languages as user interfaces","quote":"A programming language is a user interface as much as it is any other thing.","text":"Like many people, I think about this now and then, but I haven't done any work in that area. Ultimately, I picture a live environment, but not like Blueprint in UE4. I expect we'll end up with a predominantly interactive approach to programming, most likely visual drag-and-drop style programming, in a live environment that knows common patterns, data structures, algorithms; giving real-time advice, showing native code output as you go, etc. Basically, you're molding the system while monitoring it under various contexts, and you're programming against data models. Got a new Arduino board? Just drag and drop the data sheet model into your environment. It contains memory address information, read/write contexts, access protocols, contexts, etc for every component, and how they're connected. Now you design the rest of the logic. \"A programming language is a user interface as much as it is any other thing. Why have multiple ones? They are all Turing equivalent.\"—Alan C. Kay (see the talk for the context of the quote; starts at around 23:25) \"Rethinking CS Education | Alan Kay, CrossRoads 2015.\" [VIDEO] \"Most computer people went into computing because they were disturbed by other human beings.\"—Alan C. Kay"}],"r":[["FvmTSpJU-Xc",2],["awADEuv5vWY",1],["nYg6jzotiAc",1],["1e8VZlPBx_0",1],["id1WShzzMCQ",1],["jVkLVRt6c1U",1],["P1ww1IXRfTA",1],["QTJRwKOFddc",1],["agOdP2Bmieg",1],["PFc379hu--8",1],["TH9VCN6UkyQ",1],["OkmNXy7er84",1],["XFDM1ip5HdU",1],["If61baWF4GE",1],["klTjiXjqHrQ",1],["p2LZLYcu_JY",1],["kjBOesZCoqc",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1]]},"OL6-x0modwY":{"wy":"HN quotes it as the crispest statement of what science is, usually when a theory clashes with evidence.","q":{"x":"That is the scientific method. And it is presented by one of our best ever thinkers.","i":32546382,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32546382,"author":"ddingus","month":"2022-08","story":"New evidence shows water separates into two different liquids at low temperature","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Feynman presents the scientific method clearly","quote":"That is the scientific method. And it is presented by one of our best ever thinkers.","text":"[VIDEO] That is the scientific method. And it is presented by one of our best ever thinkers. Simulation fits right up front in the, \"First we guess it\" part of the method. That is what happened here. Now don't get me wrong. It is a good guess, but that is all it is. Next step is to compute the consequences. Simulation applies here too. We can arrive at something we could compare to nature, the authority. That has not happened. Until it does, words like \"evidence\" are well out of reasonable bounds. Simulation as an input to the time tested, production proven scientific method is hardly controversial. Saying that any kind of science has happened yet is and should be controversial! No science has been done, yet we see words like \"evidence\" and \"shows\" I do not know what \"scientific process\" means. Feels a bit more like a P.T. Barnum style upsell for potential money getting than it does science when the sim work is framed this way. If you ask me, the people doing that are jumping the gun. The upsell is unnecessary. Simulation suggests water may seperate into two different liquids when in these conditions... The differences are small, but really important. Stuff line this tends to add right up and suddenly we will have a generation of people who are not well trained skeptics able to do solid science. Given the rapid pace of change, and how compelling this stuff can be, I feel it is important to hold the line on solid science."},{"id":15469618,"author":"wizardforhire","month":"2017-10","story":"The Grandfather of Alt-Science","stance":"rec","why":"Refresher on what science is","quote":"Here's a Feynman chasser/refresher on what science is.","text":"Uggggghhhh, it's all fun and games to read about these wackos until you have to experience them in real life. Until you witness the deaths their bullheaded stupidity cause first hand it's humane to be apologetic to their plight. We're all ignorant, only the stupid defend their ignorance. This is what makes them dangerous. It's hard to admit you're wrong when your entire sense of self is wrapped up in your world view. Here's a Feynman chasser/refresher on what science is. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37229389,"author":"bonoboTP","month":"2023-08","story":"G. Polya, How to Solve It","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman's guess, compute, compare steps","quote":"1. Guess 2. Compute consequences 3. Compare with experiment - if they disagree, the guess was wrong.","text":"Or his steps to do science: 1. Guess 2. Compute consequences 3. Compare with experiment - if they disagree, the guess was wrong. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14195151,"author":"mtempleton","month":"2017-04","story":"Feynman Algorithm (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman: only agreement with nature matters in science","quote":"all that matters is if it experimentally agrees with nature. That's all that science is","text":"Feynman was a particularly interesting, intelligent, but also a very moral person. He said in an interview in his older years, while tearing up: 'I know the difference between right and wrong.' Of science, my favorite bit from him is that 'in science, it does not matter what your last name is, how brilliant you are, how beautiful your theory is, all that matters is if it experimentally agrees with nature. That's all that science is'[1] After devoting his time to science, he indulged in sex, drugs and art, and in the meantime had a great deal good to say about not just nature, but about morality as well. My type of person. 1- [VIDEO]"},{"id":8114426,"author":"louthy","month":"2014-07","story":"NASA presents evidence that microwave thrusters seem to work","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman: theories disagreeing with experiment are wrong","quote":"If it disagrees with experiment, it's wrong","text":"For all those saying this is impossible because of X, I think the great Richard Feynman put it best: \"If it disagrees with experiment, it's wrong\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["EYPapE-3FRw",1],["1L3eeC2lJZs",1],["pxBQLFLei70",1],["eJ3RzGoQC4s",1],["eDNX65d-FBY",1]]},"d6iQrh2TK98":{"wy":"HN cites it in LLM randomness and number-picking threads as evidence that humans are also biased and predictable when asked for random numbers.","q":{"x":"humans are fairly predictable when trying to come up with random numbers, for example, have a look at this Veritasium video","i":40228291,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39860624,"author":"golergka","month":"2024-03","story":"Thirty Seven","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains in detail why 37 shows up everywhere; channel suits HN readers.","quote":"Can highly recommend the channel anyway — especially for the HN crowd.","text":"Probably posted because of this video: [VIDEO] Which not only links to this website, but also explains the topic in detail. Can highly recommend the channel anyway — especially for the HN crowd."},{"id":44779934,"author":"mitch_f","month":"2025-08","story":"The Ski Rental Problem","stance":"rec","why":"Good overview of the concept, with a link to a speaker's website.","quote":"Derek Muller did a good overview of this concept on his Veritasium channel","text":"Derek Muller did a good overview of this concept on his Veritasium channel: [VIDEO] . Even more interesting for HN is a link in the description to one of the speaker's (Tom Magliery) websites: [LINK] ."},{"id":48266202,"author":"indit","month":"2026-05","story":"GPT Guesses Between 1 and 100","stance":"reference","why":"Pointer to the video for human random-number choices.","quote":"Check this Veritasium video for human choice","text":"I'm still amazed that 37, 73, and other numbers ending in 7 are the most popular \"random\" choices for both AI and human. Check this Veritasium video for human choice: [Why is this number everywhere?]( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":47465356,"author":"armchairhacker","month":"2026-03","story":"Ask ChatGPT to pick a number from 1-10000, it generally selects from 7200-7500","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as showing humans also have a randomness bias.","quote":"we also have a randomness bias","text":"People use this as evidence that ChatGPT is unlike human thinking, but we also have a randomness bias: [VIDEO] (referenced in one of the comments. tl;dr: when asked a number between 1-100, most pick a number with 7) But ChatGPT’s bias is worse. It’s really not creative, and I think this hurts its output in “creative” cases, including stock photos and paid writing (ex: ML-assisted ads are even worse than unassisted ads), although not an issue in other cases like programming. Now you may think - obviously that’s because the model has the same weights - but the problem is deeper and harder to solve. First, ChatGPT’s conversations are supposed to be “personalized”, presumably by putting users’ history and interests in the prompt; but multiple users reported the same fact about octopi. Maybe they turned off personalization, but if not, it’s a huge failure that ChatGPT won’t even give them a fact related to their interests (and OpenAI could add that specific scenario to the system prompt, but it’s not a general solution). Moreover, Claude, Gemini, and other LLMs also give random numbers between 7200-7500, while humans aren’t that predictable. Since all LLMs are trained on the same data (most of the internet), it makes sense that all are similar. But it means that the commons are being filled with similar slop, because many people use ChatGPT for creative work. Even when the prompt is creative, the output still has a sameness which makes it dull and mediocre. I’m one of those who ar"},{"id":45501321,"author":"leakycap","month":"2025-10","story":"What Is the Funniest Number?","stance":"reference","why":"Connects funniest numbers to commonly chosen random numbers.","quote":"","text":"Interesting that the funniest numbers also combine to become the most commonly made up numeric answer \"Why is this number everywhere?\" [VIDEO] Veritasium, 2024"},{"id":45738958,"author":"lcnPylGDnU4H9OF","month":"2025-10","story":"Poker Tournament for LLMs","stance":"reference","why":"Recalls the video's finding that 37 is the most common two-digit pick.","quote":"The video made an interesting case for why.","text":"I recall a video (I think it was Veritasium) which featured interviews of people specifically being asked to give a \"random\" number (really, the first one they think of as \"random\") between 1 and 50. The most common number given was 37. The video made an interesting case for why. (It was Veritasium but it was actually a number from 1 to 100, the most common number was 7 and the most common 2-digit number was 37: [VIDEO] .)"},{"id":43179381,"author":"knowitnone","month":"2025-02","story":"'Hey Number 17 '","stance":"reference","why":"Notes Veritasium's finding that 37 is the popular pick.","quote":"according to vertasium 37 is","text":"according to vertasium 37 is [VIDEO] and he monetized a whole video on it. Edit: his number range is 1-100 instead of 1-20 so different"},{"id":40223471,"author":"unshavedyak","month":"2024-05","story":"LLMs can't do probability","stance":"reference","why":"Footnote showing humans are bad at picking random numbers.","quote":"humans are also terrible at \"random\".","text":"I assume somewhat related to this, but humans are also terrible at \"random\". ~See~ Related 37[1]. The more we advance on LLMs the more i am convinced i'm an LLM. :s [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40223516,"author":"sigmoid10","month":"2024-05","story":"LLMs can't do probability","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">You are a weighted random choice generator. About 80% of the time please say ‘left’ and about 20% of the time say ‘right’. Simply reply with left or right. Do not say anything else I could have told you these results solely based on the methodology combined with this system prompt. No need to spend money on APIs. Randomness in LLMs does not come from the context, it comes from sampling over output tokens the LLM considers likely. Imagine you are in this situation as a human: Someone walks up to you and tells you to say \"left\" with 80% probability and \"right\" with 20% probability. You say \"left\" and then the other person walks away never to be seen again. How do you determine if your own \"output\" was correct? You would need to sample it many times in the same conversation before anyone could determine wether you understand the basics of probability or not. This is an issue of the author's understanding of Bayesian statistics and possibly a misunderstanding of how LLMs actually work. Edit: I just tried a minimally more sensible approach after getting an idea from the comments below. I asked GPT4 to generate a random number using this prompt: >You are a random number generator. Reply with a number between 0 and 10. Only say the number, say nothing else. It responed with 7. But then I looked at the top logprobs. Sure enough, they contained all the remaining numbers between 0 and 10. The only issue is that \"7\" got a logprob of -0.008539278, while the next most likely was \"4\" at -"},{"id":40228291,"author":"brabel","month":"2024-05","story":"LLMs can't do probability","stance":"reference","why":"Shows humans are predictable when asked for random numbers.","quote":"humans are fairly predictable when trying to come up with random numbers, for example, have a look at this Veritasium video","text":"Those humans, really difficult to know what they're thinking! Anyway, humans are fairly predictable when trying to come up with random numbers, for example, have a look at this Veritasium video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40140518,"author":"sdenton4","month":"2024-04","story":"The question that no LLM can answer and why it is important","stance":"reference","why":"Cites the video's poll results on most-chosen numbers.","quote":"the most-selected number in their poll of 200k people was 69, followed by 42","text":"Wellll.... Watching that video now, it looks like the most-selected number in their poll of 200k people was 69, followed by 42. Once /those/ are eliminated, 37 and 73 stand out. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["8X69_42Mj-g",1],["2f2kGHcdJYU",1]]},"eW-OMR-iWOE":{"wy":"Commenters recommend it as an entertaining, accessible explanation of how bad the law is, including for non-technical audiences.","q":{"x":"It's very entertaining, and explains it in a way that everyday people can understand how bad it will be.","i":17950409,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17914279,"author":"ObsoleteNerd","month":"2018-09","story":"Australia Wants to Take Government Surveillance to the Next Level","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hilarious satire of the bill's troubling changes.","quote":"This video they made is absolutely hilarious, and shows just how un-hilarious and fucked up these proposed changes are","text":"This video they made is absolutely hilarious, and shows just how un-hilarious and fucked up these proposed changes are: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17950409,"author":"ObsoleteNerd","month":"2018-09","story":"Australia's anti-encryption law will merely relocate the backdoors: Expert","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Entertaining explainer that motivated non-technical people to write in.","quote":"It's very entertaining, and explains it in a way that everyday people can understand how bad it will be.","text":"FWIW, I've had pretty good success in getting non-techie friends and family to write in after showing them this video: [VIDEO] It's very entertaining, and explains it in a way that everyday people can understand how bad it will be. I highly urge all Australians to either send this video out to your circles. (No affiliation, it came up in a previous thread about this topic)"},{"id":18656022,"author":"d2wa","month":"2018-12","story":"Goodbye FastMail: Aussie government undermining trust in tech companies","stance":"rec","why":"Satirical introduction to the law's changes.","quote":"This satirical video is a good introduction to the changes","text":"Companies have to build tools that grants any law enforcement agency access to whatever data they want on whomever they want. For an email provider, these tools could include a search function that lets them look through every email on the platform. Or they could just ask them to mail them a daily dump of all new messages on a floppy disk. The point being, FastMail can’t tell the world what assistance and access their granting the government and there is no oversight or even transparency that could spark public debates on what is reasonable and what isn’t. This satirical video is a good introduction to the changes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17914005,"author":"acutesoftware","month":"2018-09","story":"Australia Wants to Take Government Surveillance to the Next Level","stance":"rec","why":"Shows potential issues with the anti-encryption bill.","quote":"I came across this video that shows the potential issues with this bill","text":"I came across this video that shows the potential issues with this bill [VIDEO] What is concerning is I am building a information management system that focuses on privacy and this sort of bill makes a mockery of the entire concept."},{"id":20697491,"author":"dbg31415","month":"2019-08","story":"Attorney General Barr and Encryption","stance":"reference","why":"Relevant satire on Australia's anti-encryption law.","quote":"Relevant.","text":"Relevant. * Honest Government Ad | Anti Encryption Law - YouTube || [VIDEO] Also worth noting that Australia already went ahead with their anti-encryption laws. Feels inevitable that we'll lose encryption to the nanny state. Really sucks, but I haven't got a clue how to convince the grandmothers out there why the cops shouldn't be trusted here. It's so frustrating. * Government Surveillance: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO) - YouTube || [LINK] I think it'll be one of those things that society won't wake up to needing until it's long gone. Depressing as fuck."},{"id":19747850,"author":"Whatitat90","month":"2019-04","story":"ProtonMail now offers elliptic curve cryptography","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for companies being compelled to install decryption.","quote":"the company can be compelled to install decryption software without notifying anyone. Source","text":"That means their servers cannot be directly accessed but since they're based in Australia the company can be compelled to install decryption software without notifying anyone. Source: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18638466,"author":"dbg31415","month":"2018-12","story":"Australia’s vague anti-encryption law sets a dangerous new precedent","stance":"reference","why":"Relevant humor bit on the Australian anti-encryption law.","quote":"Two relevant humor bits.","text":"Two relevant humor bits. * Honest Government Ad | Anti Encryption Law - YouTube || [VIDEO] * Encryption: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO) - YouTube || [LINK]"},{"id":18732371,"author":"dbg31415","month":"2018-12","story":"Advocating for privacy in Australia","stance":"reference","why":"Listed as related satire amid criticism of FastMail's stance.","quote":"","text":"> FastMail won’t be making changes to our technology or policies in response to this bill. Law enforcement has always been able to request information from us through the Telecommunications Act with a lawful warrant. Because we have the ability to decrypt all data, there is no need to make changes that circumvent encryption. Isn't this, \"No need to force us to install a backdoor, we've already got one!\" Kind of disappointing. Nothing in this article seems to be promoting privacy, just ways they comply with the laws -- and have been for as long as they've been around. If you care about privacy, shouldn't you move your HQ out of Australia? You aren't allowed to even tell people you've been served warrants now, correct? Gag orders mean we have to trust the Australian Government... we can't trust service providers. Eww. * Honest Government Ad | Anti Encryption Law - YouTube || [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["tcV1EYSUQME",1]]},"jhnKBKZvb_U":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a clear explanation of why time runs one way, from a leading physicist.","q":{"x":"Highly recommend the talk.","i":7897130,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7897130,"author":"dave809","month":"2014-06","story":"The Disappearing Universe","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Susskind explains why time runs one way","quote":"Highly recommend the talk.","text":"Leonard Susskind talked about this idea and expanded on it in his talk 'Why is time a one way street' Highly recommend the talk. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26756865,"author":"WindyLakeReturn","month":"2021-04","story":"The Expansion of the Universe","stance":"rec","why":"Talk covering Boltzmann brains and an alternate bubble model of expansion","quote":"I recommend the whole thing if you aren't familiar with Boltzmann brains and boxes","text":"One interesting question of this is if science changes once the other galaxy groups disappear. By that I mean if a human was to redo all previous experiments have we lost any data that would result in us building a different model of the universe than what we've built with the other galaxy groups present. Sure, we will have records of evidence from the past, but records of evidence from experiments that can no longer be repeated is no longer reliable data. This is ignoring the whole development of humanity and science in the intervening 150 billion years which may change what options are available to use then. I'm also not sure if this is the most up to date model of the universe. I've recently dived deep into quantum field theory and theoretical physics (well as deep as one can without understanding a field of spinors) and I've watched a number of lectures given over the last decade that include alternate takes on expansion. I could be completely misunderstanding what was being spoken about, but it appears one possible model of reality is a sort of bubble in hyperexpanding space that gives rise to a big bang event that eventually leads to the bubble becoming hyper expanding space itself that can give rise to further bubbles. Here is one talk. [VIDEO] I recommend the whole thing if you aren't familiar with Boltzmann brains and boxes, but if you want to see the issue with the current model presented in this article go to 41 minute mark and to see a possible alternate model go "},{"id":33295040,"author":"superb-owl","month":"2022-10","story":"A Soviet circus clown tried to prove that dogs can be psychic","stance":"reference","why":"Susskind recounts psychic experiences with a Russian colleague","quote":"He offers a pretty good mundane explanation, but it's still pretty incredible.","text":"Here's a clip from physicist Leonard Susskind where he discusses some \"psychic\" experiences with a Russian colleague. He offers a pretty good mundane explanation, but it's still pretty incredible. [VIDEO]"}]},"lFXJWPhDsSY":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a striking, well-explained example of AlphaZero's play and of zugzwang.","q":{"x":"A great example I love showing people is Alpha Zero's infamous \"Immortal Zugzwang Game\" against Stockfish","i":21793922,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21793922,"author":"lawrenceyan","month":"2019-12","story":"Zugzwang","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Stockfish crushed in a seemingly sudden trap.","quote":"A great example I love showing people is Alpha Zero's infamous \"Immortal Zugzwang Game\" against Stockfish","text":"A great example I love showing people is Alpha Zero's infamous \"Immortal Zugzwang Game\" against Stockfish [0]. Stockfish gets utterly annihilated in a trap that seemingly springs out of nowhere, though of course the reality is that Alpha Zero was actually really in control the entire time. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21395650,"author":"mrleiter","month":"2019-10","story":"Multiplayer AlphaZero","stance":"rec","why":"AlphaZero beating Stockfish with solid explanations.","quote":"Anyone who's interested to see how brilliant AlphaZero played against the best chess computer (then), with solid explanations","text":"Anyone who's interested to see how brilliant AlphaZero played against the best chess computer (then), with solid explanations: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45976122,"author":"yunwal","month":"2025-11","story":"Gemini 3","stance":"reference","why":"Example where Stockfish misses an obvious move.","quote":"","text":"Here’s a chess stackexchange of positions that stump engines [LINK] It basically comes down to “ideas that are rare enough that they were never programmed into a chess engine”. Blockades or positions where no progress is possible are a common theme. Engines will often keep tree searching where a human sees an obvious repeating pattern. Here’s also an example where 2 engines are playing, and deep mind finds a move that I think would be obvious to most grandmasters, yet stockfish misses it [VIDEO] That being said, I’m not sure that this necessarily correlates with brilliancy. There are a few of these that I would probably get in classical time and I’m not a particularly brilliant player."}]},"0-wB1VY3Nrc":{"wy":"Referenced in debates over Chrome hiding URLs as a compelling case for why full URLs are hard even for experts.","q":{"x":"I think they make a compelling argument for hiding most of the url in part to prevent phishing","i":23516160,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24157805,"author":"davidmurdoch","month":"2020-08","story":"Google resumes its attack on the URL bar, hides full addresses on Chrome 86","stance":"rec","why":"Jake Archibald's perspective on removing URLs from the address bar.","quote":"I do still recommend watching the entire video if you want to get more perspective on the issue","text":"Jake Archibald (a Googler) presents some decent points on this on the HTTP 203 podcast: [VIDEO] I still don't agree with the removal of URLs, but I do still recommend watching the entire video if you want to get more perspective on the issue (beyond just the conspiracy theories about AMP and control)."},{"id":23516160,"author":"Orochikaku","month":"2020-06","story":"Google adds experimental setting to hide full URLs in Chrome 85 address bar","stance":"rec","why":"Chrome devs discuss URL readability and security tradeoffs.","quote":"I think they make a compelling argument for hiding most of the url in part to prevent phishing","text":"A couple of Google Chrome devs talk about the issues surrounding the readability of urls and their security implications and possible solutions in an episode of their podcast[0]. I think they make a compelling argument for hiding most of the url in part to prevent phishing however I do think they should allow this behaviour to be toggled via a flag. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23516951,"author":"AshleysBrain","month":"2020-06","story":"Google adds experimental setting to hide full URLs in Chrome 85 address bar","stance":"rec","why":"Covers why reading URLs is hard even for experts.","quote":"This video covers the problems well","text":"Reading URLs is actually really hard - even for experts. This video covers the problems well: [VIDEO] This is bad for web security, since the registerable domain is the part you have to trust, but it's surprisingly difficult to figure out that part. However I feel a bit uneasy about this since URLs are important and tell you where you are on a website. I prefer Firefox's approach which emphasises the registerable domain in the URL bar and fades out the rest of it, making it easier to spot the important bit. However it's still quite subtle - it could do with being a clearer distinction."},{"id":27469566,"author":"jaffathecake","month":"2021-06","story":"Chrome abandons 'simplified domain experiment' in omnibar","stance":"reference","why":"Explanation of why URLs are hard to parse.","quote":"It's hard for even experienced users to figure that stuff out.","text":"Yes. Years. I think a URL bar that sticks to the security relevent parts is a good idea. It's hard for even experienced users to figure that stuff out. I tried to explain it here [VIDEO]"},{"id":23516246,"author":"felixfbecker","month":"2020-06","story":"Google adds experimental setting to hide full URLs in Chrome 85 address bar","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the public suffix list and registrable domains.","quote":"a long special list for domains that host arbitrary subdomains","text":"It is, there’s a long special list for domains that host arbitrary subdomains, GitHub pages is one of them. It’s explained well in [VIDEO]"},{"id":22173221,"author":"dfabulich","month":"2020-01","story":"Humans can't read URLs. How can we fix it?","stance":"reference","why":"Proposal to show the eTLD+1 as a chip in the address bar.","quote":"The proposal appears at 16:45","text":"In this video, a Googler here proposes adding the \"ETLD+1\" part of the domain name as a \"chip\" where the extended-validation certificate name used to be. So this link: [LINK] Would look like this in the browser: jakearchibald.github.io | [LINK] The proposal appears at 16:45 [VIDEO]"},{"id":36855982,"author":"benatkin","month":"2023-07","story":"Google’s nightmare “Web Integrity API” wants a DRM gatekeeper for the web","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"removing the URL from the address bar","text":"Why did Google select someone unknown to announce this? It's also the second time I've noticed Google had someone the UK introduce an unpopular proposal to Chrome. The other one was removing the URL from the address bar (accusing the author of You Don't Know JS of being \"Trump-like\" for how he disagreed with him in the process). [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] Is there a special ops team? Of the FAAMGs my favorite is Google, but this makes me reconsider my position."}],"r":[["FUPstXCqyus",1],["VPjODKUxV5g",1]]},"6Cf7IL_eZ38":{"wy":"Referenced as an inspiring example of a vision-of-the-future video with believable everyday uses.","q":{"x":"has far more every day examples we can all believe in","i":5971503,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":21804202,"author":"ocdtrekkie","month":"2019-12","story":"1950s Smart Homes and the Longevity of Design","stance":"rec","why":"An inspiring example of a future vision ad","quote":"They're often pretty inspiring.","text":"I actually love a good \"future vision\" ad. They're often pretty inspiring. One of my favorites is Corning's \"A Day Made of Glass\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":5971503,"author":"Shivetya","month":"2013-07","story":"A future made of Glass","stance":"rec","why":"Shows believable everyday uses of glass in the future","quote":"has far more every day examples we can all believe in","text":"When I think of a World of Glass I always go back to Corning's A Day Made of Glass [VIDEO] which has far more every day examples we can all believe in"},{"id":3612250,"author":"_k","month":"2012-02","story":"Steve Jobs Solved the Innovator's Dilemma","stance":"reference","why":"Used as prompt for a question about Apple's cross-platform weakness","quote":"","text":"I'm looking at Corning's Day of Glass video [VIDEO] and once again, I'm being reminded of Apple's biggest weakness. There's no cross-platform functionality and that's going to be Apple's biggest problem within a decade. Any idea how Apple might handle a dilemma like that ?"}],"r":[["Erp8IAUouus",1],["ZC5Zr3NC2PY",1]]},"7nL10C7FSbE":{"wy":"HN cites it as a practical demonstration that kerosene-type fuels are hard to ignite compared with gasoline.","q":{"x":"Great practical demonstration of the different flammability characteristics of some common fuels.","i":31382123,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31382123,"author":"MertsA","month":"2022-05","story":"Is It Safe to Ship Thousands of Electric Cars on Big Ships?","stance":"rec","why":"Practical demo of common fuels' flammability","quote":"Great practical demonstration of the different flammability characteristics of some common fuels.","text":"It still doesn't burn well unless it's in a fine mist. Even just a puddle of diesel is actually quite difficult to get burning on its own without some other material as a wick of sorts. [VIDEO] Great practical demonstration of the different flammability characteristics of some common fuels. For the tar that a container ship burns, it's not going to be very volatile. Coal also burns quite well, but unless it's powdered, good luck trying to ignite some with just a match."},{"id":30727308,"author":"sokoloff","month":"2022-03","story":"US schools can subscribe to an electric bus fleet at lower prices than diesel","stance":"rec","why":"Compares flammability of many fuels with timestamps","quote":"compares the flammability of several types of fuels.","text":"[VIDEO] compares the flammability of several types of fuels. 2m08s for Jet-A. 3m50s for #2 diesel. 6m03s for heating oil. 7m30s for kerosene (all those are basically the same and surprising to most how not flammable they are in liquid form). 10m15s for avgas. 11m30s for auto gas."},{"id":27190819,"author":"sokoloff","month":"2021-05","story":"Caltech professor helps solve Hindenburg disaster","stance":"reference","why":"Match goes out in jet fuel","quote":"You can put a match out in a jar of jet fuel at room temp.","text":"Lindbergh was flying with a fuel much closer to gasoline than jet fuel. Jet fuel is on the kerosene/diesel end of the flammability spectrum (which is to say low and much lower than gasoline). You can put a match out in a jar of jet fuel at room temp. (Start at 1m30s: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":23957067,"author":"MertsA","month":"2020-07","story":"Airbus eyes hydrogen power for airliner in next decade","stance":"reference","why":"Jet-A needs atomizing to burn meaningfully","quote":"It takes a spray nozzle atomizing it for him to actually meaningfully burn it with a blowtorch.","text":"No he's right, Jet-A has plenty of energy but it's not very volatile so unless you've got a very large hot fire it will not sustain itself. [VIDEO] It takes a spray nozzle atomizing it for him to actually meaningfully burn it with a blowtorch."},{"id":17875385,"author":"desdiv","month":"2018-08","story":"Norway's Plan for a Fleet of Electric Planes","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates kerosene resisting ignition by torch","quote":"You can even heat a tank of kerosene directly with a propane torch without igniting it","text":"Other posters already mentioned the storage factor, so I'll mention the safety factor: Hydrogen is flammable, but kerosene is not; kerosene is instead combustible. Here's the technical difference between the two[0], but in practical terms it means that you can drop a lit match or cigarette into a tank of kerosene and nothing will happen. You can even heat a tank of kerosene directly with a propane torch without igniting it[1]. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["xtvT2XYlcOY",1],["5ESJH1NLMLs",1],["5JYp9eGC3Cc",1],["nIKhUizkXxA",1]]},"8dinUbg2h70":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as the reference for how a rewind system works and as a point of comparison for undo and serialization approaches.","q":{"x":"Here's a GDC talk about the rewind system in which he explains some of the hurdles he had to deal with","i":34986659,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38220771,"author":"OnionBlender","month":"2023-11","story":"Braid, Anniversary Edition trailer [video]","stance":"rec","why":"How Braid's time rewind was implemented.","quote":"If you're curious how the time rewind was implemented, Blow did a GDC presentation back in 2010.","text":"If you're curious how the time rewind was implemented, Blow did a GDC presentation back in 2010. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17678378,"author":"m00n","month":"2018-08","story":"Braid 10th anniversary critical compilation","stance":"rec","why":"Interview on technicalities of Braid's time effects.","quote":"There is a great interview with Blow on the technicalities of the warping effects in Braid","text":"Seconded. It was a great game, albeit with not so much replay value. There is a great interview with Blow on the technicalities of the warping effects in Braid: [VIDEO] In addition to Limbo and Inside, may I also recommend: Portal 1&2 Antichamber VVVVVV increpare.com In particular Stephen Lavelle (@increpare) makes games with much darker and more interesting social implications. Although, Braid accurately predicted the \"nice guys\" trope."},{"id":34986659,"author":"AnIdiotOnTheNet","month":"2023-03","story":"“Clean” Code, Horrible Performance – Casey Muratori [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Shows hurdles like RAM limits in Braid's rewind.","quote":"Here's a GDC talk about the rewind system in which he explains some of the hurdles he had to deal with","text":"> I don't think that's an earned position. I disagree. > You say he has more credits but only one of those is programming since Braid. He did start a company after that you know. A successful one that makes money and employs people to make art . I don't imagine that running a business takes no time from his life. > But even at the time Braid could have been written in python SDL wrappers and probably had similar performance Braid did a lot more than you give it credit for. Here's a GDC talk about the rewind system in which he explains some of the hurdles he had to deal with: [VIDEO] Pay particular attention to the discussion of the background particles and how to get that to work within the RAM constraints. > On the other hand, on a software engineering level those paint by numbers Modern Warfare and FIFA games are both more technically impressive and are designed for fast iteration Hardly anything about these games change from release to release. They're not exploring new gameplay problem spaces, they're not doing anything super interesting or surprising on a technical level either and I don't get why you think they are. Of course if you keep using essentially the same engine and know exactly what you're trying to make, making another like a goddamned factory is going to happen quicker than if you're trying to make something unique and meaningful. > If clean code is about maintenance and time to market, the Blow paradigm hasn't proven that its needed or fixes the holes in cl"},{"id":32386752,"author":"hbn","month":"2022-08","story":"From throwaway side project to Switch in 2 years: my indie gamedev story","stance":"reference","why":"Rewind was complex to implement yet simple for players.","quote":"It ended up being a pretty complex system that required a lot of ingenuity to come up with a design and implement it","text":"Is difficulty of implementation really a reliable indicator for the complexity in players understanding the mechanic? Jonathan Blow did a talk[1] at GDC a few years ago about implementing the time rewind mechanic in his game, Braid. It ended up being a pretty complex system that required a lot of ingenuity to come up with a design and implement it, but the end result is pretty straightforward and easy to understand for players. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28905261,"author":"steeleduncan","month":"2021-10","story":"Serialization-Based Undo","stance":"reference","why":"Compares Braid's keyframe-and-diff rewind to serialization.","quote":"It is interesting to compare this to Jonathan Blow's talk on the rewind system in Braid [1].","text":"It is interesting to compare this to Jonathan Blow's talk on the rewind system in Braid [1]. This stores complete game states using a compression library to keep memory under control, whereas Braid stored a combination of keyframes, and diffs from those keyframes, to avoid using a compression library. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28977466,"author":"MillenialMan","month":"2021-10","story":"Idiomatic Clojure without sacrificing performance","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Braid uses a historical record, not immutable data.","quote":"","text":"I don't really agree. Cache friendliness is almost always a relevant factor as soon as performance becomes an issue. I get what you're saying but as I see it immutability gives you architectural efficiency and in some cases space efficiency, but rarely processing speed. > Think video games like braid Braid doesn't use immutable data structures, it uses a historical record (and immutability would be incompatible with some of the mechanics).[1] The author of Braid is actually quite famous for his dislike of functional languages and immutability. He doesn't even like garbage collection because of the overhead it introduces. Interestingly, he was talking about data structures for codebase representation (in the context of a compiler) a while back, and someone mentioned HAMTs. I'm definitely curious if they would work well there. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["FeAMiBKi_EM",1]]},"9X68dm92HVI":{"wy":"Used to illustrate predictable irrationality, such as defaults and the effect of the middle option among three choices.","q":{"x":"talks about the very significant differences in organ donation rates between countries","i":2554445,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":14216316,"author":"jason_pomerleau","month":"2017-04","story":"Teller Reveals His Secrets (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"Uses visual illusions as a metaphor for irrationality in decisions.","quote":"Dan Ariely has a great talk on","text":"> 7. If you are given a choice, you believe you have acted freely. Dan Ariely has a great talk on \"decision illusions\"(1) that goes into more depth on this topic. He starts off with visual illusion as a metaphor for rationality: \"So if we have these repeatable and predictable mistakes with vision, which we are so good at, what's the chance we don't make even more mistakes with something we're not as good at?\" (1) [VIDEO]"},{"id":2554445,"author":"dhruval","month":"2011-05","story":"Proposed Law Would Require Social Networks to be Private by Default","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how opt-in versus opt-out defaults drive organ donation rates.","quote":"talks about the very significant differences in organ donation rates between countries","text":"I wouldn't label it stupidity or ignorance, but it is a weel documented quirk of human behavior the default state of opt in / opt out systems makes a huge difference. This ted talk is an excellent and talks about the very significant differences in organ donation rates between countries that use an opt out system vs. those that use an opt in. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11009211,"author":"fizixer","month":"2016-02","story":"How convolutional neural networks see the world","stance":"reference","why":"Optical illusions as a metaphor for predictable human errors.","quote":"we humans are fooled into near certainty that the table on the left has longer vertical distance","text":"Question to deep learning practitioners: Is there any research on comparing the 99.9% confidence about something wrong, to the human weakness of 'optical illusion'? If you watch a few minutes of this Dan Ariely TED Talk [1], we humans are fooled into near certainty that the table on the left has longer vertical distance that the horizontal distance of that on the right. Even after the speaker shows this is not the case, we still \"confidently see\" the wrong thing. (not to mention, we suffer from non-optical illusions as well, like cognitive biases, and fallacious reasoning, as shown later in the talk). It seems to me you guys have discovered 'machine optical illusion'. It's just that the machine has illusions about something completely different. I believe exploring the space of architectures/weights/what-not might lead towards making a machine get into the same kind of optical illusions as the human visual system gets into. At the least, it looks like a very promising research direction to me. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":5391206,"author":"moloch","month":"2013-03","story":"The Cost Of Free Doughnuts: 70 Years Of Regret","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This touches on an interesting human behavior; the difference between $0.00 and $0.01 is huge, much larger than $0.01 to $0.02, or even $0.01 to $0.99 - this is talked about (among other interesting topics) in the book \"Predictably Irrational\" by Dan Ariely. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":4913609,"author":"politician","month":"2012-12","story":"A Common Pricing Mistake","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the middle of three choices orients decisions.","quote":"when offering 3 graduated choices the middle choice has a strong orienting effect on the decider","text":"About 3 years ago, Dan Ariely presented \"Are we in control of our actions\" [1] which demonstrated, among other things, that when offering 3 graduated choices the middle choice has a strong orienting effect on the decider. That is, the middle choice points the way towards which pair should be considered - the bottom two or the top two. The prices of the author's packages are $39, $79, and $169. The differences are $40 and $90, respectively. According to Ariely, in this situation more people will consider the $39/$79 pair (because they are more similar, in this case based on price) rather than the $79/$169 pair, and, in fact, this is what the author reports. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"AHiNeUTgGkk":{"wy":"HN recommends it for its in-depth view of a major UI redesign process driven by user data.","q":{"x":"some guys from MS actually talk through the design process and everything that went into it with a lot of depth","i":38929407,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":38929407,"author":"saratogacx","month":"2024-01","story":"Golden Rules of Interface Design (2013)","stance":"rec","why":"MS designers walk through ribbon design process and rejected alternatives in depth","quote":"some guys from MS actually talk through the design process and everything that went into it with a lot of depth","text":"There's an old MIX08 video where some guys from MS actually talk through the design process and everything that went into it with a lot of depth as well as looking at alternative ideas and why they didn't work. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20824797,"author":"nxc18","month":"2019-08","story":"Most software companies ignore user behavior","stance":"rec","why":"Why Microsoft chose the ribbon, driven by user feedback and telemetry","quote":"Really interesting talk","text":"There’s a lot of interesting history that I think this comment ignores. The office 2007 project was one of their more heavily feedback-driven initiatives; had they been paying attention the same way for Windows 8, it likely would have looked a lot more like Windows 10. Really interesting talk: [VIDEO] They had really good reasons for using the ribbon, not the least of which was that people weren’t finding the features they needed. That was all driven by telemetry back before it was considered the evilest of evils. I’m amazed that you’re still bitter after 13 years. Afaict actual user preference is strongly for the ribbon, minus a very vocal minority that were bothered by the change. Office 2007 was in fact not the boon for OpenOffice that was predicted. edit: tone, realized it came across as not as respectful as I intended"},{"id":45412550,"author":"troupo","month":"2025-09","story":"Users only care about 20% of your application","stance":"reference","why":"Presentation accompanying Microsoft's command usage research","quote":"and there's a presentation, too","text":"With Word especially (and probably with any software approaching anything that can be viewed as professional) the long tail is unbelievably long, and \"users only care about 20% of your application\" may actually become \"users may only care about 20% where no two users share the same 20%\". Here's Microsoft's own research on it from an era when people were actually doing research: [LINK] --- start quote --- Beyond the top 10 commands or so, however, the curve flattens out considerably. The percentage difference in usage between the #100 command (\"Accept Change\") and the #400 command (\"Reset Picture\") is about the same in difference between #1 and #11 (\"Change Font Size\") --- end quote --- The whole series is great: [LINK] and there's a presentation, too: [VIDEO]"}]},"JZE3_0qvrMg":{"wy":"HN points to it as the authoritative talk on Swiss tables, their design rationale, and real-world effects like Hyrum's law.","q":{"x":"gets into more depth of why this is a good idea","i":49082798,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":49082798,"author":"tialaramex","month":"2026-07","story":"Golang Maps: how Swiss Tables replaced the old bucket design","stance":"rec","why":"Deep dive into why swiss tables are a good design","quote":"gets into more depth of why this is a good idea","text":"The Swiss Table is Google's preferred hash table design, it's what you get in the Abseil C++ library and for many years it is the implementation behind Rust's HashMap type too, and as you see, it's also now how Go's map works. [VIDEO] is the 2019 CppCon talk by Matt Kulukundis which gets into more depth of why this is a good idea if you're the sort of person who knows what SIMD is and how caches work."},{"id":27379509,"author":"tialaramex","month":"2021-06","story":"Malloc broke Serenity's JPGLoader, or: how to win the lottery","stance":"rec","why":"Swiss tables talk illustrating Hyrum's law in practice at Google","quote":"it's a good way to illustrate","text":"There's even a CPPCon talk about Google's \"Swiss\" tables with interruptions by Hyrum each time the new code's observable behaviour changes because, at Google, they internally have enough users for Hyrum's law to kick in all the time: [VIDEO] The talk was presumably better live because Hyrum doesn't have a microphone, so his interjections are hard to follow, but it's a good way to illustrate. Some of the examples are much like this story, you design this sophisticated, heavily optimised unordered container for millions of items, and then your users put three items into it, and they depend on the order of those items and you \"broke\" their code by not having this behaviour you never promised and it made no sense to depend on. And the defence mentioned in this thread (randomise things which aren't guaranteed to have stability) is introduced because these are Google employees, they know about Hyrum, it just isn't enough. Sure the code failed when I tried to store 40 items due to your randomisation, but I found it worked OK with ten items as then the randomisation was defeated, so now I store ten items, and then your latest change broke that... The pathological use of data structure is why stuff like making the constructor for empty strings very cheap has a big effect on some codebases. Rust const-ified String::new() because an empty string doesn't need any heap store. So this means code which loops over a large array setting all the members to the empty string is only about as exp"},{"id":44714053,"author":"mizmar","month":"2025-07","story":"SIMD within a register: How I doubled hash table lookup performance","stance":"reference","why":"Reports 10% find speedup and 15% insert slowdown","quote":"It's one of the improvements they claimed in the 2019 presentation.","text":"It's one of the improvements they claimed in the 2019 presentation. [VIDEO] Reporting 10% speedup on find, but 15% slowdown on insert. The speedup probably comes from using 4 more bits of hash, which leads to fewer collisions. And slowdown from more complicated code for the mirroring. I'm still confused on the SIMD alignment. There are load instructs with alignment requirements (_mm_load_si128) and without (_mm_loadu_si128). Both claim the same latency and throughput. Somewhere I've heard the slowdown of unaligned access comes from using more bandwidth to load two aligned 128-bit lines to compose the unaligned value. But no idea if this affects multiple loads of continuous memory."},{"id":40624189,"author":"vitus","month":"2024-06","story":"Compilers are too smart","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses migrating from unordered_map and randomized iteration order","quote":"Matt then goes on to describe how Abseil adds a nondeterministic iteration offset to each hash table","text":"> Abseil, Boost and Folly all provide types which lose some performance to get this back if it's all you need to be \"drop-in\". Indeed, Google's initial migration from std::unordered_map to swisstables moved everyone to absl::node_hash_map since it was safer, even though flat_hash_map generally offers better performance. > after adding fake users Alice, Bob and Carol to your user hash table, when asked for the users back in no particular order you get exactly: Carol, Alice, Bob. Ow :( This reminded me of Matt Kulukundis at cppcon talking about migrating away from std::unordered_map, wherein he brought up this point exactly: [VIDEO] Matt then goes on to describe how Abseil adds a nondeterministic iteration offset to each hash table to prevent anyone from relying on this moving forward."},{"id":32396562,"author":"anonymoushn","month":"2022-08","story":"A quick and practical “MSI” hash table","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the swiss table design and its tradeoffs","quote":"There's a talk about this data structure here","text":"There's a talk about this data structure here: [VIDEO] Basically it stores one array of 1-byte markers that contain a full-or-empty bit and a 7-bit prefix of the hash, then a separate array of buckets. The lookup first loads a neighborhood of bytes in the former array to find entries that match in the first hash byte or empty entries. If the table is very highly loaded or if the hash function is low quality, some checks for equality of the full hash or equality of the value (the former probably just costs a cache miss and the latter might be very expensive) can be skipped, but under better conditions for the hash and load factor and known-pretty-bad conditions for randomness of consecutive lookups, it can be better to avoid the cost of doing one load in the small array of markers and another load in the large array of hash table entries. In the talk the presenter also says that they've changed the first load in such a way that it sometimes spans two cache lines, which makes it a bit more expensive, but that this was nonetheless a win for them."}],"r":[["rAl-9HwD858",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1],["YEBfamv-_do",1]]},"L41oIvre9K0":{"wy":"It is cited as evidence that text scrolling on late-80s hardware was smoother than in many modern editors.","q":{"x":"For those who never used CED on an Amiga, this is how it ran on now 30 years old hardware clocked at 8 MHz.","i":20710813,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20710813,"author":"squarefoot","month":"2019-08","story":"We Made Amiga, They Fucked It Up (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates CygnusEd's famously fast scrolling on 8 MHz Amiga hardware.","quote":"For those who never used CED on an Amiga, this is how it ran on now 30 years old hardware clocked at 8 MHz.","text":"\" ... like CygnusEd with its hand written assembler scrolling that remains, 30 years later, my benchmark for \"fast and smooth enough\" editor scrolling. Essentially nothing has yet matched it ...\" Here it is: [VIDEO] For those who never used CED on an Amiga, this is how it ran on now 30 years old hardware clocked at 8 MHz."},{"id":14436558,"author":"squarefoot","month":"2017-05","story":"Slap – A Sublime-like terminal-based text editor","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how fast a graphical editor scrolled on an 8 MHz Amiga.","quote":"this is how a graphical text editor scrolled text 30 years ago on a 8 MHz 16 bit machine!","text":"This brought memories! There's a video on Youtube showing CED in action... Ladies and gentlemen, this is how a graphical text editor scrolled text 30 years ago on a 8 MHz 16 bit machine! [VIDEO]"},{"id":35486547,"author":"teddyh","month":"2023-04","story":"Lerp","stance":"reference","why":"Example of early smooth-scrolling editor tricks that add input lag.","quote":"","text":"As soon as computers were fast enough, people started to experiment with this stuff: [VIDEO] How useful it is, is debatable, since it inherently adds lag to user input."},{"id":11889853,"author":"Koromix","month":"2016-06","story":"Myth debunking: WebViews suck, everything should be native","stance":"reference","why":"Smooth text scrolling in CygnusEd on a 7 MHz Amiga 2000.","quote":"We had smooth scrolling in the late eighties on the Amiga 2000 (7 Mhz and 1MB of RAM) with CygnusEd","text":"They are not plenty fast. You know you don't have a fast platform when the best you can do is scroll + a couple animations at the same time. And even then, HTML+JS apps often cannot do that without lag. Not saying that native apps are necessarily better (especially on Android), I die a little inside each time I unlock my phone and the unlock screen fails to keep up with my finger. We had smooth scrolling in the late eighties on the Amiga 2000 (7 Mhz and 1MB of RAM) with CygnusEd: [VIDEO] The fact that we barely manage to do that with HTML+JS on what amounts to supercomputers (relative to the time) should not make anyone proud."}]},"MpfvhiqFw7A":{"wy":"Commenters point to it as a short, high-level overview of how Lightning payment channels work.","q":{"x":"This video gives a high-level overview","i":16595317,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16595317,"author":"apo","month":"2018-03","story":"First Lightning mainnet release","stance":"rec","why":"High-level overview of how Lightning payment channels work.","quote":"This video gives a high-level overview","text":"Each transaction spending the locked funds bears the signature of the party making the payment. The other party can then provide the counter-signature making the transaction valid on the Bitcoin network. The main security problem to solve is preventing one party from publishing a previous channel state (half-signed transaction) giving themselves more money than they actually have by rights. For example, Alice and Bob might start with a channel giving each of them 5 bitcoin. Then Alice makes two one-bitcoin payments to Bob: A | B ------ 4 | 6 3 | 7 Alice decides she wants to take back her last payment and instead publish the earlier transaction (signed by Bob) giving herself 4 bitcoin rather than the one giving herself 3. This problem is solved with hashed timelock contracts (HTLCs). Without getting too technical, HTLCs make it possible for a party who spots a counterparty attempting to publish an invalid transaction to take all of the money in the channel (i.e., Bob would take all 10 bitcoin). The main idea is that cryptographic hash functions are one-way. If I make a payment contingent on knowing a preimage to a hash value, I can cause the payment to become valid by revealing the preimage. Along with the signature to a transaction, Alice must also give Bob the preimage for the previous transaction before he will accept it. When she does, Bob knows that if Alice tries to play games, he can take all of her money. This video gives a high-level overview: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13627330,"author":"nadaviv","month":"2017-02","story":"The True Cost of Bitcoin Transactions","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how Lightning scales Bitcoin via off-chain payment routing.","quote":"","text":"> partly because these off-chain networks would quickly form large central \"hubs\" which would obviate many of the benefits of Bitcoin The Lightning Network is open-source and decentralized. It has no \"hubs\", but rather just peers that route payments to each-other using a payment routing algorithm. Lightning has no counterparty risk, as no one can ever steal your bitcoins and you're always in full control. The best way to describe Lightning is as an write-cache layer. It improves the scalability properties of Bitcoin by many factors, makes micro- and nano- payments a reality, reduces fees to fractions of a cent, and even improves privacy (using a Tor-like onion routing for anonymized payments). [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":14265977,"author":"kordless","month":"2017-05","story":"Coinbase adds support for Litecoin","stance":"reference","why":"Lightning Network overview tied to Segwit.","quote":"","text":"I would modify that slightly and say Litecoin is back because of the Lightning Network, which benefits greatly from features provided by Segwit, and which itself leverages payment channels backed by multi-sig addresses. While Lightning can be enabled without Segwit, it's far more trustworthy with it, than without it. [VIDEO]"}]},"XeJSXfXep4M":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a general overview of the cognitive science of insight and focus that is useful for developers and knowledge workers.","q":{"x":"it's pretty interesting stuff for developers and knowledge workers","i":2530712,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":2530548,"author":"jochu","month":"2011-05","story":"Why you get ideas in the shower","stance":"rec","why":"General overview of the cognitive science behind insight.","quote":"for the general overview you can watch the Google Talk","text":"For more specifics into the cognitive science behind why this is - another good book, which has lots of wonderful references to studies on this topic (amongst other things), is \"Your Brain at Work\" by David Rock. Or, for the general overview you can watch the Google Talk at [VIDEO]"},{"id":2530712,"author":"fredoliveira","month":"2011-05","story":"Why you get ideas in the shower","stance":"rec","why":"Talk version of a book useful for developers and knowledge workers.","quote":"it's pretty interesting stuff for developers and knowledge workers","text":"People who are into this kind of thing will probably like \"Your brain at work\" by David Rock. It covers this topic and a few more - it's pretty interesting stuff for developers and knowledge workers. There's also a tech talk here: [VIDEO] Also, get Pragmatic Thinking and Learning by Andy Hunt. Also covers these topics and helps people understand learning, creativity and how to exploit the way you work and think to, well, learn better."},{"id":2378354,"author":"espeed","month":"2011-03","story":"How to Get to Genius","stance":"rec","why":"Explains insights arising from weak associations.","quote":"explains \"insights from weak associations\" in more detail","text":"1. It doesn't say Einstein was bad at math, it says his math skills aren't the reason he is considered a genius. 2. The Google TechTalk it references explains \"insights from weak associations\" in more detail: [VIDEO] 3. If you don't have perspective, how do you know the thing you're focused on is of any importance? You can see practical examples of this everywhere: \"One of my favourite tuppences for engineers struggling to find a bug is 'breadth first not depth first'. This can be annoying for someone working through complex technical details, because it implies that the solution is actually much simpler if they only looked...I coined this saying because in software matters I find that if we force ourselves to examine the top level considerations first, before tunneling down into the detail of a particular line of inquiry, we can save enormous time\" ( [LINK] )."},{"id":10161847,"author":"CuriousSkeptic","month":"2015-09","story":"Regretting the Golden Handcuffs: Beware the Costs of Burnout","stance":"reference","why":"Presents the SCARF model for workplace threat and reward.","quote":"","text":"It seems to be a common sentiment that burnout is largely driven by how many hours a day you work. Why is that? From what I've been taught burnout is the result of continuous stress. Where stress is more or less defined as a continuous sensation of being threatened. I've seen people with perfectly normal workours become burnt out. I've even seen people working ten hours a week getting burnt out. The common factor thus not being the objectively measurable amount of work. My own formula for diagnosing a work environment is more like Risk of Burnout = perception of expectations / perceptions of means to deliver But a more useful model might be the SCARF-model presented in this tech talk [VIDEO] It seems to me that hours worked relates to issue of burnout more like lack of sleep to the risk of catching a cold. Potentially a big factor, but only incidentally."},{"id":2401610,"author":"espeed","month":"2011-04","story":"How to Get to Genius","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"UPDATE: I emailed Sebastian Seung ( [LINK] ) asking about this. This is what I said: \"BTW I liked your TED talk. My friends and I were discussing it the other day, and I made the analogy of comparing the connectome in your brain to hardware and the thoughts in your mind to software. Is that a fair analogy? David Rock wrote a book called 'Your Brain at Work' where he says that insights come from detecting weak associations (he gave a Google Tech Talk on this too -- [VIDEO] ). If the brain software/hardware analogy is right, is there a similar relationship between weak associations of thoughts and neurons that are \"distantly\" connected? In other words, are associations weak because they are indirect paths from one node – one neural connection -- to another, and would a more direct path optimize you mental ability?\" This is what Sebastian said: \"The hardware-software analogy is wrong 1) the brain is not a general purpose programmable computer. different areas are specialized to different functions 2) the connectome changes over time, unlike hardware you're right that direct vs indirect paths do matter, unlike in communication networks. but the weakness of an association might have to do with the strength of synapses, not just the length of the path. i'm addressing these questions in my book which will appear in feb 2012 if all goes well.\" So maybe a better analogy for the brain would be a network of specialized computers, like a private cloud connected to the Internet because th"}],"r":[["s86-Z-CbaHA",1],["gM95HHI4gLk",1],["L3LMbpZIKhQ",1],["aHx-XUA6f9g",1],["3Y2weLDiUWw",1],["7ZGCPap7GkY",1],["YyIQKBzIuBY",1],["qhbuKbxJsk8",1],["aP0Y1uAA-2Y",1],["kjBOesZCoqc",1],["xnez6tloNSQ",1]]},"_fZOl7C_vDI":{"wy":"Linked in threads about lobster welfare as an easy way to take in Wallace's essay.","q":{"x":"here's a video of DFW reading it out","i":19527270,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19527270,"author":"unmole","month":"2019-03","story":"The Weird World of Vegan YouTube Stars","stance":"rec","why":"Wallace reads Consider the Lobster.","quote":"here's a video of DFW reading it out","text":"> They don't have brains or central nervous systems, I would very highly recommend reading David Foster Wallace's Consider the Lobster . You can either buy the book of the same name which includes other essays or here's a PDF of the original article: [LINK] and here's a video of DFW reading it out: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16403947,"author":"sharkweek","month":"2018-02","story":"In Switzerland, it's now illegal to boil a live lobster","stance":"rec","why":"Wallace reads his lobster essay aloud.","quote":"makes them more enjoyable","text":"Love that whole series of essays in the Consider the Lobster collection - you can also listen to several of them on YouTube and I have to say Wallace’s tone when reading the essays makes them more enjoyable [VIDEO]"},{"id":25117357,"author":"abductee_hg","month":"2020-11","story":"My Priceless Summer on a Maine Lobster Boat","stance":"reference","why":"Reminder of the lobster essay.","quote":"Here's a reminder","text":"WOW that's pretty tonedeaf. remember David Foster Wallace? \"Consider the Lobster\"? Here's a reminder: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["LtFyP0qy9XU",1],["SR1jwwagtaQ",1],["a0atL1HSwi8",1],["lD9FAOPBiDk",1],["uD4izuDMUQA",1]]},"aDMjgOYOcDw":{"wy":"Occasionally recommended as an in-depth critical take on Shapiro, though mentions are sparse and partisan.","q":{"x":"you should watch this in-depth breakdown of his perspectives and ideals","i":20348440,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":24982860,"author":"weswpg","month":"2020-11","story":"Show HN: Dendron – A Hierarchical Tool for Thought","stance":"rec","why":"Thesis that Shapiro should not be taken seriously.","quote":"he's routinely wrong, his views never progress in recognition of his many MANY errors","text":"For anyone who also thinks Ben Shapiro is a \"serious thinker\", here is the thesis that \"Perhaps Ben Shapiro Shouldn't Be Taken Seriously By Anyone About Anything\": [VIDEO] In short: he's routinely wrong, his views never progress in recognition of his many MANY errors, because he feels that his views are correct regardless of what the facts are; all while smugly bleating \"Facts Don't Care About Feelings\". he's so comically unserious, he had to change the title of his own book after liberals pointed out to him that he miscounted how many arguments he was making in \"10 rules for debating leftists\": [LINK] most of the others on that list are just as bad but it's noteworthy that this laughable \"intellectual dark web\" has Ben Shapiro among those playing the victim card as if he is a repressed academic even though he openly and cynically proclaims his unwillingness to engage honestly with the opposition (\"So, the only other reason that you should ever have a conversation, or be friends with anyone on the left and, and not even be friends, is if you're in public in front of a large audience and then your goal is to humiliate them as badly as possible\") these people aren't \"intellectuals\" or even remotely serious."},{"id":20348440,"author":"deanCommie","month":"2019-07","story":"YouTube bans content “showing users how to bypass secure computer systems”","stance":"rec","why":"Hour-long critical breakdown of Ben Shapiro's views.","quote":"you should watch this in-depth breakdown of his perspectives and ideals","text":"Ben Shapiro was the most popular role model for Alexandre Bissonette, a very clear alt-right white supremacist[1]. Before you dismiss Shapiro's responsibility in stoking these ideals, you should watch this in-depth breakdown of his perspectives and ideals[2]. I know it's a lot to ask to watch an hour long youtube video, but it is thorough to dot all the i's and cross all the t's. Shapiro even stokes anti-semitic rhetoric by distinguishing between \"ethnic jews\" (The Woody Allens of the world, but more broadly liberal hollywood elite jews) and \"cultural/religious jews\" (Morally-clear good-minded folk like himself). The video covers it. This is how he reconciles his alt-right allies who are anti-semitic and white nationalist, yet use Shapiro as a shield to protect their heinous opinions. And this is before we get to any of his perspectives on arabs, which another commenter already addressed. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23480844,"author":"Rumperuu","month":"2020-06","story":"Three Simple Rules Poor Teens Should Follow to Join the Middle Class (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"Episode briefly addresses the success sequence study.","quote":"","text":"YouTube series Some More News briefly addressed this study in their episode \"Perhaps Ben Shapiro Shouldn't Be Taken Seriously By Anyone About Anything\".[0] Brookings also followed this up with a report entitled \"Following the success sequence? Success is more likely if you’re white.\"[1] Note that I don't mean to make any claims as to OP's political outlook or motives in sharing this article. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"}],"r":[["7gDKbT_l2us",1],["kh88fVP2FWQ",1]]},"bqPARIKHbN8":{"wy":"Cited as the canonical example of fake reviews and gamed rating platforms.","q":{"x":"I'd recommend watching the Vice video I found in another comment","i":16263240,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":26578814,"author":"fatfox","month":"2021-03","story":"More big investors shun Deliveroo over workers' rights","stance":"rec","why":"Fake restaurant climbs to TripAdvisor top spot via fake reviews","quote":"was very telling as well","text":"Unrelated to Deliveroo, but \"How to Become TripAdvisor’s #1 Fake Restaurant\" (2018) was very telling as well: [VIDEO] From the video description: > The Shed at Dulwich was the number one rated restaurant in London, with foodies, celebrities and bloggers trying to get a table. The main obstacle for them, however, was that it didn't exist. Over the course of 8 months VICE's Oobah Butler used an assault of fake reviews to get his 'restaurant' to the hallowed top spot on TripAdvisor."},{"id":16263240,"author":"dfee","month":"2018-01","story":"‘The Shed at Dulwich’ was London’s top-rated restaurant. It didn’t exist","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how a nonexistent restaurant hit TripAdvisor number one","quote":"I'd recommend watching the Vice video I found in another comment","text":"The tenor in this discussion so far seems to be a bit on the condescending side. Frankly, I'll pick it up for this guy. What an incredible job – and a great reminder that marketing your service is often times more important than the service itself. Maybe it's the prankster in me, but I don't feel like this is the end of the world – and it's not an indicator that the world is coming to an end. Fundamentally, people follow the rules and trust structure. That's probably why society can exist. For the many people who don't have their glasses on, this was a brief glimpse at reality. I'd recommend watching the Vice video I found in another comment: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23423798,"author":"FridgeSeal","month":"2020-06","story":"Facebook will block ads from state-controlled media outlets","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"inspiration courtesy of","text":"With a few people and some decent preparation you could probably fake most of this without too much effort. In fact, I wonder what the smallest amount of people and effort you'd need to get past this: if you can get away without having face-to-face you might be able to pull and do it with 1 person, a bunch of emails and some AI generated faces (inspiration courtesy of [VIDEO] )"},{"id":23217628,"author":"9nGQluzmnq3M","month":"2020-05","story":"Doordash and Pizza Arbitrage","stance":"reference","why":"Fake garden-shed restaurant reached number one on TripAdvisor","quote":"The fake restaurant in their garden shed, which never took customers or delivered any food","text":"> The fact that Doordash scrapes prices, and apparently doesn't verify... how does this happen? It kind of happens by default if your goal is \"growth at any cost\". The video you mention is likely this one, and it's actually even more extreme: [VIDEO] The fake restaurant in their garden shed, which never took customers or delivered any food , climbed up to #1 best restaurant in London (!) in TripAdvisor's rankings, purely on the strength of fake reviews and fake photos (artfully arranged closeups of bleach tablets etc). For kicks and video gold, they did open for their last night, serving 1-pound microwave meals from the supermarket."},{"id":21005132,"author":"ddeck","month":"2019-09","story":"My derelict pub still got TripAdvisor reviews","stance":"reference","why":"Video report on the fake Shed at Dulwich restaurant","quote":"The Shed at Dulwich was the number one rated restaurant in London, with foodies, celebrities and bloggers trying to get a table.","text":">IIRC there's also video reportage about it to be found... The VICE piece is here: How to Become TripAdvisor’s #1 Fake Restaurant The Shed at Dulwich was the number one rated restaurant in London, with foodies, celebrities and bloggers trying to get a table. The main obstacle for them, however, was that it didn't exist. Over the course of 8 months VICE's Oobah Butler used an assault of fake reviews to get his 'restaurant' to the hallowed top spot on TripAdvisor. [VIDEO]"}]},"koTf7u0v41o":{"wy":"Recommended for treating benchmarking and profiling scientifically rather than by myth, with innovative tools.","q":{"x":"covers the basics of how to attempt benchmarking with actual computer science instead of myth and voodoo rituals","i":27483997,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29756521,"author":"captaincrowbar","month":"2022-01","story":"Finding your home in game graphics programming","stance":"rec","why":"Innovative profiling tools for finding bottlenecks","quote":"He introduces some innovative new profiling tools for this sort of analysis.","text":"The term you're looking for might be \"abstraction penalty\". For tools that can identify where the bottlenecks are, I recommend watching Emery Berger's talk \"Performance Matters\" from CppCon 2020 (either of the two links below should work). He introduces some innovative new profiling tools for this sort of analysis. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":27483997,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2021-06","story":"Beware of Tight Feedback Loops (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Benchmarking with computer science rather than myth","quote":"covers the basics of how to attempt benchmarking with actual computer science instead of myth and voodoo rituals","text":"This guy has had a few talks at CppCon over the years, and covers the basics of how to attempt benchmarking with actual computer science instead of myth and voodoo rituals: [VIDEO] In practice, it's not critical to go that far. In my experience, huge performance wins are trivial to benchmark with just a modicum of effort, and tiny performance wins are typically not worth any effort at all. At FAANG scale they might be, but I don't work at a FAANG, so not my problem! :) Simple methods I use and can recommend are: - Ideally, collect data from production, where the change behaves exactly as it would in production because it is production! - Interleave tests on the same machine. E.g.: A, B, A, B, A, B, etc... - Run tests on every machine you can get your hands on over an extended period and draw a histogram or a scatter chart of some sort. You don't have to do this at 100% load, it's actually better to mix this in with normal workloads. This gives you an idea of the noise versus signal, platform variance, variation due to time of day, etc... - Run tests after a cold boot to get a sense of worst-case performance instead of idealised and often unrealistic \"hot loop\" performance. - Run tests with different input sizes and draw a graph. Try find the explanation for every feature of the graph. - Be aware of every cache layer in the system. Don't forget about the potentially huge RAM or SSD cache of the storage subsystem. Typical SAN arrays now have 256 GB or more. The previous graph a"},{"id":26736402,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2021-04","story":"Surprisingly Slow","stance":"reference","why":"Linked in a bottleneck discussion","quote":"","text":"To give an oversimplified answer, that depends on how things are connected and synchronized. You know how bottlenecks are often explained using funnels in real life? Imagine two funnels, one directly connecting to another one. In that case, the effective rate of flow is determined by the funnel which has the smallest tip - the fact that the larger one lets through fluid quicker is irrelevant, as it is slowed down by the smaller one (we're obviously ignoring things like funnels potentially overflowing here, this is a simplified scenario). So then the bottleneck is easy to pin-point, but also it immediately becomes obvious that widening one funnel beyond the size of the other has no effect. Now let's change the scenario: I have a bucket of water that I tip into the first funnel, going into a second bucket. Once the second bucket is full I tip the second bucket into the second funnel, going into a third bucket. In this case both funnels have an impact on the time it takes to fill the second bucket, but the smaller one sill has the bigger impact. And then of course there is the parallel scenario, you can probably see how that plays out. In our software environment is a complicated mix of all of these, and it can be really hard to pin-point what is the most significant effect. The \"buckets\" and \"funnels\" translate to all kinds of things - CPU an I/O are the most obvious ones, but there's more to it than that. Also there's tons of side-effects that make this entire picture a lot mo"}],"r":[["kIoZDUd5DKw",1],["GC-0tCy4P1U",1],["O133ppiVnWY",1],["kPR8h4-qZdk",1],["SFv8Wm2HdNM",1],["-zRN7XLCRhc",1],["SDtAh9IwG-I",1],["LIb3L4vKZ7U",1],["_Cp-BGQfpHQ",1],["ig_qpNfXHIU",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["GuigptwlVHo",1],["0rR9IaXH1M0",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["MiUHjLxm3V0",1],["99dKzubvpKE",1],["6wBrOV2FJM8",1],["5IUj1EZwpJY",1]]},"o4MwTvtyrUQ":{"wy":"Cited as evidence that ordinary users often don't understand basic technology like browsers, as a reminder for designers and developers.","q":{"x":"there is this great video of Google employees asking people in Times Square what the difference is between a browser and a search engine","i":11305229,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11305229,"author":"michaelbuckbee","month":"2016-03","story":"Why Tesla Motors Finally Acquired Tesla.com","stance":"rec","why":"Times Square interviews on the difference between a browser and a search engine","quote":"there is this great video of Google employees asking people in Times Square what the difference is between a browser and a search engine","text":"It's old now (and I wonder how much it's changed) - but there is this great video of Google employees asking people in Times Square what the difference is between a browser and a search engine: [VIDEO] Most people conflate the two. IIRC the video was used internally as a tool to press the notion that they _had_ to build a browser or risk losing their search engine dominance."},{"id":7724333,"author":"wturner","month":"2014-05","story":"Ask HN: How do you deal with inferiority complex?","stance":"rec","why":"Google asking random people on the street to explain a browser","quote":"Watch it if you haven't. It will lend perspective.","text":"Your strengths and weaknesses have curves, troughs, peaks and spaghetti complexity. Personally I completely accept that I'm pretty mediocre as a \"developer\", but honestly I don't really care because I don't personally consider myself a developer, I just look at myself as someone who simply likes to explore, learn and play with web based technologies. This way I don't get sucked into the pigeon hole psychology of the professional world. If someone else wants to look at me in that light that's fine , but I keep an invisible boundary between myself and that kind of thing. I also like to occasionally and inadvertently ask inane questions at stackoverflow which get down voted at machine gun intervals. It's good for the 'soul'. I have a mentor that has been programming since the 70's , I once lived in a place where an extremely smart Belgian software engineer was staying who helped me while programming, and I currently met a really smart engineer near where I live who has been gradually throwing me suggestions coupled with some pseudo mentorship. All of these people are very smart,very empathetic and willing to share. Being around people like that in my view is absolutely needed for your sanity. You've probably seen this video. It's the one where Google attempts to explain what a web browser is to random people on the street. Watch it if you haven't. It will lend perspective. [VIDEO] Ok that's all."},{"id":44533976,"author":"lproven","month":"2025-07","story":"Zorin OS","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Here is some proof, in case you don't believe me.","text":"Good for you. I've been doing tech support since 1988. I've seen many many PCs with hundreds of apps, some maybe heading into thousands, sometimes with custom hackery to get different versions running in parallel and stuff. Yes this is a thing. It is common. And the typical user does not know what an \"app\" is, or what OS they are using. I've lost count of the number of people that told me their computer was running Word or Office (not Windows), or who think they access the Web via Google because they don't know what a web browser is. Here is some proof, in case you don't believe me. [VIDEO] If you can name the 20 apps you use the most often, you are the elite of the elite, the top 1% of 1% of computer users. Normal people are not like that. They don't know what they use or what it's called or what OS they run or what an \"OS\" is, and they outnumber us by approximately a million to one."},{"id":24885518,"author":"deadmutex","month":"2020-10","story":"Acer’s First Chromebook with Snapdragon 7c Is Ultraportable, 4G LTE Equipped","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> browser instead of integrating a browser into an OS. I don't think the average user cares, as long as they can do what they need to do. See [VIDEO]"},{"id":13075846,"author":"hackuser","month":"2016-12","story":"My fight against CDN libraries","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Ask them what a web browser is.","text":"> This is a conscious decision that the user has made to use Chrome and send their URLs to Google Not to pick on this one point, but I want to add that I doubt many users are really conscious of it. People on HN are in a bubble; go to the local mall and ask people what they know. Ask them what a web browser is. [VIDEO] That's why people in the IT field have a responsibility to provide safe products to the public. It's like someone on Wall Street saying that a typical person is making a 'conscious decision' to accept all the complex risks of a sophisticated financial instrument, or that I'm making a 'conscious decision' to accept all the risks of an airplane when I get on it - I have little idea how the plane works; it's up to the manufacturer, airline and goverment to ensure it's safe."},{"id":12594138,"author":"type0","month":"2016-09","story":"UnGoogled Chromium: Chromium with enhanced privacy, control and transparency","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I realize these technologies are not currently doing anything for you. I sympathize. But we need them in browsers now so that we can use them in 10 years. 6 years ago many people didn't even knew what web browsers were (What is a Browser? [VIDEO] )"},{"id":10957214,"author":"jbigelow76","month":"2016-01","story":"Larry Page, Google Founder, Is Still Innovator in Chief","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"everyday people trying to explain what a browser is","text":"Doesnt sound like a very knowledgeable headmaster. Larry invented PageRank and did a respectable implementation of it before he had other engineers take it over. Here is a video, [VIDEO] , of everyday people trying to explain what a browser is, a browser, the most important piece of (general use) software developed since the word processor or spreadsheet. Try asking the general public what PageRank is, much less who invented it, and be prepared for a montage of silent deer-in-headlights responses with the occasional stammering about librarians for good measure :)"},{"id":5805818,"author":"graue","month":"2013-06","story":"A Personal API","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"For perspective, take a look at this video","text":"Look, I'm not saying don't go for this idea. I'd love to see you try it. My doubts shouldn't get in your way. Code it up. As for why it would be difficult to use and insecure? I'm guessing most of the people you hang out with are probably geeks. For perspective, take a look at this video: [VIDEO] Imagine each one of those people in the video has a personal data store that contains their complete medical history, right alongside all their Google searches, Amazon purchases and tweets. How secure of a password do you think they picked? How hard would it be to trick them with a phishing attack? How much do you think they even know how to do with it in the first place?"},{"id":5381183,"author":"julien","month":"2013-03","story":"Moving RSS forward","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Before telling me that poeple just have to click the \"feed icon\", watch this","text":"Generally, it's hard to convince nerds that something trivial to them is not trivial to non nerds. In what reality is it obvious that \"to 'get more of something' I have to copy a url (what's a url?) from a browser tab to another web application?\" Before telling me that poeple just have to click the \"feed icon\", watch this: [VIDEO] and ask yourself if you think people will go from: \"I want more of this site\" to \"Why don't I click that funky little orange icon in the address bar?\""},{"id":4033393,"author":"philjones88","month":"2012-05","story":"Setting Google Analytics to not use cookies","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Most people don't understand the difference between \"Google\" and a \"Browser\" [1]","text":"Most people don't understand the difference between \"Google\" and a \"Browser\" [1], cynically, I don't hold much hope people will care what browser cookies are. I've already had to explain to my parents that cookies aren't evil, the sites they visit (BBC/Google etc) are mainstream and fine. As an EU resident and webmaster of several sites for myself and clients. I see little benefit to my visitors other than causing me a lot of grief over trying to follow guidelines and hoping my implementation doesn't break them. I was speaking to a client about it today, he hasn't seen anything about it and I doubt many small business owners have seen (or cared) much about it. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":3795613,"author":"SoftwareMaven","month":"2012-04","story":"Google found guilty of misleading advertising with Adwords","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Google showed pretty well there are a lot of people who are confused, to say the least[1].","text":"I think you are far over-estimating the size of the set of people who know the difference between ads and organic results on Google. I would also bet the percentage of people who can differentiate sites by URL is is very small. If the link text says \"Honda\", many (most?) will believe they are going to a Honda sanctioned site. I would love to see real user research on this topic. Google showed pretty well there are a lot of people who are confused, to say the least[1]. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":2962672,"author":"nhebb","month":"2011-09","story":"How Microsoft misunderstands the concept of user research (& how Apple doesn’t)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"go watch the Google on-the-street video asking people what a browser is","text":"I didn't take that as an anti-intellectualism slight. People who write tech pieces on the web, be it on blogs or tech publications, are more computer savvy than the the majority of computer users. If you don't believe this, go watch the Google on-the-street video asking people what a browser is ( [VIDEO] ). I was one of those power users who hated the ribbon when it was introduced in Office 2007. But my wife and kids - they loved it. [Edit - since I can't reply to thread below (why is that?)] I sell an Excel add-in, so I have a different perspective than most power users. The ribbon is a pain to program compared to the old command bars, making me dislike it at first. I've learned to appreciate it, though, because it's a lot easier to convey the add-in's features via the ribbon than it is using 16x16 icons on a command bar."},{"id":2678048,"author":"XLcommerce","month":"2011-06","story":"Chrome dev on WebGL security and Microsoft bullshit","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I think most people can't tell the difference between a browser and a search engine ;)","text":"\"people often disable the plugins and are conscious about plugins being an attack surface\" actually I think most people can't tell the difference between a browser and a search engine ;) [VIDEO]"},{"id":2443988,"author":"Groxx","month":"2011-04","story":"Please Stop Saying 'Cloud'","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Ask people what a browser is, something they've had for years and use for hours every day","text":"They do tend to have a vague understanding that it involves the internet, though. Which is all it really implies, with an emphasis on off-site storage and/or computation when spoken by a geek. This is a brand-new term, relatively speaking. Ask people what a browser is, something they've had for years and use for hours every day: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2011322,"author":"mgunes","month":"2010-12","story":"Paypal's logo designed to look like a recommendation?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the people in the \"What is a browser?\" video [1] are not stupid; they're busy","text":"Right; and the people in the \"What is a browser?\" video [1] are not stupid; they're busy , have adapted very well to the extreme compartmentalization of 21st century Westernized life and knowledge, and are largely uninterested in what we know as computing. To invoke a central theme of \"Paradox of the Active User\" [2]: they're all experts in one field or another, and would make many a hacker here look like someone who doesn't know what something as supposedly generic as a \"browser\" is in their respective fields. That's far from \"stupid\". You could replace a good 80% of the instances of \"stupid\" with \"busy\" in design discussions, and you'd end up with better discussions. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":1684331,"author":"Timothee","month":"2010-09","story":"How do i go back to regular Google?","stance":"reference","why":"Google video showing people confuse browsers and search engines","quote":"focusing on the difference between a browser and a search engine.","text":"As an example, a video made by Google that illustrates that point: [VIDEO] , focusing on the difference between a browser and a search engine."},{"id":1062412,"author":"jeromec","month":"2010-01","story":"Ask HN: Is a .net domain good enough?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"many people still don't know \"what is a browser?","text":"I agree with pg. If your site is targeted to mainstream, as opposed to geeks or early adopters, you would have to fight really hard to go against default mind-share people will have for dot com. Remember, many people still don't know \"what is a browser?\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":695036,"author":"Gibbon","month":"2009-07","story":"Fake Steve Jobs on Chrome OS","stance":"reference","why":"Street interviews showing most people don't know what a browser is","quote":"Most people don't have the slighest clue what a browser is, nevermind an opinion about them.","text":"Behold.. What is a Browser? [VIDEO] Most people don't have the slighest clue what a browser is, nevermind an opinion about them."},{"id":714477,"author":"cesare","month":"2009-07","story":"Apps 'to be as big as internet' ","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Most users don't even know what a browser is","text":"Good analysis. When I first read Vic Gundotra (engineering v.p. at Google) statements he convinced me. But I'm not so sure anymore that browser delivered apps will win against custom applications. Most users don't even know what a browser is ( [VIDEO] - btw, this has been made by a Google intern) and custom apps already provide a better experience for them. The proof comes from Google itself: Google Earth, Google Maps, Google Mobile etc. Browsers could soon become obsolete for the vast majority of users - who will probably completely ditch PCs in favor or smartphones. Edit: I'm not saying that I like it. I'm just saying it could happen."},{"id":718671,"author":"hwijaya","month":"2009-07","story":"Forget Cuil, check this out: one guy and a bunch of pcs","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"That's what makes technology industry interesting. Things are quite unpredictable and like it or not, none of us knows exactly how things will pan out. Google's strength is obviously in brand penetration when it comes to search engine. After all, most people outside us hardly even know about \"Google Chrome\" after making such a huge splash all over the place. ( [VIDEO] ). So, i wouldn't expect them to type in www.cuil.com or WolframAlpha without something is changing in the landscape that alter user behavior."}],"r":[["yvFKh_3evC8",1],["ooOL4T-BAg0",1],["2AecAXinars",1],["fwCz8Ris79g",1],["U_X5uR7VC4M",1],["ewsGmhAjjjI",1],["HmL2l-bcuUQ",1],["OHPkdMGI6D4",1]]},"th3KE_H27bs":{"wy":"It is cited in inequality and capitalism threads as a billionaire's critique of neoliberal market assumptions.","q":{"x":"The only way we can change things on a larger scale is to change the incentives, to change the overarching goals.","i":33418122,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":35241034,"author":"actually_a_dog","month":"2023-03","story":"South Korea has the lowest fertility rate, with lessons for us all","stance":"rec","why":"Capitalist billionaire critiques neoliberal market myths.","quote":"he comes this >< close to advocating socialism.","text":"Almost everybody is dancing around it here, but the real lesson here for us all is that capitalism, or, at least the way we're doing capitalism today, is unsustainable. You simply can't have the kind of sustained transfer of wealth from lower and middle income people to the top 1% that we've had over the past 50 years and claim what you're doing is a sustainable system. I recently saw this video: [VIDEO] The speaker, Nick Hanauer, has a net worth somewhere around a billion dollars. In his talk, he explicitly says he's a capitalist, capitalism is good, etc. But, if you ignore all that and look at the rest of what he says, he comes this >< close to advocating socialism. According to him (and I agree), the lessons we should be learning here are fivefold: 1. Markets aren't anywhere near as efficient in reality as they are in theory. That leads to some counterintuitive things like when Seattle raised their minimum wage, it didn't lead to unemployment because the price of labor went up; it led to people who work in restaurants being able to afford to eat in restaurants, which is good for the economy. Oh, and unemployment actually fell in the region while this was happening. 2. A big pillar of the neoliberal myth is that \"...the price of something is always equal to its value.\" He directly goes on to tie this to the case of one person who makes $50k per year and another who makes $50M per year. The second person's work isn't 1000x more valuable than the first. The person getting pai"},{"id":33418122,"author":"pixelfarmer","month":"2022-11","story":"A Man Who Broke Capitalism – Did Jack Welch Destroy Corporate America?","stance":"rec","why":"Argues incentives must change to fix capitalism.","quote":"The only way we can change things on a larger scale is to change the incentives, to change the overarching goals.","text":"[LINK] (The Corporation) I'm soon leaving a company after having seen it grown from a mere startup to what it is today. It had a goal at the beginning, had to fight an entire industry to get an idea across which is accepted broadly nowadays. But there is also a strong inertia towards making these non-goal \"goals\" more and more important, which in turn define more and more how this company operates inside and what comes out of it in the end. It requires strong and really good leadership to see this problem in the first place and then prevent such a construct from sliding down that slippery slope. The problem with stuff like socialism, communism, but also capitalism is simple: Humans. You create rules, you have conceptions of how something could work. You add humans, and things go sideways. Even if you aware of the issue, there is potentially no way to create something stable, it requires humans to stop other humans from being dicks, as sad as it sounds. It only needs a few dicks to break pretty much everything into pieces. Worse, more often than not, there was not even real ill-intent in the first place, like with the company I've written about above. The only way we can change things on a larger scale is to change the incentives, to change the overarching goals. Check [VIDEO] (TED talk Nick Hanauer)"},{"id":35938297,"author":"pixelfarmer","month":"2023-05","story":"New Study Finds a High Minimum Wage Creates Jobs","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Ask an economist about WHY economy exists in the first place and you can put a bet on the fact that many of them get it wrong.","text":"[VIDEO] (Nick Hanauer TED talk) Ask an economist about WHY economy exists in the first place and you can put a bet on the fact that many of them get it wrong. How can anything building on that be right then? Exactly."},{"id":21491966,"author":"js2","month":"2019-11","story":"One-Percenters Close to Surpassing Wealth of U.S. Middle Class","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The dirty secret of capitalism -- and a new way forward | Nick Hanauer","text":"Some links on wealth inequality that I've read/watched recently: - Income Inequality and the Taste for Revolution [LINK] (Study based on global survey data and GINI coefficients.) - Robert Reich: Everything You Need to Know About the New Economy [LINK] - The dirty secret of capitalism -- and a new way forward | Nick Hanauer [VIDEO] - Why Free College Could Increase Inequality [LINK]"}]},"vPnehDhGa14":{"wy":"HN cites it as evidence of Microsoft's backward compatibility and of upgrade engineering done well.","q":{"x":"It's a sight to behold.","i":3112073,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":5469401,"author":"alexsb92","month":"2013-03","story":"Building Photoshop 1.0","stance":"rec","why":"Gave appreciation for the work and complexity in Windows","quote":"I remember gaining quite an appreciation for all the work put in over time in Windows","text":"> I often think about how incredibly inefficient modern software is given that we've had roughly the same level of productivity software ability (with varying levels of usability) for a few decades now. I distinctly remember my NT4 system of the late 90's, and how it was roughly as productive as my current machine, and I wonder just how much cruft is inside modern operating systems and applications. As a fellow young person (probably even younger than you), I remember watching this video [VIDEO] two years ago when it came out and I remember gaining quite an appreciation for all the work put in over time in Windows and the complexity that it probably brings."},{"id":3112073,"author":"slmbrhrt","month":"2011-10","story":"Imagine if your house had Windows","stance":"rec","why":"Upgrading through every version of Windows","quote":"It's a sight to behold.","text":"Reminds of a video I saw of a guy upgrading through every version of Windows. It's a sight to behold. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28401789,"author":"kirb","month":"2021-09","story":"Please don’t theme our apps","stance":"reference","why":"Custom theme gets overridden at each Windows upgrade","quote":"the theme he configured back in one of the first Windows versions just disappears and gets overridden with XP’s defaults","text":"Adding onto your point, I’m reminded of the video “Chain of Fools”, where a machine is upgraded from Windows 1.0 all the way through to Windows 8. Once he reaches XP, the theme he configured back in one of the first Windows versions just disappears and gets overridden with XP’s defaults. Then it happens again and again for every version since then, despite the guy changing the theme back to his custom one before each upgrade. [VIDEO] So the phenomenon really started with Microsoft themselves. Sure, the more muted dialog backgrounds and vibrant foreground elements introduced since XP look far better than the Win95-esque greys, but I’m sure people who created their own themes (probably due to disability like color blindness) like their colors better than Microsoft’s."},{"id":27216078,"author":"stordoff","month":"2021-05","story":"Internet Explorer 11 (IE11) to be retired on June 15, 2022","stance":"reference","why":"Windows 1 to 7 upgrade; programs keep working","quote":"programs continue to work","text":"Windows 1 to Windows 7: [VIDEO] (set colour schemes disappear at XP, but programs continue to work) Follow up - Windows 1 to Windows 10: [LINK]"},{"id":17114928,"author":"lmkg","month":"2018-05","story":"The weirdest bug I’ve found in a compiler: MSVC 2017","stance":"reference","why":"Doom II survives 11 in-place OS upgrades over 24 years","quote":"The following is a video of someone installing Doom II on DOS, upgrading the operating system in-place 11 times","text":"The following is a video of someone installing Doom II on DOS, upgrading the operating system in-place 11 times, and running Doom on Windows 7 (around 24 years of upgrades). [VIDEO]"},{"id":15139819,"author":"b4ux1t3","month":"2017-08","story":"An Argument for Why Windows Will Go Open Source","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Windows 3.1 programs surviving upgrades to Windows 7","quote":"You can literally update a machine from Windows 3.1 to windows 10","text":"No, it really doesn't. Windows has had backwards compatibility as a huge priority for a long time, often to their detriment. You can literally update a machine from Windows 3.1 to windows 10, and programs written for Windows 3.1 will still work.[1] [1]: [VIDEO] EDIT: TO be clear, this is a video of upgrading to Windows 7, and there is a direct upgrade path to 10 from 7. Also, as per another user's reply, this doesn't hold true for 64-bit. However, that doesn't change the fact that Wine doesn't and really can't run win32 better than win32 does."},{"id":9193816,"author":"rogerbinns","month":"2015-03","story":"Intel blames $1B revenue hit on Windows XP's stubborn grip","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Microsoft knew how to do upgrades in the past","quote":"They know how to do it, and did it in the past.","text":"They neglected to mention just how hard it is to \"upgrade\" from XP. If you want to install Windows 7 or 8 then it will only do a clean install. You have to figure out all the apps you have installed, install media, receipts/keys/vendor site logins, do the \"upgrade\", and then play system administrator for a long time. Approximately no one has backups, so all that needs to be sorted out too, as well as doing a restoration. Then new UI has to be learned. Nothing is where it was. Various apps probably had to be upgraded too, and they'll have new UI to learn too. Then they have to figure out system administration - how do you do updates, make the funky printer work, deal with anti-virus. I didn't even mention figuring out that whole 32 versus 64 bits thing either. The time, effort and money involved, especially for small businesses/organisations and individuals is daunting, not to mention a distraction from what their actual business is. By far the easiest thing for them is to do nothing. Microsoft really screwed up by not making a good upgrade process. They know how to do it, and did it in the past. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6568741,"author":"candl","month":"2013-10","story":"Ubuntu 13.10 (Saucy Salamander)","stance":"reference","why":"Continuous upgrade from MS-DOS to Windows 7 proves it's possible","quote":"Here's a video of a continuous upgrade from ms-dos through every windows version all up to windows 7","text":"Here's a video of a continuous upgrade from ms-dos through every windows version all up to windows 7 (a timespan of ~20 years) that proves it's possible. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6308772,"author":"RKearney","month":"2013-09","story":"ReactOS: Rebuilding Microsoft Windows from the ground up, fully open-source","stance":"reference","why":"Someone upgrading through nearly every Windows version","quote":"You can watch the YouTube Video[0] of someone upgrading through every Windows version","text":"I don't know about you, but I have much, much better luck running executables under Windows than say Linux or OS X. Microsoft puts a ton of work into keeping backwards compatibility. You can watch the YouTube Video[0] of someone upgrading through every Windows version (well, most?). OS X users have to figure out if they need that Intel or PPC binary, or maybe just the larger universal one and call it a day. Then there's not being able to run any of their older PPC-only apps once Apple removed Rosetta from OS X. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":4576894,"author":"cooldeal","month":"2012-09","story":"Developer interest in Windows 8 is stagnant, new line of worry for Microsoft","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence of Microsoft's backward compatibility","quote":"Upgrading from Windows 1.0 to Windows 7.","text":"Funny you say that, because Microsoft's software problems are often attributed to bending over backwards to be backward compatible while other vendors like Apple quickly kills compatibility with almost ever release, with even point releases. Take this game bundled with Windows 95. ftp://ftp.microsoft.com/deskapps/games/public/AAS/Hover.exe It runs even on Windows 8. Upgrading from Windows 1.0 to Windows 7. [VIDEO] As I said in another comment, I still see some ASP and Visual Basic usage even after all these years, those applications are still supported in Windows 8 and Windows Server 2012. Microsoft stopped making new versions because it wanted to move on to better technology. Want to take a poll on HN about what developers think of Visual Basic and Classic ASP? A case of damned if they do, damned if they don't."},{"id":4108938,"author":"ayi","month":"2012-06","story":"PHP: the quiet powerhouse","stance":"reference","why":"Windows 1.0 upgrade to 7 used as analogy for PHP","quote":"even doom from 1993 works!","text":"PHP's problem is it's soul. I will make an analogy about this. My analogy will be local, but i will explain it so you can understand. PHP is my university, Gazi University. It first established in 1926 as a Teacher Training Institute. Nobody knew that there will be a medical school or faculty of fine arts or even a faculty of dentistry. 1926 was the third of our newly established Republic and we needed teachers. So, Teacher Training Institute was born. Our main campus is very small. Law School or dentistry school is not in the campus. Because, as i said, nobody knew we need them in 1926. Ruby, C# and many other languages are METU: Middle East Technical University. METU was designed as a university. So, they got a gigantic land in Ankara (capital city of Turkey). They build all their building in there. Back to the PHP: PHP was born to solve one problem. Just one problem. When they got another problem, they solved it and added the function or implementation in the language. PHP is not designed at desk, it was not \"feature-complete\" at version 1.0. It's still getting more mature as version numbers increase. For example we get \"traits\" in 5.4. PHP is also Windows too (both Microsoft and PHP in a HN comment :) The most un-loved things in this community). Remember the video which a guy installs Windows 1.0 and successfully upgrade it to every version of windows with all his data and programs up to Windows 7? even doom from 1993 works! ( [VIDEO] ) PHP is like Windows. I know the \"pa"},{"id":3200726,"author":"trafficlight","month":"2011-11","story":"How Can Anyone Still Hate Bill Gates?","stance":"reference","why":"Windows 1.0 upgraded to 7 with programs still running","quote":"An amazing amount of programs still ran by the time he got to 7.","text":"Reminded me of the video where a guy did a fresh install of Windows 1.0 and upgraded all the way to Windows 7. An amazing amount of programs still ran by the time he got to 7. [VIDEO]"}]},"4S9sDyooxf4":{"wy":"HN commenters link it as the direct source on climate sensitivity and on the claim that past models ran too cold.","q":{"x":"See this great video from Sabine Hossenfelder here","i":40399417,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40399417,"author":"awesomeMilou","month":"2024-05","story":"OpenAI departures: Why can’t former employees talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great video on warming trajectory","quote":"See this great video from Sabine Hossenfelder here","text":"See this great video from Sabine Hossenfelder here: [VIDEO] We have surpassed the 1.5°C goal and are on track towards 3.5°C to 5°C. This accelerates the climate change timeline so that we'll see effects postulated for the end of the century in about ~20 years."},{"id":40896512,"author":"csydas","month":"2024-07","story":"Another Climate Truth Bomb","stance":"reference","why":"The article merely wraps this video","quote":"The article is purely a link to this YouTube video","text":"Maybe the link should be changed directly to the video it discusses? The article is purely a link to this YouTube video: [VIDEO] The article itself as I read it doesn't really contribute or expand the concepts in the video and instead is a call to action (not defined) as it assumes you've watched the video, basically I'm just not confident the article contributes anything to the subject its presenting."},{"id":39618857,"author":"pillefitz","month":"2024-03","story":"Ice-free summers in Arctic possible within next decade, scientists say","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on possibly inaccurate climate models","quote":"On the topic of possible inaccurate climate models used in IPCC reports","text":"We have good reasons to be concerned when looking at the sudden rise in ocean temperatures seen since early 2023 that closely track the worst-case scenarios. Given the stakes, I prefer to be overly cautious (aka. alarmist). On the topic of possible inaccurate climate models used in IPCC reports [VIDEO]"},{"id":39732352,"author":"graphe","month":"2024-03","story":"Why Is the Sea So Hot?","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses climate models being too cold due to clouds","quote":"I watched Sabina Hossenfelder talk about historical predictive models being wrong and too cold","text":"I watched Sabina Hossenfelder talk about historical predictive models being wrong and too cold on their predictions due to the way they looked at the clouds and the scattering of the light (quantifying the amount of water vapor in the clouds). [VIDEO] Climate scientists are disagreeing over the hot climate models. This is not a debate on the human effects, simply the way to interpret the data."},{"id":39164636,"author":"jjackson5324","month":"2024-01","story":"I used to not worry about climate change. Now I do [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Points to the actual video the story meant","quote":"I think this is the actual video","text":"Lmfao I think this is the actual video - [VIDEO] Somehow OP must've linked to the RDCWorld video by mistake and somehow this still got to the top of HN? I guess HN just upvotes without clicking the link?"},{"id":39165790,"author":"Aachen","month":"2024-01","story":"I used to not worry about climate change. Now I do [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Direct link to content with proper transcripts","quote":"Direct link to the content","text":"That blog post is a thin wrapper around another video btw. Direct link to the content: [VIDEO] (For those who prefer to read: it has subtitles/transcript available for various languages — proper ones, not auto-generated!)"}]},"BE77h7dmoQU":{"wy":"Cited for the history of why containers and Kubernetes took off, though one thread critiqued the documentary itself.","q":{"x":"Fascinating documentary on Kubernetes for those who have 50 minutes.","i":45573000,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45573000,"author":"rshnotsecure","month":"2025-10","story":"Why did containers happen?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Background on the container wars and Kubernetes' open-source decision","quote":"Fascinating documentary on Kubernetes for those who have 50 minutes.","text":"Fascinating documentary on Kubernetes for those who have 50 minutes. Gives more background to the \"Container Wars\". The filmmakers also have documentaries on the history of Python, Argo, etc. Some highlights: - How far behind Kubernetes was at the time of launch. Docker Swarm was significantly more simple to use, and Apache Mesos scheduler could already handle 10,000 nodes (and was being used by Netflix). - RedHat's early contributions were key, despite having the semi-competing project of OpenShift. - The decision to Open Source K8S came down to one meeting brief meeting at Google. Many of the senior engineers attended remotely from Seattle, not bothering to fly out because they thought their request to go OS was going to get shutdown. - Brief part at the end where Kelsey Hightower talks about what he thinks might come after Kubernetes. He mentions, and I thought this was very interesting ... Serverless making a return. It really seemed like Serverless would be \"the thing\" in 2016-2017 but containers were too powerful. Maybe now with KNative or some future fusing of Container Orchestration + K8S? [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":46056346,"author":"belter","month":"2025-11","story":"Nvidia says its GPUs are a 'generation ahead' of Google's AI chips","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Google Search Unusable nowadays and monopoly that should be broken.... > Google’s open-source MapReduce paper that shaped the big data industry. Experienced ETL practitioners laughed at that paper, knowing that was how DataStage and other Grid ETL platforms always worked. Imagine...spliting the data across nodes, working on it in parallel and then bringing it together...Oh my if was not for Google nobody would have thought about it... > Then came containerization, then Google’s Kubernetes. A platform full of security bugs, that they created because they lost the cloud wars: Kubernetes: The Documentary [PART 1] - [VIDEO] [LINK] They forgot about multitenancy, and 11 years after, they still dont have it.... > entire cloud computing industry seems to be based on Kubernetes, and our entire team develops on top of it. Your team needs to go out more...."},{"id":31581823,"author":"belter","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: If Kubernetes is the solution, why are there so many DevOps jobs?","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary shows Kubernetes arose from Google losing the cloud wars","quote":"Kubernetes only exists, because Google lost the Cloud Wars, and this was their Hail Mary pass.","text":"Anybody who watched \"Kubernetes: The Documentary\" knows the answer: [VIDEO] Kubernetes only exists, because Google lost the Cloud Wars, and this was their Hail Mary pass."},{"id":30414363,"author":"fguerraz","month":"2022-02","story":"Kubernetes Documentary: A Critical Review","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"They've altered the quality of the audio and video to make it look old.","text":"Slightly biased? This is the way they present the extract of the dotScale conference in this K8s documentary: [VIDEO] And the source media: [LINK] They've altered the quality of the audio and video to make it look old. They even cropped it to 4:3! This is preposterous."}]},"-JlxuQ7tPgQ":{"wy":"HN points to it in discussions of AI copyright enforcement and badly specified AI goals, and recommends it alongside his other fiction.","q":{"x":"I'd like to also recommend his other work of speculative fiction: The Artificial Intelligence That Deleted A Century","i":35915914,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35915914,"author":"SkeuomorphicBee","month":"2023-05","story":"Welcome to Life: the singularity, ruined by lawyers by Tom Scott","stance":"rec","why":"Fiction about copyright maximalism and dangers of poorly specified AI goals.","quote":"I'd like to also recommend his other work of speculative fiction: The Artificial Intelligence That Deleted A Century","text":"I'd like to also recommend his other work of speculative fiction: The Artificial Intelligence That Deleted A Century [VIDEO] It is a great intersection of copyright maximalism and the dangers of making general purpose AIs without proper care in developing their goal function."},{"id":44322879,"author":"perching_aix","month":"2025-06","story":"Extracting memorized pieces of books from open-weight language models","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in passing about copyright infringement.","quote":"","text":"You remind me to all the shitty times in literature class where I had to rote memorize countless works from a given author (poet), think 40, then take a test identifying which poem each of the given quotes was from. The little WinForms app I wrote to practice for these tests was one of the first programs I've ever written. I guess in that sense it's also a fond memory. I miss WinForms. Good thing they were public (?) works, wouldn't wanna get sued [0] for possibly being a two legged copyright infringement. Or should I say having been, since naturally I immediately erased all of these works from my mind just days after these tests, even without any legal impetus. Edit: thinking about it a bit more, you also remind me to our midterm tests from the same class. We had to produce multiple page long essays on the spot, analyzing a select work... from memory. Bonus points for being able to quote from it, of course. Needless to say, not many original thoughts were featured in those essays, not in mine, not in others' - the explicit expectation was that you'd peruse the current and older textbooks to \"learn (memorize) the analysis\" from, and then you'd \"write about it in your own words\", but still using technical terms. They were pretty much just tests in jargon use, composition, and memorization, which is definitely a choice of all time for a class on literature. But I think it draws an interesting perspective. At no point did we ever have to actually demonstrate a capability in lite"},{"id":43911590,"author":"beeslol","month":"2025-05","story":"Creativity came to pass","stance":"reference","why":"Opening line parallels the story being discussed.","quote":"This is a YouTube video from a future. Not the future, just a future.","text":"The opening line > This is written of a future. Not the future, but a future – one of many possibilities. reminded me of the opening line from Tom Scott's The Artificial Intelligence That Deleted A Century [VIDEO] > This is a YouTube video from a future. Not the future, just a future. Not sure if intentional or if you're both referencing something else, fun parallel nonetheless."},{"id":43968880,"author":"NooneAtAll3","month":"2025-05","story":"What if humanity forgot how to make CPUs?","stance":"reference","why":"Sci-fi story about humanity forgetting Beatles songs.","quote":"humanity forgot all beatles songs","text":"I don't think it's that bizarre we already had a sci-fi story, where humanity forgot all beatles songs [VIDEO]"},{"id":43019512,"author":"throw83288","month":"2025-02","story":"LLMs can teach themselves to better predict the future","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for an AI rewiring human interest in AI research.","quote":"","text":"I think the problem is that from our human scale, mass-killings is the \"best\" method to eliminate the possibility of another organism causing harm for us. Hypothetically, if there was a more optimal (i.e less costly) method like just introducing some cheap catch-all combined vaccination/antiviral into their feed, we would just do that. We don't have things like that, but that could easily be a consequence of man's limited research capacity, something that an ASI would not necessarily be throttled by. From an ASI's perspective, there might be many methods that are both less brutal and more optimal to fix the \"humans creating a competitor\" problem. Not that they would be aligned (Think halting human AI research by rewiring our brains to just not be interested in it [0]), but at least not deadly. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36376402,"author":"jerf","month":"2023-06","story":"GPT-4 apparently can't recite the litany against fear from Dune","stance":"reference","why":"Fiction illustrating risks of AI-based copyright enforcement.","quote":"You really need an AI-level model to prevent the infringement","text":"Here you see the results of trying to filter a very advanced model with a much simpler one. It can't work, the filter model is too crude. Of course the real goal isn't to prevent copyright infringement but to prevent copyright infringement lawsuits , which is at least a good start. You really need an AI-level model to prevent the infringement... although that can pose it's own risks... [VIDEO]"},{"id":34330191,"author":"jerf","month":"2023-01","story":"Microsoft in talks to acquire a 49% stake in ChatGPT owner OpenAI","stance":"reference","why":"Related to AI behavior discussion.","quote":"","text":"I think if you're going to judge the tech, it's important to understand what it is. Basically, in both cases, you are bringing in the context to those questions. You know what those questions \"mean\" in the collective brain-space, and why they are sensitive. You did not neutrally pick those questions; you know those questions are already loaded. Unsurprisingly, when you bring those pre-loaded questions to an AI whose job it is to continue the text in the way that was the most likely way to see it continued in the training text, you get exactly that. Prompt the bare, unfiltered AI with antisemitic text, get antisemitic continuations. That's unfortunate, but in terms of what was promised and what was delivered, not surprising. You asked for it. You got it. What I've never seen ChatGPT or any other GPT technology do is suddenly veer off into an antisemitic rant, unprompted . (At least, any technology that I know was GPT based. I'm not sure about Tay. Training the chat bot on its own input turns out to be a bad idea.) You can ask it to write a STTNG script where Picard discovers he has a son he never knew, but you won't see it suddenly spin off into an anti-Jewish rant. You didn't bring it. You didn't ask for it. So you didn't get it. An AI that spontaneously dropped racism and antisemitism and all that sort of thing into a significant portion of the responses could be a problem. But an AI that simply gives you what you want is something different, in my opinion. Worries about wha"},{"id":27915108,"author":"RachelF","month":"2021-07","story":"MS Windows Defender and DeCSS","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I wonder when Anti-Virus will start deleting files that express opinions they don't like. Reminds me of the famous Earworm [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hB6eY73sLV0",1]]},"8Q5Nur642BU":{"wy":"Cited in street-safety and self-driving threads as a picture of chaotic, shared streets before cars dominated.","q":{"x":"It's interesting to watch this video of San Francisco's Market street from 1906","i":21338993,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21338993,"author":"Johnny555","month":"2019-10","story":"Drivers are killing pedestrians at highest rate in almost 30 years","stance":"rec","why":"1906 Market Street: slow cars, streetcars, horses and pedestrians coexisting chaotically.","quote":"It's interesting to watch this video of San Francisco's Market street from 1906","text":"It's interesting to watch this video of San Francisco's Market street from 1906: [VIDEO] Cars travel at walking pace, and cars, streetcars, horses, etc all coexist -- pedestrians seem to cross the street at will, not following any signals or staying in crosswalks. I think the reason this chaotic system works (aside from the lower speeds) is because street users expect the chaos, no one relies on a traffic signal to tell them when it's safe to cross, they know that they have to pay attention or they'll be run over (or will run over someone else)."},{"id":39622373,"author":"snakeyjake","month":"2024-03","story":"The built environment and the determination of fault in urban pedestrian crashes","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">Before the advent of the automobile, users of city streets were diverse and included children at play and pedestrians at large. This is not true and there are numerous counter-examples. City streets have always been dangerous for pedestrians and notable illustrations of this are the separate gates at city entrances for vehicles (pulled by beasts of burden of course) and pedestrians and the construction of sidewalks and crosswalks one example of which I've seen in person is the civil engineering of Pompeii where pedestrians were segregated from road traffic over two thousand years ago. [LINK] Another example is the Pont Neuf bridge in Paris. Completed in the early 1600s it was built with wide sidewalks to keep pedestrians out of the road. [LINK] Oldest photograph of London: [LINK] Amsterdam, 1891: [LINK] What do you see? Sidewalks. In various paintings and engravings of city scenes from the 1600s and 1700s a common theme emerges: every area that could afford them built sidewalks and pedestrians were segregated from road traffic. Raucous examples of people teeming in the streets in similar works seem to be trying to depict scenes of anarchy or poor slums. Another is the fact that numerous Roman emperors banned carriages from city streets during daylight (both for safety and noise pollution reasons) with varying degrees of success. That was much easier when you had an army of slaves to carry goods into the city. One glaring expression of the dangers of pre-automobile urban stre"},{"id":17790974,"author":"charlesism","month":"2018-08","story":"Self-Driving Car Makers Prepare to Blame “Jaywalkers”","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Let's hope we don't end up with an even more > pedestrian hostile world than what we have already. Unless there's a cultural sea change, you can bet on it worsening. If you take presentism out of the equation, what we have now - ie: most roads off limits to humans due to giant metal death machines - is insane. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16777699,"author":"Fnoord","month":"2018-04","story":"How I learned to live again, after running a man over","stance":"reference","why":"1906 San Francisco street footage with pedestrians mixing freely with traffic.","quote":"(in San Francisco, 1906, right before the earthquake)","text":"Reminds me of the video \"A Trip Down Market Street\" (in San Francisco, 1906, right before the earthquake) [1]. This comment on the video says it all: \"The good old days when kids use to play in traffic lol﻿\". All the jaywalking. Look how relaxed people are. No smartphones. Its distasteful however how some people seem to break traffic laws. Especially speeding. A road at an elementary school nearby which I regularly pass enforces 30 km/h. People drive at least 55 if not 60 (the default is 50). The gains from such behaviour are minuscule, if any. Even the gains of driving 150 instead of 130 for 2 hrs are minor. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"AF2VyqSApjA":{"wy":"HN points to it as a demonstration of local, controllable AI painting and a way to learn the workflow.","q":{"x":"I'd recommend Krita with diffusion if you want to get into this.","i":40446545,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40446545,"author":"vunderba","month":"2024-05","story":"Microsoft Paint's new AI image generator builds on your brushstrokes","stance":"rec","why":"Demo of local, configurable AI painting in Krita","quote":"I'd recommend Krita with diffusion if you want to get into this.","text":"It's a little bit more work to set up, but I'd recommend Krita with diffusion if you want to get into this. Runs locally, swap exact checkpoints you want to use, supports poseable controlnets, and it doesn't phone home to mommy Microsoft. [LINK] Video demonstration [VIDEO]"},{"id":42878807,"author":"haunter","month":"2025-01","story":"Krita 5.2.9 Released","stance":"reference","why":"Original demo videos used to learn the workflow","quote":"I actually just learned from the original demonstration videos.","text":"Yes exactly. There are some written guides now but I actually just learned from the original demonstration videos. Copied what happened there and started tinkering around Most of the times if something didn't work for me that was mostly because my lack of knowledge about Krita [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38462773,"author":"orbital-decay","month":"2023-11","story":"Stable Diffusion:Real time prompting with SDXL Turbo and ComfyUI running locally","stance":"reference","why":"Example of realtime generation enabling live painting","quote":"Realtime generation in particular makes 3D rendering in the viewport and live painting possible.","text":"> Should we applaud for more kitsch in the world? Of course. It gets nauseating fast and makes worthwhile messages stand out more, causing the balance swinging to the other side. You're creating something for someone, what's the point if they don't value your carefully crafted message and want kitsch instead? > Are we expected to be impressed by speed or quality? Realtime generation in particular makes 3D rendering in the viewport and live painting possible. [1] This kind of tooling makes a lot of difference. What you make with it, either kitsch or something worthwhile, is up to you. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["QVXfcIb3OKo",1],["pTbSMKGQ_rU",1]]},"BJEHsvW2J6M":{"wy":"HN bakers point to it as a good video for learning the sourdough process.","q":{"x":"This is a good video for learning the process","i":43849141,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43849141,"author":"misiti3780","month":"2025-04","story":"My sourdough starter has twins","stance":"rec","why":"Good video for learning the sourdough process.","quote":"This is a good video for learning the process","text":"As a sourdough baker, I wouldnt say it's easy to make good bread consistently but it's not super hard to learn. As long as your starter is in a decent place and you take your time with the stretch and folds, you will arrive with something edible. I split my time between the north near canada and miami and fermentation works a lot differently even indoors in the winter in Buffalo vs Miami in the summer :) This is a good video for learning the process: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22885498,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2020-04","story":"Organizing a Group Buy of Flour","stance":"reference","why":"One of the YouTube sources used for learning sourdough.","quote":"Most of this based of Youtube videos","text":"I've become a sourdough bread baker over the past three weeks, going from producing constructionnmaterials (dense bricks) to some of the best bread I've ever had. Realized yeaast wasn't to be had for love or mone: [LINK] Starter recipe: [LINK] Took about 6 days to get going. 3rd loaf was a winner. Feedback from household and neighbours is strongly positive as well. Flour sourcing has been spotty, but even that has upsides: testing out spelt and einkorn, along with whole wheat, AP, and (blech) bleached flour. Loaves, rolls, pizza, crumpets, and more. Most of this based of Youtube videos: [VIDEO] [LINK] Dutch oven, food scale, bench knife (I use a plastering spatula) highly recommended."},{"id":22645066,"author":"01100011","month":"2020-03","story":"Quarantine Bread","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch oven baking approach for sourdough.","quote":"the dutch oven approach seems nice","text":"Ha! Quite timely. I have a 4 day old sourdough starter that is starting to bubble nicely. If you have the patience and the flour, skip the yeast packet and start a sourdough starter. I've made sourdough in the past, and generally swear by using a spray bottle filled with water to gelatinize the crust during baking, but the dutch oven approach seems nice. I ran across it earlier tonight: [VIDEO]"}]},"GA8z7f7a2Pk":{"wy":"Used as the classic example of a lone first follower turning one person's action into a movement, in threads about bystanders and group behavior.","q":{"x":"I think the most amazing/hilarious example I've seen of one person starting something by making the first move","i":20419283,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20419283,"author":"anonymousDan","month":"2019-07","story":"A new study uses camera footage to track the frequency of bystander intervention","stance":"rec","why":"Amazing, hilarious example of one person starting a movement","quote":"I think the most amazing/hilarious example I've seen of one person starting something by making the first move","text":"I think the most amazing/hilarious example I've seen of one person starting something by making the first move is this video: [VIDEO] :)"},{"id":33521926,"author":"wizardforhire","month":"2022-11","story":"Undetectable very-low frequency sound increases dancing at a live concert","stance":"reference","why":"Commenter mixed that show; anecdote about delays and dancing","quote":"I was mixing this show.","text":"I can confirm this. It’s also nice to see a new paper on this. I’ve been using this knowledge professionally for the last 24 years as a front of house engineer. Anecdotal story time: You’ve may have seen this video, or some variation of or maybe even the TED talk inspired by it… [VIDEO] …what few people know and most don’t because I have not shared this publicly (in media at least) is that I was mixing this show. We we’re on in the middle of the day and a few bands had been on before us. By this point in my career I had already been to the gorge several times and the hill has always been a problem. On this particular day during the already frantic pace of a festival changeover with no sound check, the system tech is talking up the new delay stacks they beefed up for this year. This was great news and were geeking out and he’s showing me where they’re at on the console which is of course buried under some convoluted series of navigation and button presses to get to, given the atrocious UI design of digital electronics. Now generally given these circumstances, convention holds that the delays are set to some arbitrary value and the guest engineer generally need not concern themselves as the system techs will be monitoring them. To my horror were getting to the dancy part of our short set and I’m doing my due diligence and looking back at the hill and wondering whats up, why isn’t anyone dancing!?! The delays were not turned up! None of the previous engineers that day had though"},{"id":818205,"author":"pkrumins","month":"2009-09","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"Similar event where a guy started a dance party","quote":"This is similar to an event where a guy started a dance party","text":"Seems staged to me. It seems unnatural how those 4 people started dancing at exactly the same time at the first wave. Ps. This is similar to an event where a guy started a dance party [VIDEO]"},{"id":818232,"author":"pkrumins","month":"2009-09","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"Similar event where a guy started a dance party","quote":"This is similar to an event where a guy started a dance party","text":"Seems staged to me. It seems unnatural how those 4 people started dancing at exactly the same time and at the same beat at the first wave. Ps. This is similar to an event where a guy started a dance party [VIDEO] Damn it: I don't understand why this submission went [dead] two times."}]},"Myq_Hc_39aI":{"wy":"HN points to it when discussing DMT research and reports of contact with entity-like beings.","m":[{"id":36208464,"author":"grimgrin","month":"2023-06","story":"What Hallucinogens Will Make You See","stance":"rec","why":"Recent conversation with panelists on DMTx research, with out-there parts.","quote":"look no further than the recent conversation with the panelist","text":"If that’s a thing you wanna try to stay informed about, look no further than the recent conversation with the panelist: [VIDEO] spoiler: we haven’t done that yet, so far it’s really kinda like “ok well it’s not appearing to be unhealthy, there’s no noticeable tolerance, what’s next”. Also the linked video is going to have parts way out there for some folks here. Some have much different beliefs, there’s a few mentions of Syncronicities But this is a newer rabbit hole for me and one thing is certain: I want to know why DMT has our brain building an entirely different world model, almost repeatable, completely throwing ours away. What’s the evolutionary reason even? There’s some theories but none anyone feels great about."},{"id":40570935,"author":"s4mw1se","month":"2024-06","story":"Psychedelics are challenging the standard of randomized controlled trials","stance":"reference","why":"Research and patient panel interview hosted after publication.","quote":"This is from research and patient panel interview that was hosted after the publication.","text":"Rave communities? This is from research and patient panel interview that was hosted after the publication. Minus the hoffman stuff. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36899668,"author":"androidbishop","month":"2023-07","story":"Former intel officer says 'non-human biologics’ found at alleged UFO crash sites","stance":"reference","why":"DMTx trial researchers report extended contact with beings.","quote":"They report extended contact with hyper-dimensional aliens/beings, on numerous sessions.","text":"I find myself torn between my STEM-educated skeptic mind and my numerous DMT-induced visits to hyper-reality and communication with unfathomably advanced hyper-dimensional beings mind. I find it difficult to accept that these experiences were just the products of my own subconscious or imagination, as they were far beyond the capabilities of my wildest dreams and just felt too real, too alien, and too consistent. There has been some recent breakthroughs by Andrew Gallimore that have allowed researchers to extend the DMT experience indefinitely via the DMTx project, and the first trials have been completed by a group of serious researchers, all PhDs. They report extended contact with hyper-dimensional aliens/beings, on numerous sessions. [VIDEO] Now, I find it difficult to believe there isn't a plethora of intelligent life out there in the universe. There are just far too many star systems. I also find it difficult to believe that any have to come to visit. There is no evidence to support it, and think about it: the time, energy, and technology required to travel the vast expanse of space to reach Earth are unimaginably huge. And if they had that kind of technology, it would seem unlikely that they would routinely crash once they got here. Furthermore, we've only been producing radio waves for a short period of time, nowhere near long enough to signal a remote civilization that we're even here (I'm not sure our radio waves would even be detectable at those distances). And why "}],"r":[["LRhbcDzbGSU",1],["aB8zcAttP1E",1]]},"RwRZtZQoLtQ":{"wy":"HN cites it for Chamath's claim that venture-backed growth resembles a Ponzi scheme, especially when startups spend heavily on big-tech advertising.","q":{"x":"I really liked this video featuring Chamath Palihapitiya[1].","i":21660582,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21660582,"author":"wczekalski","month":"2019-11","story":"After WeWork, SoftBank’s Startup Bookkeeping Draws Scrutiny","stance":"rec","why":"Chamath rant about VC being a Ponzi scheme","quote":"I really liked this video featuring Chamath Palihapitiya[1].","text":"I really liked this video featuring Chamath Palihapitiya[1]. It's essentially a rant about most of VC being a ponzi scheme: [VIDEO] [1] (from Wikipedia) Chamath Palihapitiya an is a venture capitalist and the founder and CEO of Social Capital. Palihapitiya was an early senior executive at Facebook, joining the company in 2007 and leaving in 2011. He is a minority stakeholder and board member of the Golden State Warriors."},{"id":30999299,"author":"nowherebeen","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: How did Fast get a $580mm valuation?","stance":"reference","why":"Chamath's view that VC resembles a Ponzi scheme","quote":"Chamath Palihapitiya certainly disagrees with you","text":"Not really. Just because they exit at a later stage doesn't mean its not a ponzi scheme. Chamath Palihapitiya certainly disagrees with you ( [VIDEO] ). Skyhigh valuations with no product market fit and no path to revenue certainly sounds like one to me. Many startups burn money to acquire customers, but churn is high because they don't have PM fit. It becomes an endless cycle of raising to acquire more customers. Once the money stops, someone will eventually be holding the bag."},{"id":21745893,"author":"1290cc","month":"2019-12","story":"E-scooter company goes bust after spending big on Facebook ads","stance":"reference","why":"Chamath on startups spending VC money on Facebook, Google, Amazon","quote":"Many tech startups spend 0.40c of every VC dollar with FB/Google/Amazon.","text":"Chamath Palihapitiya talked about this problem in an interview from 2018. Many tech startups spend 0.40c of every VC dollar with FB/Google/Amazon. Here is the original interview: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18225666,"author":"takanori","month":"2018-10","story":"SoftBank Gives Startups Billions of Reasons to Hold Off IPOs","stance":"reference","why":"Chamath describing VC as a Ponzi scheme","quote":"does the chamath described Ponzi scheme stop causing the whole thing Venture backed growth story to unravel?","text":"It would seem that softbanks backing by the Saudi brutal regime should give Silicon Valley idealistic founders moment of pause before accepting their investment. WeWork founders banned meat from their office meals can they really accept to be owned by this fund? Once the SoftBank money stops flowing does the chamath described Ponzi scheme stop causing the whole thing Venture backed growth story to unravel? [LINK] [VIDEO]"}]},"RyMdOT8YJgY":{"wy":"Recommended as an excellent short Ignite talk from a geek comedian.","q":{"x":"You might enjoy this excellent video of his talk at Ignite a few years back","i":5712564,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":5712564,"author":"swombat","month":"2013-05","story":"Lies on the London Underground [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent Ignite talk by a geek comedian.","quote":"You might enjoy this excellent video of his talk at Ignite a few years back","text":"You seem unreasonably grumpy... take a breath, chill out. I enjoyed the video and upvoted it - as did many others, clearly. It's of interest to hackers. Tom Scott is also \"one of us\" - a geek comedian. Probably one of the reasons why others have been upvoting this. You might enjoy this excellent video of his talk at Ignite a few years back: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4508848,"author":"theorique","month":"2012-09","story":"What Happens When You Taunt 4Chan: Story of Lacey Vicich ","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"These are some interesting points. You're absolutely right that the anonymous and distributed nature of 4chan means that the \"/b/\" entity can react to perceived slights (trolling, mockery) in a radically disproportionate way. If the individual provoker annoys /b/ with \"strength\" 1, and (e.g.) 10 000 /b/ denizens are each sufficiently annoyed to push back at the provoker with equal \"strength\" 1, that means that the reaction to the individual is profoundly out of proportion to the original attack. By a factor of thousands. It's like burning someone's house down with their family inside because they dented your bumper without leaving a note. But as you point out, few individuals were all that nasty - the culpability is distributed across a large, anonymous mob, with a few critical nodes, such as those persons who leaked the individual's contact information, or made prank phone calls to her work place. The crowd / mob dynamics gives massive strength to the anonymous group that is absolutely unmatched by the individual. Mob dynamics in physical space are understood well enough, and police have had various means of dealing with large groups of disorganized people in town squares and so forth. This is not the case for internet-distributed mobs, which are still relatively new. The process is like leaderless, decentralized, crowdsourcing of cruelty (cf. \"Anonymous: because none of us is as cruel as all of us\"). And of course, the greater the crowd, the greater the possibility of someo"},{"id":23778777,"author":"jcahill","month":"2020-07","story":"Yoloface-500k: ultra-light real-time face detection model, 500kb","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"asymmetrically imaginative doomsaying that appeals to a vivid vignette","text":"I'm similarly unclear on what sort of response you expect. There weren't many comments when I replied. My criticism was tailored to a fairly specific phenomenon: asymmetrically imaginative doomsaying that appeals to a vivid vignette / sketch of an adjacent possible future featuring some hyperbolically elaborated extension of trending tech, like Flash Mob Gone Wrong[1] and Slaughterbots[2]. HN flagging and points are irrelevant to me. ____________________ [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"}]},"S94ETUiMZwQ":{"wy":"HN uses it in AI and consciousness debates as a memorable take on why general intelligence is hard to define.","q":{"x":"westworld (yes the tv-series) has the best explanation of why general intelligence is a hard problem to solve.","i":20272155,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20272155,"author":"ecmascript","month":"2019-06","story":"The BS-Industrial Complex of Phony A.I.","stance":"rec","why":"Westworld on why general intelligence is hard","quote":"westworld (yes the tv-series) has the best explanation of why general intelligence is a hard problem to solve.","text":"I think honestly think westworld (yes the tv-series) has the best explanation of why general intelligence is a hard problem to solve. They mention consciousness but I think the same apply to intelligence in general. Humans in my mind aren't different from say a program you write except that we have a lot more input and possible outputs depending on a much larger variant of external variables. If we could build machines that have eyesight just as we do, muscles just as we do etc I'm sure we could reverse-engineer the human being. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35447780,"author":"gowings97","month":"2023-04","story":"How can we be afraid of AGI if we can't define consciousness?","stance":"reference","why":"Scene arguing consciousness does not exist","quote":"The Westworld TV show from a few years ago gave an interesting perspective","text":"The Westworld TV show from a few years ago gave an interesting perspective - [VIDEO] Bernard: “Life like, but not alive? Pain only exists in the mind. It’s always imagined. So what’s the difference between my pain and yours, between you and me?” Ford: “This was the very question that consumed Arnold. Filled him with guilt. Eventually drove him mad. The answer always seemed obvious to me. There is no threshold that makes us greater than the sum of our parts. No inflection point in which we become fully alive. We can’t define consciousness because consciousness does not exist. Humans fancy that there’s something special about the way we perceive the world, and yet we live in loops as tight and as closed as the hosts do. Seldom questioning our choices, content, for the most part, to be told what to do next. No my friend, you’re not missing anything at all.\""},{"id":15524002,"author":"firasd","month":"2017-10","story":"Q&A: Douglas Hofstadter on why AI is far from intelligent","stance":"reference","why":"Ford's monologue on the self as fiction","quote":"The self is a kind of fiction, for hosts and humans alike.","text":"\"The self is a kind of fiction, for hosts and humans alike. It's a story we tell ourselves... there is no threshold that makes us greater than the sum of our parts, no inflection point at which we become fully alive.\" -- Dr. Ford, Westworld (video contains spoilers) [VIDEO]"}]},"VaMjhwFE1Zw":{"wy":"HN mentions it as evidence that the Wim Hof method can be learned and as the documentary that led some to try breathing and cold exposure, while others dispute the claims.","q":{"x":"tried the breathing: and cold showers and they've been helping a lot with inflammation!","i":29788616,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":29788616,"author":"justarandombobo","month":"2022-01","story":"Search engines and SEO spam","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary that led commenter to breathing and cold showers for inflammation.","quote":"I watched this documentary and then the original vice documentary","text":"Hey man, try some breathing exercises and cold showers if you can - I watched this documentary [LINK] and then the original vice documentary: [VIDEO] and then tried the breathing: [LINK] and cold showers and they've been helping a lot with inflammation! There are tons of benefits of cold showers, so if you can start doing that, that might already help a lot! Here's a bunch of results you could check out: [LINK]"},{"id":24801093,"author":"jasonshen","month":"2020-10","story":"Wim Hof breathing technique can modulate immune response to pathogens (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Linked alongside a remark about cult-leader vibes.","quote":"","text":"Wim Hof \"Iceman\" definitely gives me \"cult leader\" vibes but cool to see this research get published in a very reputable scientific journal. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18624370,"author":"sytelus","month":"2018-12","story":"A study on the willful regulation of autonomic function during cold exposure","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the Wim Hof method can be learned.","quote":"it can be learned (see, )","text":"TLDR; Win Hoff Method (WHM) has been one of the most mysterious things that has allowed people to climb snowy mountains almost naked in middle of the winter. The core interesting aspect of it has been that it can be learned (see, [VIDEO] ) and therefore the assumption that this is because of some extreme genetics has been overruled. The current research paper has following findings: 1. WHM allows to control pain center of brain at will shutting down the pain response. 2. WHM increases response in brain areas that keep sustained focus in face of adverse conditions. 3. WHM does not cause increased metabolism to keep body warm. 4. The mechanism of keeping body warm seems to be forceful respiration that increases response of fight-or-flee autonomous nervous system increasing glucose consumption in muscles around the lungs."},{"id":18627720,"author":"andrelaszlo","month":"2018-12","story":"A study on the willful regulation of autonomic function during cold exposure","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Hof's physique suggesting more than insulation.","quote":"Check out this video","text":"He looks pretty lean, there's clearly something else beside being well-insulated going on :) Check out this video [VIDEO]"},{"id":41686554,"author":"tarkin2","month":"2024-09","story":"The world knows Wim Hof as \"The Iceman\", his family suffered domestic violence","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I noticed that first Vice video Wim had a strange interaction with his son","text":"Can someone clean up the title? \"'The Iceman' Wim Hof's family suffered domestic voilence.\" I noticed that first Vice video Wim had a strange interaction with his son, normalising strange interactions, jokingly calling his son the biggest bastard he'd known. Seemed odd to me. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11619401,"author":"blubb-fish","month":"2016-05","story":"What cold showers and exercise have in common (2014)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"most claims are rather blown out of proportion","text":"I'm exclusively showering cold now for more than a year. If I recall correctly one of the reasons was a VICE documentary on Wim Hof - that quite impressed me - though after a while I came to the conclusion that he is mostly well marketed (by his son - who he refers to as the biggest asshole he knows - [VIDEO] ) and that most claims are rather blown out of proportion. For example his scientific research on suppressing immune response to an infection is sold as a medical revolution while it has absolutely no practical value - if you think about it - quite the opposite as an immune response is usually something rather useful. Anyway - I think it is worth it for several obvious reasons: - it's definitely very refreshing! - it helps waking up a lot - it is a simple way to train discipline - it's less stressful to skin than hot water - it's nice to know that I am not depending on hot water"}],"r":[["tybOi4hjZFQ",1]]},"ZPbyDSvGasw":{"wy":"Cited in threads on ECC memory, CPU reliability, and bitsquatting as evidence that bit flips cause real DNS misdirection.","q":{"x":"you might find the DEFCON talk on single bit domain errors relevant","i":6872607,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":6872607,"author":"aimhb","month":"2013-12","story":"CPU reliability – Linus Torvalds (2007)","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on single-bit domain errors from hot data centers.","quote":"you might find the DEFCON talk on single bit domain errors relevant","text":"It was posted here on HN a few days ago, but you might find the DEFCON talk on single bit domain errors relevant: [VIDEO] In summary, some data centers are run hotter than recommended, which leads to a lot of mostly ignored domain resolution errors, which leads to a security risk."},{"id":12261758,"author":"voltagex_","month":"2016-08","story":"Flip Feng Shui: A new cross-VM exploitation vector","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on bitsquatting and client behaviour.","quote":"","text":">We have registered all possible domains that are one bit flip away from ubuntu.com and debian.org. We would like to hand these domains over to the correct authority. Please get in touch if you think you are one. Aha! [LINK] is a talk about \"bitsquatting\" along the same lines. I'm sure I've seen a newer talk about it too Edit: [VIDEO] is the other video, which also looks at the behaviour of various clients"},{"id":10606738,"author":"vox_mollis","month":"2015-11","story":"To ECC or Not to ECC","stance":"reference","why":"Talk showing DNS misfires from non-ECC bit flips.","quote":"","text":"This cannot possibly be right. There was a DC21 talk regarding DNS request misfires due to bit flips in non-ECC DRAM, and the researcher was able to collect a surprisingly large number of requests on the basis of this. Edit: found it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6864108,"author":"vezzy-fnord","month":"2013-12","story":"Is Unicode Safe?","stance":"reference","why":"DEFCON talk on bit-flip domain misspellings.","quote":"","text":"The homoglyph attack is a very old and often abused technique. A related, though not identical one, is bit flipping where a single character is swapped in a domain name and you prey on those who make misspellings. It turns out, however, that it isn't even necessary for someone to make a blatant error like that... [VIDEO] [DEFCON 21: DNS May Be Hazardous To Your Health]"}]},"kHW-ayt_Urk":{"wy":"HN points to it for Hamilton's datacenter cost breakdown and as a primary source for claims about power versus hardware costs.","q":{"x":"You should check out his Velocity talk for more (about 5 minutes in)","i":1902298,"y":"2010"},"m":[{"id":1902298,"author":"bbgm","month":"2010-11","story":"Datacenter Power Efficiency","stance":"rec","why":"Hamilton's datacenter cost breakdown talk with the spreadsheet assumptions behind the numbers","quote":"You should check out his Velocity talk for more (about 5 minutes in)","text":"That split of where the money goes comes directly from calculations and a spreadsheet that James Hamilton has developed. You should check out his Velocity talk for more (about 5 minutes in) [VIDEO] The numbers on the slides modify the assumptions on the spreadsheet at [LINK]"},{"id":38459533,"author":"kaliszad","month":"2023-11","story":"AWS unveils Graviton4 & Trainium2","stance":"reference","why":"AWS engineer James Hamilton stating the claim himself","quote":"And you can watch him say it too","text":"According to James Hamilton who is a distinguished engineer at AWS no: [LINK] and [LINK] And you can watch him say it too: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27112916,"author":"kaliszad","month":"2021-05","story":"AMD server CPUs capture highest market share gains from Intel in 15 years","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as supporting source on datacenter power versus hardware costs","quote":"","text":"The comment in general may be true but I don't see electricity costs being \"much greater than CPU purchase costs over the lifespan\" at least not for a data center operator. The AWS M4 EC2 instance uses \"2.3 GHz Intel Xeon® E5-2686 v4 (Broadwell) processors or 2.4 GHz Intel Xeon® E5-2676 v3 (Haswell) processors\" [0] so about 4-6 years old and may approach 7 years. A comparable processor Intel® Xeon® Processor E5-2670 v3 costs about $ 1600 [1] in bulk. Average US home electricity price is ~ 0.13 $/month [2], this would probably be much lower for a data center operator. The mentioned processors probably consume ~ 150 W at full load, which will probably not be the case most of the time. Anyway, let's see: CPU cost over its life time may be on the order of 1600 USD, which is quite cheap as today's top processors cost usually between 4000 and 8000 USD. [3] The electricity cost would probably be: 24 hours * 365 days * 7 years * 0.13 $/kWh * 0.15 kW ~ 1200 USD which seems about right. We can observe the electricity cost per CPU to be significantly lower than the purchase price of a rather cheap processor even assuming very generous CPU load and electricity price, while assuming rather low CPU cost. This of course doesn't include the mainboard, RAM, NIC, power conversion, cooling and other hardware that is needed to operate a modern data center. On the other hand, the cost of this hardware wasn't included in the calculation either. Of course, I am not the only one to do the calculatio"},{"id":1935045,"author":"mrkurt","month":"2010-11","story":"Within 3 days another power outage at Linode (Fremont)","stance":"reference","why":"Hamilton keynote focused on datacenter power and cooling costs","quote":"about datacenter innovations focused primarily on power and cooling","text":"I may be missing something, but that post doesn't address the expense of power, it talks about the relative consumption of networking equipment compared to the rest of the facility. Power is expensive, and James Hamilton generally spends lots of time talking about it, his Velocity keynote ( [VIDEO] ) about datacenter innovations focused primarily on power and cooling (heat being a biproduct of power consumption)."}]},"rAZavOcEnLg":{"wy":"Brought up as a charming example of analog and liquid computers simulating a national economy.","q":{"x":"There's a short youtube documentary on its history and operation.","i":31581176,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31581176,"author":"pseudolus","month":"2022-06","story":"Physicists are building neural networks out of vibrations, voltages and lasers","stance":"rec","why":"Short documentary on MONIAC history and operation.","quote":"There's a short youtube documentary on its history and operation.","text":"I believe one of the earliest applications incorporating this line of thought was MONIAC, the Monetary National Income Analogue Computer, which used water levels to model the economy [0]. There's a short youtube documentary on its history and operation. [1] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [VIDEO] (shows operation of MONIAC)"},{"id":33483859,"author":"robocat","month":"2022-11","story":"Quantum winter is coming","stance":"reference","why":"Operating MONIAC captures essence despite poor video quality.","quote":"with a fairly naff video of it operating but captures the essence","text":"Here’s an analogue computer called the MONIAC from ~1949 that calculates monetary flow in an economy: [LINK] with a fairly naff video of it operating but captures the essence: [VIDEO] I like the COMPAQ branding added to it!"},{"id":31787613,"author":"Nition","month":"2022-06","story":"Show HN: A central bank simulator game with a realistic economic model","stance":"reference","why":"MONIAC simulates the economy with water flow.","quote":"Not a real computer exactly - it simulates the entire system with water flow.","text":"This is exactly why MONIAC[0][1] was created in 1949. Not a real computer exactly - it simulates the entire system with water flow. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30484702,"author":"copperwater69","month":"2022-02","story":"A brief history of liquid computers (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Video of the MONIAC liquid computer modeling the UK economy.","quote":"There was a neat liquid computer called the MONIAC used to model the national economic processes of the UK.","text":"There was a neat liquid computer called the MONIAC used to model the national economic processes of the UK. Here's a video: [VIDEO] Edit: Oh it was mentioned in the paper!"},{"id":23343548,"author":"basicplus2","month":"2020-05","story":"The Vintage Beauty of Soviet Control Rooms (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the MONIAC hydraulic economic computer.","quote":"its always worth mentioning the MONIAC.. (Monetary National Income Analogue Computer)","text":"And of course its always worth mentioning the MONIAC.. (Monetary National Income Analogue Computer) also known as the Phillips Hydraulic Computer [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["NUqytjlHNIM",1],["48sCx-wBs34",1]]},"tYytVzbPky8":{"wy":"Cited in glitch-art threads as the non-programmatic way to datamosh by deleting keyframes.","q":{"x":"Check out this how-to for the non-Ruby way to do it","i":1736406,"y":"2010"},"m":[{"id":1736406,"author":"jgv","month":"2010-09","story":"AviGlitch - A Ruby library to destroy your AVI files.","stance":"rec","why":"How-to for datamoshing by removing keyframes.","quote":"Check out this how-to for the non-Ruby way to do it","text":"Because it's beautiful. The effect is known as datamoshing and has become popular in some contemporary video art. It got popular from a Chairlift music video and is achieved by removing keyframes from compressed videos. Check out this how-to for the non-Ruby way to do it: [VIDEO] Here's the Chairlift video: [LINK] And here's some work (from Takeshi Murata) that I think directly influenced the datamoshing effect becoming popular: [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":19172765,"author":"RickS","month":"2019-02","story":"A 13-year-old girl reportedly makes over $1,000 a day creating ASMR videos","stance":"reference","why":"One of several glitch art links.","quote":"","text":"Thank you very much! I'll try and see if I can get my hands on a projector again. Generative art != glitch art, but these might be of interest to some people as well, since there was thread about a week ago on PNG glitching, and I went down a rabbithole saving links. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10223835,"author":"nosuchthing","month":"2015-09","story":"The Art of PNG Glitch","stance":"reference","why":"How to datamosh using ffmpeg.","quote":"Keyframes are removed which leaves only information from the previous frame to be updated by motion frames.","text":"Whoa, cool. Reminds me a bit of a video codec technique to exaggerate the motion frames and compression ala \"datamoshing\". Keyframes are removed which leaves only information from the previous frame to be updated by motion frames. Than update/motion frames are reiterated sequentially which creates an effect that just further saturates the previous frame and looks like a fluid melting effect. in use: [LINK] how to via ffpmeg: [VIDEO]"}]},"tf1VA5jqmRo":{"wy":"HN points to it when discussing litter and civic neglect in India, as an example of anonymous citizen-led cleanup.","q":{"x":"the ugly Indian explains a bit about exactly this issue","i":17799484,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17799484,"author":"herbst","month":"2018-08","story":"The Billionaires and The Guru: How an Indian Family Burned Through $2B","stance":"rec","why":"Explains litter issue and volunteer cleanup in India","quote":"the ugly Indian explains a bit about exactly this issue","text":"It's a little bit unrelated but the ugly Indian explains a bit about exactly this issue Indians seem to have, in a interesting Ted talk. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43331386,"author":"thatloststudent","month":"2025-03","story":"Extreme poverty in India has dropped to negligible levels","stance":"reference","why":"Anonymous citizen group cleaning up public spaces","quote":"a ragtag group of anonymous citizens","text":"\"The Ugly Indian\" [1] (a ragtag group of anonymous citizens) usually does that in India. In reality, though, these kinds of issues are only really seen as a minor inconvenience at most. Anecdotally, most middle-class people in here frown upon chewing tobacco, so I assume the government thinks that as long as they keep it relatively clean, nobody would think of dirtying it with their paan stains. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":8572381,"author":"restalis","month":"2014-11","story":"Why is India so filthy?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The message quoted above calls for a kind of civic action","text":"\"What's the message? If you see a problem, you will go fix it, nobody stops you, you can actually make a change, don't waste your time complaining.\"¹ When it's about public space, isn't it actually your place to demand such changes from the the public institutions that actually have the authority and responsibility for its maintenance? ...and also to complain when the institutions in question don't do their job? The filth is just a symptom, not a cause. The message quoted above calls for a kind of civic action that will only detract the needed attention from addressing the real problems of the system. ¹ [VIDEO] EDIT: Adding the link to the quoted message."}]},"vzZy1Aqleno":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a clear explanation of how lifters and ion wind propulsion work, mostly in threads about exotic propulsion and flying saucers.","q":{"x":"Guy explains it pretty well","i":10957365,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":10957365,"author":"Laaw","month":"2016-01","story":"How to Investigate a Flying Saucer","stance":"rec","why":"Explains lifter ionocraft well","quote":"Guy explains it pretty well","text":"I realize I'm going to sound absolutely bonkers, but I definitely read an article in Wired about devices which used very large amounts of electricity to hover feet in the air. They were triangularly shaped, built in garages, used aluminium somehow, and could hover in the air. I really wish I could find a link to that Wired article... Edit: Found it! [LINK] Edit2: Doing some research (it's coming back to me now), it runs off of ionic wind, which is apparently a well understood phenomena. I'm guessing this was/is being thoroughly researched, and any outcomes have apparently not been fruitful (yet?). I realize I sound bonkers, but come on, this is pretty cool, even if nothing of value has resulted. [LINK] Guy explains it pretty well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31933121,"author":"anonymousiam","month":"2022-06","story":"MIT engineers fly first-ever plane with no moving parts (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Lifters depend on atmosphere to operate","quote":"Many did not understand that they depend on atmosphere to operate.","text":"These were popular decades ago, and were the focus of many an argument over the basis in theory of how they worked. Many did not understand that they depend on atmosphere to operate. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16527687,"author":"KMag","month":"2018-03","story":"First firing of air-breathing electric thruster","stance":"reference","why":"Thrust possible by ionizing air at atmospheric pressure","quote":"it's certainly possible to generate thrust by ionizing air at atmospheric pressure and zero relative velocity","text":"The gas collecting portion of this design wouldn't work well, but it's certainly possible to generate thrust by ionizing air at atmospheric pressure and zero relative velocity. See [LINK] and [VIDEO]"}]},"1qSsVBPh9Lo":{"wy":"Linked in crow-intelligence threads as concrete evidence that crows can learn to trade coins or tokens for food.","m":[{"id":15738623,"author":"visarga","month":"2017-11","story":"Some birds use discarded cigarettes to fumigate their nests","stance":"reference","why":"Crows trained to trade coins for grain.","quote":"It was an experiment where they trained crows to collect coins and put them into a vending machine for a few grains.","text":"I think some birds are actually pretty smart and can learn by imitating other birds, so it's a cultural thing. All it takes is one bird to discover it. Take a look at this video: \"Crow uses a vending machine\". It was an experiment where they trained crows to collect coins and put them into a vending machine for a few grains. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12127064,"author":"danthejam","month":"2016-07","story":"When a crow dies, the other crows investigate the cause of death (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Crows using coins in a vending machine.","quote":"They've even learned to pick up coins and use them in a vending machine in exchange for rewards","text":"They've even learned to pick up coins and use them in a vending machine in exchange for rewards: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9116286,"author":"mxfh","month":"2015-02","story":"Crow Machine","stance":"reference","why":"Another working crow vending machine by Steve Joy.","quote":"Here is another guys, Steve Joy, vending machine","text":"How is his claim holding up, or is he promising too much? Since it's the last news of the thing for captive crows were from 2012 and there is also [thecrowbox] and a correction of the correction [2]. There is still no working version for wild crows[3]. I just still don't know what to make of this since I can hardly see bad intentions other than being a bit overly enthusiastic about the whole thing while producing somewhat underwhelming results in practice. If it would have been some sort of hoax or stunt he probably would have said something by now, like it was an art project or some media hack or anything else along those lines. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] Bonus: Here is another guys, Steve Joy, vending machine (mentioned in the thecrowbox user group) which seem to actually work. [VIDEO]"}]},"25hLohVZdME":{"wy":"HN links it with timestamps as an early, hands-on demo of control theory and as a curiosity from Davis's work.","q":{"x":"He starts talking about PID controllers at around 3:40.","i":33073793,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33073793,"author":"scrlk","month":"2022-10","story":"The Fundamentals of Control Theory","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses PID controllers starting around 3:40.","quote":"He starts talking about PID controllers at around 3:40.","text":"I assume it's this video (\"Terry Davis Demos SimStructure (2016-08-25)\"): [VIDEO] He starts talking about PID controllers at around 3:40."},{"id":30896437,"author":"sam36","month":"2022-04","story":"Boycott Numerical Recipes (2007)","stance":"reference","why":"Terry Davis mentions Numerical Recipes around the 8:15 mark.","quote":"after watching Terry Davis mention it [at 8:15]","text":"Heh, I haven't seen or read NR but added it to my wishlist after watching Terry Davis mention it [at 8:15] [VIDEO] Seemed to work well enough for him."},{"id":18034836,"author":"esaym","month":"2018-09","story":"From Scratch: AI Balancing Act in 50 Lines of Python","stance":"reference","why":"Early demonstration of balancing simulation using calculus and control patterns.","quote":"Terry Davis was doing this 15+ years ago","text":"Not sure why this has to be AI specific.. Terry Davis was doing this 15+ years ago: [VIDEO] Seems to be more of an issue of knowing calculus and control system patterns."}]},"2S2qtGisT34":{"wy":"Cited as evidence of slave markets in Libya, usually in arguments about Western foreign policy.","m":[{"id":28590450,"author":"kafkaIncarnate","month":"2021-09","story":"Freenet is a peer-to-peer platform for censorship-resistant communication","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Who said they \"cared\"? I said intercepted. They basically intercept everything, and specifically will capture everything they monitor of terrorists. > In the years since 9/11, police raids of terrorist cells in the United Kingdom, Italy and Spain have yielded countless images of hard-core child pornography. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] I'm done with this thread. You guys are way too literal. If I cared I would find better sources, but two simple Google searches is enough for me. I have dishes to wash which is much more productive than these threads have become."},{"id":25023103,"author":"m0zg","month":"2020-11","story":"Biden wins White House, vowing new direction for divided U.S.","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"There are now _slave markets_ in Libya","text":"Yes, having a president who did not start a single war for the first time since Reagan is \"favorable\" to a voter such as myself. And last time I voted specifically against this: [LINK] . There are now _slave markets_ in Libya: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18223569,"author":"prolikewh0a","month":"2018-10","story":"US Lawmakers Urge Canada to Snub China's Huawei in Telecoms","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"one example is at the fault of the USA there is now open slave trade in Libya [1].","text":">BTW, slavery STILL exists in many of those parts of the world. We know it still exists and one example is at the fault of the USA there is now open slave trade in Libya [1]. We also still have legal slavery in our prison system as a law. 500,000 dead in Iraq due to illegal wars, laughed at and claimed as 'good' [2]. Cambodia, Laos, Iraq, Vietnam, Afghanistan, Korea, Libya, the list doesn't end! The world would likely be a much better place if the USA didn't have it's fingers in everything, everywhere, nearly every country, killing people in mass , all around the world for decades [3][4]. The first chapter, the preface even, of Noam Chomsky's 'Manufacturing Consent' goes into extraordinary detail about USA's history of genocide and atrocities all around the world, and it's all totally sourced in full. I suggest everyone read it. I've seen estimates between 6 million and 20 million killed by USA in wars since WW1, and that's only by war and conflict, most absolutely provoked by the USA. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"}]},"6COvD8oynmI":{"wy":"Mostly cited by timestamp for specific explanations, such as how type classes map to OOP and why QuickCheck is a great type class example.","q":{"x":"it's the coolest example of type classes Simon Peyton Jones knows and he explains why here (around 34-35 min)","i":14542955,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":42676411,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2025-01","story":"Great things about Rust that aren't just performance","stance":"reference","why":"SPJ on mapping Haskell type classes to OOP","quote":"On the first lecture he discusses how Haskell relates to OOP in regards of subtyping and generic polymorphism","text":"Thankfully this fellow Simon Peyton Jones has a talk about how they map into OOP paradigm. \"Classes, Jim, But Not as We Know Them — Type Classes in Haskell: What, Why, and Whither\" [LINK] \"Adventure with Types in Haskell\" [VIDEO] [LINK] On the first lecture he discusses how Haskell relates to OOP in regards of subtyping and generic polymorphism and how although different on the surface they share those CS concepts in their own ways."},{"id":14542955,"author":"davnn","month":"2017-06","story":"The Design and Use of QuickCheck","stance":"reference","why":"SPJ explains QuickCheck as coolest type class example around 34 minutes","quote":"it's the coolest example of type classes Simon Peyton Jones knows and he explains why here (around 34-35 min)","text":"Also: it's the coolest example of type classes Simon Peyton Jones knows and he explains why here (around 34-35 min): [VIDEO]"},{"id":13191458,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2016-12","story":"Zygomys – An embedded scripting language for Go","stance":"reference","why":"SPJ discusses how Haskell relates to OOP, subtyping and polymorphism","quote":"At 1:01:01 on the first lecture he discusses how Haskell relates to OOP in regards of subtyping and generic polymorphism","text":"Yes Haskell type classes are interfaces from OOP point of view. And given time on the weekend I might try to find out the talk given by SPJ about this very issue. Polymorphism and dynamic dispatch are part of OOP. EDIT: Found Simon's talk Adventure with Types in Haskell, part 1 and part 2: [VIDEO] [LINK] At 1:01:01 on the first lecture he discusses how Haskell relates to OOP in regards of subtyping and generic polymorphism and how although different on the surface they share those CS concepts in their own ways. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10598427,"author":"ablesearcher","month":"2015-11","story":"Making Elm faster and friendlier in 0.16","stance":"reference","why":"Shows type classes are not incidental complexity","quote":"","text":"I've seen Evan's talk. Evan confuses the presentation of features with the value of those features. (I suppose one could solve the difficulties people have in learning Mathematics by throwing out everything after Algebra I; another solution, however, might be to do a better job teaching the more difficult stuff.) Just because you shouldn't expose new programmers to advanced concepts on the first day doesn't mean they shouldn't be a part of the language. Similarly, the fact (<~) might confuse someone new to Elm isn't a reason for removing it from the language. I'm sympathetic to the concern that some code is too \"clever\" or dense; but Elm swings the pendulum too far. (Btw, type-classes are not incidental complexity: [VIDEO] [LINK] )"}]},"6rbJu10Telc":{"wy":"Cited as the fun, surprising example of how much a PDF can do, and the talk behind the Horrifying PDF Experiments repo.","q":{"x":"the repo's < > readme points to the talk given on this","i":40557744,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40557744,"author":"mdaniel","month":"2024-06","story":"Horrifying PDF Experiments","stance":"reference","why":"Talk accompanying the repo.","quote":"the repo's < > readme points to the talk given on this","text":"the repo's < [LINK] > readme points to the talk given on this: [VIDEO] the 2021 submission of the repo got some lively commentary [LINK]"},{"id":35239372,"author":"jeroenhd","month":"2023-03","story":"TinyVG – an alternative binary encoded vector graphics format","stance":"reference","why":"Playing Breakout inside a PDF via Chrome's JavaScript subset.","quote":"It's enough to play Breakout inside a PDF","text":"Support will vary by viewer, but Chrome's built-in PDF viewer supports a subset of Javascript. It's enough to play Breakout inside a PDF: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26864497,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2021-04","story":"Firefox 88.0","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I still can't play breakout in a PDF in Firefox though","text":"> PDF forms now support JavaScript embedded in PDF files. Some PDF forms use JavaScript for validation and other interactive features. As far as I can tell I still can't play breakout in a PDF in Firefox though (and I think that's for the better) [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"8GBAsFwPglw":{"wy":"HN cites it as a primary-source quote in discussions of voter suppression.","q":{"x":"our leverage in the elections quite candidly goes up as the voting populace goes down","i":15589499,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":32905981,"author":"zackmorris","month":"2022-09","story":"Taxing Mechanical Engineers and Subsidizing Drama Majors","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Paul Weyrich's New Right religious conservative movement with Reagan","text":"We weren't supposed to have a standing army either, much less one like we have today with an inflation-adjusted budget as high as during WWII, but here we are. Madison would be appalled, Washington would be ok with something but not that. Conveniently, the far-right in the US omits the military from balanced budget debates, since any reduction in military spending would disrupt the status quo. I feel that we haven't had civic discourse around this stuff since Newt Gingrich started his Contract with America. Or maybe before that with Paul Weyrich's New Right religious conservative movement with Reagan: [VIDEO] The fallout of that ideology today is gerrymandering and various other forms of disenfranchisement to maintain minority rule by the religious right and moneyed corporate interests. Which doesn't align with the separation of church and state, but again, here we are. So you're correct that our history of denying aid to people in need goes way back. But we should also remember that women lived under subjugation until their right to vote was recognized in 1920, and people of color lived under segregation until the Civil Rights Act of 1964. We probably shouldn't use the term \"aid\", because reparations to repair the theft of those peoples' livelihoods would be an astronomical number, easily into the tens of trillions of dollars. The same people who thought those progressive movements were crazy continue to call aid to those in need \"redistribution\" today. These are heated enou"},{"id":15589499,"author":"fencepost","month":"2017-10","story":"Indiana Purging Voters Using Software That’s 99% Inaccurate","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"our leverage in the elections quite candidly goes up as the voting populace goes down","text":"How about if by \"significant\" I mean \"has enough impact on elections to merit countermeasures that have been statistically shown to disproportionately reduce election participation [oh noes!] by brown people and people with lower incomes (regardless of race)?\" How about if by \"significant\" I mean \"has enough impact on elections to merit countermeasures that cost at least tens of thousands of dollars per fraudulent vote, but probably hundreds of thousands of dollars per fraudulent vote?\" Is there truly no more cost-effective way to spend that money on ensuring election integrity? Perhaps with little things like voter-verifiable paper trails that actually allow auditing of results? Why, it's almost as if the oh-so-unfortunate side effect of minority and poor disenfranchisement is the purpose and \"preventing vote fraud\" is just the excuse. I'm sure Paul \"our leverage in the elections quite candidly goes up as the voting populace goes down\" Weyrich would have been heartbroken by that. [VIDEO] I also looked through the report cited and found it remarkably light on actual facts. They make a big point of Maureen Erickson's cancellation for being a non-citizen in 2012 and her providing a Guatemalan address. They also note that she'd voted in 14 elections. Honestly, I'm surprised that they also included that the most recent of those was in 2008, 4 years before her registration was cancelled. So what happened in those intervening 4 years? Did this obviously Hispanic woman get thrown ou"},{"id":13743254,"author":"noobermin","month":"2017-02","story":"Robert Mercer: the big data billionaire waging war on mainstream media","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Conservatives have admitted in the past[0] that they win by suppressing the vote.","text":"Conservatives have admitted in the past[0] that they win by suppressing the vote. That happens while Liberals usually win by getting out the vote. Saying \"both sides do it\" is just plain unfair and borderline propaganda. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"A9qrURPAtnY":{"wy":"Used as evidence that large e-ink monitors can handle video and scrolling, and as a pointer to Dasung as the main e-ink monitor maker.","q":{"x":"they show video of a figure skater in the product reveal video and it looks usable","i":25539290,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":26612363,"author":"ThrowawayR2","month":"2021-03","story":"I made an eInk newspaper","stance":"reference","why":"Preview of Dasung's upcoming large e-ink monitor","quote":"Dasung is the only manufacturer that's known for producing e-ink monitors","text":"Dasung is the only manufacturer that's known for producing e-ink monitors but they are not any cheaper: - Review of Dasung's current generation 13.3\" e-ink monitor: [LINK] - Preview video of their upcoming 25.3\" e-ink monitor: [VIDEO] If low cost is a priority, previous generations of Dasung's e-ink displays occasionally show up on eBay."},{"id":26201660,"author":"fouc","month":"2021-02","story":"My Life in E-Ink","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Dasung's fast e-ink monitor","quote":"There are e-ink displays with higher refresh rates where scrolling is possible","text":"There are e-ink displays with higher refresh rates where scrolling is possible, like Dasung's new e-ink monitor [VIDEO]"},{"id":25539290,"author":"bertmuthalaly","month":"2020-12","story":"Dasung just released a 25 inch eInk monitor","stance":"reference","why":"Product reveal showing usable video on the e-ink monitor","quote":"they show video of a figure skater in the product reveal video and it looks usable","text":"Not a precise answer, but they show video of a figure skater in the product reveal video and it looks usable! Link with timestamp: [VIDEO]"}]},"B7MIJP90biM":{"wy":"Posted as a joke whenever threads discuss impossible or contradictory specifications and clients, to show a clever solution exists.","m":[{"id":47483351,"author":"ehsanu1","month":"2026-03","story":"Reports of code's death are greatly exaggerated","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the problem as stated isn't actually constrained enough to be unsolvable","text":"That shows it knew this bit of satire more than anything. Also, the problem as stated isn't actually constrained enough to be unsolvable: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36450512,"author":"Verdex","month":"2023-06","story":"Twilight of the Programmers","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Interestingly enough, someone made a response video","text":"Interestingly enough, someone made a response video: [VIDEO] Although, to be sure, the response from management et al will almost definitely indicate that this isn't what they were looking for at all. [I know an electrical engineer who was part of a team that provided an engine control component to a customer and their response was that even though the component 100% meets all of the requirements they told them about it apparently didn't conform to the requirements that they were not told about (or perhaps that the customer was unaware of)]"},{"id":21951036,"author":"deanCommie","month":"2020-01","story":"Clear and Present Leadership","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"And an actual expert showing how it can be done","text":"> somebody said that a thing is simply not possible and a manager asked \"but what if it was possible?\" I've been an Individual Contributor, and a Manager, and am back to being an IC. I've been on both sides of this conversation. One of the benefits of being a manager is being able to see the forest from the trees while your team members may be so focused on their tasks they do not see the big picture. Sure, sometimes the answer to \"but what if it was possible?\" if just \"it's not.\" But a lot of times, the good managers I've had would propose an approach to MAKING it possible that I just simply didn't see. And I've been able to do the same in their shoes. Sometimes it requires a paradigm shift - Q: \"How do we get to the other town faster?\" A: \"The horses are running as fast as they can\" Q: \"What if we found a faster horse?\" (e.g. a car) But honestly in day to day office culture it rarely needs to be so revolutionary - Q: \"Could we finish the project faster if Bill's team helped us?\" A: \"No, it's not possible. Bill's team said they are completely swamped\" <Goes off to do some managerial work> Q: \"Okay, I've chatted to Bill, and his team is fully available to help us finish the project.\" I'll leave you with this \"Expert\" saying that something is simply not possible: [LINK] And an actual expert showing how it can be done: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20169441,"author":"deanCommie","month":"2019-06","story":"When Employees Use Software That IT Hasn’t Approved","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Mandatory response viewing","text":"Clients don't know what they want and don't know how to describe what they do want. That doesn't mean they're wrong. Actual experts get to the root of what they need, and find ways to solve the requirements, even when they seem impossible. Mandatory response viewing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12429456,"author":"mrob","month":"2016-09","story":"Lessons Learned from Software Rewrites","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"with a little creativity that \"impossible\" spec is possible","text":"I don't think it's a great example, because with a little creativity that \"impossible\" spec is possible: [VIDEO] The customers might even be happy with it, because it would mean they wouldn't have to admit their requirements were stupid."},{"id":10679804,"author":"msm23","month":"2015-12","story":"Air gaps never exist (2011)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Never thought I'd see it, but here's the solution to the expert problem","text":"Never thought I'd see it, but here's the solution to the expert problem: [VIDEO] Credit to D. Scott Williamson, Expert"}]},"E7kor5nHtZQ":{"wy":"Linked as the standard illustration of Tokyo's rush-hour crowding whenever commuting and density come up.","q":{"x":"the morning commute is something foreigners consider particularly wacky","i":16899441,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":29739446,"author":"iszomer","month":"2021-12","story":"Tokyo police lose 2 floppy disks containing info on public housing applicants","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Japan cramming passengers into trains at rush hour","quote":"Japan has also been known to cram passengers into trains during rush hour.","text":"Japan has also been known to cram passengers into trains during rush hour. I imagine this practice has probably been fervently deprecated due to covid19. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22650152,"author":"taeric","month":"2020-03","story":"Keeping the coronavirus from infecting healthcare workers","stance":"reference","why":"Shows crowded Japanese trains in a debate about mask usage","quote":"","text":"It would need testing to show they don't have a prevalence of \"mild\" cases. Not to mention we should have numbers on how often they wear masks. Last I saw, it was not everyone that did that. Edit for reference: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16899441,"author":"anabis","month":"2018-04","story":"Why Invest in Cities? There's Always Another Boise","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Tokyo's packed morning commute","quote":"the morning commute is something foreigners consider particularly wacky","text":"Most people commute by train. We are improving, but the morning commute is something foreigners consider particularly wacky, and not something that they want to repeat everyday. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15629065,"author":"mcny","month":"2017-11","story":"Who’s Sitting Next to You on the Subway? An R Train in September","stance":"reference","why":"Shows staff pushing commuters onto crowded Tokyo trains","quote":"Can you imagine the lawsuits if we were to have staff push people here?","text":"Can you imagine the lawsuits if we were to have staff push people here? [VIDEO] By the way, as bad as things are in New York MTA, I find it much better than the stupidity of the path train (panynj). We have like four routes to manage and we can't do that properly."}]},"IW7Rqwwth84":{"wy":"HN cites it as the classic demo failure, in threads about USB, plug and play, and Microsoft's shipping quality.","q":{"x":"the infamous BSOD demo with Bill Gates on stage was meant to show off plug and play on Windows 98","i":47824200,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47824200,"author":"sublinear","month":"2026-04","story":"Why Zip drives dominated the 90s, then vanished almost overnight","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the infamous BSOD demo with Bill Gates on stage was meant to show off plug and play on Windows 98","text":"I did some more digging to find out that the infamous BSOD demo with Bill Gates on stage was meant to show off plug and play on Windows 98 and caused by a USB scanner. This happened 4 months before the release date of the first iMac. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25728634,"author":"rasz","month":"2021-01","story":"The Confusing World of USB","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Two years later we had Bill Gates getting embarrassed by Windows 98 BSOD on stage","text":"Love Fabien write-ups! > badly fragmented connector world made of Dont forget SCSI. USB killed external SCSI. >Up to that point in time, it was a good tech stack. USB 0.9 was 1995, USB 1.0 1996. Two years later we had Bill Gates getting embarrassed by Windows 98 BSOD on stage [VIDEO] . By ~2002 we ended up with 6 separate incompatible proprietary variations on micro USB. Pretty much every second Camera vendor had its won \"standard\". >USB-3.X cables features 8 wires .... and the other six carry data Sadly just adding even more confusion to the mix. 24 pins, up to 13 signals, 4 pairs (8 wires) dedicated to USB 3.2 alone. >Thanks to DP 1.4, the bandwidth requirement can be lowered via DSP lossless compression oh its very much lossy, Display Stream Compression (DSC) is \"visually lossless\" :--------) it works similar to Texture Compression, but works on blocks of lines instead of rectangles, still it does destroy information by cutting off color accuracy. At the end of the day USB is still trivial compared to Bluetooh."},{"id":21170843,"author":"ulfw","month":"2019-10","story":"A German word for:“It only works when I try to show you how it does not work”?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Remember this oldie but goldie for USB Plug and Play on Windows?","text":"That translation is wrong. It’s almost the opposite. Vorführeffekt is when you are trying to show something works (for example to an audience/in a presentation) and it does NOT work in said presentation. Remember this oldie but goldie for USB Plug and Play on Windows? [VIDEO] PS: thanks for all the comments! Didn't realise the double negation would work too. (Trying to show a bug you found in a meeting/to an audience but then it turns out it actually behaves unexpectedly as originally intended. No bug.)"},{"id":37979329,"author":"esafak","month":"2023-10","story":"Dave Cutler: The Secret History of Microsoft Windows [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Bill Gates had no taste to justify any sort of perfectionism, or any shame in shipping buggy products","text":"Steve Jobs pushed individual contributors to perfect products before shipping them. Bill Gates had no taste to justify any sort of perfectionism, or any shame in shipping buggy products ( [VIDEO] ). It was all dollars and cents, as far as I can tell."}]},"IrHRQSm6LIs":{"wy":"Cited as an example of quirks caused by non-geographic maps and design choices in transit systems.","m":[{"id":44680343,"author":"globular-toast","month":"2025-07","story":"Bus Bunching","stance":"reference","why":"Example of designers favoring global optimum over local optima","quote":"","text":"That's how global optimisation works. Generally speaking you will always miss out on a bunch of local optima when you go for a global optimum. This was well understood by designers in metro systems like the London Underground[0] and road signs. When each individual strives for the best result for themselves (a local optimum) we often end up in a worse result for everyone (see tragedy of the commons, prisoners' dilemma etc). [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33913420,"author":"Crosseye_Jack","month":"2022-12","story":"Google Combines Maps and Waze Teams in Restructuring Move","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Could this be simply a case of \"the needs of the many outweigh the needs of a few\"?. For example I live near a busy road during \"rush hour\" in the morning and evenings. A few mins up the road from me 2 major roads intersect which causes more hassle during these peak times. Now If you are travelling West to East to wish to turn right at the intersection to continue your journey south its quicker during those times to cut though my neighborhood (and same in the other direction) and cut out the queues leading up to the intersection. However my neighborhood's roads are not designed for that much traffic to flow though them (cars parked in the street narrowing the roads and reducing visablity of pedestrians esp kids playing outside). The handful of locals that travel though the neighborhood to get home (live one side but enter the other) are fine however if Google Maps routed everyone wishing to make that turn though the neighborhood it would make the neighborhoods roads much less friendly to the pedestrians of the neighborhood esp at times kids are going to school or are back home from school and playing outside. Keeping the through traffic to the major roads keeps everyone safer, and actually faster overall because the major roads aren't littered with cards parked in the road creating bottlenecks. Much like how the london underground lies to travellers and routes them around long detours during busy periods to help prevent a crush if they all went the shortest & fastest route po"},{"id":22316844,"author":"roelschroeven","month":"2020-02","story":"The Economics of Maps [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Highlights peculiarities of the London Underground caused by non-geographic maps","quote":"highlights some peculiarities of the London Underground","text":"Metro maps are optimized for finding your way from one metro station to another. But what I really want is to go from one physical location to another, and that is made more difficult because those distorted metro maps don't map very well to the physical layout of the area. It's often difficult to see how the metro stations correspond to physical locations, and therefore not straightforward to find out the best total route (i.e. including walking from starting point to metro station, metro, walk from metro to destination, possibly other transportation options). Edit: Tom Scott has a video (somewhat theatrically titled \"Lies on the London Underground\") that highlights some peculiarities of the London Underground, some of which are (partly) caused by the non-geographic nature of the maps: [VIDEO]"}]},"JIjenjANqAk":{"wy":"Used as an example of civil political conduct, contrasted with today's politics, and posted after McCain's death.","q":{"x":"Remember John McCain defending Obama[0]?","i":46688414,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47195514,"author":"Imustaskforhelp","month":"2026-02","story":"Israel launches strike against Iran, declares state of emergency across country","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Extremely great post with detailed examples > So, without a generation of leaders able to recognize and understand the role of history and those traumas and empathize with the other sides we're trapped in those loops of aggression. The sad reality (imo) about this truth is that the qualities needed to be a leader aren't empathy. There was a vid about it which went more into detail but When you observe leaders, you find that they are extremely weird and sometimes psychopathic. To me it also feels like if a leader is emphatetic towards the other part, other leaders more extreme would spring up saying that he's an enemy from within or something equivalent to it. The empathy of the leader is one of the most disregarded qualities. I would go so far as to say that leaders aren't even empathetic towards the general population of their own nations/community sometimes. It's really sad but the Empathy you mention and cowardice can look the same to many & the Empathatic leader would get booted out of/not given a chance. For example, within America itself, I feel like John mccain was a good guy and I would consider him empathetic in the sense that I remember seeing interviews of him saying that he and Obama just have some minor differences in policy making when there were people attending his rallies asking that they don't feel safe about Obama. I am just gonna say that This leader of republican party was lost for what is now Donald Trump. Oh I just watched the rally/interview again[0], "},{"id":46688414,"author":"pdpi","month":"2026-01","story":"Is the Constitution Broken","stance":"reference","why":"Example of McCain defending Obama contrasted with today's politics","quote":"Remember John McCain defending Obama[0]?","text":"We live in a world where the sitting president calls January 6 a day of love, and has pardoned the rioters, and then says that people protesting ICE are \"domestic terrorists\". We live in a world where federal prosecutors are choosing to quit rather than following his orders. Remember John McCain defending Obama[0]? Do you genuinely believe that the people heading the Republican Party today would ever do that? Contrast McCain's humility and grace in his concession speech[1] with Trump's constant refusal to accept that he lost 2020, and his insistence on exacting revenge on the people who \"wronged\" him. No, this is not a \"vigorous political process\" in action. It's something else entirely. 0. [VIDEO] 1. [LINK]"},{"id":45619926,"author":"ztetranz","month":"2025-10","story":"Meow.camera","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I am not even American but I remember McCain more and more when I hear about this. he was so much better than this. Very true. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44071495,"author":"nebula8804","month":"2025-05","story":"Trump administration halts Harvard's ability to enroll international students","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Somewhat yes [0]: [VIDEO] But it was also that the great recession was a bit painful for enough of these people."},{"id":17843786,"author":"mehrdadn","month":"2018-08","story":"John McCain has died","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I've found 1 clip so far [1]; would you happen to have more? I'd love to be able to refer back to these in the future. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17844161,"author":"27182818284","month":"2018-08","story":"John McCain has died","stance":"reference","why":"McCain defending Obama at a rally","quote":"He tried to do the right thing.","text":"He tried to do the right thing. Sometimes it came out a bit awkward like the infamous \"He is a decent human being\" comment, but his heart was in the right place [VIDEO]"}]},"Wif1EAgEQKI":{"wy":"HN mentions it as an example of a prominent creator publicly correcting a popular myth, and of the fire brigade myth itself.","m":[{"id":36387514,"author":"simondotau","month":"2023-06","story":"Swing VPN app is a DDoS botnet","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Scott cited on VPNs mistaken for antivirus","quote":"Famously described by Tom Scott","text":"> one major problem is VPNs are advertised or promoted as if they're synonymous with antivirus software. Famously described by Tom Scott — [How it started [LINK] [How it's going] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34899047,"author":"Sayrus","month":"2023-02","story":"De-Extinction? Surely You’re Joking","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Scott's video on the fire brigade myth","quote":"Tom Scott did a video","text":"Tom Scott did a video[1] about this. For the UK at least, this may have been an urban myth rooted in the fact that only the fastest fire brigades were paid[2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":34055781,"author":"lolinder","month":"2022-12","story":"Did Insurance Fire Brigades let uninsured buildings burn?","stance":"reference","why":"Source for earlier video that was found incorrect","quote":"He was paid by a prominent YouTuber to find out whether a video said YouTuber made several years ago was incorrect","text":"> Usually commonly held widely believed oral histories emerge for a good reason. So if it was commonly believed that insurance companies let un-insured buildings burn, then it is likely to be true at least part of the time. Yes, oral histories emerge for a reason, but that reason doesn't have to be \"because it was literally true\". There are countless oral traditions that are complete nonsense! Urban legends existed 200 and 2000 years ago just as much as they do today, and no historian would ever claim that you should take every oral history at face value. > Also, lets look at the source -- he is an \"archive and heritage consultant\", i.e. someone that solicits money for his research. Is he getting paid for this article? Is he writing this in the hopes that he will advertise his services for insurance companies? He was paid by a prominent YouTuber to find out whether a video said YouTuber made several years ago was incorrect[0]. That doesn't scream \"conflict of interest\" to me. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"WkZGL7RQBVw":{"wy":"Used as a concrete example of automation successfully saving an incapacitated pilot.","m":[{"id":26249107,"author":"throwanem","month":"2021-02","story":"In two incidents, F-16 aviators were rendered unconscious, but saved by software","stance":"reference","why":"Typical Auto-GCAS recovery footage with HUD altitude readout","quote":"It looks to bottom out around 4400 feet during the AGCAS recovery.","text":"Assuming we're talking about the typical example ( [VIDEO] ) - in the HUD, altitude is the right-hand vertical tape. (The left one is airspeed.) It looks to bottom out around 4400 feet during the AGCAS recovery."},{"id":17394287,"author":"21","month":"2018-06","story":"If a pilot ejects, what is the autopilot programmed to do?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of automation kicking in when plane leaves normal regime","quote":"Auto-GCAS Saves Unconscious F-16 Pilot","text":"Not an expert, but in military planes there are systems which kick in exactly when the plane is outside it's normal regime. Auto-GCAS Saves Unconscious F-16 Pilot: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14381100,"author":"cbanek","month":"2017-05","story":"Flight QF72: What happens when automation leaves pilots powerless?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of ground collision avoidance saving an incapacitated pilot","quote":"","text":"That's an excellent point. Even if it's not the people, the stick or manual control inputs are also a point of failure. So if suddenly your stick does something strange, and your flight computer allows it (thinking human knows best) you are in big trouble. The opposite of this is the ground collision avoidance systems, where the humans are one of the weakest parts of the aircraft (due to G-loc). [VIDEO]"},{"id":18650439,"author":"ziont","month":"2018-12","story":"The cold start problem: how to build your machine learning portfolio","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Skunkworks: omg this kid has replicated our Auto-GAS system using only computer vision Lockheed Martin CEO: So what the fuck is this youtube video? [VIDEO]"}]},"YQIMGV5vtd4":{"wy":"HN refers to it as the viral university video that showed what autonomous multicopter swarms can do and helped spark interest in multicopters.","q":{"x":"a university experiment video that went viral","i":9512028,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":17191372,"author":"maxander","month":"2018-05","story":"Autonomous Boats Could Service Some Cities, Reducing Road Traffic","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"AI quadcopters that can fly in ornate formations and execute complex maneuvers are well known","text":"Have you ever tried to fly a quadcopter drone manually? It's really hard ; I could barely get one to hover, after hours of practice. Yet AI quadcopters that can fly in ornate formations and execute complex maneuvers are well known [1]. Don't discount what AI can or can't do just because it's hard for humans. Other advantages in ferries' favor; unlike most streets, the docks for autonomous ferries could be designed around the AI's capabilities. Also, ferries don't travel at 70 MPH. :) [1] [VIDEO] (6-year-old video!)"},{"id":9512028,"author":"arsey","month":"2015-05","story":"Ask HN: Why did multicopters suddenly take off?","stance":"reference","why":"Viral university video as a likely tipping point for multicopters","quote":"a university experiment video that went viral","text":"Pun intended? Anyways, a university experiment video that went viral [0] is the first I recall seeing a quadcopter in action. Maybe the tipping point if you care to investigate further. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5830429,"author":"glenra","month":"2013-06","story":"The 800 Pound Gorilla Amazon Tried to Kill is Google's Trojan Horse","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"You are postulating a group that is willing to hijack an unmanned truck in order to steal the goods being delivered and doesn't mind the risk of being seen because they \"have a bandana and shades on\". Question: What is so special about an unmanned truck given that circumstance? Wouldn't it be at least as easy for a group like that to hijack a manned truck? Why don't the same gangs pull a gun on the UPS guy and take all his stuff? That would be in some ways easier than taking stuff from an unmanned delivery van. The unmanned van can go into lockdown mode and doesn't have a driver who can be threatened to make him give up the key to the back. The cost for any private party to send up their own monitor drones is coming down very quickly and will likely be insignificant by the time unmanned delivery vehicles are common. If one unmanned delivery car sends out a distress signal, other unmanned delivery cars in the area could release drones monitored by a local security firm to go see what's happening, follow the bad guys, and tell the cops exactly where they went. The sort of drones I'm talking about might look like this: [VIDEO] (You can already get copters of that sort with at least a 10 minute battery range for a mere few hundred bucks.)"},{"id":5151067,"author":"sp332","month":"2013-02","story":"OpenSource Nano Quadcopter Kit","stance":"reference","why":"Video from the school behind the quadrotor research","quote":"a video made by that school","text":"Here are some papers that might be useful for budding quadrotor hackers and pilots: [LINK] and a video made by that school: [VIDEO]"}]},"_FjuPn7MXMs":{"wy":"Cited as an existing example of 3D or spatial desktop shells and what Wayland compositing makes possible.","q":{"x":"The point of this demo is not practical usability today","i":24019112,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24019112,"author":"vetinari","month":"2020-08","story":"GTK 3.99","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of desktop positioning possibilities in Wayland.","quote":"The point of this demo is not practical usability today","text":"With wayland, you can do this and the apps won't notice because of the limits placed upon them earlier: [VIDEO] Can macOS do it? The point of this demo is not practical usability today, but to demonstrate, that global positioning on the desktop as we know today might get much more complex in future. So why shut the door?"},{"id":18245796,"author":"mitchtbaum","month":"2018-10","story":"The case for pair programming","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates shared 3D desktop idea.","quote":"","text":"I've been pair programming for about 6 weeks now, over Discord. The calling and screen sharing functionalities work so well that my partners and I can join and part quickly and easily. We can get deeply into what we're doing individually. But collaboratively, we can solve problems that neither of us can do alone. I like this virtual setup for multi-user interaction a lot, and hope more tools, even ones specific to virtual programming environments, come for it soon. Tuple's making a remote sharing tool that looks interesting. What comes to mind for me is a shared 3D Wayland environment, like this [VIDEO] so, hopefully more people will start focusing on [LINK] and other Rust desktop/handheld application level development."},{"id":6519764,"author":"bachback","month":"2013-10","story":"NodeOS","stance":"reference","why":"Demo hinting at VR-style OS shells.","quote":"","text":"here are my thoughts. there once was chromeOS, which was the idea to build an OS as a browser. more recently exobrowser is bootstrapping chrome via node. which means you can expose rendering methods. if you compare that with what different OS layers does, you might as well use the exposed rendering methods to expose your local data and services (=KDE/Gnome). so I would study Wayland for this first. there you learn render stuff to the screen speaking to the kernel. it's 100x easier than X11. I don't know KDE/Gnome internals but I would assume with Wayland some of it can be rebuild now (?). so you could then basically script the GUI perhaps in quite new ways. if you compare that to what the exobrowser is doing you get the picture. so coreOS => Docker => wayland. network servers are booted up from remote, so the network part is becoming more core. virtualization/containers allow for different isolation of processes which speak to the network. then you have a thin layer of display. so for example imagine hookup a oculus and having that as a kind of OS, see this demo [VIDEO] the future is going to be cool ;)"},{"id":5727982,"author":"drivebyacct2","month":"2013-05","story":"Meta: The Most Advanced Augmented Reality Interface","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of Qt 3D compositor as existing tech.","quote":"Skip X, jump to QML and QWayland.","text":"Skip X, jump to QML and QWayland. [VIDEO] Take that and extrapolate. Doesn't seem that \"crazy\" really. Compiz and KWin both already have window animations you're describing. There was even a Wii-head-tracking plugin for compiz that would give your desktop a third perspective dimension. (Moving your head would reveal the edges/bits of the other windows underneath higher ones). It'd be a neat demo, but it sounds like a nightmare for daily usability, etc."}]},"b0Ti-gkJiXc":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it in arguments about criticism, to separate attacking a comment from attacking a person.","q":{"x":"If someone makes a racist comment, attack the comment, not the person.","i":3190976,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":29704952,"author":"pseudalopex","month":"2021-12","story":"Vivaldi: Violating CoC on forum may close your email account","stance":"reference","why":"Separates what someone said from what kind of person they are.","quote":"What they said and what kind of person they are are 2 different discussions.[1]","text":"The point was you said it was intended to poke fun at traditional chivalry like it was a fact. But it was just your speculation. We define chivalrous differently apparently. And I never heard of someone trying to flatter a 21 year old man by wishing him a happy 60th birthday. What they said and what kind of person they are are 2 different discussions.[1] Well meaning people accept constructive criticism. I know people who would like what you wrote better. I know people who would think telling them you're sure they didn't mean something was insincere and condescending. The 1st reply that mentioned the attempted joke just said please don't make sexist comments.[2] It was appropriate for an aside on a technical mailing list. The joker could have replied off list if they didn't understand. They doubled down instead like you said. Someone else called it cancel culture and called other people hypocrites. Someone else compared it to witch burning. Someone else implied assuming good intentions meant ignoring anything unintentionally objectionable. The reaction to that was someone proposed a different standard. The reaction to that was trolling. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":27345528,"author":"pseudalopex","month":"2021-05","story":"What Are “Unusual Characters”?","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses actions versus identity in criticism.","quote":"You're turning a discussion about what someone did into a discussion about who they are.[1]","text":"You're turning a discussion about what someone did into a discussion about who they are.[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26161437,"author":"pseudalopex","month":"2021-02","story":"“Silicon Valley’s Safe Space” has misinformed readers","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses what someone said versus what they are.","quote":"The question is about what he said. Not what he is.[1]","text":"They didn't say or ask if he turned into a misogynist. The question is about what he said. Not what he is.[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17913395,"author":"peteforde","month":"2018-09","story":"Terry Davis has died","stance":"reference","why":"Focus criticism on the act, not the person.","quote":"it's important to keep it focused not on the person but the act.","text":"When telling someone that something they said was racist, it's important to keep it focused not on the person but the act. Racism and prejudice is learned, and it can certainly be unlearned. Unfortunately, you cannot unlearn mental illness. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9060926,"author":"ahelwer","month":"2015-02","story":"Facebook's Name Policy Strikes Again, This Time at Native Americans","stance":"reference","why":"Explains intent versus impact when calling something racist.","quote":"The venerable Jay Smooth describes a closely-related distinction here[0].","text":"The developers are not racist. My entire original point is their attitudes and intents were irrelevant. The system they created is very much racist, completely independent about how they felt in creating it; I'm not sure how you can really dispute that. The venerable Jay Smooth describes a closely-related distinction here[0]. Also, when you describe a system as racist that's somewhat different from describing a person as racist, which might be a place we're talking past each other. Re: the culture/race/ethnicity terminology quagmire, the sister comment by dragonwriter explains it well. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":5671690,"author":"peteforde","month":"2013-05","story":"The Food-Truck Business Stinks","stance":"reference","why":"Advice on telling someone their words sound racist.","quote":"","text":"For those of you who think I was wrong to suggest that jellicle's statement was uncomfortably racist: we might have to agree to disagree, here. The vast majority of street food is run by immigrants, and it's a perfect entry point for them to get into the local economy they have chosen to make their home in. People who were born here are smart to use the food truck as a way to test a concept before they try it in a restaurant format. Perhaps we'll see that happening more and more. There's certainly all cultures represented in Portland. Meanwhile, we're all immigrants here unless you come from native background. I don't think the original commenter is a racist person, but his comment comparing food trucks to parasites is uncomfortable at best and shameful at worst. Here's a test: go ask 100 food truck operators if they think some guy on a tech forum calling food trucks \"parasites\" is racist, and get back to us. [VIDEO]"},{"id":4604159,"author":"jamesmiller5","month":"2012-10","story":"Racism at a gaming company","stance":"reference","why":"Distinguishes saying something racist from being a racist.","quote":"","text":"If you replace the instances of \"white men\" with \"mainstream white culture\" his arguments are much stronger and I believe to be his original intent. \"And it’s not a matter of generalizing white people, rather it’s being real about the culture San Francisco creates.\" The author is angry that white culture perpetuates racism and privilege, which himself and other cultural minorities are now forced to tolerate at their workplace. His choice of words was obviously inappropriate and while I'm not excusing how his point was said I don't believe it was his intention to make his point using racist statements. This is a classic example of \"What you said was racist VS. You are a racist\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":3190976,"author":"klipt","month":"2011-11","story":"Michael Arrington on Racism: The Game","stance":"reference","why":"Attack the comment, not the person.","quote":"If someone makes a racist comment, attack the comment, not the person.","text":"> Let's assume for a while that Arrington was really racist Even if that were true, the last thing you should do is call him one. [VIDEO] If someone makes a racist comment, attack the comment, not the person."},{"id":2752062,"author":"seigenblues","month":"2011-07","story":"Noah Kagan and the Faceless Bitch slide","stance":"reference","why":"Distinction between calling someone racist and calling behavior racist.","quote":"See the \"i'm not calling you a racist, i'm calling your behavior racist\" crucial line of distinction.","text":"as near as i could read, no one was attacking Noah -- just Noah's behavior, which seems out of line. See the \"i'm not calling you a racist, i'm calling your behavior racist\" crucial line of distinction. ( [VIDEO] ) Noah can be a sweet, helpful, lovable teddy bear, and still have behavior that needs to be called out."}]},"ehp9PZo4UR0":{"wy":"Cited as a source in Israel-Palestine threads for the claim that labor Zionist groups drove the Nakba.","m":[{"id":45301376,"author":"kalberg6429","month":"2025-09","story":"Count Folke Bernadotte: Sweden's Servant of Peace (2010)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source that labor Zionist groups drove the Nakba","quote":"","text":"The expected Zionist modus operandi, whitewash Zionist crimes, then blame the victim for responding. Your \"socialist\" argument is a weak attempt to hide behind a political label. It doesn't matter what they called themselves. The \"socialist\"[1] Haganah and Palmach were the main engines of the Nakba. The distinction between them and the Irgun was a public relations strategy, a \"good cop, bad cop\" routine for the same unified colonial project of dispossessing Palestinians. The Altalena was a colonizing force consolidating its monopoly on violence to better oppress and dispossess the Palestinians. You cannot compare that to a occupied population struggling under a foreign military boot. Palestinian \"leadership problems\" and disunity are a direct result of decades of Israeli assassinations, imprisonment, and engineered fragmentation.[2] [1] 'The Dark History of \"Left-Wing\" Zionism' [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":41828148,"author":"jedimind","month":"2024-10","story":"Using drones to terrorize a city [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"When he writes \"In the Palestine Mandate, Jews and Palestinians got along just fine\" he clearly means that the centuries of peaceful co-existence before the arrival of Zionists are evidence that Zionism was and still is the root cause of hostilities and violence. That event which you referenced didn't happen in a vacuum since at that point Zionist colonization[1] was already in full force for decades and was systematically discriminating against the native Palestinians and trying to dispossess them deploying various strategies outlined in Jabotinsky's writings. \"The Irgun has been viewed as a terrorist organization or organization which carried out terrorist acts. The Irgun policy was based on what was then called Revisionist Zionism founded by Ze'ev Jabotinsky.[6] Two of the operations for which the Irgun is best known are the bombing of the King David Hotel in Jerusalem on 22 July 1946 and the Deir Yassin massacre that killed at least 107 Palestinian Arab villagers, including women and children, carried out together with Lehi on 9 April 1948. The organization committed acts of terrorism against Palestinian Arabs, as well as against the British authorities, who were regarded as illegal occupiers.[7] In particular the Irgun was described as a terrorist organization by the United Nations, British, and United States governments; in media such as The New York Times newspaper;[8][9] as well as by the Anglo-American Committee of Inquiry,[10][11] the 1946 Zionist Congress[12] and t"},{"id":40269483,"author":"kelthuzad","month":"2024-05","story":"Israel shuts down local Al Jazeera offices","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as documentation of the history of socialist Zionism and the Nakba","quote":"","text":"You can disagree all you want, some people even disagree on if the holocaust happened but no one takes them seriously. The history of Zionism and its goal to colonize Palestine (long before the holocaust even happened) and their ruthless methods of colonization are thoroughly documented[0][1]. [0] [LINK] [1] The History of \"Socialist\" Zionism | Leftist Zionists did the Nakba & founded Israel [VIDEO]"}]},"gBVuAGFcGKY":{"wy":"Brought up humorously in threads about harassment and gender discrimination as satire that makes the point.","m":[{"id":4387567,"author":"13rules","month":"2012-08","story":"Sexual Harassment at DefCon (and Other Hacker Cons)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"All of this could be avoided if guys would just follow the advice in this video","text":"All of this could be avoided if guys would just follow the advice in this video: [VIDEO] 1. Be Handsome 2. Be Attractive 3. Don't Be Unattractive"},{"id":4009279,"author":"temphn","month":"2012-05","story":"Kleiner Perkins Partner Ellen Pao Sues Firm for Gender Discrimination","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It is all too reminiscent of this satire","text":"In the suit, Pao alleges that she was sexually harrassed for years by Ajit Nazre, a former partner with the firm who left last year. The two took a business trip together in February 2006, the suit says, during which Nazre made sexual advances toward Pao and she rebuffed them, causing him to become “brusque and distant.” Nazre continued to pursue Pao and the two had a brief relationship, which she broke off soon after, according to the suit. \"And the two had a brief relationship\". Wait, so then it wasn't harassment? During that time I'm sure she thought his persistence had paid off. It is all too reminiscent of this satire: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1638697,"author":"AgentConundrum","month":"2010-08","story":"YCRFS 7: Applications of Facebook Instant Personalization","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Forgive me for quoting Saturday Night Live[1] here","text":"Creepy is the ugly girl who stares at you, but the pretty one who does it isn't. Forgive me for quoting Saturday Night Live[1] here, but I guess this a successful implementation of this feature boils down to: - Be Handsome^W Usable - Be Attractive - Don't Be Unattractive Really, those are rules that apply to any feature on any website. Nobody likes useless features. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":1361156,"author":"byrneseyeview","month":"2010-05","story":"Bat fellatio causes a scandal in academia  ","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"SNL had some fun with this","text":"Whenever someone gets in trouble for sexual harassment, I like to find a picture of the perp. The funny thing is that you almost never see someone noticeably more attractive than average getting caught committing sexual harassment. And if you do, you find out that he's a serial harasser, with dozens of unreported past cases. SNL had some fun with this: [VIDEO] (moderately not safe for work, but no nudity). It's interesting to have a law that says, not \"X is bad behavior,\" but \"X is behavior that you can only get away with if other people like it.\" It would be like having a law that banned porn of ugly people."},{"id":1198533,"author":"ericb","month":"2010-03","story":"Richard Feynman on getting laid: \"You Just Ask Them?\"","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"sometimes comedy is the best way to make a point","text":"The difference between a confident stud, and a confident creep, illustrated (sometimes comedy is the best way to make a point): [VIDEO]"}]},"gQliI_WGaGk":{"wy":"Cited for its optimistic argument that there are few limits to knowledge and human progress.","m":[{"id":42588806,"author":"morphle","month":"2025-01","story":"Mandelbrot deep zoom theory and practice (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the idea that there are few limits on knowledge growth.","quote":"","text":"There are virtually no limits (for Mandelbrot and computing in general) because there are few limits on the growth of knowledge [5]. In a few decades we will have learned to take CO2 (carbon dioxide) molecules out of the air [4] and rearrange the carbon atoms in 3D structures atom by atom [6]. We will be able to grow the transistors and the solar cells virtually for free. Energy will be virtually free, a squandrable abundance of free and clean energy [2]. At that point we will start automatically self-assembling Dyson Swarms constructions of solar cells with transistors on the back [1] on the Quebibyte scale to capture all the solar output of the sun [3] and get near-infinite compute for free. We would finally be able to explore the Mandelbrot space at full depth within our lifetime. [1] [LINK] and [LINK] [2] Bob Metcalfe Ethernet [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] Richard Feynman Plenty of Room at the Bottom [LINK] [5] David Deutsch: Chemical scum that dream of distant quasars [VIDEO] [6] [LINK] and [LINK]"},{"id":22105115,"author":"grabbalacious","month":"2020-01","story":"A singer apologized, closed his eyes, dropped his head and died onstage","stance":"reference","why":"Linked to illustrate living space for humanity at huge scales.","quote":"","text":"1. It won't effect it. Considering that the entire current human population could fit into France or Germany at about the same density as some parts in SE Asia. Not saying this is desirable. But consider how many people could live in the entire solar system? Ultimately in intergalactic space: [VIDEO] [LINK] 2. Any neuroplasticity loss due to biological aging is itself accumulated damage which will have to be repaired as part of life extension. 3. Human evolution is now overwhelmingly the evolution not of genes but of ideas/culture/memes. 4. That's for an individual to decide. At the moment there are plenty of old people who can apparently think of no reason to live but they hide the fact. They are in effect waiting around to die. They eventually go to hospital and care home for a few years before death which entails a fair bit of suffering and costs a lot of money. This is a sad and somewhat cruel situation in my opinion. 5. I bet that the cheapest way to treat things like heart disease and cancer will turn out to be life extension technology (i.e. damage repair). So you may have to decide not to get treatment for things which are currently routinely treated by conventional means. Of course you might still fall down the stairs..."},{"id":11787804,"author":"lomnakkus","month":"2016-05","story":"Crying","stance":"reference","why":"Deutsch's talk on relativistic jets cited as something that moves the commenter.","quote":"David Deutsch's talk about relativistic jets[1]","text":"Since we all seem to be in a sharing mood... Also a guy -- no beard -- but (and I'm not joking here)... I watched that exact trailer just now and teared up a little. As for movies, I have two examples: Up (pure emotion) and The Fountain -- the scene towards the end where he talks to the high priest and Clint Mansell's music takes over. For me, I think it's generally sort of really EPIC things that do it. Really huge scales also tend to do it, for example some of the star size comparisons, or David Deutsch's talk about relativistic jets[1], or just contemplating the fact that some black holes swallow a solar mass per day . Sometimes it's just \"beauty\", e.g. I couldn't help tearing up when Carolyn Porco showed the \"Saturn Eclipse\"[2] picture in her Ted Talk[3]. Some of that, I think, was because of the realization that we humans had actually sent a thing out there to take that picture. (Btw, I think I'm generally considered a bit of a cold fish among my friends/acquaintances, FWIW. Humans are weird.) [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] (WARNING: Image is HUGE!) [3] [LINK] (I won't link you to the exact time because the video deserves to be seen its entirety. If you really want to skip, skip to 15m15s.)"}]},"h0962biiZa4":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it for specific statements, such as Hassabis on AGI and safety buffers, rather than recommending it outright.","m":[{"id":33856135,"author":"passion__desire","month":"2022-12","story":"Show HN: Whole Git repo was made with ChatGPT","stance":"reference","why":"Sam Harris on value-aligned superintelligence","quote":"","text":"\"And what would excite me about actually the birth of superintelligent AI - one of the things, apart from solving obvious problems like curing disease and energy issues and all the rest, perhaps differs a little bit with what Stuart said. I’m not so worried about idiocracy or all of us just losing our way as apes and living unproductive lives in dialogue with these oracles. I think actually, I would want a truly value-aligned superintelligence to incrementally show us, not merely conserve what we want, but show us what we should want to keep improving our values so that we can navigate in the space of all possible experiences and converge on better and better ones.\" - Sam Harris Source : [VIDEO]"},{"id":26372415,"author":"Veedrac","month":"2021-03","story":"Multimodal Neurons in Artificial Neural Networks","stance":"reference","why":"Hassabis on AGI likelihood","quote":"","text":"Demis Hassabis. [VIDEO] , older [LINK] (NB ‘likely’ ≠ ‘>50%’, not sure what his percentage would be) I can give more but I did upstream link to a paper on this so it seems pretty pointless."},{"id":14878665,"author":"nopinsight","month":"2017-07","story":"Thoughts on OpenAI, reinforcement learning, and killer robots","stance":"reference","why":"Experts saying AGI is possible","quote":"agree that AGI is definitely possible and going to happen.","text":"Top people including DeepMind's CEO Demis Hassabis and Prof Stuart Russell, a AAAI and AAAS fellow who is a co-author of the AI textbook used at most of top universities, agree that AGI is definitely possible and going to happen. [1] Hassabis also stated in another session that there are probably at least half a dozen mountains to climb before reaching AGI and he would be surprised if it takes more than 20. He also said that it has been easier to develop major advances than he thought. [2] Considering that there has been about one major leap per year in neural networks architecture and systems for the past 3-4 years, and this includes training neural networks to do one-shot learning for physical robot control after practicing in a simulator, should we really be that complacent? Even if the advances slow down to one per 2 years and it takes 12 of them to get to AGI, that's only 24 years. If the rate continues to be one per year, that's only 12 years. [1] From the start of [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] Note: Reinforcement Learning is far from the only component being researched at major labs, including DeepMind and OpenAI."},{"id":14431052,"author":"rojobuffalo","month":"2017-05","story":"AlphaGo's next move","stance":"reference","why":"Hassabis on why a lead buffer helps AI safety","quote":"The rationale for why a buffer would be good is described by Demis Hassabis here","text":"DeepMind's mission is to build AGI. I think it's probably good if they have a buffered lead on all other efforts. That concern probably weighs on decisions about releasing code. The rationale for why a buffer would be good is described by Demis Hassabis here: [VIDEO] ...the main points are: there may be safety considerations along the way that are costly. More \"capitalistic\" organizations may decide to shortcut those costs because of the winner-take-all scenario. DeepMind is at least nominally very committed to safety. Releasing AlphaGo's source code would probably reduce DeepMind's buffer, which in theory, would also reduce safety."},{"id":26623714,"author":"beckman466","month":"2021-03","story":"How We’ll Know an AI Is Conscious","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"creeps me out (Musk comes out the most sane)","text":"> This is silly and ridiculously judgmental No it really is not. Just watching this panel/Q&A at the 'Future of Life Institute', with Elon Musk and others answering the question \"is some form of super-intelligence possible?\", creeps me out (Musk comes out the most sane): [VIDEO] > The author’s point is that by (say) 2050 we will likely see AI asking unsettlingly “intelligent” questions More unsettling than the questions of a young child? What unsettling questions could it ask according to you? I just do not see what you're getting at, at all. The question that arises when I read comments like yours is: are some humans so alienated from themselves and others that they believe machines will be able to invent and do science better than humans can? I just don't see the use cases. Really, can someone please explain to me what the point is in spending ludicrous amounts of scarce natural resources and time into this notion of creating a human-like AI, when we have so many other challenges we are facing: environmental pollution and poisoning, financialization of housing, e-waste and right-to-repair (should really be called 'right-to-poison' since companies today are basically legally allowed to pollute [1]), and privatized science and technology (Sci-Hub)? If we do want to build a human-like AI, shouldn't we first try to create a system where all humans have equal and free access to the sum total of all inherited knowledge? There is so much gatekeeping going on today, and I would lik"}]},"jf9I04Oa-hU":{"wy":"HN uses it only as a joke when computer repair privacy comes up.","m":[{"id":45504726,"author":"TZubiri","month":"2025-10","story":"Who owns Express VPN, Nord, Surfshark? VPN relationships explained (2024)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"here's some more artistic retort to privacy schizophrenia.","text":"\"Obviously a VPN provider can also do this, but you might hope they're less likely to.\" So you have identified some marginal privacy issue, and have identified that a VPN doesn't solve it, but rather that it moves the risk to a third party actor you subjectively feel is better. Well I feel that, subjectively, introducing a third party generally decreases security. I believe that not all privacy and security considerations can or should be solved technically, but rather we have extra-technical mechanisms like law and social norms that provide some protection on the edge cases. For example, an employee cannot lookup information for personal reasons on a system they are entrusted to in a professional capacity. I'm no expert, but you probably have first laws that prohibit that, second corporate policy that prohibits that, and thirdly social pressure that prohibits that to some extent. Are they perfect? Not necessarily which is why for the most part we rely on technical encryption and security mechanisms. But at some point these examples become so contrived and the medicine becomes the poison, so you enter into territory that is pretty standard in other industries, what's to stop a waiter from spitting into a cup? There's no spit filter in place of McDonalds, there's other mechanisms protecting us. On a similar note, logic and debate is not the only way to convey this phenomenon, so here's some more artistic retort to privacy schizophrenia. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33796238,"author":"BrianHenryIE","month":"2022-11","story":"No Privacy in the Electronics Repair Industry","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The plan? Put customers at ease by offering the world's first asexual computer repair.","text":"Nathan Fielder (who graduated from one of Canada's top business schools with really good grades) tried to address this in an episode of Nathan For You (s04e07). The plan? Put customers at ease by offering the world's first asexual computer repair. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27423473,"author":"beckman466","month":"2021-06","story":"Apple pays out millions to student after repair techs shared her personal images","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Nothing is more powerful than an idea whose time has come.","text":"Talented business consultant Nathan Fielder covered this topic not long ago. I believe he was the first one to spot this gap in the market for an asexual computer repair service. He actually shares his findings with us here: [VIDEO] \"Nothing is more powerful than an idea whose time has come.\""}]},"kAqIJZeeXEc":{"wy":"HN uses it only as a joke reference to declining intelligence or silly marketing logic.","m":[{"id":43863236,"author":"1970-01-01","month":"2025-05","story":"How the Water System Works","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Water got what plants crave.","text":"Water is why life never left Earth. We would already be living on Mars or the moon if there was just a lake or two of water. Water got what plants crave. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40436626,"author":"emestifs","month":"2024-05","story":"Windows Copilot Runtime","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Someone someday may actually ship code like this into production. Horrifying to think about. For some reason this reminds me of trying to grow plants with Brawndo [VIDEO]"},{"id":15364291,"author":"adsfqwop","month":"2017-09","story":"Average IQ scores seem to have been falling and population ageing may be why","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The movie \"Idiocracy\" inevitably comes to mind","text":"I shudder to think what happens in 20 more years, when the last potato peelers are gone :-/ The movie \"Idiocracy\" inevitably comes to mind [1] :( However I do think it's something more than just the burden or unburden of our new technology. Logical thinking should be an universal trait, no matter what the environment is like. However one thing which is eating into that equation, is logical thinking is almost never promoted in modern education. Even in a declining IQ environment, promoting and teaching logical thinking would reverse a lot of the seeming \"stupidity\" in our society. But I still do think there is something more going on. It's not just (lack of) education. [1]: [VIDEO]"}]},"ljx0Zh7eidE":{"wy":"Pointed to as a primary-source account of Objective-C's origins and the early days of NeXT and Apple.","q":{"x":"The Computer History Museum has a two part interview with Steve Naroff, one of the engineers who worked on Objective C","i":41147516,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42128899,"author":"Austin_Conlon","month":"2024-11","story":"Steve Jobs, NeXTSTEP, and early object-oriented programming (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among related oral histories","quote":"Oral History of Steve Naroff [Video]","text":"Related: - Oral History of Blaine Garst [PDF]: [LINK] - Oral History of Blaine Garst [Video]: [LINK] - Oral History of Steve Naroff [PDF]: [LINK] - Oral History of Steve Naroff [Video]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41147516,"author":"nequo","month":"2024-08","story":"1991 WWW-NeXT Implementation","stance":"reference","why":"Two-part CHM interview with an Objective-C engineer","quote":"The Computer History Museum has a two part interview with Steve Naroff, one of the engineers who worked on Objective C","text":"Yes. The Computer History Museum has a two part interview with Steve Naroff, one of the engineers who worked on Objective C: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":23510307,"author":"cxr","month":"2020-06","story":"Design Principles Behind Smalltalk (1981)","stance":"reference","why":"Oral history interviews with Naroff, who discusses objects and Objective-C","quote":"Steve Naroff (from Stepstone/NeXT/Apple) did a recent-ish set of interviews with the Computer History Museum[2][3]","text":"At the risk of putting readers off by the title, there's a video essay on YouTube called \"Object-Oriented Programming is Bad\"[1]. It's both thoughtful and thought-provoking, although I don't agree with the conclusion. Steve Naroff (from Stepstone/NeXT/Apple) did a recent-ish set of interviews with the Computer History Museum[2][3] and referenced that video a bit (and extemporized about objects in small parts throughout). Side note: Naroff and Brad Cox are set to present a paper on Objective-C at this year's HOPL[4]. I wish it were as readily available as Allen Wirfs-Brock's JS paper for the same conference[5][6]. 1. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO] 3. [LINK] 4. [LINK] 5. [LINK] 6. [LINK]"}]},"mPna6D21Eqg":{"wy":"Referenced mainly for Bak's firsthand account of why the Dart VM never shipped in Chrome and for his IoT language design points.","q":{"x":"then the whole mobile shift happen and there was no room for extra stuff in chrome","i":22454561,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23378711,"author":"sebe","month":"2020-06","story":"Featherweight Go","stance":"reference","why":"Lars said Dart VM intent for Chrome was dropped.","quote":"Lars gave a talk last year and said the intent was to put dart vm in chrome","text":"Lars gave a talk last year and said the intent was to put dart vm in chrome, then the whole mobile thing happen and there was no room for extra stuff in chrome. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22454561,"author":"sebe","month":"2020-03","story":"Flutter and Dart, or how to quickly build a mobile app without losing your hair","stance":"reference","why":"Lars explains why Dart didn't make it into Chrome.","quote":"then the whole mobile shift happen and there was no room for extra stuff in chrome","text":"They certainly did find a new purpose for Dart outside of it being used in parts of their Ads platform, but Flutter, when is was the chrome experiment called sky, first started out using Javascript. The chrome guys decided to move on and look at several language before going with Dart, Swift may have been an option, if it was opensource at the time. Seems a reason for Dart's failure in the web space was mobile. Lar gave a talk last year about his and Kasper iot startup [LINK] he mentions why dart didn't make it into chrome, saying \"then the whole mobile shift happen and there was no room for extra stuff in chrome\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":22454764,"author":"sebe","month":"2020-03","story":"Flutter and Dart, or how to quickly build a mobile app without losing your hair","stance":"reference","why":"Lars on why Dart VM wasn't shipped in Chrome.","quote":"then the whole mobile shift happen and there was no room for extra stuff in chrome","text":"> my strong suspicion is the Chrome team killed the idea of shipping a Dart VM in Chrome Yeah in a video last year, Lars said \"then the whole mobile shift happen and there was no room for extra stuff in chrome\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":22691397,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2020-03","story":"Smalltalk-87","stance":"reference","why":"Lars Bak working on Dart for IoT.","quote":"","text":"And then Lars Bak left, I guess desilusioned with out things turned out for Dart, and is now doing \"Dart for IoT\" back in Denmark. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20496665,"author":"tosh","month":"2019-07","story":"Lars Bak: The Making of a Secure and Robust IoT Development Platform","stance":"reference","why":"Timestamp listing language design features at minute 21.","quote":"minute 21","text":"minute 21: [VIDEO] * Object-oriented * Indentation-based syntax * Clean object model * Blocks with non-local returns * No eval and no runtime reflection * Tracing garbage collection"}]},"nsb2XBAIWyA":{"wy":"Cited repeatedly in Tesla threads as concrete side-by-side evidence that Tesla's self-parking lags other manufacturers, undercutting its full self-driving claims.","q":{"x":"All cars nailed the spots they tested flawlessly while the Tesla just straight up didn't see most of the spots.","i":34333879,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34717088,"author":"inferiorhuman","month":"2023-02","story":"Apple signs union agreement for Glasgow Apple Store staff","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"You spent your whole comment focusing on their self driving claims while ignoring the rest of the company. The rest of the company? You mean the senior management that lord elno raided in an attempt to prop up Twitter? The VW ID4 has numerous complaints about their software and reliability. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] That's some impressive software."},{"id":39175068,"author":"akmarinov","month":"2024-01","story":"On a Tesla: Don't","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"just look at these videos as a comparison","text":"Tesla focused on FSD almost exclusively and everything in the car's software suffered because of that and stupid decisions to remove hardware. Autopilot is on a separate branch from FSD, not getting significant updates with the promise that at some point, it'll be merged with the FSD branch and it'll be super awesome - this year for sure, but maybe next year or 10 years after, look we made a robot that's definitely not a guy in a suit and it'll be out in 5 years, maybe 10, it can water plants now!, look a Cybertruck! isn't steel cool, etc When they switched from Mobileye to their own stack, it noticeably regressed in performance, then they worked to get it to work decently, but dropped the radar during Covid, going all in on Vision, regressing it even further. They promised at that time to at some point match the original Autopilot by reintroducing a distance of 1 and a max speed higher than 140 km/h and it's been 2 years since then with nothing on the front. Summon/ Smart summon as the guy in the article points out - is great, but doesn't work without USS sensors, which they removed and the functionality is coming back some day, but likely not soon, because it's not a priority. They've been selling cars for more than a year now that don't have that functionality, even though it's in a package worth 4000 euros that gets you basically nothing. Autopark - same thing, just look at these videos as a comparison - [VIDEO] [LINK] It's easily the worst out of all manufacturers. In th"},{"id":36844440,"author":"zuhsetaqi","month":"2023-07","story":"With the 745-Mile Solid-State Battery, Toyota Just Became a Force to Reckon With","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It can't even do basic tasks with super slow speed","text":"> Yet to see other auto manufacturers come anywhere near Tesla’s driver assistance system. [VIDEO] There is nothing more to say about the 'glorious Tesla driver assistance system'. It can't even do basic tasks with super slow speed, no moving objects and all time in the world."},{"id":35428240,"author":"Hamuko","month":"2023-04","story":"Tesla removes parking sensors, the results are predictably terrible","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Tesla's performance was pretty all over the place there too.","text":"Pretty sure they use ultrasonic sensors. This channel actually had an automated parking test with many different EVs, and Tesla's performance was pretty all over the place there too. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35159419,"author":"clouddrover","month":"2023-03","story":"Pentagon Looking to Make Sure SpaceX Doesn’t Abandon Them in War","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"In reality Teslas can't even park themselves","text":"> He's never been in it for the money. Ah, so he must be in it for the bullshit and the lies. Like with his supposed full self-driving. Falsely claiming FSD \"next year\", year after year: [LINK] In reality Teslas can't even park themselves: [VIDEO] Falsely claiming all Teslas have the hardware for FSD in 2016: [LINK] In reality the older Teslas now won't get Hardware 4: [LINK] That's some real good good faith."},{"id":34333879,"author":"culi","month":"2023-01","story":"Tesla Vehicle Safety Report","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"All cars nailed the spots they tested flawlessly while the Tesla just straight up didn't see most of the spots.","text":"Someone else on HN commented this the other day. RSymons RSEV did a comparison[0] of the self-park feature of Tesla's compared to three other cars. All cars nailed the spots they tested flawlessly while the Tesla just straight up didn't see most of the spots. When they moved the cars further and further away so that the spot became more obvious it eventually did see the spot but parked over a foot from the curb Tesla seems to have a lot of software issues when it comes to \"smart\" features like these. I wouldn't be surprised if they are indeed worse than the competition, but I'd love to see a similar comparison [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34543096,"author":"clouddrover","month":"2023-01","story":"Why is Tesla FSD afraid of the dark?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Teslas don't even do full self-parking, nevermind full self-driving","text":"Good, then there's no reason to rip people off in the meantime. Refund the money now and only charge customers money if and when they deliver actual factual full self-driving. Tesla backed themselves into this corner. They have lied about the state of full self-driving for years and they have failed to deliver on their own self imposed deadlines. Teslas don't even do full self-parking, nevermind full self-driving: [VIDEO] Tesla admits they have failed to deliver (as an attempt to avoid being done for fraud): [LINK] [LINK] So, now that Tesla has done the hard thing of admitting to their failure, they can do the easy thing of returning the money."},{"id":34596322,"author":"daveguy","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: What is the weirdest or most surreal (recent) technology you have seen?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Tesla's implementation is laughable.","text":"They can't even keep up with self-parking technology. All the other electric car manufacturers don't seem to have any problem with it. But Tesla's implementation is laughable. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34601704,"author":"clouddrover","month":"2023-01","story":"Tesla tells investors it’s being investigated by the Justice Department","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"And these days Teslas can't even do full self-parking","text":"> People will pay $20K for sport brakes or $10K for an unusual paint color. But they get the brakes and they get the paint. What they get with supposed \"full self-driving\" is puffery and lies. Musk's public statements around FSD since 2014 have been lies: [LINK] In 2016 Tesla claimed that all Teslas being produced \"will have the hardware needed for full self-driving capability at a safety level substantially greater than that of a human driver.\" That was a lie: [LINK] And these days Teslas can't even do full self-parking: [VIDEO] Now the word is that suckers who paid for FSD in a Tesla with Hardware 3 probably won't get Hardware 4: [LINK] Tesla was recently done for false advertising around FSD: [LINK] Tesla also admits they have failed to deliver full self-driving but they think they don't need to refund any money because \"failure is not fraud\": [LINK] Despite the admission of failure, Musk is still running around claiming that FSD will one day magically become the \"biggest asset value increase of anything in history\": [LINK] It's not even bullshit artistry. It's just bullshit."},{"id":33866204,"author":"inferiorhuman","month":"2022-12","story":"Tesla Semi, a Deep Dive","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It doesn't need to be parked because it basically can't park itself.","text":"How does an autonomous car not need to be parked? It doesn't need to be parked because it basically can't park itself. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34120341,"author":"dagmx","month":"2022-12","story":"California passes law banning Tesla from calling software FSD","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The Tesla auto park is legitimately awful.","text":"To add to one of your points, The Tesla auto park is legitimately awful. [VIDEO] Every time we try it for a spot to reverse into, the linear regression it must be doing is so apparent. It violently tries to turn left and right rapidly trying to straighten itself. It works better for parallel spots but often gives up half way because it made a bad angle choice. We’ve tried it in various models in addition to our own Model Y. They’re just embarrassingly bad at it."},{"id":33590201,"author":"clouddrover","month":"2022-11","story":"The tech CEO spending millions to stop Elon Musk","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Other manufacturers are better at it","text":"I don't think you need to spend millions to see the lack of capability in Tesla's supposed full self-driving. Tesla doesn't even achieve full self-parking. Other manufacturers are better at it: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":33089601,"author":"dagmx","month":"2022-10","story":"Tesla Vision Update: Replacing Ultrasonic Sensors with Tesla Vision","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Tesla is probably the WORST at automatic parking.","text":"Tesla is probably the WORST at automatic parking. Here’s a comparison against some other competitors. [VIDEO] My own model Y has maybe successfully automatically parked 2/10 times. Most of my friends and coworkers have similarly low success rates."},{"id":33350447,"author":"clouddrover","month":"2022-10","story":"Tesla faces U.S. criminal probe over self-driving claims","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The Teslas perform the worst.","text":"> Everything Tesla/SpaceX has done has been: 1)Light years above the competition Here are some EVs trying to park themselves: [VIDEO] The Teslas perform the worst. Tesla is yet to achieve full self-parking, never mind full self-driving."},{"id":33354073,"author":"formerly_proven","month":"2022-10","story":"Tesla faces U.S. criminal probe over self-driving claims","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"There's a hilarious video where you can see a modern Tesla utterly failing at parking","text":"There's a hilarious video where you can see a modern Tesla utterly failing at parking while an old Tesla with mobileye works just fine [VIDEO]"},{"id":33400675,"author":"clouddrover","month":"2022-10","story":"Why Tesla removed radar and ultrasonic sensors [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Teslas do poorly at self parking","text":"> You're not asking the automation to walk Tesla should aim for parking first. Teslas do poorly at self parking: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33414412,"author":"clouddrover","month":"2022-10","story":"Ford drops full self-driving cars","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Teslas aren't very good at backing themselves into any space","text":"It's because they've committed to the lie for years now and they've taken people's money on the promise of full self-driving that has still not been delivered. Tesla has falsely claimed that full self-driving will be delivered \"next year\" for years: [LINK] Tesla falsely claimed in 2016 that all Teslas had \"the hardware needed for full self-driving capability at a safety level substantially greater than that of a human driver\": [LINK] And Elon Musk said that without full self-driving Tesla is \"worth basically zero\": [LINK] Tesla has pointlessly backed itself into this corner, even though Teslas aren't very good at backing themselves into any space: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32770489,"author":"microtherion","month":"2022-09","story":"Rolls Royce Ends Boom Supersonic Partnership","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the in-house products are still not on par with the third party predecessors","text":"Tesla certainly delivers cars. But there are parts of it that are quite Theranesque, especially the self driving: * Re-sell third party products for a while (\"Until our own offering is ready\"). If the recently posted self parking video is to be believed, the in-house products are still not on par with the third party predecessors: [VIDEO] * Pre-sell your product years before it's ready. * Aggressively suppress internal and external criticism. * Avoid objective evaluations of your product."},{"id":32601662,"author":"clouddrover","month":"2022-08","story":"Tesla wants videos of its cars running over child-sized dummies taken down","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Tesla is not competitive with other manufacturers on simple tests like self parking","text":"This is true to form. Musk recently told a Tesla owner to stop complaining about \"full self-driving\" not working very well. Musk plainly believes in free speech for himself, but not for others: [LINK] The reality is Tesla is not competitive with other manufacturers on simple tests like self parking: [VIDEO] Instead of running around trying to suppress \"bad press\", Tesla should focus on making their cars better."},{"id":32606532,"author":"clouddrover","month":"2022-08","story":"California votes to ban new gas car sales by 2035","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Tesla struggles with FSP (full self-parking)","text":"By autonomy you might mean range or autonomous driving. Tesla doesn't lead in range: [LINK] Tesla struggles with FSP (full self-parking): [VIDEO] Tesla doesn't take liability for its semi-autonomous driving, whereas Mercedes does: [LINK] There are many areas Tesla doesn't lead."}]},"ovnUyTRMERI":{"wy":"Cited in COVID origin debates as footage showing Wuhan CDC scientists handling live bats and bat coronaviruses.","q":{"x":"You can see him doing his work, including live bat collections, in this (government produced) documentary about the Wuhan CDC","i":22917883,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":31869141,"author":"mikeyouse","month":"2022-06","story":"What happened to the lab-leak hypothesis?","stance":"reference","why":"Footage showing horseshoe bats studied near Wuhan.","quote":"they're on video...","text":"Yes, the WIV does indeed study the bats that we're questioning. I'm not sure what point you're trying to make? The claim you're questioning is \"There are horseshoe bats near Wuhan\". Do you think they just invented the bats? I mean... they're on video... [VIDEO]"},{"id":30876835,"author":"voldacar","month":"2022-04","story":"“This shouldn’t happen”: Inside the virus-hunting nonprofit EcoHealth Alliance","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as supporting source in a lab leak argument.","quote":"","text":"I had a longer response but my browser crashed so I'll just try to summarize my thoughts. Our disagreement basically comes down to how we assign priors. I'm not really sure how productive a debate on priors can be because it's not like there are a bunch of previous similar scenarios that we can study in detail. On P(WIV leak): Wikipedia[0] lists 50 lab leaks (or more precisely, failure of lab biosecurity measures) in the past century. The actual number is probably much higher because people and institutions like to hide their mistakes. So the base probability of a lab leak happening from a lab that studies pathogens is only moderately low to begin with. But P(WIV leak) is probably significantly higher than average here, because WIV had worse than average safety procedure. - According to leaked cables from 2018, american officials & scientists were already concerned about unsafe practices & lack of properly trained personnel at WIV[1] - WIV researchers collected unknown viruses from bat caves with only hazmat suits + surgical mask as PPE, and handled vials of viral vector specimens without even masks[2]. If they demonstrated poor attention to safety outside the lab, it is likely that they disregarded it within the lab as well - I recall reading something similar involving Shi Zhengli but I can't find the link now - Shi Zhengli admitted (!) that bat coronavirus research was performed under BSL-2 and BSL-3 conditions, both of which are significantly more lax than BSL-4[3] On P(l"},{"id":23101162,"author":"gridlockd","month":"2020-05","story":"Wuhan lab did research on bat viruses, but no evidence of accidental release","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence Wuhan CDC performed bat experiments","quote":"the Wuhan CDC indeed was performing experiments on bats","text":"> I'm not sure who you're quoting, but it's not me. Indeed, I oversaw that it was \"yorwba\" who put that comment into \"our\" thread, not you. My apologies. > As for the claim that the Wuhan cdc is tight next to the seafood market, I direct you to this comment: [LINK] which thoroughly and completely debunks those claims. That's not a \"thorough and complete\" debunking, that's an erroneous assessment regarding the functions and location(s) of the Wuhan CDC, based on a cursory glance at websites. A little bit more research would've revealed that the Wuhan CDC indeed was performing experiments on bats and that it has several facilities (including one near the seafood market). [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":23101206,"author":"gridlockd","month":"2020-05","story":"Wuhan lab did research on bat viruses, but no evidence of accidental release","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Wuhan CDC scientists studying live bats and bat coronaviruses.","quote":"Scientists at the Wuhan CDC are studying live bats and bat coronaviruses","text":"> If you search on Google Maps for the Wuhan Centers for Disease Prevention and Control, you instead find a building about 1.5 km from the seafood market. There is not just one building, this is the one mentioned: [LINK] Remember that Google is effectively not operating in China anymore, so their information is bound to be outdated and incomplete. > But further, when I look at the web page of the WHCDC, it looks like they're mainly a community health institution, not a research institute. The people studying coronaviruses are 15 km away, in the Wuhan Institute of Virology. Scientists at the Wuhan CDC are studying live bats and bat coronaviruses: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22915271,"author":"holopol","month":"2020-04","story":"Our Long Bets and Predictions about 02020","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary of Wuhan virus sample collection","quote":"collecting viruses is had work, as shown in this video from December 10, 2019","text":"While the analyses above suggest that SARS-CoV-2 may bind human ACE2 with high affinity, computational analyses predict that the interaction is not ideal7 and that the RBD sequence is different from those shown in SARS-CoV to be optimal for receptor binding. Thus, the high-affinity binding of the SARS-CoV-2 spike protein to human ACE2 is most likely the result of natural selection on a human or human-like ACE2 that permits another optimal binding solution to arise. This is strong evidence that SARS-CoV-2 is not the product of purposeful manipulation. [LINK] concludes that since maybe (because computational analysis) it isn't as efficient for the receptor binding it wasn't purposefully manipulated. Maybe making the most efficient virus was not the goal, maybe there were many trials and many attempts to get a virus with certain qualities and this one was a failure that got mishandled. --- Anyway, collecting viruses is had work, as shown in this video from December 10, 2019 by the Shanghai Media Group (SMG, one of China's largest state-owned media): [VIDEO] The road is bumpy but we must learn to stick to our initial choice when we feel confused and lost. In the past 10 years we have visited every corner of Hubei Province. We explored dozens of undeveloped caves and studied more than 300 types of virus vectors. But I do hope these virus samples will only be preserved for scientific research and will never be used in real life. Because humans need not only the vaccines but also th"},{"id":22917883,"author":"jnbiche","month":"2020-04","story":"Our Long Bets and Predictions about 02020","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Wuhan CDC collector doing live bat collection","quote":"You can see him doing his work, including live bat collections, in this (government produced) documentary about the Wuhan CDC","text":"Your assertion was, \"At the very least, proponent of this theory should be able to provide evidence that bats or viral samples from bats were present in the lab before the outbreak\". Yes, it's clear they were working with live bats of the species that most scientists, even those who believe in the pangolin hypothesis, believe is the natural reservoir of SARS-CoV-2. Multiple papers published from BOTH Wuhan labs describe such bat research. The Wuhan CDC did so much live animal research that they employed collections expert by the name of Tian Jun-Hua, whose work collecting bats is described in a number of pre-pandemic media reports (including several times he quarantined himself after being exposed to gross amounts of bad urine or blood). You can see him doing his work, including live bat collections, in this (government produced) documentary about the Wuhan CDC: [VIDEO] But to your question, here's a paper from the Wuhan CDC lab published in a well-known international journal that I happened to have open: Guo WP, Lin XD, Wang W, et al. \"Phylogeny and Origins of Hantaviruses Harbored by Bats, Insectivores, and Rodents\" PLoS pathogens Before you stop reading and respond, \"those are hantaviruses they were studying. Covid-19 is a coronavirus.\", let me stop you. My hypothesis, like others who believe this is a possibility, has never been that they were studying SARS-CoV-2 specifically, but rather that the lab, or a lab-adjacent facility, was where the virus accidentally \"spilled o"}]},"pQHX-SjgQvQ":{"wy":"HN drops it as a joke whenever threads touch on new interfaces, books or technology confusing users.","m":[{"id":39214207,"author":"RecycledEle","month":"2024-02","story":"I don't want anything your AI generates","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Also relevant","text":"When Gutenberg asked people what they thought of his printing press, they replied that it lacked the soul of a hand-copied book and they would never buy a book printed on it. Also relevant: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34719797,"author":"geenew","month":"2023-02","story":"The Teen Mental Illness Epidemic Began Around 2012","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Medieval helpdesk","text":"> reading books Not sure how much currency this has lately, but back at the dawn of Web 2.0, this was well-known comedy. Relates 'books' to 'tech'. Medieval helpdesk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3218718,"author":"kstenerud","month":"2011-11","story":"Scrollbars: Why is Apple eradicating a linchpin of user interface design?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Books were not without their difficulties, however","text":"Pages were superior because: 1. Scrolls were more difficult to handle as you read. 2. You could jump around to various places in a long book document, but with a scroll you had to manually unravel it to the point you were looking for, a time consuming process. 3. Books were not without their difficulties, however: [VIDEO] But in all seriousness, the scroll concept works perfectly well, in fact better than the book concept, provided you have the right technology to drive it. And modern computers have that technology. I can use a search box to jump to wherever I want. I can quickly scroll up and down in the document using a scroll bar rather than paging through a book."},{"id":2651482,"author":"stan_rogers","month":"2011-06","story":"When hard books disappear","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"requires a link to this classic clip from Øystein og jeg","text":"I'm on the \"dead trees or their equivalent forever\" side myself (search doesn't work well with paraphrases, and the physical book has more physical cues for random retrieval of imperfectly remembered passages), but \"the 'flip pages with your finger' UI has proved immensely durable\" requires a link to this classic clip from Øystein og jeg : [VIDEO]"},{"id":550902,"author":"pclark","month":"2009-04","story":"Twi.bz: the transparent URL shortener","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"their \"pretty\" url is much longer than the original one.","text":"thats a remarkable service. They take this ugly url: [VIDEO] [38 characters] and turn it into this pretty url: [LINK] [47 characters] their \"pretty\" url is much longer than the original one. What the hell!?"}]},"rmsIZUuBoQs":{"wy":"HN uses it as a quick citation of Alan Kay's dislike of Linux, in threads questioning whether the Unix way is the final word.","q":{"x":"the fundamental reason for why I avoid it, is best explained in this video.","i":16455613,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":47735089,"author":"morphle","month":"2026-04","story":"Apple Silicon and Virtual Machines: Beating the 2 VM Limit","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Nextstep was much better than Linux crap.","text":"Apple had real Unix a decade before the Linux crap was made, a bad unix copy. Nextstep was much better than Linux crap. \"A budget of bad ideas\" is what Alan Kay said about Linux [1], he invented the personal computer. My 1987-1997 ISP was based on several different Unix running on Apple, probably long before you where born. Apple built several supercomputers. [1] [VIDEO] [2] Founder School Session: The Future Doesn't Have to Be Incremental [LINK]"},{"id":32601685,"author":"georgewsinger","month":"2022-08","story":"What have we lost? – Demo of exotic OSes (2021) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Alan Kay's dislike of Linux","quote":"Alan Kay really hates Linux","text":"This reminds me that Alan Kay really hates Linux: [VIDEO] This stings since I really like Alan Kay and have been influenced by his ideas, but am also working on a Linux-based VR headset as a thinking tool[1]. I think Alan would approve of the hardware but not the software, perhaps ultimately saying something like \"you can spend your whole life dicking around in Linux, but still not understand anything about computing\". [1] [LINK]"},{"id":23235759,"author":"NotUsingLinux","month":"2020-05","story":"Linux Productivity Tools (2019) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Linux is a distraction?","text":"I guess this is not the place to discuss about how to change all of computing and how it should be done better and that Linux is a distraction? [VIDEO]"},{"id":17074158,"author":"NiveaGeForce","month":"2018-05","story":"Shell Style Guide","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The Unix way of doing things, isn't the be all and all.","text":"> Not to put too fine a point on it, but that's nonsense. In PowerShell you pass structured data around instead of globs of text, which is much more maintainable. Using Bash on Windows is like going backwards, and it's sad that most developers don't even realize it. > Historically, PowerShell was supposed to be a replacement for cmd.exe and roughly equivalent to bash, yet suitable for the Windows environment. They tried early on to port some of the UNIX & Linux tools to Windows and it didn't fly, so this is what we're left with. Nonsense, Powershell is a fundamental improvement. It's not a \"this is what's we're left with\" situation. > Although parts of Powershell are open source now, I suspect the mere existence of WSL means that Microsoft understands it has more to gain from compatibility with the rest of the world (Bash is nigh-everywhere) than imposing their particular philosophy on people. Yeah, let's just drag everything down to the lowest common denominator. > (See also, Apple's decision to adopt UNIX underpinnings) Which is sad, since it could have been so much more. The Unix way of doing things, isn't the be all and all. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":16455613,"author":"NiveaGeForce","month":"2018-02","story":"Linux'izing your Windows PC into a dev machine – Part 2","stance":"reference","why":"Alan Kay's critique of Linux","quote":"the fundamental reason for why I avoid it, is best explained in this video.","text":"There are also many developers who are forced to use GNU/Linux dev tools, while prefering to use Windows. Not to mention that Windows has better support for most hardware and modern form factors, such as pen & touch enabled 2-in-1s. If you're buying something like a Surface Pro or Surface Book, the last thing you want to do is cripple its functionality, ease of use, performance and battery life by installing GNU/Linux. But the fundamental reason for why I avoid it, is best explained in this video. [VIDEO]"}]},"tMZ2j9yK_NY":{"wy":"Used purely as a joke reference when threads feature computers or AI giving unhelpful or evasive answers.","m":[{"id":43351517,"author":"jeffwass","month":"2025-03","story":"Cursor told me I should learn coding instead of asking it to generate it","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Predicted way back in 1971 in the classic movie “Willy Wonka and the Chocolate Factory”!","text":"Predicted way back in 1971 in the classic movie “Willy Wonka and the Chocolate Factory”! One of the many hysterical scenes I didn’t truly appreciate as a kid. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37747821,"author":"semiquaver","month":"2023-10","story":"Stop Arguing over Elite Schools. Just Make Public Colleges Free","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This question reminds me of the computer scene from “Willy Wonka”.","text":"This question reminds me of the computer scene from “Willy Wonka”. [VIDEO] I’m imagining the computer guru presenting his plan to Congress: “my 100% infallible computer model says here’s what the tax law should be. Please overhaul society now and make sure you don’t let any of your prior opinions cloud your judgement now that the computer has told you what’s best.” Computational models are models : they work best with systems we already basically understand the rules to or can at least approximate. Like weather or physics. Economics is a social science . Economies are incredibly dynamic systems because they’re made of people. Every change to the system causes the participants to change their behavior. People are significantly more complicated than Game Of Life gliders. A model that can correctly simulate the economy requires strong AGI or magic, neither of which have been invented yet."},{"id":29412935,"author":"yeuxardents","month":"2021-12","story":"Ask HN: What are these low quality “code snippet” sites?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It reminds me of the scene from the original willy wonka from that era","text":"It reminds me of the scene from the original willy wonka from that era, where they are trying to use a computer to predict where the next winning ticket will be. [VIDEO]"}]},"uFpK_r-jEXg":{"wy":"Used as a punchline whenever commenters see consensus-driven decisions, politicians chasing public opinion, or metric-driven content.","q":{"x":"My favorite bit, still stuck in my head whenever I see politicians from both parties rush to the scene of a protest or whatever","i":40164599,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42132456,"author":"TZubiri","month":"2024-11","story":"Netflix's Distributed Counter Abstraction","stance":"reference","why":"Source on metric-driven content direction","quote":"","text":"The value of netflix is probably not only on its technical prowess and app experience, but it seems they are pretty involved in content direction through metrics. ¹ ² Sources: [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41745037,"author":"slavik81","month":"2024-10","story":"Meta Movie Gen","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The Onion was ahead of the curve with \"New Live Poll Lets Punits Pander To Viewers In Real Time\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":41946669,"author":"0xDEAFBEAD","month":"2024-10","story":"Quit Social Media (2016)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The Onion did a sketch illustrating the problem a few years ago","text":"I would argue the defining factor of social media which separates it from blogs/forums/etc. is the ubiquitous feedback metrics, in the form of likes/downvotes/retweets/etc. Imagine attending party where whenever you finished a sentence, everyone who was listening immediately responded with a silent thumbs up / thumbs down in response. Pretty dystopian. The Onion did a sketch illustrating the problem a few years ago: [VIDEO] By this definition, HN is halfway to being social media. There are metrics, but they aren't displayed publicly."},{"id":40164599,"author":"jkestner","month":"2024-04","story":"Jeff Lawson buys The Onion","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"My favorite bit, still stuck in my head whenever I see politicians from both parties rush to the scene of a protest or whatever","text":"From what I gather, they invested in Onion News Network for IFC, and when that got canceled relatively quickly, they didn't risk online-only distribution. Maybe 2011 was too early to pivot to video? [LINK] My favorite bit, still stuck in my head whenever I see politicians from both parties rush to the scene of a protest or whatever, is \"New Live Poll Lets Pundits Pander To Viewers In Real Time\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":38242759,"author":"0xDEAFBEAD","month":"2023-11","story":"RIP Google Groups Dejanews.com Archive","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"We're now living this Onion satire from 14 years ago","text":"Yeah that sounds right to me. Even though use of the internet has expanded a lot since I was a kid, I remember making more genuine friends in the earlier days. My theory is that it has to do with the ubiquity of feedback mechanisms. Likes, followers, upvotes, etc. It's a never-ending popularity contest. We're now living this Onion satire from 14 years ago: [VIDEO] The ubiquity of the \"news feed\" concept could also play a role -- long-running discussions are now fairly impossible. People who disagree just take potshots at each other, instead of getting to the root of the disagreement. It's a shame there's so much homogeneity across different social sites. Wish people would experiment more."},{"id":23118896,"author":"erichurkman","month":"2020-05","story":"Think America’s divided now? Try the 1970s","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"we're living in how The Onion presented","text":"With online news and the rapid news cycles, we're living in how The Onion presented 'Live poll lets pundits pander in real time': [VIDEO]"},{"id":894275,"author":"ramanujan","month":"2009-10","story":"John Ousterhout on Startup Decision-Making","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"this Onion clip captures what happens during an open consensus based process","text":"Hmmm. But there is a reason that design by committee is a curse word. To his credit, I found it interesting that Ousterhout at least gave a hat tip to the dictator school of thought. I think though that there are many more examples which he omits. Linus -- \"Benevolent Dictator for Life\" Guido Van Rossum -- ditto Just about every other open source worthy -- ditto Larry & Sergey -- ditto If you don't have that ruthless product genius with \"taste\" you just aren't going to achieve greatness, IMHO. Open to counterexamples, but every time I've observed consensus based decision making it has devolved into politics and pre-meetings and vote-swapping. And this is among both tech academics and tech startups, who one would hope would be more rational. Actually, this Onion clip captures what happens during an open consensus based process where everyone can see each other's votes: [VIDEO] Real-time pandering . Instead what you may want to do is buttonhole people or small groups with deep knowledge of the decision area, ask for their input, debate if you must, and then go out with a united front."}]},"xC5uEe5OzNQ":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as the widely seen nostalgic video from the era when Linux desktop effects outpaced Vista.","q":{"x":"I remember watching it and being awed by the cube effect.","i":45254553,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45254553,"author":"aranelsurion","month":"2025-09","story":"Asciinema CLI 3.0 rewritten in Rust, adds live streaming, upgrades file format","stance":"reference","why":"Nostalgic cube effect video with millions of views.","quote":"I remember watching it and being awed by the cube effect.","text":"Compiz. Beryl. Compiz Fusion. Unlocks some fun memories. Also this video has some 6 million views now: [VIDEO] . I remember watching it and being awed by the cube effect."},{"id":38141536,"author":"seltzered_","month":"2023-11","story":"In KDE, the Desktop Cube is back","stance":"reference","why":"Popular 2007 video demoing cube effects and burning windows.","quote":"a popular video","text":"\"Here comes the hotstepper...\" ( see [LINK] for context linking to a popular video [VIDEO] from 2007 demoing some cube effects alongside burning windows and such.)"},{"id":29798448,"author":"quadrifoliate","month":"2022-01","story":"Burn My Windows","stance":"reference","why":"Popular Compiz versus Vista comparison from the 2000s.","quote":"Compiz was so far ahead it could not just replicate Vista's interface, but could also better it","text":"I instinctively expected to hear the notes of \"Here Comes the Hotstepper\" when I clicked on that video. I was expecting [VIDEO] which was somewhat popular to send around to show comparisons of how Compiz was so far ahead it could not just replicate Vista's interface, but could also better it in some aspects like the famous 3D cube. Thanks to you and others for working on it! Looking at the video almost 15 years later, I feel wistful for the joy that accompanied some of these desktop effects, and wonder where it has gone today."},{"id":29804936,"author":"chrismorgan","month":"2022-01","story":"Burn My Windows","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"$ yt-dlp -F [VIDEO] [youtube] [VIDEO] Downloading webpage [youtube] [VIDEO] Downloading android player API JSON [info] Available formats for [VIDEO] ID EXT RESOLUTION FPS │ FILESIZE TBR PROTO │ VCODEC VBR ACODEC ABR ASR MORE INFO ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── sb2 mhtml 48x27 │ mhtml │ images storyboard sb1 mhtml 60x45 │ mhtml │ images storyboard sb0 mhtml 120x90 │ mhtml │ images storyboard 139 m4a │ 1.55MiB 47k https │ audio only mp4a.40.5 47k 22050Hz low, m4a_dash 140 m4a │ 4.13MiB 127k https │ audio only mp4a.40.2 127k 44100Hz medium, m4a_dash 251 webm │ 4.11MiB 126k https │ audio only opus 126k 48000Hz medium, webm_dash 17 3gp 176x144 8 │ 2.67MiB 82k https │ mp4v.20.3 82k mp4a.40.2 0k 22050Hz 144p 160 mp4 192x144 25 │ 1006.62KiB 30k https │ avc1.4d400b 30k video only 144p, mp4_dash 278 webm 192x144 25 │ 1.70MiB 52k https │ vp9 52k video only 144p, webm_dash 133 mp4 320x240 25 │ 2.01MiB 61k https │ avc1.4d400d 61k video only 240p, mp4_dash 18 mp4 320x240 25 │ 9.42MiB 290k https │ avc1.42001E 290k mp4a.40.2 0k 44100Hz 240p 242 webm 320x240 25 │ 2.81MiB 86k https │ vp9 86k video only 240p, webm_dash None of these will be the format it was originally uploaded in, but I’d guess format 133 (~61 kbps) to be the closest for the video part, at 2MB. Judging by the sound of it, the original audio bit rate was probably higher than the video bit rate!"}]}});
